PMID,QID,Question,Answer,Evidence,Rationale
18715920,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors.","The paper does not mention obtaining HIV sequences from patient samples. It focuses on in vitro selection experiments and analysis of resistance mutations in HIV-1 integrase against elvitegravir and other integrase inhibitors, but it does not indicate that clinical specimens were sequenced for this purpose. The research was conducted using HIV-1 molecular clones and laboratory-generated virus samples, not patient-derived sequences."
18715920,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors. In addition, mutations that do not confer reduced susceptibility when present alone (H114Y, L74M, R20K, A128T, E138K, and S230R) are also discussed in relation to their position in the catalytic core domain and their proximity to known structural features of integrase.","The paper contains in vitro drug susceptibility data, detailing the susceptibility of HIV-1 strains to various integrase inhibitors, including elvitegravir, raltegravir, PACA, L-870,810, PICA, and L-731,988. The authors performed in vitro selection experiments to identify resistance mutations and assessed the susceptibility of selected strains to these inhibitors."
18715920,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors.","The paper does not mention any sequences being deposited in public databases. The study primarily focuses on laboratory-derived viruses and selected mutants, and it does not provide GenBank accession numbers or indicate that any sequences were made publicly available."
18715920,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors.","The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It primarily discusses laboratory-derived HIV-1 strains and selected mutants, focusing on their susceptibility to integrase inhibitors and the mutations associated with resistance."
18715920,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,Zero,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors.",The paper does not report obtaining samples from individuals for HIV sequencing. It focuses on in vitro selection experiments and analysis of resistance mutations in HIV-1 integrase against elvitegravir and other integrase inhibitors.
18715920,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors.","The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on laboratory-derived HIV-1 strains and in vitro selection experiments, not on clinical samples from specific countries."
18715920,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors.","The paper does not provide information about the years from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on in vitro studies and laboratory-derived HIV-1 strains, not on clinical samples with associated dates."
18715920,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"SDMs of IN coding sequences were constructed in the pUC19-5′HXB2D vector (XbaI-SalI fragment of pHXB2D), containing the HIV-1 clone HXB2D IN coding sequence, by using a QuikChange site-directed mutagenesis kit (Stratagene, La Jolla, CA) and high-performance liquid chromatography-purified primers (Genset Oligos, La Jolla, CA). Viral RNA was extracted from culture supernatant or virus stock using a NucliSens easyMAG apparatus (bioMérieux, Marcy l'Etoile, France), a high-throughput automated nucleic acid extraction system. cDNA encoding reverse transcriptase and IN was generated with Expand Reverse Transcriptase (Roche Diagnostics, Basel, Switzerland), followed by amplification of the IN region by nested PCR (1,456 bp). PCR products were genotyped by automated population-based full-sequence analysis (ABI Prism BigDye Terminator cycle sequencing; Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA).","The paper describes the construction of site-directed mutants (SDMs) and the sequencing of viral RNA from culture supernatant or virus stock, but it does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing."
18715920,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Integrase (IN), Reverse Transcriptase (RT)","Viral RNA was extracted from culture supernatant or virus stock using a NucliSens easyMAG apparatus (bioMérieux, Marcy l'Etoile, France), a high-throughput automated nucleic acid extraction system. cDNA encoding reverse transcriptase and IN was generated with Expand Reverse Transcriptase (Roche Diagnostics, Basel, Switzerland), followed by amplification of the IN region by nested PCR (1,456 bp). PCR products were genotyped by automated population-based full-sequence analysis (ABI Prism BigDye Terminator cycle sequencing; Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA).","The paper mentions the sequencing of the integrase (IN) gene and reverse transcripatse (RT) gene, from which it generates NGS assay."
18715920,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Population sequencing,"Viral RNA was extracted from culture supernatant or virus stock using a NucliSens easyMAG apparatus (bioMérieux, Marcy l'Etoile, France), a high-throughput automated nucleic acid extraction system. cDNA encoding reverse transcriptase and IN was generated with Expand Reverse Transcriptase (Roche Diagnostics, Basel, Switzerland), followed by amplification of the IN region by nested PCR (1,456 bp). PCR products were genotyped by automated population-based full-sequence analysis (ABI Prism BigDye Terminator cycle sequencing; Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA).","The paper discusses the use of population-based full-sequence analysis for genotyping the PCR products, which involves sequencing the bulk viral population rather than individual clones."
18715920,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Cell culture supernatant in vitro HIV stock,"Viral RNA was extracted from culture supernatant or virus stock using a NucliSens easyMAG apparatus (bioMérieux, Marcy l'Etoile, France), a high-throughput automated nucleic acid extraction system. cDNA encoding reverse transcriptase and IN was generated with Expand Reverse Transcriptase (Roche Diagnostics, Basel, Switzerland), followed by amplification of the IN region by nested PCR (1,456 bp). PCR products were genotyped by automated population-based full-sequence analysis (ABI Prism BigDye Terminator cycle sequencing; Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA).","The paper specifies that viral RNA was extracted from culture supernatant or virus stock for sequencing, indicating that the samples were not clinical specimens from patients, but from in vitro selections."
18715920,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors. The locations of the mutations are highlighted in the catalytic sites of integrase, and we correlate the mutations with expected drug-protein contacts. In addition, mutations that do not confer reduced susceptibility when present alone (H114Y, L74M, R20K, A128T, E138K, and S230R) are also discussed in relation to their position in the catalytic core domain and their proximity to known structural features of integrase. These data broaden the understanding of antiviral resistance against integrase inhibitors and may give insight facilitating the discovery of second-generation compounds.",The paper does not report obtaining sequences from individuals with virological failure. It focuses on in vitro selection experiments and laboratory-derived HIV-1 strains to study resistance mutations.
18715920,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors. The locations of the mutations are highlighted in the catalytic sites of integrase, and we correlate the mutations with expected drug-protein contacts. In addition, mutations that do not confer reduced susceptibility when present alone (H114Y, L74M, R20K, A128T, E138K, and S230R) are also discussed in relation to their position in the catalytic core domain and their proximity to known structural features of integrase. These data broaden the understanding of antiviral resistance against integrase inhibitors and may give insight facilitating the discovery of second-generation compounds.",The paper does not mention any clinical trials involving patients. It focuses on in vitro studies and laboratory-derived HIV-1 strains.
