18715920,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome is an essential step in the life cycle of retroviruses and is facilitated by the viral integrase enzyme. The first generation of integrase inhibitors recently approved or currently in late-stage clinical trials shows great promise for the treatment of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, but virus is expected to develop resistance to these drugs. Therefore, we used a novel resistance selection protocol to follow the emergence of resistant HIV in the presence of the integrase inhibitor elvitegravir (GS-9137). We find the primary resistance-conferring mutations to be Q148R, E92Q, and T66I and demonstrate that they confer a reduction in susceptibility not only to elvitegravir but also to raltegravir (MK-0518) and other integrase inhibitors. The locations of the mutations are highlighted in the catalytic sites of integrase, and we correlate the mutations with expected drug-protein contacts. In addition, mutations that do not confer reduced susceptibility when present alone (H114Y, L74M, R20K, A128T, E138K, and S230R) are also discussed in relation to their position in the catalytic core domain and their proximity to known structural features of integrase. These data broaden the understanding of antiviral resistance against integrase inhibitors and may give insight facilitating the discovery of second-generation compounds.",The paper does not report that individuals received any specific drugs before sample sequencing. It focuses on in vitro studies and laboratory-derived HIV-1 strains.
19104010,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"GS-9160 is a novel and potent inhibitor of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) integrase (IN) that specifically targets the process of strand transfer.; It is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration, since treatment of infected cells results in an elevation of two-long terminal repeat circles and a decrease of integration junctions.; Construction of infectious HIV-1 DNA clones with IN mutations. Mutations conferring resistance to IN inhibitors were introduced into the infectious wild-type HIV-1 DNA clone HXB2 by site-directed mutagenesis. Mutation(s) was confirmed by sequencing.; The antiviral activity of GS-9160 was evaluated in HIV-1-infected MT-2 and MT-4 cell lines as well as in primary human T lymphocytes (Table ​ Table2). 2 ).","The paper does not provide any data or mention that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the laboratory evaluation of GS-9160, an integrase inhibitor, using HIV-1 strains in cell culture systems rather than patient-derived sequences."
19104010,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"GS-9160 has potent and selective antiviral activity in primary human T lymphocytes producing a 50% effective concentration (EC ~50~ ) of ∼2 nM, with a selectivity index (50% cytotoxic concentration/EC ~50~ ) of ∼2,000.; The antiviral activity of GS-9160 was evaluated in HIV-1-infected MT-2 and MT-4 cell lines as well as in primary human T lymphocytes (Table ​ Table2). 2 ). The potency of GS-9160 was comparable in all three cell types, with an EC ~50~ range of 0.7 to 2 nM, and the selectivity indices (SI; 50% cytotoxic concentration (CC ~50~ )/EC ~50~ ) for GS-9160 were ∼2,000 in MT-2 cells and ∼2,600 in primary human T cells.; GS-9160 is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration in tissue culture as measured by both an elevation of 2-LTR circles and a decrease of integration junctions in HIV-1-infected cells.","The paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data by detailing the antiviral activity of GS-9160 against HIV-1 in various cell lines, including the determination of 50% effective concentrations (EC ~50~ ) and selectivity indices (SI) of the drug in these systems."
19104010,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"GS-9160 is a novel and potent inhibitor of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) integrase (IN) that specifically targets the process of strand transfer.; It is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration, since treatment of infected cells results in an elevation of two-long terminal repeat circles and a decrease of integration junctions.; Construction of infectious HIV-1 DNA clones with IN mutations. Mutations conferring resistance to IN inhibitors were introduced into the infectious wild-type HIV-1 DNA clone HXB2 by site-directed mutagenesis. Mutation(s) was confirmed by sequencing.; The antiviral activity of GS-9160 was evaluated in HIV-1-infected MT-2 and MT-4 cell lines as well as in primary human T lymphocytes (Table ​ Table2). 2 ). The potency of GS-9160 was comparable in all three cell types, with an EC ~50~ range of 0.7 to 2 nM, and the selectivity indices (SI; 50% cytotoxic concentration (CC ~50~ )/EC ~50~ ) for GS-9160 were ∼2,000 in MT-2 cells and ∼2,600 in primary human T cells. GS-9160 is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration in tissue culture as measured by both an elevation of 2-LTR circles and a decrease of integration junctions in HIV-1-infected cells.",The paper does not mention or provide any information regarding the public availability of any sequences. It focuses on the drug's efficacy and resistance patterns in cell culture models without discussing data submission to public databases.
19104010,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers,"GS-9160 is a novel and potent inhibitor of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) integrase (IN) that specifically targets the process of strand transfer.; It is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration, since treatment of infected cells results in an elevation of two-long terminal repeat circles and a decrease of integration junctions.; Construction of infectious HIV-1 DNA clones with IN mutations. Mutations conferring resistance to IN inhibitors were introduced into the infectious wild-type HIV-1 DNA clone HXB2 by site-directed mutagenesis. Mutation(s) was confirmed by sequencing.; The antiviral activity of GS-9160 was evaluated in HIV-1-infected MT-2 and MT-4 cell lines as well as in primary human T lymphocytes (Table ​ Table2). 2 ). The potency of GS-9160 was comparable in all three cell types, with an EC ~50~ range of 0.7 to 2 nM, and the selectivity indices (SI; 50% cytotoxic concentration (CC ~50~ )/EC ~50~ ) for GS-9160 were ∼2,000 in MT-2 cells and ∼2,600 in primary human T cells. GS-9160 is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration in tissue culture as measured by both an elevation of 2-LTR circles and a decrease of integration junctions in HIV-1-infected cells.","The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, as it does not report on any patient-derived sequences or their corresponding accession numbers."
