19104010,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"GS-9160 is a novel and potent inhibitor of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) integrase (IN) that specifically targets the process of strand transfer.; It is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration, since treatment of infected cells results in an elevation of two-long terminal repeat circles and a decrease of integration junctions.; Construction of infectious HIV-1 DNA clones with IN mutations. Mutations conferring resistance to IN inhibitors were introduced into the infectious wild-type HIV-1 DNA clone HXB2 by site-directed mutagenesis. Mutation(s) was confirmed by sequencing.; The antiviral activity of GS-9160 was evaluated in HIV-1-infected MT-2 and MT-4 cell lines as well as in primary human T lymphocytes (Table ​ Table2). 2 ). The potency of GS-9160 was comparable in all three cell types, with an EC ~50~ range of 0.7 to 2 nM, and the selectivity indices (SI; 50% cytotoxic concentration (CC ~50~ )/EC ~50~ ) for GS-9160 were ∼2,000 in MT-2 cells and ∼2,600 in primary human T cells. GS-9160 is an authentic inhibitor of HIV-1 integration in tissue culture as measured by both an elevation of 2-LTR circles and a decrease of integration junctions in HIV-1-infected cells.",The paper does not mention obtaining sequences from individuals with virological failure. It describes laboratory studies and resistance selections in cell culture models.
