23749954,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The preferential selection of K65R in HIV-1 subtype C is attenuated by nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation sites. We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations.",The paper does not mention any patient samples or sequences derived from patient samples. It focuses on in vitro studies and cell culture drug selections rather than clinical samples from individuals with HIV.
23749954,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Methods Cell culture drug selections were performed with various drugs in MT2 cells. Results The use of nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors N(t)RTIs as single drugs or in combination confirmed the more frequent selection of K65R by multiple N(t)RTIs in a subtype B virus that contained the 64/65 nucleotide polymorphisms of subtype C than in a wild-type subtype B virus. This effect was attenuated in the presence of several silent TAM nucleotide polymorphisms, except when stavudine was employed in the selection protocol.","The paper describes in vitro drug susceptibility experiments using cell culture drug selections. It provides data on the selection of the K65R mutation in response to various nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (N(t)RTIs). This indicates that the study does report in vitro drug susceptibility data, specifically related to the selection of resistance mutations in vitro."
23749954,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The preferential selection of K65R in HIV-1 subtype C is attenuated by nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation sites. We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations.",The paper does not mention any public availability of sequences. It focuses on in vitro studies and does not provide information about depositing sequences in public databases.
