23749954,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not reported,"The preferential selection of K65R in HIV-1 subtype C is attenuated by nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation sites. We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations.",The paper does not specify any sequencing methods used. It focuses on in vitro studies and does not provide details about sequencing techniques.
23749954,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,In vitro cultured virus,"The preferential selection of K65R in HIV-1 subtype C is attenuated by nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation sites. We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations. We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations. Here we report that silent nucleotide polymorphisms at TAM sites 70, 210 and/or 219 attenuate the previously described preferential selection of K65R in subtype C HIV-1. The introduction of silent TAM polymorphisms at these three sites into NL4-3 (64/65), which is a variant that behaves like subtype C on the 64/65 nucleotide polymorphisms at the 64/65 sites, resulted in a diminished K65R selection in cell cultures with multiple N(t)RTIs. In contrast, the K65R mutation was more frequently selected by N(t)RTIs in the wild-type NL4-3 virus without TAM polymorphisms. These results suggest that, within selected populations of viruses, nucleotide polymorphisms at TAM sites can attenuate the frequency of N(t)RTI resistance mutation selection in subtype C HIV-1 and provide additional insight into the in vivo development of K65R. In a follow-up study, we endeavored to clarify whether these silent TAM polymorphisms were able to exert their effects on K65R selection at the level of the single drug. Cell culture drug selections were performed. Thus, we investigated the contributions of subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation (TAM) sites 70, 210 and/or 219 that might reduce the previously described preferential selection of K65R in subtype C HIV-1 associated with subtype C-specific nucleotide polymorphisms at sites 64/65. Lentiviral constructs of the various viruses expressing the 64/65 sequences from both subtypes B and C and containing single nucleotide polymorphisms at positions 70, 210 or 219 were engineered as previously described and cell culture drug selection experiments with the various drugs were performed. The HIV-1 molecular clones with genes derived from the env and/or nef genes of isolates obtained in Africa, Asia, Europe or North America, which resemble the C viruses found in these countries, respectively. The gene fragments of antiterminator mutants, which allowed for repeated passage of viruses at drug selection, were amplified from these clones and opened in the pBlueScript vector as previously described. These clones were used in K65R studies. The K65R studies utilized dual tropic HIV-1NL4-3 viruses and the HAART drugs tenofovir DF (TDF), TRUVADA (Gilead), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, 3TC and abacavir.","The paper discusses the selection of the K65R mutation in vitro. The experiments were conducted using the HIV-1NL4-3 molecular clone expressing the 64/65 genotype and containing various TAM-polymorphism, which were in vitro cultured, so the study focuses on in vitro studies rather than clinical specimens."
23749954,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The preferential selection of K65R in HIV-1 subtype C is attenuated by nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation sites. We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations.",The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure. It focuses on in vitro studies and cell culture experiments.
