23749954,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The preferential selection of K65R in HIV-1 subtype C is attenuated by nucleotide polymorphisms at thymidine analogue mutation sites. We recently reported the preferential selection of the K65R resistance mutation in subtype C HIV-1 compared with subtype B and showed the underlying mechanism to be dependent on subtype C-specific silent nucleotide polymorphisms, i.e. genomic mutations that change the genotype but not the phenotype. The number of clinical reports demonstrating elevated numbers of K65R nevertheless suggests the existence of factors limiting the increased incidence of K65R mutations.",The paper does not mention any specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. It focuses on in vitro studies and cell culture experiments.
24227862,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. N348I confers resistance to both the NRTI zidovudine (ZDV) and the NNRTI nevirapine (NVP) and was also found to be associated with M184V and to compensate for deficits associated with the latter mutation. Now, we show that both N348I alone and N348I/M184V can prevent or delay the emergence of E138K under pressure with RPV or a related NNRTI, termed etravirine (ETR).","The paper does not provide specific patient sample sequences or refer to public databases for such sequences. It focuses on laboratory studies and mutations in HIV-1 RT without mentioning actual patient-derived sequences. The in vitro studies conducted do not involve sequencing of patient samples but rather recombinant viruses and cell culture models. Therefore, the evidence suggests that the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples."
24227862,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Phenotypic susceptibility analyses of RT inhibitors were performed with recombinant HIV-1 ~NL4.3~ clones in a TZM-bl cell-based *in vitro* assay, as described previously. To determine whether the N348I mutation, alone or in combination with M184V, might prevent the emergence of E138K under ETR or RPV pressure, we introduced the M184V, N348I, and M184V/N348I mutations into the HIV-1 NL4.3 proviral clone and performed viral passage experiments in CBMCs using increasing drug concentrations over a period of 19 weeks. Figure 1 shows the weekly increment of drug concentrations employed. In the selection experiments with ETR and RPV, we were unable to increase drug concentrations at the same rate as with the WT virus when the M184V/N348I double mutant virus was employed. With RPV, there was also a delay in the escalation of drug concentrations with the N348I single mutant virus compared to the WT virus or virus containing M184V.","The paper contains in vitro drug susceptibility data, as it discusses the use of TZM-bl cells to measure the susceptibility of HIV-1 ~NL4.3~ clones to RT inhibitors. The authors performed phenotypic susceptibility analyses, which involve testing the effectiveness of reverse transcriptase inhibitors on recombinant HIV-1 clones in a cell-based assay. This is evident in the Materials and Methods section where they describe the use of TZM-bl cells for in vitro susceptibility assays."
24227862,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"An HIV-1 RNA template, ∼500 nucleotides (nt) in size and spanning the 5′ untranslated region (UTR) to the primer-binding site (PBS), was transcribed in vitro from AccI-linearized pHIV-PBS DNA. To construct HIV-1 RT expression plasmids and recombinant HIV-1 NL4.3 infectious clones harboring the desired mutations in the RT gene, site-directed mutagenesis reactions were carried out by using a QuikChange II XL site-directed mutagenesis kit (Stratagene, La Jolla, CA). This work was performed with HIV-1 RT expression plasmid pbRT6H-PROT DNA and plasmid pNL4.3PFB DNA to generate recombinant RT enzymes and HIV-1 NL4.3 viruses containing the desired RT mutations. DNA sequencing was performed to verify the absence of spurious mutations and the presence of any desired mutation in the RT coding sequences.","The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences, particularly those associated with GenBank. It primarily discusses laboratory findings, mutational analyses, and resistance studies without providing or referencing any specific patient-derived HIV sequences that would be made publicly available in a database like GenBank."
24227862,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession number provided,"The Connection Domain Mutation N348I in HIV-1 Reverse Transcriptase Enhances Resistance to Etravirine and Rilpivirine but Restricts the Emergence of the E138K Resistance Mutation by Diminishing Viral Replication Capacity. N348I confers resistance to both the NRTI zidovudine (ZDV) and the NNRTI nevirapine (NVP) and was also found to be associated with M184V and to compensate for deficits associated with the latter mutation. Now, we show that both N348I alone and N348I/M184V can prevent or delay the emergence of E138K under pressure with RPV or a related NNRTI, termed etravirine (ETR). N348I also enhanced levels of resistance conferred by E138K against RPV and ETR by 2.2- and 2.3-fold, respectively.",The paper does not provide any specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on laboratory studies and mutations in HIV-1 RT without mentioning actual sequenced isolates or their corresponding accession numbers.
24227862,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. M184I/V was also common, together with E138K, in patients who failed combination antiretroviral FTC/TDF/RPV therapy in the ECHO and THRIVE clinical studies (36), due to mutual compensatory effects between the E138K and M184I/V substitutions in regard to enzyme processivity, polymerization efficiency, and viral replication capacity (9, 43, 44, 96). A strong association between N348I and M184V has also been observed, and the selection of N348I is associated primarily with the use of ZDV and NVP (8, 22, 24).",The paper does not mention any specific number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on laboratory studies and mutational analyses without providing information on the number of individuals involved in sample collection.
24227862,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. M184I/V was also common, together with E138K, in patients who failed combination antiretroviral FTC/TDF/RPV therapy in the ECHO and THRIVE clinical studies (36), due to mutual compensatory effects between the E138K and M184I/V substitutions in regard to enzyme processivity, polymerization efficiency, and viral replication capacity (9, 43, 44, 96). A strong association between N348I and M184V has also been observed, and the selection of N348I is associated primarily with the use of ZDV and NVP (8, 22, 24).",The paper does not provide information about the specific countries from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on laboratory studies and mutational analyses without mentioning the geographical origins of the samples.
24227862,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. M184I/V was also common, together with E138K, in patients who failed combination antiretroviral FTC/TDF/RPV therapy in the ECHO and THRIVE clinical studies (36), due to mutual compensatory effects between the E138K and M184I/V substitutions in regard to enzyme processivity, polymerization efficiency, and viral replication capacity (9, 43, 44, 96). A strong association between N348I and M184V has also been observed, and the selection of N348I is associated primarily with the use of ZDV and NVP (8, 22, 24).",The paper does not mention the years from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on laboratory studies and mutational analyses without providing a timeline for sample collection.
24227862,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The following reagents and cells were obtained through the NIH AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program: the infectious molecular clone pNL4-3, from Malcolm Martin; the TZM-bl (JC53-bl) cell line, from John C. Kappes, Xiaoyun Wu, and Tranzyme Inc.; NVP; and EFV. Selection for viral resistance mutations was performed by using increasing concentrations of RT inhibitors at starting concentrations below the 50% effective concentration (EC50), as described previously. As controls, all viruses were simultaneously passaged without drugs. Reverse transcriptase assays were performed, as described previously, to monitor viral replication.","The paper mentions that the molecular clone pNL4-3 was used to construct â€˜infectious clones harboring the desired mutations in the RT geneâ€™, which means that the samples were not cloned prior to sequencing, but samples were amplified using the molecular clone."
24227862,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,HIV-1 RT,"The Connection Domain Mutation N348I in HIV-1 Reverse Transcriptase Enhances Resistance to Etravirine and Rilpivirine but Restricts the Emergence of the E138K Resistance Mutation by Diminishing Viral Replication Capacity. In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. We now show that both N348I alone and N348I/M184V can prevent or delay the emergence of E138K under pressure with RPV or a related NNRTI, termed etravirine (ETR).","The paper focuses on the reverse transcriptase (RT) gene of HIV-1, specifically discussing mutations and their effects on drug resistance and viral replication capacity. The introduction and the molecular methods section all indicate that the RT gene was sequenced and analyzed in detail."
24227862,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not reported,"In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. We now show that both N348I alone and N348I/M184V can prevent or delay the emergence of E138K under pressure with RPV or a related NNRTI, termed etravirine (ETR). An HIV-1 RNA template, ~500 nucleotides (nt) in size and spanning the 5â€™ untranslated region (UTR) to the primer-binding site (PBS), was transcribed in vitro from AccI-linearized pHIV-PBS DNA by using an Ambion T7-MEGAshortscript kit. An HIV-1 RNA template, ~500 nucleotides (nt) in size and spanning the 5â€™ untranslated region (UTR) to the primer-binding site (PBS), was transcribed in vitro from AccI-linearized pHIV-PBS DNA (54) by using an Ambion T7-MEGAshortscript kit (Invitrogen, Burlington, ON, Canada) as described previously (55).","The paper defines the sequences that were used as templates for in vitro transcription and does not define the sequencing methodology, implying that traditional methods of sequencing were not utilized."
24227862,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,In vitro transcribed viral RNA,"An HIV-1 RNA template, ~500 nucleotides (nt) in size and spanning the 5â€™ untranslated region (UTR) to the primer-binding site (PBS), was transcribed in vitro from AccI-linearized pHIV-PBS DNA by using an Ambion T7-MEGAshortscript kit. An HIV-1 RNA template, ~500 nucleotides (nt) in size and spanning the 5â€™ untranslated region (UTR) to the primer-binding site (PBS), was transcribed in vitro from AccI-linearized pHIV-PBS DNA (54) by using an Ambion T7-MEGAshortscript kit (Invitrogen, Burlington, ON, Canada) as described previously (55).","The paper focuses on laboratory-generated viral RNA templates and recombinant viruses, not on clinical specimens. This focus indicates that the samples sequenced were not standard clinical specimens."
24227862,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We have recently shown that E138K consistently emerges in CBMCs under ETR selection pressure but not in MT2 cells that have larger dNTP pools (37, 48). We now also demonstrate that the addition of N348I to E138K further decreased viral replication in both cell types and that the addition of M184V to the E138K/N348I double mutant slightly increased viral replication capacity compared to E138K/N348I alone. These data help to explain the impact of N348I, alone or in combination with M184V, on the emergence of E138K. In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. N348I also enhanced levels of resistance conferred by E138K against RPV and ETR by 2.2- and 2.3-fold, respectively.","The paper mentions that the N348I mutation is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients, but it does not mention that the sequences were obtained from these individuals, furthermore,  laboratory studies were used to assess phenotypic drug susceptibility in TZM-bl cells."
24227862,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Our study also supports the idea that the connection domain should be monitored in routine HIV resistance genotyping, because of its potential to enhance levels of drug resistance. We have recently shown that E138K consistently emerges in CBMCs under ETR selection pressure but not in MT2 cells that have larger dNTP pools (37, 48). We now also demonstrate that the addition of N348I to E138K further decreased viral replication in both cell types and that the addition of M184V to the E138K/N348I double mutant slightly increased viral replication capacity compared to E138K/N348I alone. These data help to explain the impact of N348I, alone or in combination with M184V, on the emergence of E138K. In the ECHO and THRIVE clinical trials that led to the approval of RPV for use in treatment-naive patients, E138K was the most frequent NNRTI mutation at treatment failure (36), indicating that E138K is a signature mutation for this drug. In contrast, E138K is much rarer in patients who have failed therapy with ETR, even though this mutation confers resistance to ETR in tissue culture.",The paper does not mention whether the patients were part of a clinical trial. It focuses on laboratory studies and mutational analyses without providing information about clinical trial participation.
24227862,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We have recently shown that E138K consistently emerges in CBMCs under ETR selection pressure but not in MT2 cells that have larger dNTP pools (37, 48). We now also demonstrate that the addition of N348I to E138K further decreased viral replication in both cell types and that the addition of M184V to the E138K/N348I double mutant slightly increased viral replication capacity compared to E138K/N348I alone. These data help to explain the impact of N348I, alone or in combination with M184V, on the emergence of E138K. In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. N348I also enhanced levels of resistance conferred by E138K against RPV and ETR by 2.2- and 2.3-fold, respectively.","While the paper discusses mutations in HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT) and their association with antiretroviral (ARV) drugs, it does not provide specific sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. The focus is on laboratory studies and the implications of mutations on drug resistance."
24227862,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"We have recently shown that E138K consistently emerges in CBMCs under ETR selection pressure but not in MT2 cells that have larger dNTP pools (37, 48). We now also demonstrate that the addition of N348I to E138K further decreased viral replication in both cell types and that the addition of M184V to the E138K/N348I double mutant slightly increased viral replication capacity compared to E138K/N348I alone. These data help to explain the impact of N348I, alone or in combination with M184V, on the emergence of E138K. In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. M184I/V was also common, together with E138K, in patients who failed combination antiretroviral FTC/TDF/RPV therapy in the ECHO and THRIVE clinical studies (36), due to mutual compensatory effects between the E138K and M184I/V substitutions in regard to enzyme processivity, polymerization efficiency, and viral replication capacity (9, 43, 44, 96).","The paper mentions the role of N348I mutation in treatment experienced patients and that M184I/V was also common in patients who failed CTA-, but without reporting the specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing."
24227862,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"We have recently shown that E138K consistently emerges in CBMCs under ETR selection pressure but not in MT2 cells that have larger dNTP pools (37, 48). We now also demonstrate that the addition of N348I to E138K further decreased viral replication in both cell types and that the addition of M184V to the E138K/N348I double mutant slightly increased viral replication capacity compared to E138K/N348I alone. These data help to explain the impact of N348I, alone or in combination with M184V, on the emergence of E138K. In this context, we have investigated the role of a N348I connection domain mutation that is prevalent in treatment-experienced patients. M184I/V was also common, together with E138K, in patients who failed combination antiretroviral FTC/TDF/RPV therapy in the ECHO and THRIVE clinical studies (36), due to mutual compensatory effects between the E138K and M184I/V substitutions in regard to enzyme processivity, polymerization efficiency, and viral replication capacity (9, 43, 44, 96).","Although the paper discusses various antiretroviral drugs and their effects on HIV-1 mutations, it does not provide a specific list of drugs that were received by individuals before sample sequencing. The focus is on laboratory studies and the effects of mutations on drug resistance rather than on individual drug histories."
25637519,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The WT infectious plasmid pBRGX was constructed and confirmed from a clinical isolate of CRF08_BC subtype in our previous study. Drug resistance testing was performed using drug-susceptible virus. For the 96-well plates, drugs were diluted twofold from 100 μM to 0.1 nM for each drug except nevirapine (10 mM to 1 nM) and tested in triplicate in a virus-free clinker-based titer assay as described. The data were evaluated for statistical significance with an unpaired Student's t-test using Prism (version 5.0, GraphPad, Inc.). Individual data points from three independent experiments are presented as mean±SD. Viral replication capacity (RC) of drug-resistant mutants. Abstract- Objectives. & Abstract- Conclusions. & Materials and methods- Cells, plasmids and drugs. & Materials and methods- Drug susceptibility testing of the virus mutants. & Results- Phenotypic drug susceptibility of the virus mutants.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on laboratory-generated mutations and their effects on drug resistance and viral replication capacity, but there is no indication that patient-derived sequences were used or reported."
25637519,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Viral susceptibility to nine RT inhibitors, viral RC and the DNA polymerase activity of viral RT of the constructed virus mutants were investigated. TZM-bl cells were infected with virus variants and treated with serially diluted RT inhibitors. Individual data points from three independent experiments are presented as mean±SD. Viral replication capacity (RC) of drug-resistant mutants. Abstract- Objectives. & Abstract- Conclusions. & Results- Phenotypic drug susceptibility of the virus mutants.","The paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data. The results include specific measurements of resistance levels to various NNRTIs and NRTIs in mutant viruses, which were tested using a single-cycle cell-culture-based phenotypic assay."
25637519,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The WT infectious plasmid pBRGX was constructed and confirmed from a clinical isolate of CRF08_BC subtype in our previous study. Drug resistance testing was performed using drug-susceptible virus. For the 96-well plates, drugs were diluted twofold from 100 μM to 0.1 nM for each drug except nevirapine (10 mM to 1 nM) and tested in triplicate in a virus-free clinker-based titer assay as described. The data were evaluated for statistical significance with an unpaired Student's t-test using Prism (version 5.0, GraphPad, Inc.). Individual data points from three independent experiments are presented as mean±SD. Viral replication capacity (RC) of drug-resistant mutants. Abstract- Objectives. & Abstract- Conclusions. & Materials and methods- Cells, plasmids and drugs. & Materials and methods- Drug susceptibility testing of the virus mutants. & Results- Phenotypic drug susceptibility of the virus mutants.",The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or any other indication that sequences were made publicly available. The focus is on laboratory-generated mutations and their effects on drug resistance.
25637519,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,"The WT infectious plasmid pBRGX was constructed and confirmed from a clinical isolate of CRF08_BC subtype in our previous study. CD4 T cells were obtained from HIV-1 integrase strand transfer inhibitor Evan (Stribild) naïve subjects in Melbourne, Australia or naïve subjects at Tianjin First Center Hospital in Tianjin, China. Abstract- Objectives. & Abstract- Conclusions. & Materials and methods- Cells, plasmids and drugs. & Materials and methods- Drug susceptibility testing of the virus mutants.  & Results- Phenotypic drug susceptibility of the virus mutants.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers. It focuses on laboratory-generated mutations and their phenotypic effects rather than on sequences from patient isolates.
