25637519,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"The WT infectious plasmid pBRGX was constructed and confirmed from a clinical isolate of CRF08_BC subtype in our previous study. The WT infectious plasmid pNL4-3 was constructed based on the pBRGX plasmid. Materials and methods- Cells, plasmids and drugs.","The paper does not mention any specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing, as it does not report on any patient data."
26246578,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"We have previously shown that the addition of the raltegravir/elvitegavir (RAL/EVG) primary resistance mutation N155H to the R263K dolutegravir (DTG) resistance mutation partially compensated for the fitness cost imposed by R263K while also slightly increasing DTG resistance in vitro (K. Anstett, T. Mesplede, M. Oliveira, V. Cutillas, and M. A. Wainberg, J Virol 89: 4681--4684, 2015, doi: 10.1128/JVI.03485-14).

Since many patients failing RAL/EVG are given DTG as part of rescue therapy, and given that the N155H substitution often is found in combination with other compensatory resistance mutations in such individuals, we investigated the effects of multiple such substitutions within integrase (IN) on each of integrase function, HIV-1 infectivity, and levels of drug resistance.

To this end, each of the L74M, E92Q, T97A, E157Q, and G163R substitutions were introduced into NL4.3 subtype B HIV-1 vectors harboring N155H and R263K in tandem termed NL4.3 IN(N155H/R263K).

Relevant recombinant integrase enzymes also were expressed, and purified and biochemical assays of strand transfer efficiency as well as viral infectivity and drug resistance studies were performed.","The paper does not mention any patient samples from which HIV sequences were obtained. Instead, it focuses on laboratory-based studies using recombinant HIV-1 vectors and biochemical assays to study drug resistance and viral infectivity. The absence of patient-derived sequences is a clear indication that the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples, as it primarily involves in vitro experiments and does not provide details about patient demographics or sample collection procedures. Additionally, the mention of laboratory constructs and recombinant proteins further reinforces the lack of patient samples in the study, indicating a focus on controlled experiments rather than clinical data. Therefore, the answer to the question is 'No.'"
26246578,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"We have previously shown that the addition of the raltegravir/elvitegavir (RAL/EVG) primary resistance mutation N155H to the R263K dolutegravir (DTG) resistance mutation partially compensated for the fitness cost imposed by R263K while also slightly increasing DTG resistance in vitro (K. Anstett, T. Mesplede, M. Oliveira, V. Cutillas, and M. A. Wainberg, J Virol 89: 4681--4684, 2015, doi: 10.1128/JVI.03485-14).
HIV susceptibilities to DTG, RAL, and EVG were measured by the infection of 30,000 TZM-bl cells using 100,000 RT units per well of each virus in the presence of 1:10 serial dilutions of drugs. After 48 h, cells were lysed and luciferase production was measured using the luciferase assay system (Promega, Madison, WI)","The paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data, specifically focusing on the susceptibilities of HIV-1 to integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) such as dolutegravir (DTG), raltegravir (RAL), and elvitegravir (EVG). The experiments included measuring the HIV susceptibilities to these drugs by infecting TZM-bl cells with various NL4.3 viral clones and assessing the levels of infection at different drug concentrations. The results of these susceptibility assays are presented in the figures and tables within the paper and are discussed in detail in the results and discussion sections. Therefore, the answer to the question is 'Yes.'"
