26559830,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Four Amino Acid Changes in HIV-2 Protease Confer Class-Wide Sensitivity to Protease Inhibitors. We constructed HIV-2 ~ROD9~ molecular clones encoding the corresponding wild-type HIV-1 amino acids (I32V, V47I, M76L, and I82V) either individually or together (clone PRΔ4) and compared the phenotypic sensitivities (50% effective concentration [EC50]) of mutant and wild-type viruses to nine FDA-approved PI.","The paper does not mention sequencing HIV from patient samples. It focuses on in vitro studies using molecular clones of HIV-1 and HIV-2, discussing the genetic modifications made to these clones and the resulting effects on protease inhibitor susceptibility. There's no mention of patient samples, demographic data, or clinical histories, which are typically included when reporting sequences from patient samples."
26559830,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Four Amino Acid Changes in HIV-2 Protease Confer Class-Wide Sensitivity to Protease Inhibitors. We constructed HIV-2 ~ROD9~ molecular clones encoding the corresponding wild-type HIV-1 amino acids (I32V, V47I, M76L, and I82V) either individually or together (clone PRΔ4) and compared the phenotypic sensitivities (50% effective concentration [EC50]) of mutant and wild-type viruses to nine FDA-approved PI.","The paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data by comparing the phenotypic sensitivities of mutant and wild-type HIV-2 clones to nine FDA-approved protease inhibitors. The results are presented in terms of 50% effective concentrations (EC ~50~ s), demonstrating the varying susceptibilities of different clone constructs to the inhibitors tested."
26559830,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Four Amino Acid Changes in HIV-2 Protease Confer Class-Wide Sensitivity to Protease Inhibitors. We constructed HIV-2 ~ROD9~ molecular clones encoding the corresponding wild-type HIV-1 amino acids (I32V, V47I, M76L, and I82V) either individually or together (clone PRΔ4) and compared the phenotypic sensitivities (50% effective concentration [EC50]) of mutant and wild-type viruses to nine FDA-approved PI.","The paper does not mention that any sequences were made publicly available. It focuses on the construction and testing of molecular clones in vitro, without providing GenBank accession numbers or any information about public data availability."
