26559830,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"Four Amino Acid Changes in HIV-2 Protease Confer Class-Wide Sensitivity to Protease Inhibitors. The protease inhibitors amprenavir (APV), atazanavir (ATV), darunavir (DRV), indinavir (IDV), lopinavir (LPV), nelfinavir (NFV), ritonavir (RTV), saquinavir (SQV), and tipranavir (TPV) were obtained from the NIH AIDS Reagent Program. We constructed HIV-2 ROD9 molecular clones encoding the corresponding wild-type HIV-1 amino acids (I32V, V47I, M76L, and I82V) either individually or together (clone PRΔ4) and compared the phenotypic sensitivities (50% effective concentration EC50) of mutant and wild-type viruses to nine FDA-approved PI.",The paper mentions that the wild-type HIV-2 constructs were obtained from patient samples but does not mention which drug classes were received by individuals.
26559830,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"Four Amino Acid Changes in HIV-2 Protease Confer Class-Wide Sensitivity to Protease Inhibitors. The protease inhibitors amprenavir (APV), atazanavir (ATV), darunavir (DRV), indinavir (IDV), lopinavir (LPV), nelfinavir (NFV), ritonavir (RTV), saquinavir (SQV), and tipranavir (TPV) were obtained from the NIH AIDS Reagent Program. We constructed HIV-2 ROD9 molecular clones encoding the corresponding wild-type HIV-1 amino acids (I32V, V47I, M76L, and I82V) either individually or together (clone PRΔ4) and compared the phenotypic sensitivities (50% effective concentration EC50) of mutant and wild-type viruses to nine FDA-approved PI.",The paper mentions that the wild-type HIV-2 constructs were obtained from patient samples but does not mention which drugs were received by individuals.
27009474,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N. The sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to the GenBank under the accession number KU761565.,"The paper discusses a specific individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance and provides details about the HIV-1 sequences obtained from this patient. Additionally, it mentions that the sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to GenBank, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples are reported."
27009474,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N. We created a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility. These clones were subjected for phenotypic susceptibility testing using the PhenoSense assay (Monogram).,"The paper mentions that the clones were subjected to phenotypic susceptibility testing using the PhenoSense assay, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data is reported."
27009474,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to the GenBank under the accession number KU761565.,"The paper explicitly states that the sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to GenBank, indicating that the sequences from the study were made publicly available."
27009474,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,KU761565,The sequence of the clinical clone has been submitted to the GenBank under the accession number KU761565.,The paper specifies that the GenBank accession number for the sequenced HIV isolate is KU761565.
27009474,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,One,We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N.,"The paper describes a single individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance, indicating that only one individual had samples obtained for HIV sequencing."
27009474,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not provide specific information regarding the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained. It only mentions the individual's demographics.
27009474,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2014-2015,"A 50-year-old hispanic homosexual man underwent HIV-1 seroconversion in August 2014. In November 2014, the patient changed clinics and an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample. In 2015, we created a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility.",The paper provides specific timeframes indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from 2014 to 2015.
27009474,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Yes,"In 2015, we created a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility. The panel included site-directed HIV-1 mutants containing G140S+Q148N, Q148N alone, G140S+Q148H, and Q148H alone.","The paper mentions the creation of a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the mutation, indicating that samples were cloned prior to sequencing."
27009474,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase,We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance whose virus sequence had the previously uncharacterized mutation Q148N. Q148 is conserved in all lentiviruses and plays an essential role in integrase strand transfer activity.,"The paper discusses mutations in the integrase gene and their impact on drug resistance, indicating that the integrase gene was sequenced."
27009474,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify the exact sequencing method used.
27009474,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"In November 2014, the patient changed clinics and an INSTI resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample. The test revealed G140S, a nonpolymorphic accessory INSTI-resistance mutation, and Q148N, a previously uncharacterized mutation in the INSTI-binding pocket of integrase enzyme. At the same time, the plasma HIV-1 RNA level was <50 copies/ml and CD4 count was 767 cells/mm3.","The paper mentions the plasma HIV-1 RNA level was <50 copies/ml that indicates HIV is in plasma, which means HIV was obtained from plasma sample."
27009474,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Infectious molecular HIV-1 clones containing Q148N alone and in combination with G140S demonstrated ∼2.4--4.5 reduced elvitegravir susceptibility depending on the virus's genetic context but retained susceptibility to raltegravir and dolutegravir. This level of reduced elvitegravir susceptibility is lower than that observed with Q148H/K/R and in fact the infected individual responded to an initial treatment regimen containing tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat. Q148N was associated with a higher replication capacity than Q148H, suggesting that this mutation may be more fit in the absence of selective INSTI therapy.",The paper describes an individual who experienced virological failure on an INSTI-containing regimen and mentions the low susceptbility of HIV-1 to elvitegravir which indicates virological failure.
27009474,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention that the patient was enrolled in a clinical trial; it focuses on a case report.
27009474,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"In 2015, we created a panel of infectious molecular clones to assess the effect of the novel mutation Q148N on INSTI susceptibility. The patient was started on the once daily integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing fixed-dose combination tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat. Q148N was associated with a higher replication capacity than Q148H, suggesting that this mutation may be more fit in the absence of selective INSTI therapy.",The paper describes a patient who received treatment with an insti-containing regimen and mentions the patient received the fixed-dose combination suggesting that the patient had previously received ARV drugs.
27009474,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","We describe an individual with transmitted four-class drug resistance. The patient was started on the once daily integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing fixed-dose combination tenofovir/emtricitabine/elvitegravir/cobicistat. A baseline genotypic resistance test revealed the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor-resistance mutations L210W and T215D that were interpreted as causing intermediate resistance to zidovudine and low-level resistance to tenofovir; the non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-resistance mutations K101P and K103S that were interpreted as causing high-level resistance to each of the NNRTIs; and the protease inhibitor-resistance mutations D30N, L33F, I54V, N88D, and L90M that were interpreted as causing intermediate or high-level resistance to each of the PIs except darunavir. All drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing.","The paper mentions the presence of resistance mutations associated with NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, indicating that the individuals had received drugs from all these classes."
27009474,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"tenofovir, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, cobicistat, zidovudine, tenofovir DF, K103N, efavirenz, nevirapine, lopinavir, atazanavir, darunavir, raltegravir","Our patient underwent HIV-1 seroconversion in August 2014 with a CD4 count of 405 cells/mm3 and an HIV-1 RNA level of 3980 copies/ml. A baseline genotypic resistance test revealed nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI)-resistance mutations L210W and T215D, interpreted as associated with intermediate resistance to zidovudine (AZT) and low-level resistance to tenofovir DF (TDF) [29]. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-resistance mutations K101P and K103S, interpreted as conferring high-level resistance to all NNRTIs, were also present K102N, K103N]. After baseline genotypic resistance testing, the patient was started on the once daily integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI)-containing fixed-dose combination tenofovir/emmtricitabine/evlitegravir/cobicistat. In November 2014, the individual changed clinics and an INSTI-resistance test was ordered on his August 2014 sample. This test revealed G140S, a nonpolymorphic accessory INSTI-resistance mutation, and Q148N, a previously uncharacterized mutation. The patient’s baseline genotypic resistance test was reported as being associated with intermediate resistance to raltegravir and elvitegravir based on the presence of G140S. Q148N was present in viruses from three raltegravir-experienced persons in the Monogram database as of May, 2014. Six K148 Paediar study participants in Thailand on raltegravir and non-nucleoside FDCs (in efavirenz and nevirapine) have all showed phenotypic virological suppression (>0.3 log viral rebounds), meaning low susceptibility to both drugs, though no Q148N mutations were documented.","The paper mentions the presence of resistance mutations associated with NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, indicating that the individuals had received drugs from all these classes."
27124362,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The paper discusses the antiviral activity of Rilpivirine and Doravirine against NNRTI-resistant HIV-1 mutants, but it does not mention obtaining HIV sequences from patient samples. It focuses on in vitro studies and molecular modeling rather than patient-derived sequences.","The content of the paper primarily revolves around the efficacy of RPV and DOR against various NNRTI-resistant HIV-1 mutants and their binding interactions with the RT. There is no mention or indication that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, as the emphasis is on laboratory studies and drug susceptibility assays."
27124362,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Using a single round HIV-1 infection assay, we tested RPV and DOR against a broad panel of NNRTI resistant mutants to determine their respective activities. We also used molecular modeling to determine if the susceptibility profile of each compound was related to how they bind RT.
The ability of RPV and DOR to inhibit the replication of WT HIV-1 and drug-resistant mutants was measured using a previously described single-round infection assay [19]. We initially investigated the L100I, K103N, Y181C, Y188L, H221Y, and K103N/Y181C RT mutants.","The paper provides in vitro drug susceptibility data by testing the antiviral activity of Rilpivirine (RPV) and Doravirine (DOR) against various NNRTI-resistant HIV-1 mutants using a single-round infection assay. The study measures the IC50 values of the drugs against wild-type and mutant viruses, indicating the susceptibility of the virus to the drugs tested."
