28559249,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"There is a pressing need to identify more effective antiretroviral drugs for HIV-2 treatment. Here, we show that the investigational compound MK-8591 (4′-ethynyl-2-fluoro-2′-deoxyadenosine B EFdA D ) is highly active against group A and B isolates of HIV-2; 50% effective concentrations B EC 50 D for HIV-2 were, on average, 4.8-fold lower than those observed for HIV-1. MK-8591 also retains potent activity against multinucleoside-resistant HIV-2 mutants (EC 50 B ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ~ ␀A0 50 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ~ ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ~ ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 Less ␀A0 ␀A0 Less ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 Less ␀A0 Less 0","The paper discusses the efficacy of the investigational compound MK-8591 against HIV-2 isolates and drug-resistant HIV-2 mutants. It focuses on in vitro drug susceptibility testing and does not mention any clinical samples or patient-derived HIV sequences. The study evaluates MK-8591's activity against HIV-2 strains isolated from ART-naive individuals, but it does not provide details on sequencing HIV from patient samples, Therefore, the answer is 'No.' The paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples as it focuses on in vitro drug susceptibility of MK-8591 against HIV-2 isolates."
28559249,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Here, we show that the investigational compound MK-8591 (4′-ethynyl-2-fluoro-2′-deoxyadenosine B EFdA D ) is highly active against group A and B isolates of HIV-2; 50% effective concentrations B EC 50 D for HIV-2 were, on average, 4.8-fold lower than those observed for HIV-1. MK-8591 also retains potent activity against multinucleoside-resistant HIV-2 mutants (EC 50 B ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ~ ␀A0 50 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ~ ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ~ ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ~ ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 ␀A0 Less ␀A0 Less 0}","The paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data for the investigational compound MK-8591 against HIV-2 isolates and drug-resistant HIV-2 mutants. The study includes detailed measurements of 50% effective concentrations (EC50) for MK-8591 against various HIV-2 strains and mutants, demonstrating the compound's efficacy in culture. The data include comparisons of MK-8591's activity against HIV-1 and HIV-2, as well as against mutants resistant to other nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Therefore, the answer is 'Yes.' The paper provides in vitro drug susceptibility data, including EC50 measurements for MK-8591 against HIV-2 isolates."
