32601157,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs).",The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples as it focuses on the effectiveness of integrase inhibitors against drug-resistant mutants rather than on patient-derived sequences.
32601157,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"In order to compare the effectiveness of the INSTIs, we calculated the fold change (FC) in the 50% effective concentration (EC50) values for the IN mutants versus wild-type (WT) HIV-1. We determined the antiviral potencies of DTG, BIC, CAB, and our INSTIs against the new accessory amino acid substitutions C56S and G149A, which are within approximately 18.5 Å and 11.5 Å of the metal ions bound at the IN active site and were reported not to enhance the fitness of the virus in cultured cells. HIV-based viral vectors with either WT or mutant IN were used in single-round infectivity assays to determine the antiviral potencies (EC50 values) of the compounds as previously described. Briefly, vesicular stomatitis virus-g (VSV-g)-pseudotyped HIV was produced by transfections of 293T cells with pNLNgoMIVR-ΔLUC and pHCMV-g (obtained from Jane Burns, University of California, San Diego, CA) using the calcium phosphate method. Six to seven h after the calcium phosphate precipitate was added, 293T cells were washed twice with phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) and incubated with fresh medium for 48 h. The virus-containing supernatants were then harvested, clarified by low-speed centrifugation, filtrated, and further diluted for preparation in antiviral infection assays. On the day prior to the screen, human osteosarcoma (HOS) cells were seeded in a 96-well luminescence cell culture plate at a density of 4,000 HOS cells in 100 μl per well. The luciferase activity was set to 100, and the infectivity of the mutant viruses was measured as a percentage of that of the WT. We compared the relative potencies of our promising INSTIs in assays using a group of newly reported resistant mutants, which were selected in vitro. The development of INSTIs has led to improvements in the treatment options available for HIV-1-infected individuals, and this family of compounds is widely used in combination therapies.",The paper includes in vitro drug susceptibility data as it mentions the effectiveness of various integrase inhibitors against drug-resistant mutants in vitro assays.
32601157,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Both DTG and BIC potently inhibit most INSTI-resistant IN mutants selected by the INSTIs raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG). BIC has not been reported to select for resistance in treatment-naive patients, and DTG has selected for a small number of resistant viruses in treatment-naive patients. However, some patients who had viruses with substitutions selected by RAL and EVG responded poorly when switched to DTG-based therapies, and there are mutants that cause a considerable decrease in the potencies of DTG and BIC in in vitro assays. The new INSTI cabotegravir (CAB), which is in late-stage clinical trials, has been shown to select for novel resistant mutants in vitro. Thus, it is important to develop new and improved INSTIs that are effective against all the known resistant mutants.","The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or public availability of sequences, focusing instead on drug efficacy."
32601157,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,"The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Both DTG and BIC potently inhibit most INSTI-resistant IN mutants selected by the INSTIs raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG). BIC has not been reported to select for resistance in treatment-naive patients, and DTG has selected for a small number of resistant viruses in treatment-naive patients. However, some patients who had viruses with substitutions selected by RAL and EVG responded poorly when switched to DTG-based therapies, and there are mutants that cause a considerable decrease in the potencies of DTG and BIC in in vitro assays. The new INSTI cabotegravir (CAB), which is in late-stage clinical trials, has been shown to select for novel resistant mutants in vitro. Thus, it is important to develop new and improved INSTIs that are effective against all the known resistant mutants.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.
