32601157,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No,"The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Both DTG and BIC potently inhibit most INSTI-resistant IN mutants selected by the INSTIs raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG). BIC has not been reported to select for resistance in treatment-naive patients, and DTG has selected for a small number of resistant viruses in treatment-naive patients. However, some patients who had viruses with substitutions selected by RAL and EVG responded poorly when switched to DTG-based therapies, and there are mutants that cause a considerable decrease in the potencies of DTG and BIC in in vitro assays. The new INSTI cabotegravir (CAB), which is in late-stage clinical trials, has been shown to select for novel resistant mutants in vitro. Thus, it is important to develop new and improved INSTIs that are effective against all the known resistant mutants.","The paper does not mention any drug classes received by individuals prior to sample sequencing, focusing instead on in vitro effectiveness of integrase inhibitors."
32601157,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No,"The currently recommended first-line therapy for HIV-1-infected patients is an integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI), either dolutegravir (DTG) or bictegravir (BIC), in combination with two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs). Both DTG and BIC potently inhibit most INSTI-resistant IN mutants selected by the INSTIs raltegravir (RAL) and elvitegravir (EVG). BIC has not been reported to select for resistance in treatment-naive patients, and DTG has selected for a small number of resistant viruses in treatment-naive patients. However, some patients who had viruses with substitutions selected by RAL and EVG responded poorly when switched to DTG-based therapies, and there are mutants that cause a considerable decrease in the potencies of DTG and BIC in in vitro assays. The new INSTI cabotegravir (CAB), which is in late-stage clinical trials, has been shown to select for novel resistant mutants in vitro. Thus, it is important to develop new and improved INSTIs that are effective against all the known resistant mutants.","The paper does not mention any specific drugs received by individuals prior to sample sequencing, focusing instead on the in vitro study of integrase inhibitors."
33855437,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Patient-derived subtype B ( *n =* 3) and non-B clinical isolates ( *n =* 3), as well as the HIV NL4.3 recombinant clone were subjected to *in vitro* cell culture selections to ascertain emergent resistance to doravirine and rilpivirine.","The paper mentions patient-derived subtype B and non-B clinical isolates, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples are reported."
33855437,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,Cell culture selections reveal favourable drug resistance profiles for doravirine and islatravir. Cell-based phenotypic assays assessed cross-resistance patterns to NNRTIs by acquired resistance mutations.,The paper discusses in vitro drug susceptibility data through cell culture selections and phenotypic assays.
33855437,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Isolate 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894 , MW484895 , MW484893 , DQ380549 , respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent. The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618 ), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma'ayan (Israel).17 The 96USSN20 isolate18 (GenBank MW48492 ) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493 ) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program.","The paper provides GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
33855437,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"MW484894, MW484895, MW484893, DQ380549, AF492618, MW48492, AF324493","Isolate 14637, 14969, 5326 and 6343 (GenBank accession numbers MW484894 , MW484895 , MW484893 , DQ380549 , respectively) were derived from drug-naive individuals at Clinique Actuel obtained with ethics approval (Veritas) and informed consent. The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618 ), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma'ayan (Israel).17 The 96USSN20 isolate18 (GenBank MW48492 ) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493 ) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program.","The paper lists several GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, providing a direct answer to the question."
33855437,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,6,"Patient-derived subtype B ( *n =* 3) and non-B clinical isolates ( *n =* 3), as well as the HIV NL4.3 recombinant clone were subjected to *in vitro* cell culture selections to ascertain emergent resistance to doravirine and rilpivirine. Subtype B ( *n =* 3), non-B subtype ( *n =* 3), and pNL4.3 viral isolates were passaged in cord blood mononuclear cells with progressively increasing concentrations of drug(s).","The paper mentions that samples were obtained from 3 subtype B and 3 non-B clinical isolates, indicating a total of 6 individuals."
33855437,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Ethiopia, Israel, USA, Canada","The subtype C isolate, 4742 (GenBank accession number AF492618), originating from Ethiopia was kindly provided by Shlomo Ma'ayan (Israel). The 96USSN20 isolate18 (GenBank MW48492) and pNL4.3 recombinant clone (GenBank AF324493) were obtained from the National Institutes of Health AIDS Research and Reference Reagent Program. As  of July 2020 the total number of subjects genotyped in the Quebec Genotyping program was 5357.  Quebec is located in Canada. All subjects were treatment-naïve individuals. This work was supported in part by grants from Merck investigator grant (58422) and Fonds de Recherche du Québec Santé-Réseau SIDA.","The paper mentions isolates originating from Ethiopia, and Canada, and isolates obtained from the US NIH repository. The study is conducted in Canada."
33855437,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not provided,No relevant evidence,"The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained, leaving this question unanswered."
33855437,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence provided.,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that samples were not cloned."
33855437,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not specifically provided,"Genotypic analysis compared the acquisition and accumulation of drug resistance mutations at weeks 8 and 24 following drug pressure. Viral RNA was extracted, and genotypic analyses of all clinical isolates was performed at weeks 8 and 24 of selective drug pressure to evaluate the monitor the acquisition and accumulation of amino acid substitutions (i.e. drug resistance mutations). Cell-based phenotypic assays assessed cross-resistance patterns to NNRTIs by acquired resistance mutations.","The paper mentions genotypic resistance analysis, retrospective genotypic analysis, cross-resistance assays, but does not mention any RT or codon sequences being directly reported as sequenced."
33855437,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not provided,No relevant evidence,"The paper does not provide details on the sequencing method used, leaving this question unanswered."
33855437,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not specifically provided,"Viral isolates were serially passaged in cord blood mononuclear cells (CBMCs) in the presence of escalating concentrations of doravirine and rilpivirine alone or paired with islatravir or lamivudine (3TC) as described previously.19–21 Briefly, stepwise drug-dose escalations from the half-maximal effective concentrations (EC50 values) were performed based on weekly reverse transcriptase (RT) assays that determined the extent of viral inhibition of drug-treated samples as compared with matching no-drug controls for wild-type isolates.19 At each weekly passage, an aliquot of culture supernatant was stored at −70°C for subsequent genotypic and phenotypic analyses. Viral RNA was extracted, and genotypic analyses of all clinical isolates was performed at weeks 8 and 24 of selective drug pressure to evaluate the monitor the acquisition and accumulation of amino acid substitutions (i.e. drug resistance mutations).21 A cell-based in vitro phenotypic assay monitored the impact of the progressive accumulation of select mutations on drug susceptibility to doravirine, rilpivirine, etravirine and efavirenz.21 Briefly, resistant viruses were amplified from stored cell cultural supernatants at designated weeks (8, 13, 17, 24 or 30) following in vitro selection with doravirine or rilpivirine and re-genotyped.",The papper does not mention any clinical specimens or types being directly sequenced.
33855437,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,No evidence provided,The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure.
33855437,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,No evidence provided,The paper does not mention any clinical trial involvement for the patients.
33855437,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,All subjects were treatment-naïve individuals.,The paper mentions all individuals were treatment naïve during the study or in Quebec in its resistance monitoring program.
33855437,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,All subjects were treatment-naïve individuals.,The paper mentions all individuals were treatment naïve during the study or in Quebec in its resistance monitoring program.
33855437,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,All subjects were treatment-naïve individuals.,The paper mentions all individuals were treatment naïve during the study or in Quebec in its resistance monitoring program.
34516245,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"To study EFdA resistance patterns that may emerge in naive or tenofovir (TFV)-, emtricitabine/lamivudine (FTC/3TC)-, or zidovudine (AZT)-treated patients, we performed viral passaging experiments starting with WT, K65R, M184V, or D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q HIV-1. Using recombinant viruses, we validated the role for M184V as the primary determinant of EFdA resistance; none of the observed connection subdomain (R358K and E399K) or RNase H domain (A502V) mutations significantly contributed to EFdA resistance.","The paper focuses on viral passaging experiments and recombinant viruses, which means sequences were not obtained from patient samples. Instead, it mentions using stock viruses and laboratory assays to study drug resistance, so the answer is No."
34516245,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"To evaluate the degree of resistance selected in the passaging experiments, sensitivity to EFdA was determined for virus supernatants from passage 6 (P ~6~ ) onward. Data are presented for the viruses obtained from P ~6~ , P ~9~ , and, in one case, P ~10~ . P ~6~ was the first passage for which extensive sequencing analysis was performed, P ~9~ was the effective endpoint for the WT-, M184V-, and D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q-derived viruses, and P ~10~ was chosen as the endpoint for the K65R-derived strain. For each of the viruses, the later passages demonstrated increased EC ~50~ values compared to WT and earlier passage viruses, demonstrating that resistance had been acquired as expected ( Fig. 2 ).","The paper includes in vitro drug susceptibility data, particularly related to the drug 4′-Ethynyl-2-fluoro-2′-deoxyadenosine (EFdA). It discusses the selection of resistance to EFdA by serial passage, dose-response studies to assess the degree of resistance, and the susceptibility of select mutants to other antiretroviral drugs such as tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (FTC). These experiments provide detailed in vitro data on the effectiveness of the drug against various HIV-1 mutants and their resistance profiles, including EC50 values, which are standard measurements of drug susceptibility in vitro.. The ability of the viruses to grow in the presence of EFdA was assessed by time to viral breakthrough (defined as 75% syncytium formation) at each passage. This time to breakthrough and the concentration of EFdA in each passage is shown in Fig. 1 . For WT, M184V, and D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q strains, virus breakthrough typically occurred after approximately 7 days. As EFdA concentrations were raised in passages 8 and 9, the time to virus breakthrough increased, until virus replication was no longer observed in passage 10. Passage 10 corresponded to an EFdA concentration of 550 to 800 nM ( Fig. 1A , ​ ,C, C , and ​ andD D )."
34516245,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,Mutation sequences for BankIt2501100 Seq1 to Seq14 have been deposited in GenBank under accession no. OK263149 to OK263162.,"The paper states that the mutation sequences have been deposited in GenBank, which is a public database, indicating that the sequences are publicly available."
34516245,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OK263149 to OK263162,Mutation sequences for BankIt2501100 Seq1 to Seq14 have been deposited in GenBank under accession no. OK263149 to OK263162.,"The paper explicitly lists the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which are provided as a specific range."
34516245,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"To study EFdA resistance patterns that may emerge in naive or tenofovir (TFV)-, emtricitabine/lamivudine (FTC/3TC)-, or zidovudine (AZT)-treated patients, we performed viral passaging experiments starting with WT, K65R, M184V, or D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q HIV-1. Using recombinant viruses, we validated the role for M184V as the primary determinant of EFdA resistance; none of the observed connection subdomain (R358K and E399K) or RNase H domain (A502V) mutations significantly contributed to EFdA resistance.",The paper does not mention any patients from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. The study focuses on viral passaging experiments and using recombinant viruses.
34516245,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"To study EFdA resistance patterns that may emerge in naive or tenofovir (TFV)-, emtricitabine/lamivudine (FTC/3TC)-, or zidovudine (AZT)-treated patients, we performed viral passaging experiments starting with WT, K65R, M184V, or D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q HIV-1. Using recombinant viruses, we validated the role for M184V as the primary determinant of EFdA resistance; none of the observed connection subdomain (R358K and E399K) or RNase H domain (A502V) mutations significantly contributed to EFdA resistance.","The paper does not provide any information about the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not report on patient samples."
34516245,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"To study EFdA resistance patterns that may emerge in naive or tenofovir (TFV)-, emtricitabine/lamivudine (FTC/3TC)-, or zidovudine (AZT)-treated patients, we performed viral passaging experiments starting with WT, K65R, M184V, or D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q HIV-1. Using recombinant viruses, we validated the role for M184V as the primary determinant of EFdA resistance; none of the observed connection subdomain (R358K and E399K) or RNase H domain (A502V) mutations significantly contributed to EFdA resistance.","The paper does not mention the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not involve patient samples."
34516245,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Yes,"Approximately 105 ng of full-length PCR product was ligated into the pGEM-T vector system (Promega, Madison, WI) at a 3:1 insert/vector molar ratio and incubated overnight at 4°C. Ligations were transformed into MAX Efficiency DH5α competent cells (Invitrogen) by heat shock. Individual mutant virus stocks were then generated, and TZM-GFP cells were infected with serially diluted EFdA, TDF, or FTC, and population assays were done to determine effectiveness at blocking replication in TZM-GFP cells expressing β-galactosidase, Luciferase, and green fluorescent protein as described above. Dose-response curves were produced for HIV-1 mutants A114S, M184I, M184V, and A502V in the existing TZM-GFP cell system. A TCL plasmid-based assay for intracellular ATP determination was previously described.67 The production of select mutants with molecular clones in the NL4-3 infectious clone background parallels the transport and replication kinetics by 4′-ethynyl NRTI (EFdA, tenofovir dephosphonyl methyl ester, merbromin, and maribavir) commensurate to isoform hENT1 and 3 transporters SLA.9195 Aldose reductase-like intracellular adenosine kinase catalyzes dephosphorylation commensurate to in vivo concentrations of 3TC, ZDV, ddA, BDVR, EDT, EFdA, TFV, FTC, and FDC. The full-length infectious clones were generated as previously described.","The paper mentions the use of clonal sequencing to confirm the identity of the viruses, as well as the use of clonal sequencing to identify changes in the reverse transcriptase gene during passaging. These processes involve cloning samples before sequencing."
34516245,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,RT,"Viral RNA was purified from supernatants using the QIAamp Viral RNA minikit (Qiagen, Valencia, CA), the concentration was determined with a Spectronic BioMate3 UV spectrophotometer (Thermo Scientific), and 500 ng was used as the template for cDNA synthesis. First-strand PCR was performed using random hexamer primers and the SuperScript III first-strand synthesis system for RT-PCR (Invitrogen, Carlsbad, CA). The resulting cDNA was PCR amplified using the HIV-1 LAI-specific primers ABR-RT-OF (1763 5′-GGAGCCGATAGACAAGGAACTG-3′) and ABR-RT-OR2 (3863 5′-GGCTACTATTTCTTTTGCTACTACAGG-3′). These primers anneal to the 3′ end of gag and the 5′ end of integrase, respectively, and generate a 2,127-bp product spanning the full length of the reverse transcriptase gene. PCR was performed using the Expand High-Fidelity PCR System dNTPack (Roche Diagnostics GmbH, Mannheim, Germany), with 4.5 mM MgCl2 and 3.5 U of enzyme mix used for each reaction. Reactions were run in a PCR Sprint bench-top PCR cycler (Thermo Electron) with an initial denaturation of 3 min at 94°C, followed by 30 amplification cycles, each consisting of 30 s at 94°C, 45 s at 58°C, and 150 s at 72°C. After a 7-min final extension at 72°C, the samples were used immediately or stored at −20°C. Clonal sequencing confirmed the identity of the P0 stock viruses. Similar sequencing was carried out on clones from various passages to identify any changes in RT that may have arisen during replication in the presence of EFdA. Table 1 summarizes the data for P6 and P9 isolates for all parental viruses, as well as viruses from P6, P9, and P10 for K65R. The WT P6 population sequencing revealed only M184I (Table 1); by P9, approximately 30% of sequences were M184I, 30% were M184I/E399K, and the remainder were divided between M184V and WT.","The paper focuses on the sequencing of the reverse transcriptase (RT) gene of HIV-1. It details the use of specific primers for the RT gene, the cloning of RT sequences, and the analysis of mutations in the RT gene during passaging."
34516245,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Population sequencing,"To evaluate the degree of resistance selected in the passaging experiments, sensitivity to EFdA was determined for virus supernatants from passage 6 (P6) onward. Data are presented for the viruses obtained from P6, P9, and, in one case, P10. P6 was the first passage for which extensive sequencing analysis was performed, P9 was the effective endpoint for the WT-, M184V-, and D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q-derived viruses, and P10 was chosen as the endpoint for the K65R-derived strain. For each of the viruses, the later passages demonstrated increased EC50 values compared to WT and earlier passage viruses, demonstrating that resistance had been acquired as expected (Fig. 2).",The paper mentions the use of population sequencing to analyze the viruses obtained from the passaging experiments. It discusses the use of consensus sequences and the analysis of the proportion of different mutations in the viral population.
34516245,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Viral RNA purified from supernatents,"To evaluate the degree of resistance selected in the passaging experiments, sensitivity to EFdA was determined for virus supernatants from passage 6 (P6) onward. Data are presented for the viruses obtained from P6, P9, and, in one case, P10. P6 was the first passage for which extensive sequencing analysis was performed, P9 was the effective endpoint for the WT-, M184V-, and D67N/K70R/T215F/K219Q-derived viruses, and P10 was chosen as the endpoint for the K65R-derived strain. For each of the viruses, the later passages demonstrated increased EC50 values compared to WT and earlier passage viruses, demonstrating that resistance had been acquired as expected (Fig. 2).",The paper mentions the use of virus supernatants for sequencing after the virus was passaged in MT-2 cells and the virus was harvested. The sequencing is done on the viruses obtained after these passaging experiments.
