36415058,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The propensity for long-acting cabotegravir and rilpivirine every 2 months among HIV-infected people eligible for treatment. After clinical chart review, individuals with HIV-RNA <50 copies/ml, a negative HBsAg, and no previous failure or documented resistance to integrase inhibitors or non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (i.e., eligible for cabotegravir and rilpivirine), coming to our clinic for a routine ambulatory visit during 2021, were consecutively interviewed by the referring physician.",The paper specifically mentions that individuals with no previous failure or documented resistance to integrase inhibitors or non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors were included.
36415058,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The propensity for long-acting cabotegravir and rilpivirine every 2 months among HIV-infected people eligible for treatment. CARISEL is a Phase 3b, multicenter, open-label, implementation-effectiveness study that examined the acceptability, appropriateness, and feasibility of LA cabotegravir and rilpivirine injections in 18 HIV centers in Europe.","The paper does not indicate that the patients were part of a clinical trial, it describes a study on patient preferences."
36415058,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"This study investigated the propensity to switch to a long-acting regimen of cabotegravir and rilpivirine in people living with HIV-1, all of whom were receiving effective ART, as indicated by an HIV-RNA <50cop/ml; after clinical chart review, individuals who had already experienced casirivimab/evoshid, had a positive history of previous experience their regimen, of injectable CAB+ RPV, and did not possess previous failure/resistance to NRTIs/NNRTIs were interviewed.","The paper mentions that individuals had a median 12.5 years of exposure to ART, and the study population had to have experienced previous therapy with CAB+ RPV, and the history of previous experienced regimen."
36415058,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, II","This study investigated the propensity to switch to a long-acting regimen of cabotegravir and rilpivirine in people living with HIV-1, all of whom were receiving effective ART, as indicated by an HIV-RNA <50cop/ml; after clinical chart review, individuals who had already experienced casirivimab/evoshid, had a positive history of previous experience their regimen, of injectable CAB+ RPV, and did not possess previous failure/resistance to NRTIs/NNRTIs were interviewed.","The paper mentions that individuals had a history of previous experience antiretroviral therapy regimens including NRTI, NNRTI, PI, II, though without specific drugs listed, but still indicating the received drug classes."
36415058,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, II","This study investigated the propensity to switch to a long-acting regimen of cabotegravir and rilpivirine in people living with HIV-1, all of whom were receiving effective ART, as indicated by an HIV-RNA <50cop/ml; after clinical chart review, individuals who had already experienced casirivimab/evoshid, had a positive history of previous experience their regimen, of injectable CAB+ RPV, and did not possess previous failure/resistance to NRTIs/NNRTIs were interviewed.","The paper mentions that individuals had a history of previous experience antiretroviral therapy regimens including NRTI, NNRTI, PI, II, though without specific drugs listed, but still indicating the received drugs."
36454248,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"HIV-1 pol sequences were recovered from 67 (88.2%) of 76 children/adolescents (median age 12 years), carrying 65 subtype B, 1 subtype G and 1 unique recombinant URF_A1B.","The paper includes a study that analyzes HIV-1 drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in 76 HIV-infected children and adolescents in Panama. It mentions that HIV-1 pol sequences were recovered from 67 of these samples, indicating that sequences from patient samples were reported."
36454248,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This study describes the DRM prevalence in HIV-infected paediatric patients in Panama. During 2018–19, plasma was collected from 76 HIV-infected children/adolescents (5 ART-naive, 71 treated) in Panama for HIV-1 DRM pol analysis, predicted antiretroviral (ARV) susceptibility by Stanford, and HIV-1 variant phylogenetic characterization. HIV-1 pol sequences were recovered from 67 (88.2%) of 76 children/adolescents (median age 12 years), carrying 65 subtype B, 1 subtype G and 1 unique recombinant URF_A1B. Five were ART-naive and 62 ART-treated under virological failure (viraemia >50 copies/mL) with previous exposure to NRTIs, (100%), NNRTIs (45.2%), PIs (95.2%) and integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs, 17.7%). Among the treated patients, 34 (54.8%) carried resistant strains, with major DRMs to one (40.3%), two (9.7%) or three (4.8%) ARV families. Most of them harboured DRMs to NRTIs (58.5%) or NNRTIs (39%), but also major DRMs to PIs (4.9%) and INSTIs (6.5%). We also found dual-class NRTI + NNRTI (12.2%) and NNRTI + PI (2.6%) resistance. Two naive subjects carried viruses with DRMs to NRTIs and NRTI + NNRTI, respectively. Sequenced viruses presented high/intermediate resistance mainly to emtricitabine/lamivudine (48.9% each) and efavirenz/nevirapine (33.3% each). Most participants were susceptible to PIs (91.3%) and INSTIs (88.1%).","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing. Instead, it focuses on the prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in patient samples and uses software to predict antiretroviral (ARV) susceptibility based on the sequences obtained."
36454248,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,HIV-1 sequences were submitted to GenBank with the following accession numbers: OM201778 -- OM201846.,"The paper explicitly states that the HIV-1 sequences were submitted to GenBank, a public database for genetic sequences, confirming that the sequences were made publicly available."
36454248,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OM201778 -- OM201846,HIV-1 sequences were submitted to GenBank with the following accession numbers: OM201778 -- OM201846.,"The paper provides the specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, confirming their availability in the database."
36454248,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,76,Plasma samples from 76 HIV-positive naive or ART-treated children and adolescents with therapeutic failure under clinical follow-up at this hospital were collected during 2018--19.,"The paper mentions that plasma was collected from 76 HIV-infected children/adolescents, indicating the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
36454248,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Panama,"This study describes the DRM prevalence in HIV-infected paediatric patients in Panama. Plasma samples from 76 HIV-positive naive or ART-treated children and adolescents with therapeutic failure under clinical follow-up at this hospital were collected during 2018–19. The samples were kept at −20°C until transported on dry ice to the HIV-1 Molecular Epidemiology Laboratory in Madrid, Spain, where they were stored at −80°C until processing.","The paper clearly states that the samples were collected from HIV-infected children/adolescents in Panama, specifying the country of origin for the sequenced samples."
36454248,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018-2019,"Plasma samples from 76 HIV-positive naive or ART-treated children and adolescents with therapeutic failure under clinical follow-up at this hospital were collected during 2018–19. The inadequacy of resistance monitoring in Latin America leads to circulation of HIV strains with drug resistance mutations (DRMs), compromising ART effectiveness. This study describes the DRM prevalence in HIV-infected paediatric patients in Panama.","The paper mentions that the samples were collected during 2018–19, providing a specific time frame for when the sequencing was conducted."
36454248,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the direct amplification and sequencing of the HIV-1 pol region from the plasma samples.
36454248,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","Viral sequences included the complete HIV-1 PR (codons 1--99), partial RT (1--345) and IN (48--285) for genotyping study of DRMs to PIs, NRTIs, NNRTIs and integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs).","The paper describes the sequencing of the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) regions of the HIV-1 genome, confirming the genes that were sequenced."
36454248,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not Specified,"The extracted RNA was amplified by RT–PCR and nested PCR to obtain the HIV-1 pol region, using primers designed by WHO for protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) amplification and ANRS primers for integrase (IN) amplification, 25 , 26 as previously described. 27, 28 PCR amplicons were purified with illustraTM ExoProStarTM (Cytiva) and sequenced by Macrogen Inc. Lasergene software was used to assemble and manually edit the sequences.","The paper does not specify the exact sequencing method used (such as Sanger sequencing, NGS, etc.). It mentions sequencing by Macrogen, but does not give further detail."
36454248,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Plasma samples from 76 HIV-positive naive or ART-treated children and adolescents with therapeutic failure under clinical follow-up at this hospital were collected during 2018–19. HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma by automated magnetic silica extraction using the EasyMAG extractor (bioMérieux). The extracted RNA was amplified by RT–PCR and nested PCR to obtain the HIV-1 pol region, using primers designed by WHO for protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) amplification and ANRS primers for integrase (IN) amplification, 25 , 26 as previously described. 27, 28 PCR amplicons were purified with illustraTM ExoProStarTM (Cytiva) and sequenced by Macrogen Inc. Lasergene software was used to assemble and manually edit the sequences. Viral sequences included the complete HIV-1 PR (codons 1--99), partial RT (1--345) and IN (48--285) for genotyping study of DRMs to PIs, NRTIs, NNRTIs and integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs). Stanford HIVdb Program v9.0 (Stanford University, Palo Alto, CA, USA) ( https://hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-sequences/ ) was used to characterize DRMs in pre-treated children/adolescents and predict the resistance level to 25 ARVs in pol genotypes. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutation prevalence was established among the ART-naive population by the WHO TDR list 2009 implemented in the Calibrated Population Resistance (CPR) tool v8.0 ( https://hivdb.stanford.edu/cpr/ ) and by the Stanford algorithm v9.0, both available on the Stanford HIV website ( https://hivdb.stanford.edu/ ).","The paper specifies that plasma samples were collected and used for sequencing the HIV-1 pol region, confirming the type of clinical specimens analyzed."
36454248,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"All 62 children/adolescents treated with *pol* sequence had received NRTIs, 45.2% non-NRTIs, 95.2% PIs and only 11 (17.7%) INSTIs. Despite the use of ART, all treated children/adolescents with *pol* sequence showed virological failure (VL > 50 copies/mL).","The paper mentions that 62 of the 71 treated subjects were under virological failure, indicating that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
36454248,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a cohort study of children and adolescents receiving clinical follow-up.
36454248,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Plasma samples from 76 HIV-positive naive or ART-treated children and adolescents with therapeutic failure under clinical follow-up at this hospital were collected during 2018–19. The 71 treated subjects were under their first (33.8%), second (29.6%) or third (16.9%) ART regimen, and 14 (19.7%) had received four to eight different ART regimens at sampling, having failed at least three previous ART regimens. At sampling, all 71 treated children/adolescents had received NRTIs, 45.1% NNRTIs, 95.8% PIs and just 11 (15.5%) patients were INSTI-experienced .","The paper mentions that 71 of the 76 children and adolescents were treated with antiretroviral drugs, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
36454248,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Most (95%) were infected perinatally and all are under ART, 43% of them reaching HIV viral suppression. At sampling, all 71 treated children/adolescents had received NRTIs, 45.1% NNRTIs, 95.8% PIs and just 11 (15.5%) patients were INSTI-experienced (Table 1). The regimen composition of the latest ART before sampling of the 71 treated patients is summarized in Table S2 (available as Supplementary data)   .JAC Online). Most treated children (98.6%) had received lamivudine + zidovudine, followed by lopinavir / ritonavir (95.8%) and efavirenz (45.1%). About half (64.3%) of the 70 treated children with available data had received ART immediately after HIV diagnosis. However, four (5.7%) suffered a long delay in treatment, ranging from 7 months to 7 years from the first diagnosis and the start of ART. Three of the five naive children started ART immediately upon HIV diagnosis, right when the analysed sample was collected. However, the remaining two started ART 5 months and 3 years, respectively, after HIV diagnosis","The paper lists the various antiretroviral drugs that were part of the regimens received by the 71 treated subjects, indicating the classes of drugs used."
36454248,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"lamivudine, zidovudine, lopinavir/ritonavir, efavirenz, emtricitabine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, raltegravir","Most (95%) were infected perinatally and all are under ART, 43% of them reaching HIV viral suppression. At sampling, all 71 treated children/adolescents had received NRTIs, 45.1% NNRTIs, 95.8% PIs and just 11 (15.5%) patients were INSTI-experienced (Table 1). The regimen composition of the latest ART before sampling of the 71 treated patients is summarized in Table S2 (available as Supplementary data)   .JAC Online). Most treated children (98.6%) had received lamivudine + zidovudine, followed by lopinavir / ritonavir (95.8%) and efavirenz (45.1%). About half (64.3%) of the 70 treated children with available data had received ART immediately after HIV diagnosis. However, four (5.7%) suffered a long delay in treatment, ranging from 7 months to 7 years from the first diagnosis and the start of ART. Three of the five naive children started ART immediately upon HIV diagnosis, right when the analysed sample was collected. However, the remaining two started ART 5 months and 3 years, respectively, after HIV diagnosis","The paper provides detailed information on the specific drugs that were part of the treatment regimens for the 71 ART-treated subjects, indicating the drugs received before sequencing."
36519389,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper discusses the CHARACTERISE study, which focuses on weight and metabolic changes after switching antiretroviral therapy regimens. There is no mention of obtaining HIV sequences from patient samples, as the study primarily evaluates clinical outcomes related to treatment regimens and their effects on weight and metabolic parameters rather than genetic sequencing of the virus or drug resistance data. Therefore, the answer is 'No'. Overall, the content of the paper does not suggest that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported. The primary focus is on clinical outcomes rather than genetic sequencing or drug resistance data. While the study involves HIV treatment and monitoring HIV RNA levels, it does not delve into the genetic analysis of the virus or the sequencing of patient samples. Hence, the conclusion is that the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples. Although the study involves HIV treatment and monitoring HIV RNA levels, it does not delve into the genetic analysis of the virus or the sequencing of patient samples. Hence, the conclusion is that the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples."
