36519389,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Not applicable,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper does not mention that any samples were cloned prior to sequencing, as it does not report on any sequencing of HIV samples. The focus is on the clinical outcomes of different antiretroviral therapy regimens and their effects on weight and metabolic parameters, rather than on the preparation of samples for sequencing. The study involves monitoring HIV RNA levels and patient demographics but does not include any information regarding sample cloning procedures, as no samples were sequenced. The study is clearly centered around clinical outcomes of switching ART regimens, including changes in weight, metabolic parameters, and HIV RNA levels. No mention of cloning samples prior to sequencing is made, indicating that this procedure was not performed. Hence, the conclusion is that sample cloning is not applicable."
36519389,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,None,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper does not report on any sequencing of HIV genes, as it primarily focuses on clinical outcomes related to different antiretroviral therapy regimens. The study evaluates weight and metabolic changes as well as HIV RNA levels, but does not involve the sequencing of any HIV genes. The focus is on the clinical effectiveness and safety of the treatment regimens, rather than on the genetic analysis of the virus. The study centers on clinical outcomes of switching ART regimens, including changes in weight, metabolic parameters, and HIV RNA levels. No genetic sequencing of HIV genes is mentioned, making it clear that no such sequencing was performed. Hence, the conclusion is that no HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced."
36519389,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not applicable,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper does not report on any sequencing of HIV samples, as it primarily focuses on clinical outcomes related to different antiretroviral therapy regimens. The study evaluates weight and metabolic changes as well as HIV RNA levels, but does not involve the sequencing of any HIV samples, thereby not providing any details regarding the methodology used for sequencing. The focus is on the clinical effectiveness and safety of the treatment regimens, rather than on the genetic analysis of the virus. The study centers on clinical outcomes of switching ART regimens, including changes in weight, metabolic parameters, and HIV RNA levels. No sequencing is mentioned, thus no sequencing method is applicable."
36519389,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,None,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper does not report on any sequencing of samples, as it primarily focuses on clinical outcomes related to different antiretroviral therapy regimens. The study evaluates weight and metabolic changes as well as HIV RNA levels, but does not involve the sequencing of any patient samples. The focus is on the clinical effectiveness and safety of the treatment regimens, rather than on the sample types used for sequencing. The study centers on clinical outcomes of switching ART regimens, including changes in weight, metabolic parameters, and HIV RNA levels. No sequencing of samples is mentioned, indicating that no samples were sequenced."
36519389,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper mentions that participants switching between different antiretroviral regimens showed HIV RNA levels that were undetectable, indicating effective viral suppression and thus no virological failure. The study focuses on the clinical outcomes of switching antiretroviral therapy regimens, including changes in weight, metabolic parameters, and monitoring of HIV RNA levels, but does not provide details suggesting that sequences were obtained from individuals experiencing virological failure. The study also mentions adherence counseling for participants with elevations in HIV RNA, indicating a focus on managing potential virological failure rather than obtaining sequences from such individuals. Additionally, virological failure typically entails a monitoring of HIV RNA levels, which is mentioned in the study, but does not include obtaining sequences. Hence, the conclusion is that no sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
36519389,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,The trial was approved by the University of the Witwatersrand Human Research Ethics Committee and received local regulatory approval. All patients provided informed consent before any study procedures.,"The paper describes the CHARACTERISE study, which is an extension of the ADVANCE trial, indicating that the patients were part of a clinical trial. The study involves randomized participants and the evaluation of clinical outcomes after switching antiretroviral therapy regimens, which are characteristic features of clinical trials. The approval from a human research ethics committee and the process of informed consent are also indicative of a clinical trial setting. Hence, the conclusion is that the patients were in a clinical trial."
36519389,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper discusses the CHARACTERISE study, which focuses on weight and metabolic changes after switching antiretroviral therapy regimens. Although it mentions individuals had received multiple tenofovir-based regimens, it does not mention sequences from individuals who had previously received antiretroviral drugs. It primarily evaluates clinical outcomes related to treatment regimens, including changes in weight, metabolic parameters, and HIV RNA levels, but does not provide information about sequencing or the reporting of sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. Hence, the conclusion is that the paper does not report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
36519389,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not applicable,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper describes the CHARACTERISE study, which focuses on the clinical outcomes of switching between different antiretroviral regimens. The specific antiretroviral drugs mentioned are all nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), but the study does not mention the sequencing of samples, hence this question is not applicable. The study does not involve direct sequencing of samples, therefore the specific drug classes received prior to sequencing is not applicable."
36519389,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not applicable,"Participants randomized to first-line tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)/emtricitabine (FTC)+dolutegravir (DTG), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)/FTC + DTG, or TDF/FTC/efavirenz (EFV) for 192 weeks were then switched to TDF/lamivudine (3TC)/DTG for 52 weeks. Participants switching either TAF/FTC + DTG or TDF/FTC/EFV to TDF/3TC/DTG showed statistically significant reductions in weight, low-density lipoprotein, triglycerides, glucose and glycated hemoglobin.","The paper discusses the CHARACTERISE study, which focuses on the clinical outcomes of switching between different antiretroviral regimens. The specific antiretroviral drugs mentioned are all nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), but the study does not mention the sequencing of samples, hence this question is not applicable. The study does not involve direct sequencing of samples, therefore the specific drugs received prior to sequencing is not applicable."
36571282,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Reverse transcriptase and INSTI sequences were provided by participating centers. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion.","The paper mentions that sequences were provided by participating centers, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
36571282,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing-based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)-based assay. For NGS sequences, a 20% consensus was generated and used for this study. The Stanford HIV database (version 9.1) was used for sequence alignment, quality assessment, resistance interpretation, and subtype assignment.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. The focus is on the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors and nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors in newly diagnosed people with HIV.
36571282,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Data are available to investigators upon reasonable request.,"The paper states that data are available to investigators upon reasonable request, but there is no mention of whether the sequences were made publicly available."
36571282,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
36571282,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,2705,"We included 2705 PWH, 72% men, median age of 37 years (interquartile range, 30--48); 43.7% were infected by non-B subtypes.","The paper reports that 2705 people with HIV (PWH) were included in the study, indicating that samples were obtained from this number of individuals for HIV sequencing."
36571282,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain","MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018--2021.","The paper mentions that the study included participants from France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from these countries."
36571282,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018-2021,"MeditRes is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH newly diagnosed in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain during 2018--2021.","The paper states that the study included participants diagnosed during 2018--2021, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from these years."
36571282,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"A total of 358 integrase sequences (13%) only reached codon 170 because in some Spanish centers, especially during 2018 and 2019, an NGS protocol that did not cover positions of interest 230 and 263 was used.","There is no mention of samples being cloned prior to sequencing. The paper describes the use of different sequencing protocols, including Sanger sequencing and NGS."
36571282,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Reverse transcriptase and integrase,"We evaluated the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) and nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and of clinically relevant resistance (CRR) in newly diagnosed people with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV; PWH) naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Europe. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing--based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)--based assay. The Stanford HIV database (version 9.1) was used for sequence alignment, quality assessment, resistance interpretation, and subtype assignment. We followed Stanford HIVDB recommendations to recategorize Stanford resistance interpretation into a 3-level category (resistant-R, susceptible-S, and intermediate-I).",The paper mentions that reverse transcriptase and integrase sequences were provided by participating centers and used for the study.
36571282,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and next-generation sequencing,"The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing--based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)--based assay.","The paper describes the use of different sequencing protocols at testing sites, including Sanger sequencing and next-generation sequencing (NGS)."
36571282,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Methods. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. The integrase region from HIV was sequenced using different protocols at testing sites, including the Sanger sequencing-based assay and next-generation sequencing (NGS)-based assay. For NGS sequences, a 20% consensus was generated and used for this study. The Stanford HIV database (version 9.1) was used for sequence alignment, quality assessment, resistance interpretation, and subtype assignment. We followed Stanford HIVDB recommendations to recategorize Stanford resistance interpretation into a 3-level category (resistant-R, susceptible-S, and intermediate-I).",The study includes all newly diagnosed people living with HIV from the participating centers with an available plasma sample.
36571282,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We evaluated the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) and nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and of clinically relevant resistance (CRR) in newly diagnosed people with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV; PWH) naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Europe. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase and NRTI-SDRM prevalence was measured in ART-naive, newly diagnosed PWH included in MeditRes 2018--2021.",The paper focuses on newly diagnosed people with HIV who are naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) and does not mention sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure.
36571282,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"MeditRes HIV is a consortium that includes ART-naive PWH in France, Greece, Italy, Portugal, and Spain who have been newly diagnosed during 2018--2021. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included.","The paper describes a consortium MeditRes  for surveillance of transmitted drug resistance in five European countries, and does not mention inclusion in clinical trials."
36571282,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We evaluated the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) and nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and of clinically relevant resistance (CRR) in newly diagnosed people with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV; PWH) naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Europe. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase and NRTI-SDRM prevalence was measured in ART-naive, newly diagnosed PWH included in MeditRes 2018--2021.",The paper focuses on newly diagnosed people with HIV who are naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) and does not mention sequences obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.
36571282,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We evaluated the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) and nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and of clinically relevant resistance (CRR) in newly diagnosed people with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV; PWH) naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Europe. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase and NRTI-SDRM prevalence was measured in ART-naive, newly diagnosed PWH included in MeditRes 2018--2021.",The paper focuses on newly diagnosed people with HIV who are naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) and does not mention sequences obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.
36571282,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We evaluated the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) to integrase strand-transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) and nucleoside/nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and of clinically relevant resistance (CRR) in newly diagnosed people with human immunodeficiency virus (HIV; PWH) naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Europe. For this study, participating sites were asked to provide a FASTA viral sequence, encoding the HIV integrase and RT obtained at the time of inclusion. All naive PWH consequently diagnosed during the study period from the participating centers with an available sample were included. Integrase and NRTI-SDRM prevalence was measured in ART-naive, newly diagnosed PWH included in MeditRes 2018--2021.",The paper focuses on newly diagnosed people with HIV who are naive to antiretroviral therapy (ART) and does not mention sequences obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.
36597160,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Our primary objective was to assess the efficacy and tolerance of the switch to E/C/F/TDF measured by determination at W48 of the proportion of patients still on E/C/F/TDF and with plasma HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL. At baseline (date of the switch), the following data were extracted: date of HIV infection, date of first ART, number of previous ART and type of regimen, nadir CD4 T cell count or CD4 T cell count at ART initiation, CD4 T cell count (within 6 months before the switch), the reason for switching to E/C/F/TDF, creatinine levels and estimated Glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) using MDRD, history of prior virologic failure and previous genotypes. During the study period, genotypes were performed using Sanger sequencing.","The study does not mention the presence of HIV sequences from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on the efficacy and tolerance of a specific treatment regimen for HIV-1 infection, genotypic resistance testing, and clinical outcomes related to treatment switching. While resistance to integrase inhibitors was mentioned, it does not confirm the sample sequence from the patient in the paper.."
36597160,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We assessed the virologic efficacy of switching to co-formulated elvitegravir, cobicistat, emtricitabine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (E/C/F/TDF) in patients with controlled HIV infection. Most patients (69.9%) were male, with median age 44 years (IQR 38--51), who had been on ART for a median of 7 years (IQR 4--13). Median CD4 count was 614/mm3 and 24.6% of the patients had a history of previous virological failure. The reasons for switching were simplification (67.0%) and tolerance issues (22.0%). At week 48, 314 (82.0% [95% CI 78.4--86.0]) patients had HIV RNA < 50 copies/mL, 13 (3.5% [95% CI 3.64--8.41]) experienced virological failure. Genotype at failure was available in 6/13 patients with detection of resistance-associated mutations to integrase inhibitors and NRTIs in 5/6 (83.3%) patients.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen and the genotypic analysis of resistance mutations in patients who experienced virological failure.
36597160,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Data will not be shared publicly. Data could be made available to any researcher interested. Deidentified participant data would be made available with a data dictionary and shared under a Data Transfer Agreement. Data can be requested at the following email address: nathalie.de-castro@aphp.fr.,"The paper explicitly states that data will not be shared publicly. While it mentions that deidentified participant data could be made available under certain conditions, this does not imply that the sequences were made publicly available."
36597160,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates were provided in the provided text. The focus was on the clinical outcomes and resistance mutations rather than on the specific accession numbers for sequences.,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of the treatment regimen and the genotypic analysis of resistance mutations.
36597160,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,382,"Among the 10,128 patients on ART during the study period, 661 were receiving E/C/F/TDF and 382 were included in the present study. We conducted a retrospective multicenter observational cohort study within the COREVIH IDF Est clinical centers enrolling ART-experienced patients.","The paper states that 382 patients were included in the study, and implies that DNA samples were obtained from these individuals for sequencing."
36597160,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,France,"COREVIH IDF Est is a group of 27 clinical centers located in the Paris area, with approximately 11,000 HIV-infected patients on follow-up in 2014.","The study was conducted within the COREVIH IDF Est clinical centers located in the Paris area, France, and the patient population was predominantly from these centers."
36597160,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2014-2015,We conducted a retrospective multicenter observational cohort study within the COREVIH IDF Est clinical centers enrolling ART-experienced patients. Eligible patients were adult patients (≥ 18 years old) with controlled HIV-1 infection on any stable ART regimen (no limit on the previous number of regimens) who switched to E/C/F/TDF between February 2014 (date of approval of E/C/F/TDF in France) and August 2015.,"The study included patients who switched to E/C/F/TDF between February 2014 and August 2015, which indicates the period during which the samples were obtained."
36597160,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Genotype at failure was available in 6/13 patients with detection of resistance-associated mutations to integrase inhibitors and NRTIs in 5/6 (83.3%) patients. During the study period, genotypes were performed using Sanger sequencing.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger sequencing for genotypic resistance testing.
36597160,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Integrase gene, NRTI mutations.","Our primary endpoint was the proportion of success defined as plasma HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL using the FDA snapshot algorithm [17]. Due to the retrospective design of this real-life study, we defined a window period for the primary endpoint analysis of - 12 weeks/+ 24 weeks to limit missing values. Virologic success was thus defined as plasma HIV-1 RNA below 50 copies/mL at week 48 with a window period of W36 to 72 weeks and no discontinuation of E/C/F/TDF. The secondary endpoints were: reasons for the switch as reported in the patient's EHR (simplification, patient demand, tolerance, other or unknown); the proportion of patients with virological failure defined as a confirmed viral load ≥ 50 copies/ml at two consecutive measurements within one year after the switch or one HIV RNA ≥ 50 copies and E/C/F/TDF discontinuation; resistant mutations in case of virologic failure; reasons for treatment discontinuation after the switch and tolerability of the treatment; risk factors for virologic failure.",The paper mentions the detection of resistance-associated mutations to integrase inhibitors and NRTIs in patients who experienced virological failure.
36597160,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"During the study period, genotypes were performed using Sanger sequencing.",The paper states that genotypes were performed using Sanger sequencing during the study period.
36597160,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"We also assessed risk factors associated with virological failure (VF). 382 patients with HIV RNA < 50 copies/mL who switched to E/C/F/TDF were included in the study. Most patients (69.9%) were male, with median age 44 years (IQR 38--51), who had been on ART for a median of 7 years (IQR 4--13). Median CD4 count was 614/mm3 and 24.6% of the patients had a history of previous virological failure. The reasons for switching were simplification (67.0%) and tolerance issues (22.0%). At week 48, 314 (82.0% [95% CI 78.4--86.0]) patients had HIV RNA < 50 copies/mL, 13 (3.5% [95% CI 3.64--8.41]) experienced virological failure. Genotype at failure was available in 6/13 patients with detection of resistance-associated mutations to integrase inhibitors and NRTIs in 5/6 (83.3%) patients.",The evidence suggest that plasma samples were sequenced for resistance testing.
36597160,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Most patients (69.9%) were male, with median age 44 years (IQR 38--51), who had been on ART for a median of 7 years (IQR 4--13). Median CD4 count was 614/mm3 and 24.6% of the patients had a history of previous virological failure. The reasons for switching were simplification (67.0%) and tolerance issues (22.0%). At week 48, 314 (82.0% [95% CI 78.4--86.0]) patients had HIV RNA < 50 copies/mL, 13 (3.5% [95% CI 3.64--8.41]) experienced virological failure. Genotype at failure was available in 6/13 patients with detection of resistance-associated mutations to integrase inhibitors and NRTIs in 5/6 (83.3%) patients.",The paper mentions that 24.6% of the patients had a history of previous virological failure and 3.5% experienced virological failure after switching to the new regimen.
36597160,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted a retrospective multicenter observational cohort study within the COREVIH IDF Est clinical centers enrolling ART-experienced patients. Our study has however several of limitations. Due to its retrospective design, a number of data are missing.

The strength of our study is to have enrolled patients with different treatment histories, and to provide an opportunity to better assess the antiviral activity and genetic barrier to resistance of E/C/F/TDF in these difficult-to-treat patients in a real-world setting.","The paper describes a retrospective observational cohort study, not a clinical trial."
36597160,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Most patients (69.9%) were male, with median age 44 years (IQR 38--51), who had been on ART for a median of 7 years (IQR 4--13). Median CD4 count was 614/mm3 and 24.6% of the patients had a history of previous virological failure. The reasons for switching were simplification (67.0%) and tolerance issues (22.0%). At week 48, 314 (82.0% [95% CI 78.4--86.0]) patients had HIV RNA < 50 copies/mL, 13 (3.5% [95% CI 3.64--8.41]) experienced virological failure. Genotype at failure was available in 6/13 patients with detection of resistance-associated mutations to integrase inhibitors and NRTIs in 5/6 (83.3%) patients.","The paper included patients who had been on ART for a median of 7 years and had a history of previous virological failure, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs."
36597160,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, RTIs, INSTIs","Before switching to E/C/F/TDF, a majority of patients received a combination of two NRTI and a third agent: ritonavir- boosted PI (PI/r) in 195/382 (51.0%), NNRTI in 84/382 (22.0%) and INSTI in 67/382 (17.5%) patients.","The paper mentions that a majority of patients received a combination of NRTIs and a third agent which could be a PI, NNRTI or INSTI before switching to the new regimen."
36597160,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Ritonavir- boosted PI (PI/r), NNRTI, INSTI","Before switching to E/C/F/TDF, a majority of patients received a combination of two NRTI and a third agent: ritonavir- boosted PI (PI/r) in 195/382 (51.0%), NNRTI in 84/382 (22.0%) and INSTI in 67/382 (17.5%) patients. We included in the study 382 patients treated with E/C/F/TDF between February 2014 and August 2015 who had been on ART for a median of 7 years (IQR 4–13) and for whom history of prior virological failure, previous genotypes and blood creatinine levels were known.","The paper lists specific classes of antiretroviral drugs that patients were using prior to the switch to E/C/F/TDF, although it does not provide a comprehensive list of every drug received."
36645792,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,This study does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.,The content of the paper focuses on a clinical trial evaluating the efficacy of a dolutegravir-based regimen in patients with virologic failure on a first-line tenofovir-emtricitabine-efavirenz (TEE) therapy. There is no mention of sequencing HIV samples or reporting HIV sequences from patient samples.
36645792,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Baseline GART was performed retrospectively for all participants after completion of week 24 visits, on plasma samples stored at enrollment (with results not returned to clinicians). GART was performed at the National Health Laboratory Service Virology Laboratory at Tygerberg Hospital in Cape Town, South Africa, and drug susceptibility prediction was performed with the Stanford algorithm (version 9.1)","The paper mentions the use of baseline genotypic antiretroviral resistance tests (GART) to predict drug susceptibilities. However, it does not provide specific in vitro susceptibilities in the results. The study focuses on clinical outcomes of treatment regimens and the development of resistance mutations, but it does not detail in vitro susceptibility testing against specific drug concentrations or effects in vitro on viral replication. Therefore, while drug susceptibility testing is mentioned, the specific data on in vitro susceptibilities is not reported in detail within the content provided in the paper. The focus is primarily on clinical outcomes rather than laboratory susceptibilities to specific drug concentrations in vitro, which is typically what is meant by in vitro drug susceptibility data, would be baseline gart with 4 drugs."
36645792,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No sequences were made publicly available in this study.,"The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences. The focus is on the clinical outcomes of the trial, and while genotypic resistance testing was performed, there is no indication that the sequences obtained were deposited in public databases."
36645792,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers reported,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,There is no mention of GenBank accession numbers or any other identifiers for sequences in the paper. The focus is on clinical outcomes and resistance testing rather than on the specifics of sequence data.
36645792,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,No individuals had samples obtained for sequencing,The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing.,"The study did not report collecting samples for sequencing, focusing instead on clinical outcomes of the treatment regimens."
36645792,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No samples reported,The paper does not mention any sequenced samples or their countries of origin.,"The content focuses on a clinical trial conducted in South Africa but does not provide details on the origin of any sequenced samples, as no sequencing was reported."
36645792,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,No samples reported,The paper does not mention any sequenced samples or the years they were obtained.,"Since the paper does not report any sequencing, there are no years associated with sequenced samples."
36645792,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.,"The paper does not discuss any processes involving the cloning of samples, as sequencing was not performed."
36645792,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,No genes sequenced,The paper does not report any sequenced HIV genes.,"The content does not mention any specific HIV genes that were sequenced, as no sequencing was conducted."
36645792,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sequencing not reported,The paper does not mention any sequencing methods.,"The study focuses on clinical outcomes and resistance testing without detailing any sequencing methods, as no sequencing was performed."
36645792,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,No samples sequenced,The paper does not mention any type of samples that were sequenced.,"The content does not provide any information on the type of clinical samples that were sequenced, as no sequencing was reported."
36645792,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Not reported,No sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure.,"The paper does not report obtaining sequences from individuals with virological failure, focusing instead on treatment outcomes."
