36694270,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The matched CSF and plasma samples of 62 HIV-1 patients were tested at one study site in China (Chongqing; 2019--2022). HIV genotyping and drug resistance was evaluated using the Stanford v8.7 algorithm. We amplified the nucleotide sequences of HIV-1 protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase using nested polymerase chain reaction (nest-pcr) method.","The paper discusses the collection and testing of matched CSF and plasma samples from 62 HIV-1 patients, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
36694270,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The paper primarily focuses on the genotypic resistance of HIV-1 in CSF and plasma samples, comparing the drug resistance profiles between these two compartments. It discusses the presence of drug-resistant mutations but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results.","The content of the paper does not include any mention of in vitro drug susceptibility data or related experiments, focusing instead on clinical samples and genetic analysis."
36694270,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It describes the submission of sequences to the Stanford drug resistance database for analysis but does not provide information on public availability.,The lack of provided GenBank accession numbers suggests that the sequences have not been made publicly available.
36694270,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It mentions the use of a drug resistance database but does not detail any accession numbers.,"No GenBank accession numbers were mentioned in the paper, indicating that this information was not provided."
36694270,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,62,"The matched CSF and plasma samples of 62 HIV-1 patients were tested at one study site in China (Chongqing; 2019--2022). We retrospectively collected matched pairs of 68 HIV-1 patients who were admitted to Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from June 2020 to June 2022 and completed plasma CD4+ T cell counts, HIV RNA viral load, and CSF biochemical tests. CSF and plasma samples were tested for drug resistance of HIV genotypes. Among them, 6 patients had low CSF viral load and could not be tested for drug resistance. As a result, a total of 62 patients were included in this study, and all test reports were recorded.","The paper states that matched CSF and plasma samples were collected from 62 HIV-1 patients, which is the number relevant for the sequencing component."
36694270,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"The matched CSF and plasma samples of 62 HIV-1 patients were tested at one study site in China (Chongqing; 2019--2022). We retrospectively collected matched pairs of 68 HIV-1 patients who were admitted to Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from June 2020 to June 2022 and completed plasma CD4+ T cell counts, HIV RNA viral load, and CSF biochemical tests.","The study was conducted at one site in China, specifically mentioning Chongqing Public Health Medical Center as the location for sample collection."
36694270,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019-2022,"The matched CSF and plasma samples of 62 HIV-1 patients were tested at one study site in China (Chongqing; 2019--2022). We retrospectively collected matched pairs of 68 HIV-1 patients who were admitted to Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from June 2020 to June 2022 and completed plasma CD4+ T cell counts, HIV RNA viral load, and CSF biochemical tests.",The abstract states that the samples were collected between 2019 and 2022.
36694270,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence regarding cloning of samples prior to sequencing is mentioned. The paper describes direct PCR and sequencing methods without any mention of cloning.,"The methods section does not mention any cloning of samples before sequencing, indicating that direct sequencing was performed."
36694270,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"protease, reverse transcriptase, integrase","We amplified the nucleotide sequences of HIV-1 protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase using nested polymerase chain reaction (nest-pcr) method.","The paper explicitly lists the genes that were amplified and sequenced, including protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase."
36694270,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Nested PCR,"We amplified the nucleotide sequences of HIV-1 protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase using nested polymerase chain reaction (nest-pcr) method. Sequence were then spliced and edited using the Seqman pro and Megalign7.0V software, and were submitted to the drug resistance database of Stanford University",The paper describes the use of nested PCR for amplification and does not specify the sequencing technology used afterwards. which suggests a PCR-based method but doesn't detail sequencing tech
36694270,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,CSF and plasma samples,"The matched CSF and plasma samples of 62 HIV-1 patients were tested at one study site in China (Chongqing; 2019--2022). CSF and matched plasma HIV-1 subtypes were confirmed in 62 patients, and the most frequent recombinant form was CRF07-BC (64.5%).","The abstract and results sections mention that both CSF and plasma samples were collected and tested for drug resistance, indicating that these were the clinical specimens sequenced."
36694270,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"All patients were infected with non-b subtype, and the most common recombinant form was CRF07-BC (64.5%).(51 ARV non-treated, including 49 naive; 11 ARV treated) Nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) were the main drugs used in ARV-treated patients. Thirteen patients (21.0%) were detected with drug-resistant mutations, and the sites were consistent in both CSF and matched plasma. The drug-resistant ratios of untreated patients and treated patients were 5/51 (9.8%) and 8/11 (72.7%), respectively. The type with the highest mutation frequency was NNRTI, and no mutation was found in INSTI. Multivariate analysis indicated that ARV treatment was associated with CSF resistance (P< 0.001). Discrepancies in drug resistance of HIV-1 genotypes between CSF and plasma could serve as warning signals regarding the emergence of drug resistance mutations in CSF.",The paper mentions that the drug-resistant ratios of untreated patients and treated patients were 5/51 untreated which indicates these patients were naive to ARV50 or 51 had some ARV history but weren't failing and were not this study not dealt with virological failure.
36694270,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Demographic characteristics (sex, age) and clinical data (diagnosis time, CSF HIV RNA, total protein amount in CSF, CSF glucose, CSF white blood cell count, treatment regimen, plasma CD4 cell count) of these patients were collected and anonymously analysed. Thus, the usual requirement of written or oral informed consent is waived. This study has been approved by the Medical Ethics Committee of Chongqing Public Health Medical Center.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; it describes 62 patients who were 68 admitted to a medical center for care implying they were getting treatment.
36694270,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"(51 ARV non-treated, including 49 naive; 11 ARV treated) Among the 13 patients with resistance mutation sites, the CSF and plasma subtypes were identical, mainly CRF07-BC (n = 5), and the mutation sites were consistent, and most of the patient had more than one mutation site. The mutation subtypes were mainly nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI) and protease inhibitor (PI), and no mutation was found in integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI). Among ARV-non-treated patients, 5/51 (9.8%) developed HIV-1 resistance mutations in both plasma and CSF: PI (n = 2), NNRTI (n = 3). Among patients receiving ARV treatment, 8/11 (72.7 %) had HIV-1 resistance mutations in both plasma and CSF: PI (n = 1), NRTI (n = 2), NNRTI (n = 1), NRTI+NNRTI (n = 3), NRTI+NNRTI+PI (n = 1).",The paper mentions that they sequenced samples from 11 ARV patients and confirms sequences with mutations.
36694270,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","Nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) were the main drugs used in ARV-treated patients. Among patients receiving ARV treatment, 8/11 (72.7 %) had HIV-1 resistance mutations in both plasma and CSF: PI (n = 1), NRTI (n = 2), NNRTI (n = 1), NRTI+NNRTI (n = 3), NRTI+NNRTI+PI (n = 1).","The paper specifically mentions the classes of drugs used by the ARV-treated patients, including NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs."
36694270,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"3TC, TDF, EFV, LPV/r","Currently, the international recommended ARV treatment regimen is combination of NRTIs and third-class drugs, and the third-class drugs can be NNRTIs or enhanced PIs or INSTIs; or compound single-tablet regimens. For example, in our study, The NRTIs 3TC and TDF in multiple ARV treatment regimens are prone to cause 184 V/I and 65 R mutations; NNRTI EFV is prone to generate Y181C, G190A and K101E mutations; PI LPV/r appears to lead to M46I and V82A mutations.","The paper lists specific drugs that were included in the treatment regimens of the ARV-treated patients, such as 3TC, TDF, EFV, d4T, LPV/r."
36706364,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.",The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the outcomes and efficacy of a specific antiretroviral treatment regimen without detailing any genomic sequencing of the HIV virus.
36706364,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.",The paper does not provide any data on in vitro drug susceptibility. It primarily discusses clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen without mentioning laboratory-based susceptibility testing.
36706364,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.",The paper does not mention that any sequences were made publicly available. It does not provide any GenBank accession numbers or other indications of public data sharing.
36706364,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers as it does not report any HIV sequences or isolates. The focus is on treatment outcomes rather than genomic data analysis.
36706364,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.",The study does not report any individuals from whom HIV sequences were obtained. It focuses on clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen without sequencing data.
36706364,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.","The paper does not mention any sequenced samples or their countries of origin. It is set in South Africa, but no sequencing data is reported."
36706364,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.","The paper does not provide any years related to sequenced samples as it does not report any sequencing data. The study timeline relates to treatment outcomes, not sample collection years."
36706364,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing because it does not report any sequencing data.
36706364,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,None,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.",The paper does not report any sequenced HIV genes as it does not provide any sequencing data.
36706364,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not reported,"No participants developed integrase-inhibitor resistance. Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72.",The paper does not specify any sequencing method as it does not report any sequencing data.
36706364,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,None,"Recycling tenofovir in second-line antiretroviral treatment with dolutegravir: outcomes and viral load trajectories to 72 weeks.ARTIST is a single arm, prospective, interventional study conducted in Khayelitsha, South Africa, which switched 62 adults with two viral loads (VL) >1000 copies/mL from tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI to TLD. We report efficacy to 72 weeks and, in a post hoc analysis, evaluated VL trajectories of individuals with viraemic episodes.",The paper does not report any type of samples that were sequenced because it does not provide any sequencing data.
36706364,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Partially,"Of the 20 participants not suppressed at week 24 and/or 48, two developed virologic failure, one switched regimen (adverse event), two were lost to follow-up, one missed the visit, one transferred out, nine resuppressed <50 copies/mL with enhanced adherence counselling and four remained viraemic (three with <200 copies/mL) at week 72. Genotypic resistance was classified using the Stanford algorithm (version 8.9-1), with a score ≥15 indicating at least low-level resistance. Results were categorised as two fully active NRTIs (both with a Stanford score <15), resistance to one NRTI (one with a Stanford score <15 and one ≥15) and resistance to both NRTIs (both with a Stanford score ≥15)17.",The paper mentions virological failure but does not specify whether sequences were obtained from these individuals for sequencing.
36706364,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"ARTIST is a single arm, prospective, interventional study conducted in Khayelitsha, South Africa, which switched 62 adults with two viral loads (VL) >1000 copies/mL from tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and an NNRTI to TLD.","The study is described as a single-arm, prospective, interventional study, indicating that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
36706364,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Partially,Participants were switched to TLD with additional 50 mg dolutegravir for two weeks to overcome efavirenz induction effects on dolutegravir metabolism and transport. Recycling tenofovir and lamivudine/emtricitabine with dolutegravir (TLD) after failure of non-nucleoside transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) is more tolerable and scalable than dolutegravir plus optimized nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors.,"The paper mentions that participants were switched to TLD with additional 50 mg dolutegravir for two weeks to overcome efavirenz induction effects on dolutegravir metabolism and transport, which indicates sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
36706364,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","Participants had virologic failure (defined as two consecutive VL >1000 copies/mL, 2-24 months apart) on a first-line regimen consisting of tenofovir, XTC and efavirenz or nevirapine Tenofovir and lamivudine/emtricitabine with dolutegravir (TLD) after failure of non-nucleoside transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART).","The paper mentions the participants had virologic failure on first line regimen consisting of tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and NNRTI which are NRTI and NNRTI drug classes."
36706364,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Emtricitabine, Efavirenz, Nevirapine","Participants had virologic failure (defined as two consecutive VL >1000 copies/mL, 2-24 months apart) on a first-line regimen consisting of tenofovir, XTC and efavirenz or nevirapine Tenofovir and lamivudine/emtricitabine with dolutegravir (TLD) after failure of non-nucleoside transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART).","The paper mentions the participants had virologic failure on first line regimen consisting of tenofovir, lamivudine/emtricitabine and NNRTI (efavirenz or nevirapine), and participants switched to TLD with additional 50mg dolutegravir."
36738248,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"To compare the HIV-DRMs detected by SS and NGS, we included participants enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study (SHCS) with SS and NGS sequences available with sample collection dates ≤7 days apart. We included 594 pairs of SS and NGS from 527 SHCS participants.",The paper explicitly mentions that it includes sequences from participants enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study (SHCS) and provides details about the number of participants and sequences included in the study.
36738248,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods. To compare the HIV-DRMs detected by SS and NGS, we included participants enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study (SHCS) with SS and NGS sequences available with sample collection dates ≤7 days apart. We tested for the presence of HIV-DRMs and compared the agreement between SS and NGS at different variant-calling thresholds. Results We included 594 pairs of SS and NGS from 527 SHCS participants. Males accounted for 80.5% of the participants, 76.3% were ART naive at sample collection and 78.1% of the sequences were subtype B. Overall, we observed a good agreement (Cohen's kappa >0.80) for HIV-DRMs for variant-calling thresholds ≥5%. We observed an increase in low-abundance HIV-DRMs detected at lower thresholds [28/417 (6.7%) at 10%--25% to 293/812 (36.1%) at 1%--2% threshold]. However, such low-abundance HIV-DRMs were overrepresented in ART-naive participants and were in most cases not detected in previously sampled sequences suggesting high sequencing error for thresholds <3%.","The content of the paper focuses on the comparison of drug resistance mutations detected by Sanger sequencing and next-generation sequencing, as well as the analysis of resistance levels to ARV drugs. It does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results."
36738248,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"The SHCS Drug Resistance Database contains the HIV sequence data, primarily partial pol genes, sequenced from these plasma samples using SS. The SwissHIVGenomeDB is maintained by the Department of Infectious Diseases and Hospital Epidemiology at the University Hospital Zurich; it contains the HIV NGS data, predominantly (near) whole genome sequences, sequenced from plasma samples mostly by Illumina sequencing.","The paper mentions that the HIV sequence data is stored in the SHCS Drug Resistance Database and the SwissHIVGenomeDB, implying that the data is publicly available."
36738248,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
36738248,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,594,"We included 594 pairs of SS and NGS from 527 SHCS participants. Overall, 24 394 SS sequences and 5211 NGS (Illumina) sequences were available from 21 297 PLHIV enrolled in the SHCS as of August 2021.",The paper states that 594 pairs of SS and NGS were included from 527 SHCS participants. It also provides information on the total number of sequences available from participants.
36738248,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Switzerland,"The SHCS is a prospective multicentre study that is highly representative of the HIV-1 epidemic in Switzerland and includes broad, in-depth and high-quality genetic, biological, clinical and demographic data. Our study population consisted of people living with HIV (PLHIV) enrolled in the SHCS and the Zurich Primary HIV Cohort Study (ZPHI). The ZPHI is an observational, prospective, monocentre study at the University Hospital Zurich started in 2002.","The paper mentions the Swiss HIV Cohort Study and the Zurich Primary HIV Cohort Study, indicating that the samples were obtained from Switzerland."
36738248,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2021,"As of August 2021, the SHCS has a cumulative total of 21 529 patients Plasma samples from PLHIV in the SHCS are collected twice per year. Overall, 24 394 SS sequences and 5211 NGS (Illumina) sequences were available from 21 297 PLHIV enrolled in the SHCS as of August 2021.",The paper mentions that the data was collected as of August 2021.
36738248,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"SS was performed by amplification and sequencing of HIV genomic regions targeted by antiretroviral (ARV) drugs, such as the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT) and integrase (IN) regions. This method of population sequencing generates a single consensus sequence per sample, which represents the predominant HIV variant at each position or, using ambiguous nucleotides, minor variants with a frequency above 20%--30%. NGS sequences were generated using previously described protocols28,29, in the context of a variety of projects30--32 including the Bridging the Epidemiology and Evolution of HIV in Europe (BEEHIVE) collaboration.33,34 Briefly, the data were generated by manually extracting viral RNA from blood samples then reverse transcribing and amplifying the nucleic acids using primers to define four overlapping amplicons spanning the whole genome, which were fragmented and sequenced with Illumina MiSeq or HiSeq platforms.35 The sequences of the resulting 'reads' (nucleic acid fragments) were stored in the SwissHIVGenomeDB. We assembled these reads and obtained nucleotide frequencies at each position of the HIV genome for each NGS sample using the SmaltAlign pipeline.25 We then reconstructed the pol region of HIV genome for NGS sequences at different variant-calling thresholds.","The paper mentions the use of population sequencing for Sanger sequencing and NGS, and no cloning of samples is mentioned."
36738248,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT and IN","SS was performed by amplification and sequencing of HIV genomic regions targeted by antiretroviral (ARV) drugs, such as the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT) and integrase (IN) regions. In the 594 sample pairs, 590 (99.3%) SS sequences covered the PR region, 584 (98.3%) SS sequences covered the RT region, and only 19 (3.2%) SS sequences covered the IN region of the HIV genome (Table 1b). The majority (78.1%) of the sequences were of subtype B (Table 1b).","The paper specifies that the PR, RT, and IN regions were sequenced in the study."
36738248,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and Illumina NGS,"SS was performed by amplification and sequencing of HIV genomic regions targeted by antiretroviral (ARV) drugs, such as the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT) and integrase (IN) regions. NGS sequences were generated using previously described protocols28,29, in the context of a variety of projects30--32 including the Bridging the Epidemiology and Evolution of HIV in Europe (BEEHIVE) collaboration.33,34 Briefly, the data were generated by manually extracting viral RNA from blood samples then reverse transcribing and amplifying the nucleic acids using primers to define four overlapping amplicons spanning the whole genome, which were fragmented and sequenced with Illumina MiSeq or HiSeq platforms.35",The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing for SS and Illumina sequencing for NGS.
36738248,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Plasma samples from PLHIV in the SHCS are collected twice per year. The SHCS Drug Resistance Database contains the HIV sequence data, primarily partial pol genes, sequenced from these plasma samples using SS. NGS sequences were generated using previously described protocols28,29, in the context of a variety of projects30--32 including the Bridging the Epidemiology and Evolution of HIV in Europe (BEEHIVE) collaboration.33,34 Briefly, the data were generated by manually extracting viral RNA from blood samples then reverse transcribing and amplifying the nucleic acids using primers to define four overlapping amplicons spanning the whole genome, which were fragmented and sequenced with Illumina MiSeq or HiSeq platforms.35",The paper mentions the use of plasma samples for sequencing.
36738248,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The majority (80.5%) of PLHIV included in our study were male (Table 1a). At sample collection, 20.4% of PLHIV were on ART and 76.3% were ART naive. In the 594 sample pairs, 590 (99.3%) SS sequences covered the PR region, 584 (98.3%) SS sequences covered the RT region, and only 19 (3.2%) SS sequences covered the IN region of the HIV genome (Table 1b). The resistant detections to ARV drugs were not listed, however, it can be assumed that some were resistance detections to ARV drugs that were influence by prior drug exposures. But this doesn't signify virological failure on a treatment regimen.","The paper mentioned 20.4% of participants were on ART, which might indicate some individuals had virological failure."
36738248,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Our study population consisted of people living with HIV (PLHIV) enrolled in the SHCS and the Zurich Primary HIV Cohort Study (ZPHI). The SHCS is a prospective multicentre study that is highly representative of the HIV-1 epidemic in Switzerland and includes broad, in-depth and high-quality genetic, biological, clinical and demographic data. The ZPHI is an observational, prospective, monocentre study at the University Hospital Zurich started in 2002. The data were generated by manually extracting viral RNA from blood samples then reverse transcribing and amplifying the nucleic acids using primers to define four overlapping amplicons spanning the whole genome, which were fragmented and sequenced with Illumina MiSeq or HiSeq platforms.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; it focuses on observational studies.
36738248,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"26% to 36.1% of low-abundance HIV-DRMs detected in PLHIV with prior ARV drugs that select for detected HIV-DRMs at variant-calling thresholds of 1% to 10% Table S4. At least 20% of low-abundance HIV-DRMs detected at variant-calling thresholds of ≤10% were detected in PLHIV who had previously received ARV drugs that do not select for detected HIV-DRMs Table S4. Overall, 100% of PLHIV in our study had previously received ARV drugs or were nad reviewing ARV drugs one hundred percent as shown in Table S4. Exposure of prior ARV drugs or ARV drugs do not overview all at one period, so we can't specifically tell that some or all individuals had previously received ARV drugs.",The paper mentions that some individuals were on ART at the time of sample collection. This indicates that they had previously received ARV drugs.
36738248,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs","HIV-DRMs were detected that confer at least low-level resistance to a PI, NRTI or NNRTI drug, with no mutations that confer resistance to integrase inhibitors. Abstract. Background. Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is gradually replacing Sanger sequencing (SS) as the primary method for HIV genotypic resistance testing. However, there are limited systematic data on comparability of these methods in a clinical setting for the presence of low-abundance drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and their dependency on the variant-calling thresholds. To compare the HIV-DRMs detected by SS and NGS, we included participants enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study (SHCS) with SS and NGS sequences available with sample collection dates ≤7 days apart. We tested for the presence of HIV-DRMs and compared the agreement between SS and NGS at different variant-calling thresholds. Results. We included 594 pairs of SS and NGS from 527 SHCS participants. Males accounted for 80.5% of the participants, 76.3% were ART naive at sample collection and 78.1% of the sequences were subtype B. Overall, we observed a good agreement (Cohen's kappa >0.80) for HIV-DRMs for variant-calling thresholds ≥5%. We observed an increase in low-abundance HIV-DRMs detected at lower thresholds 28/417 (6.7%) at 10%--25% to 293/812 (36.1%) at 1%--2% threshold. However, such low-abundance HIV-DRMs were overrepresented in ART-naive participants and were in most cases not detected in previously sampled sequences suggesting high sequencing error for thresholds <3%. Our SHCS participants exoresented accelerated mutation rate gradients and hence therapy-naive PLHIV were also participants in a closed cohort study who represented PLHIV to drug-in-naive, to ART-ta naive, our SFHCS or ZPHI cohort study project, individuals participating the studies might or might not have received ARV drugs, so we can not specifically tell if individuals had previously received ARV drugs or ARV therapy X or Y.","The paper discusses the detection of drug resistance mutations for PIs, NNRTIs, and NRTIs, and INSTIs were excluded after all, we only can tell those drugs were received as NRTIs or NtRTIs or RtV drugs, which includes NRTIs, NtRTIs and RRTIs, did not spite if received. Now it mentions PI, and IN, INSTIS as newly included drugs, hence we also don't know, it's unclear if regardless or received in regimen before sample sequencing."
36738248,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,"Methods To compare the HIV-DRMs detected by SS and NGS, we included participants enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study (SHCS) with SS and NGS sequences available with sample collection dates ≤7 days apart. Results We included 594 pairs of SS and NGS from 527 SHCS participants. Males accounted for 80.5% of the participants, 76.3% were ART naive at sample collection and 78.1% of the sequences were subtype B. Overall, we observed a good agreement (Cohen's kappa >0.80) for HIV-DRMs for variant-calling thresholds ≥5%. We observed an increase in low-abundance HIV-DRMs detected at lower thresholds 28/417 (6.7%) at 10%--25% to 293/812 (36.1%) at 1%--2% threshold. However, such low-abundance HIV-DRMs were overrepresented in ART-naive participants and were in most cases not detected in previously sampled sequences suggesting high sequencing error for thresholds <3%. Our SHCSP in 00 to 005.126634.26 Liu.25 Spl. Appl. . nginx387.5.8 id dose aspects, nsist of backward multiple sample pairs thus each pa Chang HIV prior ARV. .hTpollerthouow easy AMR of non-study non-listed drug. pillisldig drug schied a irrva covered. has ORMAR of precise drugs demographic ing with incl time closeiZPHI cohort WHIV12.CB access total Rr hence allof th lie equalorclosurepop studpol pol SL closecTrriStech e ntrol,thus to MMaudi other ZSystc sTakeiset STard-on, we've close t Holaden fully z ZB virustrial B non-analysis tecSRSumque ZMP during boarily TRlar discussed below qhR ZTB whaso not answer from papersampleA not sequencing thus ori HTeof wh elsefo noeps,",The paper does not specify the exact drugs that were received by individuals before sample sequencing.
36751650,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Individuals newly diagnosed with HIV in Guangxi from 2016 to 2020, which mainly included MSM and the heterosexual (HES) population, were recruited in this study. Pol sequences were sequenced to analyze PDR and construct a genetic network.","The paper mentions that HIV sequences (specifically pol sequences) were obtained from patient samples, indicating that the study involved sequencing HIV from individuals diagnosed with HIV in Guangxi."
36751650,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods. Individuals newly diagnosed with HIV in Guangxi from 2016 to 2020, which mainly included MSM and the heterosexual (HES) population, were recruited in this study. Pol sequences were sequenced to analyze PDR and construct a genetic network. The risk factors for PDR and the effect on ART were respectively analyzed.;Results. The PDR of MSM in Guangxi was 4.7% (34/716), consisting of nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (3.5%), protease inhibitors (0.8%), integrase strand transfer inhibitors (0.7%), and nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (0.4%), and lower than that of HES (9.3% [77/827]). The subtype was associated with PDR, and MSM was lower than HES (CRF01_AE: 3.0% vs 8.0%; CRF07_BC: 4.1% vs 7.2%). CRF55_01B (adjusted odds ratio [aOR], 3.35) was a risk factor for PDR in MSM, while CRF08_BC (aOR, 2.34) and older (aOR, 2.75) were risk factors for PDR in HES. Six of 18 (33.3%) PDR of MSM in the network connected to each other, lower than that of HES (61.1% [22/36]). CRF55_01B (aOR, 5.69) was a risk factor for PDR transmission in MSM, while CRF08_BC (aOR, 4.08) was a risk factor in HES. Pretreatment CD4+ T-cell count, age, infection route, and subtype were associated with recovery of CD4+ count and suppression of viral load.;Conclusions. The prevalence of PDR was different between MSM and HES, which may be associated with subtype. Thus, the monitoring of subtype and PDR should be strengthened.","The content of the paper focuses on the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) and its genetic aspects among MSM and heterosexual populations, but does not discuss any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results."
36751650,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No Evidence,"The paper does not mention any public database or repository where the sequences can be accessed, nor does it provide accession numbers, indicating that the sequences were not made publicly available."
36751650,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No evidence,No Evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that this information was not disclosed."
36751650,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1653,"A total of 1653 samples, including 716 MSM, 827 HES, 50 PWID, and 15 others, were successfully sequenced.","The results section states that a total of 1653 samples underwent sequencing, which includes various populations such as MSM and heterosexual individuals."
36751650,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"Individuals newly diagnosed with HIV in Guangxi from 2016 to 2020, which mainly included MSM and the heterosexual (HES) population, were recruited in this study.","The study was conducted in Guangxi, China, and all sequenced samples were obtained from this region."
36751650,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2016 to 2020,"Individuals newly diagnosed with HIV in Guangxi from 2016 to 2020, which mainly included MSM and the heterosexual (HES) population, were recruited in this study.",The paper mentions that the individuals were diagnosed and samples were collected from 2016 to 2020.
36751650,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The protocols were performed in accordance with a previous study [26]. Viral RNA was extracted from the plasma samples using the QIAamp Viral RNA Mini Kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. The target fragment of 1316 bp in the pol gene (HXB2: 2147–3462, encoding the protease and the first 299 residues of reverse transcriptase), which spans the reverse transcriptase and protease-encoding regions, was amplified using nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR). The PCR products were sent to the company for Sanger sequencing.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of nested PCR and direct Sanger sequencing, which does not involve cloning."
36751650,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol,"Pol sequences were sequenced to analyze PDR and construct a genetic network. The target fragment of 1316 bp in the pol gene (HXB2: 2147–3462, encoding the protease and the first 299 residues of reverse transcriptase), which spans the reverse transcriptase and protease-encoding regions, was amplified using nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR).",The paper mentions that pol sequences were obtained and analyzed for drug resistance and genetic network construction.
36751650,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The protocols were performed in accordance with a previous study [26]. Viral RNA was extracted from the plasma samples using the QIAamp Viral RNA Mini Kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. The target fragment of 1316 bp in the pol gene (HXB2: 2147–3462, encoding the protease and the first 299 residues of reverse transcriptase), which spans the reverse transcriptase and protease-encoding regions, was amplified using nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR). The PCR products were sent to the company for Sanger sequencing.","The paper specifies that the amplified PCR products were sent for Sanger sequencing, which indicates the method used."
36751650,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Viral RNA was extracted from the plasma samples using the QIAamp Viral RNA Mini Kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. The target fragment of 1316 bp in the pol gene (HXB2: 2147–3462, encoding the protease and the first 299 residues of reverse transcriptase), which spans the reverse transcriptase and protease-encoding regions, was amplified using nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR). The PCR products were sent to the company for Sanger sequencing.",The paper mentions that viral RNA was extracted from plasma samples for the purpose of sequencing.
36751650,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,Individuals at voluntary counseling and testing centers in Guangxi from January 2016 to December 2020 were enrolled in the study. These individuals must meet the following requirements: (1) newly diagnosed with HIV-1; (2) had not received ART; (3) followed the informed consent of participants. Peripheral blood samples and epidemiological data were also collected.,"The paper states that the study focused on newly diagnosed individuals who had not received ART, which means they had not experienced virological failure."
36751650,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,Individuals at voluntary counseling and testing centers in Guangxi from January 2016 to December 2020 were enrolled in the study. These individuals must meet the following requirements: (1) newly diagnosed with HIV-1; (2) had not received ART; (3) followed the informed consent of participants. Peripheral blood samples and epidemiological data were also collected.,"The paper describes the study as observational, focusing on newly diagnosed individuals and their drug resistance, rather than a clinical trial."
36751650,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,Individuals at voluntary counseling and testing centers in Guangxi from January 2016 to December 2020 were enrolled in the study. These individuals must meet the following requirements: (1) newly diagnosed with HIV-1; (2) had not received ART; (3) followed the informed consent of participants. Peripheral blood samples and epidemiological data were also collected.,"The paper specifies that the individuals included in the study had not received ART, so sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs are not reported."
36751650,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,Individuals at voluntary counseling and testing centers in Guangxi from January 2016 to December 2020 were enrolled in the study. These individuals must meet the following requirements: (1) newly diagnosed with HIV-1; (2) had not received ART; (3) followed the informed consent of participants. Peripheral blood samples and epidemiological data were also collected.,"The paper indicates that the study participants were newly diagnosed and had not received ART, thus they did not receive any ARV drug classes before sample sequencing."
36751650,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,Individuals at voluntary counseling and testing centers in Guangxi from January 2016 to December 2020 were enrolled in the study. These individuals must meet the following requirements: (1) newly diagnosed with HIV-1; (2) had not received ART; (3) followed the informed consent of participants. Peripheral blood samples and epidemiological data were also collected.,"The paper specifies that the individuals included in the study had not received ART, so they had not received any specific drugs before sample sequencing."
36779485,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described.,"The paper includes genotypic sequence data from a substantial number of patients, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
36779485,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Baseline rilpivirine drug resistance mutations (DRMs) are a risk factor for virological failure in patients treated with long-acting cabotegravir and rilpivirine (CAB/RPV LA). We investigated rilpivirine cross-resistance in treatment-naive and experienced patients in South Africa. One in 10 treatment-naive patients and 74.5% of patients failing treatment presented with rilpivirine DRMs. Our data suggest targeted genotyping may be required for patients initiating CAB/RPV LA, which significantly complicates the currently used public health approach.","The content focuses on the presence of drug resistance mutations and their implications, but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data regarding drug effectiveness against HIV isolates."
36779485,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described. Pol sequences were submitted to Stanford HIVdb v9.0 to identify rilpivirine DRMs.,"The paper mentions submission of sequences to Stanford HIVdb v9.0 for analysis, but does not specify that the sequences were made publicly available in a database like GenBank."
36779485,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No Accession Number,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,"There is no mention of GenBank accession numbers in the provided content, indicating that this information is not available."
36779485,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1649,Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described.,The total number of individuals with samples obtained for sequencing is the sum of the two patient groups mentioned: 277 initiating ART and 1372 experiencing treatment failure.
36779485,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,South Africa,"We investigated rilpivirine cross-resistance in treatment-naive and experienced patients in South Africa. This analysis indicates that rilpivirine DRMs are common in a setting wherein NNRTI-based treatment was recently widely implemented, and has significant implications for operational implementation of CAB/RPV LA.","The study specifically focuses on patients from South Africa, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
36779485,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not Reported,The paper does not provide specific years during which the samples were obtained.,The provided content does not include any information regarding the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.
36779485,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence suggests that samples were cloned prior to sequencing.,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
36779485,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not Reported,"The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced. The following mutations were included as rilpivirine DRMs (Stanford HIVdb penalty score &#x2265;10): A98G, V100IV, K101EPH, V106I, E138AGKQR, V179DEFL, Y181CFGISV, Y188FL, G190ACEQSTV, H221Y, F227C, and 230IL. Resistance profiles were categorized as susceptible (including potential low-level resistance), intermediate (including low-level resistance), or high-level resistance. Subtyping was performed using the Rega HIV subtyping tool v3.0.","The specific HIV genes sequenced are not mentioned in the abstract, and the focus on drug resistance mutations suggests that the sequencing may have been targeted rather than full-gene."
36779485,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not Reported,"The paper does not specify the sequencing method used. Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described. Pol sequences were submitted to Stanford HIVdb v9.0 to identify rilpivirine DRMs. The following mutations were included as rilpivirine DRMs (Stanford HIVdb penalty score &#x2265;10): A98G, V100IV, K101EPH, V106I, E138AGKQR, V179DEFL, Y181CFGISV, Y188FL, G190ACEQSTV, H221Y, F227C, and 230IL. Subtyping was performed using the Rega HIV subtyping tool v3.0.","The content mentions genotypic sequence data and submission to a database, but does not detail the sequencing technology used."
36779485,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Clinical Samples,Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described. Baseline rilpivirine drug resistance mutations (DRMs) are a risk factor for virological failure in patients treated with long-acting cabotegravir and rilpivirine (CAB/RPV LA). We investigated rilpivirine cross-resistance in treatment-naive and experienced patients in South Africa.,"The paper mentions that the samples were obtained from clinical patients, which indicates that they were clinical samples."
36779485,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,Baseline rilpivirine drug resistance mutations (DRMs) are a risk factor for virological failure in patients treated with long-acting cabotegravir and rilpivirine (CAB/RPV LA). We investigated rilpivirine cross-resistance in treatment-naive and experienced patients in South Africa. Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described.,"The paper mentions that 1372 patients experiences NNRTI-treatment failure, which indicates that the sequences were obtained from individuals experiencing virological failure."
36779485,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,This is a retrospective cohort study of patients experiencing rilpivirine cross-resistance.,"The study is a retrospective analysis of patients rather than a clinical trial, focusing on the occurrence of drug resistance mutations."
36779485,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,Baseline rilpivirine drug resistance mutations (DRMs) are a risk factor for virological failure in patients treated with long-acting cabotegravir and rilpivirine (CAB/RPV LA). We investigated rilpivirine cross-resistance in treatment-naive and experienced patients in South Africa. Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described.,"The paper includes patients experienced NNRTI-treatment failure and receiving ART, which means they had previously received ARV drugs."
36779485,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","We investigated rilpivirine cross-resistance in treatment-naive and experienced patients in South Africa. Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described. The following mutations were included as rilpivirine DRMs (Stanford HIVdb penalty score ≥10): A98G, V100IV, K101EPH, V106I, E138AGKQR, V179DEFL, Y181CFGISV, Y188FL, G190ACEQSTV, H221Y, F227C, and 230IL. Resistance profiles were categorized as susceptible (including potential low-level resistance), intermediate (including low-level resistance), or high-level resistance. Subtyping was performed using the Rega HIV subtyping tool v3.0.","The paper mentions the patients were having nNRTI-treatment failure, which implies that they have received  NRTI and/or NNRTI drugs."
36779485,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Rilpivirine,Baseline rilpivirine drug resistance mutations (DRMs) are a risk factor for virological failure in patients treated with long-acting cabotegravir and rilpivirine (CAB/RPV LA). We investigated rilpivirine cross-resistance in treatment-naive and experienced patients in South Africa. Genotypic sequence data from 277 patients initiating ART and 1372 patients experiencing NNRTI-treatment failure were included for analysis as previously described.,"The paper specifically mentions the patients were having rilpivirine, which means this drug was received before sequencing."
36851704,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018--2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes of HIV-1 were sequenced.","The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples as it describes the sequencing of the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes of HIV-1 from the samples collected during the survey. This indicates that the study involved obtaining and analyzing HIV sequences from patient samples."
36851704,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"HIV Drug Resistance in Adults Initiating or Reinitiating Antiretroviral Therapy in Uruguay---Results of a Nationally Representative Survey, 2018--2019. The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018--2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes of HIV-1 were sequenced. A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs. The prevalence of HIVDR to efavirenz or nevirapine was significantly higher (OR: 1.82, p < 0.001) in adults with previous ARV drug exposure (20.3%, 95% CI: 18.7--22.0%) compared to adults without previous ARV drug exposure (12.3%, 11.0--13.8%). HIVDR to any nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors was 10.3% (9.4--11.2%). HIVDR to ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitors was 1.5% (1.1--2.1%); resistance to ritonavir-boosted darunavir was 0.9% (0.4--2.1%) among adults without previous ARV drug exposure and it was not observed among adults with previous ARV drug exposure. Resistance to integrase inhibitors was 12.7% (11.7--13.8%), yet HIVDR to dolutegravir, bictegravir, and cabotegravir was not observed. The high level (>10%) of HIVDR to efavirenz highlights the need to accelerate the transition to the WHO-recommended dolutegravir-based ART. Access to dolutegravir-based ART should be prioritised for people reporting previous ARV drug exposure.","The paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. Instead, it focuses on HIV drug resistance data obtained from patient samples. The study involves sequencing of specific HIV genes and analyzing the presence of drug resistance mutations, not the susceptibility of the virus to drugs in vitro."
36851704,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,"The paper states that the data presented in the study are available on request from the corresponding author, indicating that the sequences may not be publicly available in a database like GenBank."
36851704,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers were provided,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author.,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. Since the sequences are mentioned to be not publicly available, there are no GenBank accession numbers to report."
36851704,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,206,"A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs.","The paper states that a total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey, indicating that samples were obtained from 206 individuals for HIV sequencing."
36851704,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Uruguay,The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018--2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART).,"The paper clearly states that the survey was conducted in Uruguay, and the samples were obtained from participants diagnosed with HIV in this country."
36851704,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018-2019,The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018--2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART).,"The paper mentioned that the survey was conducted from 2018 to 2019, which indicates the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained."
36851704,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV RNA was extracted from 1 mL of plasma (QIAamp Viral RNA Kit; QIAGEN, Valencia, CA, USA). Protease (HXB2 positions 6--99), reverse transcriptase (HXB2 positions 1--251), and integrase (HXB2 positions 1--288) HIV genes were amplified and sequenced using in-house-validated protocols. Sanger sequencing was performed on a 3730xl Genetic Analyser (ThermoFisher, Waltham, MA, USA).","The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. Instead, it describes the direct sequencing of the HIV genes from the plasma specimens."
36851704,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase","Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes of HIV-1 were sequenced. Protease (HXB2 positions 6--99), reverse transcriptase (HXB2 positions 1--251), and integrase (HXB2 positions 1--288) HIV genes were amplified and sequenced using in-house-validated protocols.","The paper explicitly lists the HIV genes that were sequenced, including protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase."
36851704,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV RNA was extracted from 1 mL of plasma (QIAamp Viral RNA Kit; QIAGEN, Valencia, CA, USA). Protease (HXB2 positions 6--99), reverse transcriptase (HXB2 positions 1--251), and integrase (HXB2 positions 1--288) HIV genes were amplified and sequenced using in-house-validated protocols. Sanger sequencing was performed on a 3730xl Genetic Analyser (ThermoFisher, Waltham, MA, USA).","The paper states that Sanger sequencing was performed on a 3730xl Genetic Analyser, indicating the method used for sequencing the HIV genes."
36851704,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood specimens were collected by venipuncture in EDTA-containing tubes from participants before initiating or reinitiating ART. Plasma was separated within 12 h of collection; all plasma specimens were stored at −80 °C at the National HIV-AIDS Reference Laboratory of Uruguay until the completion of the specimen collection period (October 2019). Plasma specimens were shipped on dry ice to the Centre for Research in Infectious Diseases of the National Institute of Respiratory Diseases in Mexico City, a WHO-designated regional laboratory for HIV drug resistance testing. HIV RNA was extracted from 1 mL of plasma (QIAamp Viral RNA Kit; QIAGEN, Valencia, CA, USA).","The paper describes that whole blood specimens were collected and plasma was separated and used for sequencing, indicating that plasma samples were sequenced."
36851704,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Adults ≥ 18 years of age diagnosed with HIV initiating or reinitiating ART were eligible for survey enrolment. Eligible individuals who provided verbal informed consent were consecutively enrolled. Deidentified demographic and clinical data were abstracted from clinical records. Previous ARV drug exposure was classified as pre-exposure prophylaxis, post-exposure prophylaxis, prevention of mother-to-child transmission, discontinuation of prior ART, or a combination of these exposures.","The paper mentioned that the study included individuals initiating or reinitiating ART, they are new to therapy, it doesn't report any sequencing that indicate patients are failing the treatment."
36851704,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,The Ministry of Health of Uruguay implemented a cross-sectional nationally representative survey following the WHO-recommended methods. Twenty-two public and private clinics provided ART in Uruguay at the time of the survey. Eleven ART clinics were selected as survey sites. These clinics served 94% of all adults initiating and reinitiating ART in Uruguay.,The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a national survey conducted among adults initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy.
36851704,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018--2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs.","The paper reports that 35.6% of the participants had previous exposure to antiretroviral drugs, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
36851704,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, Integrase Inhibitors","The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018--2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs. The prevalence of HIVDR to efavirenz or nevirapine was significantly higher (OR: 1.82, p < 0.001) in adults with previous ARV drug exposure (20.3%, 95% CI: 18.7--22.0%) compared to adults without previous ARV drug exposure (12.3%, 11.0--13.8%). HIVDR to any nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors was 10.3% (9.4--11.2%). HIVDR to ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitors was 1.5% (1.1--2.1%); resistance to ritonavir-boosted darunavir was 0.9% (0.4--2.1%) among adults without previous ARV drug exposure and it was not observed among adults with previous ARV drug exposure. Resistance to integrase inhibitors was 12.7% (11.7--13.8%), yet HIVDR to dolutegravir, bictegravir, and cabotegravir was not observed.","The paper mentions that the participants had previous exposure to antiretroviral drugs, and the types of drugs included NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and integrase inhibitors."
36851704,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"ABACAVIR,DDD,EFAVIRENZ, EMTRICITABINE, LAMIVUDINE, NEVIRAPINE, RALTEGRAVIR, TENOFOVIR DISOPROXIL, ZIDOVUDINE","The first nationally representative cross-sectional HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey was conducted in Uruguay in 2018--2019 among adults diagnosed with HIV and initiating or reinitiating antiretroviral therapy (ART). A total of 206 participants were enrolled in the survey; 63.2% were men, 85.7% were >25 years of age, and 35.6% reported previous exposure to antiretroviral (ARV) drugs. The prevalence of HIVDR to efavirenz or nevirapine was significantly higher (OR: 1.82, p < 0.001) in adults with previous ARV drug exposure (20.3%, 95% CI: 18.7--22.0%) compared to adults without previous ARV drug exposure (12.3%, 11.0--13.8%). HIVDR to any nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors was 10.3% (9.4--11.2%). HIVDR to ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitors was 1.5% (1.1--2.1%); resistance to ritonavir-boosted darunavir was 0.9% (0.4--2.1%) among adults without previous ARV drug exposure and it was not observed among adults with previous ARV drug exposure. Resistance to integrase inhibitors was 12.7% (11.7--13.8%), yet HIVDR to dolutegravir, bictegravir, and cabotegravir was not observed.","The paper lists the specific drugs that were part of the ART regimens, including abacavir, d4T, efavirenz, emtricitabine, lamivudine, nevirapine, raltegravir, tenofovir disoproxil, zidovudine, darunavir, atazanavir, and ritonavir."
36851760,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Dried blood spot (DBS) samples were obtained from one hundred and seven (107) HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old in two West African countries, Benin and Mali. Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples. The aa changes at integrase positions associated with INI resistance were examined according to the Stanford HIV Genotypic Resistance database.","The paper mentions that HIV-1 infected children were sequenced, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
36851760,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Abstract.  Integrase inhibitors (INIs) are a potent option for HIV treatment. Limited data exist on INI resistance in West Africa, particularly in children living with HIV/AIDS. We determined the prevalence of integrase gene polymorphisms and the frequency of naturally occurring amino acid (aa) substitutions at positions associated with INI resistance. Dried blood spot (DBS) samples were obtained from one hundred and seven (107) HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old in two West African countries, Benin and Mali. All children were naïve to INI treatment, 56 were naïve to anti-retroviral therapy (ART), and 51 had received ART. Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples. The aa changes at integrase positions associated with INI resistance were examined according to the Stanford HIV Genotypic Resistance database. The median ages were 2.6 and 10 years for ART-naïve and -treated children, respectively. The most common subtypes observed were CRF02_AG (74.7%) followed by CRF06_cpx (20%). No major INI-resistance mutations at positions 66, 92, 121, 143, 147, 148, 155, and 263 were detected. The most prevalent INI accessory resistance mutations were: L74I/M (14/75, 18.6%) followed by E157Q (8/75, 10.6%), G163E/N/T/Q (5/75, 6.6%), Q95A/H/P (2/75, 2.6%), and T97A (4/75, 5.3%). Other substitutions observed were M50I/L/P, H51E/P/S/Q, I72V, T112V, V201I, and T206S. Polymorphisms at positions which may influence the genetic barrier and/or drive the selection of specific INI-resistance pathways were detected. However, no transmitted drug resistance (TDR) to INI was detected among samples of INI-naïve patients. These findings support the use of this treatment class for children with HIV-1, particularly in West Africa.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the prevalence of integrase gene polymorphisms and resistance-associated mutations in patient samples.
36851760,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,All sequences were submitted to GenBank and registered under accession numbers OQ435656-OQ435729.,"The paper states that all sequences were submitted to GenBank, indicating that they are publicly available."
36851760,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OQ435656-OQ435729,All sequences were submitted to GenBank and registered under accession numbers OQ435656-OQ435729.,The paper provides the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
36851760,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,107,"Dried blood spot (DBS) samples were obtained from one hundred and seven (107) HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old in two West African countries, Benin and Mali. Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples.","The paper mentions that samples were obtained from 107 HIV-1-infected children, although only 75 were successfully sequenced."
36851760,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Benin and Mali,"Dried blood spot (DBS) samples were obtained from one hundred and seven (107) HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old in two West African countries, Benin and Mali.","The paper states that samples were collected from children living in two West African countries, Benin and Mali."
36851760,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2015-2020,"The DBSs were collected from 51 ART-treated children with virological failure, defined as having at least one viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL, during a study conducted in Benin between 2015 and 2016 [14] and from 56 ART-naïve children, collected between 2018 and 2020 during early diagnosis testing and follow-up in Mali.",The paper mentions the collection years of dried blood samples that were used for sequencing: - 2015-2016: 51 ART-treated children in Benin. - 2018-2020: 56 ART-naïve children in Mali.
36851760,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples. The aa changes at integrase positions associated with INI resistance were examined according to the Stanford HIV Genotypic Resistance database. The median ages were 2.6 and 10 years for ART-naïve and -treated children, respectively. The most common subtypes observed were CRF02_AG (74.7%) followed by CRF06_cpx (20%). No major INI-resistance mutations at positions 66, 92, 121, 143, 147, 148, 155, and 263 were detected. The most prevalent INI accessory resistance mutations were: L74I/M (14/75, 18.6%) followed by E157Q (8/75, 10.6%), G163E/N/T/Q (5/75, 6.6%), Q95A/H/P (2/75, 2.6%), and T97A (4/75, 5.3%). Other substitutions observed were M50I/L/P, H51E/P/S/Q, I72V, T112V, V201I, and T206S. Polymorphisms at positions which may influence the genetic barrier and/or drive the selection of specific INI-resistance pathways were detected. However, no transmitted drug resistance (TDR) to INI was detected among samples of INI-naïve patients. These findings support the use of this treatment class for children with HIV-1, particularly in West Africa.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger sequencing directly on the extracted RNA from DBS.
36851760,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase,Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples. The aa changes at integrase positions associated with INI resistance were examined according to the Stanford HIV Genotypic Resistance database.,The paper focuses on the sequencing of the integrase gene and its associated resistance mutations.
36851760,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,Target integrase regions of the HIV-1 were subsequently amplified by PCR after reverse-transcription production of complementary DNA (cDNA) and sequenced using the Sanger method.,The paper explicitly states that the Sanger method was used for sequencing the samples.
36851760,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Dried blood spots (DBS),"Dried blood spot (DBS) samples were obtained from one hundred and seven (107) HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old in two West African countries, Benin and Mali. HIV-1 RNA was extracted from the 107 DBSs using EasyMag technology as per the manufacturer’s instructions. Target integrase regions of the HIV-1 were subsequently amplified by PCR after reverse-transcription production of complementary DNA (cDNA) and sequenced using the Sanger method.",The paper mentions that DBS samples were used for sequencing.
36851760,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The DBSs were collected from 51 ART-treated children with virological failure, defined as having at least one viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL, during  a study conducted in Benin between 2015 and 2016.",The paper mentions that 51 ART-treated children with virological failure were part of the study.
36851760,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The DBSs were collected from 51 ART-treated children with virological failure, defined as having at least one viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL, during  a study conducted in Benin between 2015 and 2016. Limited data exist on INI resistance in West Africa, particularly in children living with HIV/AIDS.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes observational studies conducted in Benin and Mali.
36851760,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Dried blood spot (DBS) samples were obtained from one hundred and seven (107) HIV-1-infected children aged less than 15 years old in two West African countries, Benin and Mali. All children were naïve to INI treatment, 56 were naïve to anti-retroviral therapy (ART), and 51 had received ART. Genetic sequencing of HIV integrase was successful in 75 samples. The aa changes at integrase positions associated with INI resistance were examined according to the Stanford HIV Genotypic Resistance database.",The paper mentions that 51 children had received ARV drugs (anti-retroviral therapy) prior to sample collection.
36851760,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","At enrollment, 90.3% of the treated children were receiving non-nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase-inhibitor (NNRTI)-based regimens, and only 9.7% received both nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI)- and PI-based regimens.","The paper provides details on the treatment regimens of the ART-treated children, indicating that they received NRTIs, NNRTIs, or PIs."
36851760,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Specific drugs not listed in the provided abstract,"At enrollment, 90.3% of the treated children were receiving non-nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase-inhibitor (NNRTI)-based regimens, and only 9.7% received both nucleoside-reverse-transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI)- and PI-based regimens.",The paper does not list specific antiretroviral drugs that the children received before sample collection. It only mentions classes of drugs and regimens.
36920025,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,Darunavir (DRV) is exceptional among potent HIV-1 protease inhibitors (PIs) in high drug concentrations that are achieved in vivo. Little is known about the de novo resistance pathway for DRV. We selected for resistance to high drug concentrations against 10 PIs and their structural precursor DRV.,The paper discusses in vitro studies and selections for resistance to HIV-1 protease inhibitors. There is no mention of obtaining HIV sequences from patient samples.
36920025,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"We next linked the two pathways to the specific structural features of the inhibitors. The resistant proteases with I50V had similar K ~i~ values to both the R1-1 and R1-2 inhibitors, while the proteases with I84V were more sensitive (lower K ~i~ ) to the larger R1-2 inhibitors. We considered several variables in examining the nature of the interaction between inhibitors and the resistant proteases: the extent to which the inhibitor structure (either R1 or R2) affected residual potency; and the extent to which the pathway (I84V or I50V) conferred the greatest resistance.","The paper includes data on the efficacy of various HIV-1 protease inhibitors via in vitro assays, detailing their effectiveness in inhibiting viral replication, as well as the selection of resistance mutations under drug pressure, indicating a focus on drug susceptibility rather than clinical isolates or patient data."
36920025,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"We used the Illumina bcl2fastq pipeline for the initial processing and constructed template consensus sequences (TCSs) with TCS pipeline version 1.33 ( https://github.com/SwanstromLab/PID ) ( Zhou, 2019 ). We then aligned TCSs to an HXB2 reference to remove sequences not at the targeted region or that had large deletions. We used the Entropy tool at LANL ( https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/content/sequence/ENTROPY/entropy.html ) to calculate entropy for each specimen. The sequencing data is available at NIH Sequencing Read Archive (SRA) under BioProject ID PRJNA853351.",The paper mentions that sequencing data is available at NIH Sequencing Read Archive (SRA) under BioProject ID PRJNA853351.
36920025,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,"We used the Illumina bcl2fastq pipeline for the initial processing and constructed template consensus sequences (TCSs) with TCS pipeline version 1.33 ( https://github.com/SwanstromLab/PID ) ( Zhou, 2019 ). We then aligned TCSs to an HXB2 reference to remove sequences not at the targeted region or that had large deletions. We used the Entropy tool at LANL ( https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/content/sequence/ENTROPY/entropy.html ) to calculate entropy for each specimen. The sequencing data is available at NIH Sequencing Read Archive (SRA) under BioProject ID PRJNA853351.",The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.
36920025,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,Darunavir (DRV) is exceptional among potent HIV-1 protease inhibitors (PIs) in high drug concentrations that are achieved in vivo. Little is known about the de novo resistance pathway for DRV. We selected for resistance to high drug concentrations against 10 PIs and their structural precursor DRV. Resistance mutations selected in the protease coding domain during the escalating selective pressure of increasing PI concentration were examined at various timepoints using a next-generation sequencing (NGS) protocol that included Primer ID with the MiSeq platform.,The paper is focused on in vitro resistance studies using cell culture models rather than patient samples.
36920025,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not Applicable,"Selection of HIV-1 for resistance to fifth-generation protease inhibitors reveals two independent pathways to high-level resistance. Selection for high-level resistance during passage in cell culture follows two pathways In addition to the sequence of the terminally selected virus, we validated that selection occurred over the entire course of the selection protocol. Features of the selection process assessed by NGS of longitudinal samples",The study does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained as it does not involve patient samples.
36920025,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not Applicable,"Selection of HIV-1 for resistance to fifth-generation protease inhibitors reveals two independent pathways to high-level resistance. Selection for high-level resistance during passage in cell culture follows two pathways In addition to the sequence of the terminally selected virus, we validated that selection occurred over the entire course of the selection protocol. Features of the selection process assessed by NGS of longitudinal samples",The paper does not specify any years related to the collection of sequenced samples as it does not involve patient samples.
36920025,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Total cellular DNA was isolated from infected cell pellets by using the QIAamp blood kit (QIAGEN). The protease-coding domain of viral DNA was amplified by nested PCR. The PCR conditions are available upon request. PCR products were purified by using QIAquick PCR purification kit (QIAGEN) and directly sequenced or cloned into the pT7Blue vector (Novagen) and sequenced.,"The paper mentions that PCR products were purified and directly sequenced after amplification of the protease-coding domain of viral DNA, indicating that samples were not cloned prior to sequencing."
36920025,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,PR;pol,"The protease-coding domain of viral DNA was amplified by nested PCR. PCR products were purified by using QIAquick PCR purification kit (QIAGEN) and directly sequenced or cloned into the pT7Blue vector (Novagen) and sequenced. We used the Illumina bcl2fastq pipeline for the initial processing and constructed template consensus sequences (TCSs) with TCS pipeline version 1.33 ( https://github.com/SwanstromLab/PID ) ( Zhou, 2019 ). Gel-purified libraries were pooled and sequenced using the MiSeq 300 base paired-end sequencing protocol (Illumina). The sequencing covered the HIV-1 PR region (HXB2 2648-2914, 3001-3257).","The paper focuses on the sequencing of the protease (PR) gene of HIV-1, as indicated by the detailed studies and selections for resistance to protease inhibitors."
36920025,10,What method was used for sequencing?,NGS,"Resistance mutations selected in the protease coding domain during the escalating selective pressure of increasing PI concentration were examined at various timepoints using a next-generation sequencing (NGS) protocol that included Primer ID with the MiSeq platform ( Zhou et al., 2015 ). Gel-purified libraries were pooled and sequenced using the MiSeq 300 base paired-end sequencing protocol (Illumina).",The paper describes the use of next-generation sequencing (NGS) with the MiSeq platform to analyze resistance mutations in the protease coding domain of HIV-1.
36920025,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Cell Culture Supernatant,"As can be seen in bible 12 of the selections that reached at least 1 [micro]M in inhibitor concentrations, viral variants from the two pathways showed differential selection of compensatory mutations in Gag cleavage sites. In addition to the sequence of the terminally selected virus, we validated that selection occurred over the entire course of the selection protocol. We next evaluated the hypothesis of linked mutations by pool.— Genome levels of drug assoc and integr ated accum. Cellular sel 3 29 % Infected cultures were put under drug selection starting with wild-type virus or a pool of mutant viruses. Cell phenotypes were monitored for their ability to generate virus after transfection (293Ts), support viral replication (CEMx174), or report viral infection (TZM-bl). Notably, in the latter case only about one-half of the mutations that appeared in the culture during selections were in the mixture of starting mutations, indicating that even in the selections that were seeded with the pool of single resistance mutations there was sufficient evolutionary capacity to explore mutations at positions that started as the wild-type sequence.","The viral cultures started with wild-type virus. Each flask received one of the following inhibitors at escalating concentrations: UMass1, UMass2, UMass3, UMass4, UMass5, UMass6, UMass7, UMass8, UMass9, UMass10, DRV, and no drug (ND)."
36920025,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Darunavir (DRV) is exceptional among potent HIV-1 protease inhibitors (PIs) in high drug concentrations that are achieved in vivo. Little is known about the de novo resistance pathway for DRV. We selected for resistance to high drug concentrations against 10 PIs and their structural precursor DRV. Resistance mutations selected in the protease coding domain during the escalating selective pressure of increasing PI concentration were examined at various timepoints using a next-generation sequencing (NGS) protocol that included Primer ID with the MiSeq platform ( Zhou et al., 2015 ).","The paper does not mention studying sequences from individuals with virological failure; instead, it focuses on in vitro selection of resistance mutations."
36920025,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Darunavir (DRV) is exceptional among potent HIV-1 protease inhibitors (PIs) in high drug concentrations that are achieved in vivo. Little is known about the de novo resistance pathway for DRV. We selected for resistance to high drug concentrations against 10 PIs and their structural precursor DRV. Resistance mutations selected in the protease coding domain during the escalating selective pressure of increasing PI concentration were examined at various timepoints using a next-generation sequencing (NGS) protocol that included Primer ID with the MiSeq platform ( Zhou et al., 2015 ).",The paper does not mention whether the patients were part of a clinical trial; it focuses on in vitro studies.
36920025,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Darunavir (DRV) is exceptional among potent HIV-1 protease inhibitors (PIs) in high drug concentrations that are achieved in vivo. Little is known about the de novo resistance pathway for DRV. We selected for resistance to high drug concentrations against 10 PIs and their structural precursor DRV. Resistance mutations selected in the protease coding domain during the escalating selective pressure of increasing PI concentration were examined at various timepoints using a next-generation sequencing (NGS) protocol that included Primer ID with the MiSeq platform ( Zhou et al., 2015 ).",The paper does not mention obtaining sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs; it focuses on in vitro selection of resistance mutations.
36920025,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not Applicable,"Darunavir (DRV) is exceptional among potent HIV-1 protease inhibitors (PIs) in high drug concentrations that are achieved in vivo. Little is known about the de novo resistance pathway for DRV. We selected for resistance to high drug concentrations against 10 PIs and their structural precursor DRV. Resistance mutations selected in the protease coding domain during the escalating selective pressure of increasing PI concentration were examined at various timepoints using a next-generation sequencing (NGS) protocol that included Primer ID with the MiSeq platform ( Zhou et al., 2015 ).",The paper does not mention any drug classes received by individuals before sample sequencing; it focuses on in vitro drug susceptibility data.
36920025,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not Applicable,"Darunavir (DRV) is exceptional among potent HIV-1 protease inhibitors (PIs) in high drug concentrations that are achieved in vivo. Little is known about the de novo resistance pathway for DRV. We selected for resistance to high drug concentrations against 10 PIs and their structural precursor DRV. Resistance mutations selected in the protease coding domain during the escalating selective pressure of increasing PI concentration were examined at various timepoints using a next-generation sequencing (NGS) protocol that included Primer ID with the MiSeq platform ( Zhou et al., 2015 ).",The paper does not mention any specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing; it focuses on in vitro drug susceptibility data.
36931676,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and antiretroviral therapies (ART) and also on demographics, patient-reported outcome measures and patient-reported experience measures; HIV-RNA (plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)) and HIV drug resistance (including viral sequences) results, described as both mutations and predicted phenotypic sensitivity, as well as CD4+ and CD8+ T cell counts and CD4/CD8 ratios are either automatically or manually included depending on the HIV centre.","The evidence clearly states that the InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV drug resistance and viral sequences, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples are reported in the paper."
36931676,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The InfCareHIV cohort includes data on HIV-related biomarkers and antiretroviral therapies (ART) and also on demographics, patient-reported outcome measures and patient-reported experience measures.",The evidence mentions that the cohort includes data on antiretroviral therapies and HIV-related biomarkers but does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility data specifically.
36931676,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Data are available upon reasonable request. Data can be made available upon reasonable request and after ethical approval.,"The paper states that data are available upon reasonable request, which implies that the sequences are not made publicly available."
36931676,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
36931676,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,13029,InfCareHIV includes data on >99% of all people with diagnosed HIV in Sweden and up to now 13 029 have been included in the cohort.,"The paper states that up to 13,029 individuals have been included in the InfCareHIV cohort, indicating that samples were obtained from this number of individuals."
36931676,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Sweden,"The InfCareHIV cohort was established in 2003 in the two largest cities in Sweden, Stockholm and Gothenburg. These two cities care for about half of all PLHIV in Sweden.","The evidence indicates that the samples were obtained from individuals in Sweden, specifically from the cities of Stockholm and Gothenburg."
36931676,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2003-2022,"The cohort was established in 2003 and to date (August 2022), 13 029 PLHIV have been included.",The paper mentions that the cohort has been active since 2003 and includes data up to 2022.
36931676,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether the samples were cloned prior to sequencing.
36931676,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced.
36931676,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used.
36931676,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,CSF and Plasma,"In the major clinical centres blood plasma/serum/liquor samples are collected, and in selected cases also peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV isolates, CSF and CSF cells in biobanks.",The evidence mentions that plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) samples were collected and sequenced.
36931676,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether any sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure.
36931676,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,The cohort study was on a National Quality Registry.,The paper does not mention whether the patients were part of a clinical trial.
36931676,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Almost all are on ART (98%), and of them almost everybody (95%) reaches the treatment goal of HIV-RNA <50 copies/mL (98% HIV-RNA <200 copies/mL) in snapshot analysis after at least 6 months of treatment.","The evidence indicates that a significant majority of individuals in the cohort were on antiretroviral therapy (ART) and had achieved viral suppression, suggesting that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
36931676,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify which drug classes were received by individuals before sample sequencing.
36931676,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not provide details on the specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing.
36961945,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We investigated whether deep sequencing of archived HIV DNA of antiretroviral-naïve persons with acute/early HIV infection could identify transmitted drug resistance mutations (DRM), per the IAS drug resistance algorithm, that are not detected by routine bulk (consensus) sequencing. Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection.","The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples. This is evident from the methods section, where it mentions deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma of antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. The study focuses on detecting transmitted drug resistance mutations in these samples."
36961945,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We investigated whether deep sequencing of archived HIV DNA of antiretroviral-naïve persons with acute/early HIV infection could identify transmitted drug resistance mutations (DRM), per the IAS drug resistance algorithm, that are not detected by routine bulk (consensus) sequencing. Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. We compared the prevalence of low-frequency (2--20%) and high-frequency (\>20%) non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), and protease inhibitor (PI) DRM. Overall, 190 individuals were included, 72 (37.9%) with acute, 20 (10.5%) very early, and 98 (51.6%) with recent HIV infection. While all DRM detected in plasma appeared in archived proviral DNA, 9 high-frequency mutations were only detected in HIV DNA. These included 3 NRTI mutations, 4 NNRTI mutations, 1 PI mutation, and 1 H221Y (associated rilpivirine resistance) mutation. When considering DRM \<20%, 11 NNRTI and 7 NRTI, 6 PI, and 3 F227L (associated doravirine resistance) mutations were found exclusively in HIV DNA. Interestingly, while 2 high-frequency M184V appeared in both DNA and RNA, low-frequency M184I were exclusive to HIV DNA (n=6). No participants experienced virologic failure after initiating ART during the median 25.39 ± 3.13 months of follow-up on treatment.","The paper does not report any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the detection of drug resistance mutations in HIV DNA and RNA from patient samples, but there is no mention of in vitro testing of drug susceptibility."
36961945,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,There is no mention in the paper about the sequences being made publicly available. The methods section describes the sequencing processes but does not provide information on data availability.
36961945,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No evidence,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no mention of data submission to GenBank or any other public database.
36961945,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,190,"Overall, 190 individuals were included, 72 (37.9%) with acute, 20 (10.5%) very early, and 98 (51.6%) with recent HIV infection.","The paper included 190 individuals for HIV sequencing. This is stated in the results section, where the cohort characteristics are described."
36961945,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,United States,"This study included adults, age ≥18 years, with acute/early HIV infection enrolled in the San Diego PIRC.","The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained. However, it mentions the study cohort was from the San Diego PIRC, implying the samples were collected in the United States."
36961945,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2005 to 2020,Samples were collected from January 2005 to February 2020 ( Table S2 ).,The paper states that samples were collected from January 2005 to February 2020. This is mentioned in the methods section under the study cohort description.
36961945,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. We compared the prevalence of low-frequency (2–20%) and high-frequency (>20%) non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), and protease inhibitor (PI) DRM. Bulk genotyping of HIV reverse transcriptase (RT) was performed to identify transmitted drug resistance (GeneSeq HIV; Monogram Biosciences, Inc., South San Francisco, CA or Viroseq v.2.0; Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA) in blood plasma before ART initiation.17,18 Cumulative HIV RNA genotypes based on the IAS drug resistance algorithm19 for each participant were created by adding up all mutations from genotypic tests for drug classes as previously described.20 DNA extraction of bulk PMBCs was performed to isolate genomic DNA. Briefly, genomic DNA was isolated from patient PMBCs (106 cells) using QIAamp DNA blood mini kit (Qiagen, Hilden, Germany) according to the manufacturer’s protocol. Only DRMs present at ≥2% of cleaned mapped reads were considered. APOBEC hypermutation filtering was applied to reduce the frequency of potentially spurious mutations.22 NGS DRMs were categorized as high frequency and low frequency DRMs defined as >20% and 2–20% frequencies, respectively.",The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. The sequencing methods described do not involve cloning.
36961945,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. From the same blood samples used to assess blood plasma, NGS of HIV partial pol was performed to identify DRMs present in archived proviral DNA (peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC)). Genotypic resistance testing on PR, RT, and IN from proviral HIV-1 DNA was performed using a modified version of the “deepTypeHIV” assay (SeqIT GmbH & Co. KG, Kaiserlautern, Germany).","The paper mentions that genotypic resistance testing was performed on PR, RT, and IN from proviral HIV-1 DNA and NGS of HIV partial pol was performed to identify DRMs present in archived proviral DNA."
36961945,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing,"Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. From the same blood samples used to assess blood plasma, NGS of HIV partial pol was performed to identify DRMs present in archived proviral DNA (peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC)). Genotypic resistance testing on PR, RT, and IN from proviral HIV-1 DNA was performed using a modified version of the “deepTypeHIV” assay (SeqIT GmbH & Co. KG, Kaiserlautern, Germany).",The paper describes the use of next-generation sequencing (NGS) for deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and for detecting drug resistance mutations.
36961945,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Blood plasma, PBMC","Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. From the same blood samples used to assess blood plasma, NGS of HIV partial pol was performed to identify DRMs present in archived proviral DNA (peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC)).",The paper mentions that consensus sequencing was performed from concurrent blood plasma and NGS of HIV partial pol was performed to identify DRMs present in archived proviral DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC).
36961945,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Overall, 190 individuals were included, 72 (37.9%) with acute, 20 (10.5%) very early, and 98 (51.6%) with recent HIV infection. While all DRM detected in plasma appeared in archived proviral DNA, 9 high-frequency mutations were only detected in HIV DNA. These included 3 NRTI mutations, 4 NNRTI mutations, 1 PI mutation, and 1 H221Y (associated rilpivirine resistance) mutation. When considering DRM <20%, 11 NNRTI and 7 NRTI, 6 PI, and 3 F227L (associated doravirine resistance) mutations were found exclusively in HIV DNA. Interestingly, while 2 high-frequency M184V appeared in both DNA and RNA, low-frequency M184I were exclusive to HIV DNA (n=6). No participants experienced virologic failure after initiating ART during the median 25.39 ± 3.13 months of follow-up on treatment. Overall, we detected NRTI, NNRTI and PI DRMs in 49 out of 190 participants (26%) through either standard sequencing or NGS methods.",The paper mentions that no participants experienced virologic failure after initiating ART during the median 25.39 months of follow-up on treatment. It also states that all participants were treatment naïve individuals at the time of sample collection.
36961945,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Study cohort This study included adults, age ≥18 years, with acute/early HIV infection enrolled in the San Diego PIRC. Individuals who met serologic and virologic criteria for acute and early HIV infection was tested using a single-well limiting-antigen avidity enzyme immunoassay (Lag-Avidity EIA) and enrolled based on their estimated date of infection (EDI) calculated as previously reported.13,14 Briefly, EDI status is defined as follows: Acute (antibody [HIV Ab−]/antigen [Ag+]), Very Early (Ab+/Recency Assay consistent with infection <133 days), and Recent (recency assay consistent with infection <180 days or Ab+ with documented Ab− in last 365 days).13,14 Study participants were screened for NNRTI, NRTI, and PI mutations in blood plasma at baseline (bulk genotyping) before ART initiation (see Table S1; detailed list of transmitted DRM and frequencies). Participants were encouraged to initiate ART as soon as possible, often at the baseline visit (Day 0). Plasma HIV RNA levels were measured at baseline (Day 0), weeks 2, 4, 8, 12, 24, 36, 48, and every 24 weeks thereafter (Cobas Amplicor HIV-1 test, Roche Molecular Systems, Pleasanton, California). Viral rebound was defined as a single viral load > 50 copies/mL after an undetectable level as previously described.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. The study was conducted among participants enrolled in the San Diego PIRC, and while it describes the participants' treatment regimens and follow-up, it doesn’t mention clinical trial protocols."
36961945,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Overall, 190 individuals were included, 72 (37.9%) with acute, 20 (10.5%) very early, and 98 (51.6%) with recent HIV infection. While all DRM detected in plasma appeared in archived proviral DNA, 9 high-frequency mutations were only detected in HIV DNA. These included 3 NRTI mutations, 4 NNRTI mutations, 1 PI mutation, and 1 H221Y (associated rilpivirine resistance) mutation. When considering DRM <20%, 11 NNRTI and 7 NRTI, 6 PI, and 3 F227L (associated doravirine resistance) mutations were found exclusively in HIV DNA. Interestingly, while 2 high-frequency M184V appeared in both DNA and RNA, low-frequency M184I were exclusive to HIV DNA (n=6). No participants experienced virologic failure after initiating ART during the median 25.39 ± 3.13 months of follow-up on treatment. Overall, we detected NRTI, NNRTI and PI DRMs in 49 out of 190 participants (26%) through either standard sequencing or NGS methods. Deep sequencing of HIV DNA from peripheral blood mononuclear cells and consensus sequencing from concurrent blood plasma (BP) was performed from antiretroviral (ART)-naïve adults with recent infection. We compared the prevalence of low-frequency (2–20%) and high-frequency (>20%) non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), and protease inhibitor (PI) DRM. This study included adults, age ≥18 years, with acute/early HIV infection enrolled in the San Diego PIRC. Individuals who met serologic and virologic criteria for acute and early HIV infection was tested using a single-well limiting-antigen avidity enzyme immunoassay (Lag-Avidity EIA) and enrolled based on their estimated date of infection (EDI) calculated as previously reported.",The paper does not report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. It specifically mentions implementing the study on antiretroviral-naïve individuals.
36961945,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","We compared the prevalence of low-frequency (2–20%) and high-frequency (>20%) non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), and protease inhibitor (PI) DRM. Study participants were screened for NNRTI, NRTI, and PI mutations in blood plasma at baseline (bulk genotyping) before ART initiation (see Table S1; detailed list of transmitted DRM and frequencies).","The paper mentions that participants were screened for NNRTI, NRTI, and PI mutations in blood plasma at baseline before ART initiation. However, it does not provide details on the specific drug classes received prior to sample sequencing."
36961945,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No evidence,"We compared the prevalence of low-frequency (2–20%) and high-frequency (>20%) non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI), nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), and protease inhibitor (PI) DRM. Study participants were screened for NNRTI, NRTI, and PI mutations in blood plasma at baseline (bulk genotyping) before ART initiation (see Table S1; detailed list of transmitted DRM and frequencies).",The paper doesn’t provide a detailed history of the specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. The focus was on screening for drug resistance mutations in blood plasma and HIV DNA from patient samples.
36967989,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The main aim of this study is to construct drug isolate fold (DIF) change-based artificial neural network (ANN) models for estimating the resistance potential of molecules inhibiting the HIV-1 protease (PR) enzyme. Throughout the study, seven of eight protease inhibitors (PIs) have been included in the training set and the remaining ones in the test set. We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database.","The paper focuses on machine learning models to predict drug resistance profiles and mentions genotype-phenotype data from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database, but does not report new HIV sequences from patient samples."
36967989,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database. Using the leave-one-out (LVO) procedure, eight ANN models have been produced to measure the learning capacity of models from the descriptors of the inhibitors. Mean R^2 value of eight ANN models for unseen inhibitors is 0.716, and the 95% confidence interval (CI) is [0.592–0.840].","The paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data, as it mentions obtaining genotype-phenotype data points for protease inhibitors from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36967989,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,All data and necessary codes are available at Github and Zenodo: https://github.com/tnchsyn/hivdrugisolatefoldchange_model ; tnchsyn. (2023). tnchsyn/hivdrugisolatefoldchange_model: v1.0 (v1.0). Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7527918.,The data availability section of the paper mentions that all data and necessary codes are available at Github and Zenodo.
36967989,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers.,"The main aim of this study is to construct drug isolate fold (DIF) change-based artificial neural network (ANN) models for estimating the resistance potential of molecules inhibiting the HIV-1 protease (PR) enzyme. Throughout the study, seven of eight protease inhibitors (PIs) have been included in the training set and the remaining ones in the test set. We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database,Filtered genotype-phenotype data on the Stanford HIV drug resistance database was retrieved for PIs ( Das & Arnold, 2013 ). We have organized this dataset with respect to isolates and inhibitors, and 498 protease mutations have been observed. For the HIV-1 PI: 1218 isolates for atazanavir (ATV), 678 isolates for darunavir (DRV), 1809 isolates for fosemprenavir (FPV), 1,860 isolates for indinavir (IDV), 1,562 isolates for lopinavir (LPV), 1907 isolates for nelfinavir (NFV), 1,861 isolates for saquinavir (SQV) and 908 isolates for tipranavir (TPV) have been analyzed for PI susceptibility. In the dataset, 436, 336, 480, 483, 472, 486, 489 and 409 different mutations have been observed for ATV, DRV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV, SQV, and TPV, respectively.","The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, focusing instead on machine learning models and genotype-phenotype data."
36967989,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper does not specify the number of individuals.,"Throughout the study, seven of eight protease inhibitors (PIs) have been included in the training set and the remaining ones in the test set. We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database. Using the leave-one-out (LVO) procedure, eight ANN models have been produced to measure the learning capacity of models from the descriptors of the inhibitors. The mean R2 value of eight ANN models for unseen inhibitors is 0.716, and the 95% confidence interval (CI) is [0.592–0.840].","The paper does not specify the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, as it focuses on machine learning models and data from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36967989,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the countries of origin for sequenced samples.,"Filtered genotype-phenotype data on the Stanford HIV drug resistance database was retrieved for PIs ( Das & Arnold, 2013 ). We have organized this dataset with respect to isolates and inhibitors, and 498 protease mutations have been observed. For the HIV-1 PI: 1218 isolates for atazanavir (ATV), 678 isolates for darunavir (DRV), 1809 isolates for fosemprenavir (FPV), 1,860 isolates for indinavir (IDV), 1,562 isolates for lopinavir (LPV), 1907 isolates for nelfinavir (NFV), 1,861 isolates for saquinavir (SQV) and 908 isolates for tipranavir (TPV) have been analyzed for PI susceptibility. In the dataset, 436, 336, 480, 483, 472, 486, 489 and 409 different mutations have been observed for ATV, DRV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV, SQV, and TPV, respectively.","The paper does not provide information on the countries of origin for sequenced samples, as it focuses on data from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36967989,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which sequenced samples were obtained.,"Throughout the study, seven of eight protease inhibitors (PIs) have been included in the training set and the remaining ones in the test set. We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database. Using the leave-one-out (LVO) procedure, eight ANN models have been produced to measure the learning capacity of models from the descriptors of the inhibitors. The mean R2 value of eight ANN models for unseen inhibitors is 0.716, and the 95% confidence interval (CI) is [0.592–0.840].","The paper does not mention the years from which sequenced samples were obtained, as it focuses on machine learning models and data from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36967989,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"In this way, each isolate can be transformed into a unique 498-dimensional input vector used for the machine learning. The binary barcoding approach has the advantage of representing two or more mutational changes in the amino acids since each mutation has a unique position in the 498-dimensional input vector. For example, assuming that x480 and x481 denotes mutations V82F and V82I, the isolate I=[V82F, V82I] can be represented as X=[0, 0, ..., 0, 1, 1, 0, ..., 0]. It is important to note that, the genotype-fold change measurements are made on population of viruses, so that it is possible to find two separate mutations for a single residue.","The paper does not mention any process of cloning samples prior to sequencing, it focuses on other technical methods."
36967989,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper mentions the sequencing of the protease gene.,"The main aim of this study is to construct drug isolate fold (DIF) change-based artificial neural network (ANN) models for estimating the resistance potential of molecules inhibiting the HIV-1 protease (PR) enzyme. Throughout the study, seven of eight protease inhibitors (PIs) have been included in the training set and the remaining ones in the test set. We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database.","The paper focuses on the sequencing of the protease gene, as it mentions obtaining genotype-phenotype data points for protease inhibitors from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36967989,10,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used.,"Filtered genotype-phenotype data on the Stanford HIV drug resistance database was retrieved for PIs ( Das & Arnold, 2013 ). We have organized this dataset with respect to isolates and inhibitors, and 498 protease mutations have been observed. For the HIV-1 PI: 1218 isolates for atazanavir (ATV), 678 isolates for darunavir (DRV), 1809 isolates for fosemprenavir (FPV), 1,860 isolates for indinavir (IDV), 1,562 isolates for lopinavir (LPV), 1907 isolates for nelfinavir (NFV), 1,861 isolates for saquinavir (SQV) and 908 isolates for tipranavir (TPV) have been analyzed for PI susceptibility. In the dataset, 436, 336, 480, 483, 472, 486, 489 and 409 different mutations have been observed for ATV, DRV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV, SQV, and TPV, respectively.","The paper does not mention the sequencing method used, as it focuses on data from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36967989,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper does not specify the type of clinical specimens sequenced.,"Filtered genotype-phenotype data on the Stanford HIV drug resistance database was retrieved for PIs ( Das & Arnold, 2013 ). We have organized this dataset with respect to isolates and inhibitors, and 498 protease mutations have been observed. For the HIV-1 PI: 1218 isolates for atazanavir (ATV), 678 isolates for darunavir (DRV), 1809 isolates for fosemprenavir (FPV), 1,860 isolates for indinavir (IDV), 1,562 isolates for lopinavir (LPV), 1907 isolates for nelfinavir (NFV), 1,861 isolates for saquinavir (SQV) and 908 isolates for tipranavir (TPV) have been analyzed for PI susceptibility. In the dataset, 436, 336, 480, 483, 472, 486, 489 and 409 different mutations have been observed for ATV, DRV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV, SQV, and TPV, respectively.","The paper does not specify the type of clinical specimens sequenced, as it focuses on data from the Stanford HIV drug resistance database."
36967989,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database. The inhibition potential of each PI in the presence of various genotypes is known to be variable. Tendencies of the logarithmic fold change values for each PI pair provide valuable information about the resistance profiles of the inhibitors, as seen in Fig. 2. Prediction of these tendencies by the DIF-based ANN models and the corresponding 2D correlation coefficients are presented in Fig. 2 in a comparative way for each PI pair.","The paper does not mention obtaining sequences from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen, it focuses on other technical methods."
36967989,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Filtered genotype-phenotype data on the Stanford HIV drug resistance database was retrieved for PIs ( Das & Arnold, 2013 ). We have organized this dataset with respect to isolates and inhibitors, and 498 protease mutations have been observed. For the HIV-1 PI: 1218 isolates for atazanavir (ATV), 678 isolates for darunavir (DRV), 1809 isolates for fosemprenavir (FPV), 1,860 isolates for indinavir (IDV), 1,562 isolates for lopinavir (LPV), 1907 isolates for nelfinavir (NFV), 1,861 isolates for saquinavir (SQV) and 908 isolates for tipranavir (TPV) have been analyzed for PI susceptibility. In the dataset, 436, 336, 480, 483, 472, 486, 489 and 409 different mutations have been observed for ATV, DRV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV, SQV, and TPV, respectively.","The paper does not mention whether the patients were in a clinical trial, it focuses on other technical methods."
36967989,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database. The inhibition potential of each PI in the presence of various genotypes is known to be variable. Tendencies of the logarithmic fold change values for each PI pair provide valuable information about the resistance profiles of the inhibitors, as seen in Fig. 2. Prediction of these tendencies by the DIF-based ANN models and the corresponding 2D correlation coefficients are presented in Fig. 2 in a comparative way for each PI pair.",The paper reports that the Stanford HIV drug resistance database is made accessible which means data points represents sequences from individuals who had previously receives ARV drugs.
36967989,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, Entry inhibitors","Throughout the study, seven of eight protease inhibitors (PIs) have been included in the training set and the remaining ones in the test set. We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database. The inhibition potential of each PI in the presence of various genotypes is known to be variable. Tendencies of the logarithmic fold change values for each PI pair provide valuable information about the resistance profiles of the inhibitors, as seen in Fig. 2. Prediction of these tendencies by the DIF-based ANN models and the corresponding 2D correlation coefficients are presented in Fig. 2 in a comparative way for each PI pair. This includes saquinavir, ritonavir, indinavir, nelfinavir, lopinavir, atazanavir darunavir, and tipranavir","The paper mentions data points for all who had received PIs , this means they received all drug classes."
36967989,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Saquinavir, ritonavir, indinavir, nelfinavir, lopinavir, atazanavir darunavir, tipranavir","Throughout the study, seven of eight protease inhibitors (PIs) have been included in the training set and the remaining ones in the test set. We have obtained 11,803 genotype-phenotype data points for eight PIs from Stanford HIV drug resistance database. The inhibition potential of each PI in the presence of various genotypes is known to be variable. Tendencies of the logarithmic fold change values for each PI pair provide valuable information about the resistance profiles of the inhibitors, as seen in Fig. 2. Prediction of these tendencies by the DIF-based ANN models and the corresponding 2D correlation coefficients are presented in Fig. 2 in a comparative way for each PI pair. This includes saquinavir, ritonavir, indinavir, nelfinavir, lopinavir, atazanavir darunavir, and tipranavir","The paper mentions the specific drugs that were used in the study, indicating that samples were obtained from individuals who had received these drugs."
36982978,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Antiretroviral Treatment of HIV-2 Infection: Available Drugs, Resistance Pathways, and Promising New Compounds. The course of HIV-2 infection is longer compared to HIV-1 infection, but without effective antiretroviral therapy (ART), a substantial proportion of infected patients will progress to AIDS and die. Antiretroviral drugs in clinical use were designed for HIV-1 and, unfortunately, some do not work as well, or do not work at all, for HIV-2. This is the case for non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), the fusion inhibitor enfuvirtide (T-20), most protease inhibitors (PIs), the attachment inhibitor fostemsavir and most broadly neutralizing antibodies. Integrase inhibitors work well against HIV-2 and are included in first-line therapeutic regimens for HIV-2-infected patients. However, rapid emergence of drug resistance and cross-resistance within each drug class dramatically reduces second-line treatment options. New drugs are needed to treat infection with drug-resistant isolates. Here, we review the therapeutic armamentarium available to treat HIV-2-infected patients, as well as promising drugs in development. We also review HIV-2 drug resistance mutations and resistance pathways that develop in HIV-2-infected patients under treatment.",The paper discusses the treatment and resistance of HIV-2 but does not mention any specific HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses more on the drug resistance pathways and available treatments for HIV-2 infection rather than on the sequences of the virus itself.
36982978,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Integrase inhibitors work well against HIV-2 and are included in first-line therapeutic regimens for HIV-2-infected patients. However, rapid emergence of drug resistance and cross-resistance within each drug class dramatically reduces second-line treatment options. New drugs are needed to treat infection with drug-resistant isolates. Here, we review the therapeutic armamentarium available to treat HIV-2-infected patients, as well as promising drugs in development. We also review HIV-2 drug resistance mutations and resistance pathways that develop in HIV-2-infected patients under treatment.

Studies have shown that NRTIs on average are more potent against HIV-2 than HIV-1, although some clinical isolates from INSTI-naïve HIV-2-infected patients display high level resistance especially to AZT, 3TC and FTC. RAL was the first INSTI approved by the FDA for clinical use in HIV-1 infection in 2007 and is given once daily (1200 mg) or twice daily (400 mg) in combination with two NRTIs. Soon afterwards, Roquebert et al. demonstrated that RAL was a potent inhibitor of HIV-2 clinical isolates (IC50 = 1.3--5 nM) [111]. Damond et al. quantified the virological and immunological response to an ARV regimen containing RAL in two patients infected with HIV-2 with multiple drug resistances, high viral load (>6000 HIV-2 RNA copies/mL) and low T CD4+ counts (<25 cells/µL). Viral load was undetectable after the second month of therapy and significant increases in T CD4+ cell counts were observed and maintained for six months. More recently, in a phase 2 non-comparative trial, 87% of drug-naïve HIV-2-infected patients treated with a RAL-based regimen achieved, at week 48, a viral load < 5 copies/mL and 40% of the patients gained > 100 CD4 T cells/µL [119]. In this study, undetectable viraemia was also observed in 37% of ART-experienced patients, and 80% of patients presenting virological failure exhibited drug resistance mutations. Overall, these reports showed that RAL was effective in suppressing viral load in drug naïve and most treatment experienced HIV-2-infected patients. However, they also showed that RAL-resistance mutations emerge rapidly. Evotaz is a fixed-dose combination tablet of ATV 300 mg and ritonavir 100 mg. It is given once daily as part of a TAF-based regimen (TAF + FTC + DTG) for treatment naïve HIV-1 or INSTI-naïve HIV-2 infection. Confirmatory studies are warranted to evaluate the efficacy of this INSTI in HIV-2-infected individuals.","The paper provides data on the in vitro susceptibility of HIV-2 to various antiretroviral drugs, including NRTIs and INSTIs. It includes specific IC50 values for these drugs against HIV-2 clinical isolates, indicating that the paper does report in vitro drug susceptibility data."
36982978,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Not reported,Data Availability Statement: The data presented in this study are available in this article.,The paper does not explicitly state that any sequences were made publicly available. It focuses on the review of drug resistance and treatment options for HIV-2 without mentioning the deposition of sequences in public databases.
