37017009,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces. Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies.","The study involves sequencing from patient samples as it discusses HIV drug resistance patterns in children, resistance genotyping, and NGS techologies."
37017009,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods: This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces. Resistance genotyping was performed using next-generation sequencing technologies. Descriptive analysis taking into account survey design was used to determine outcomes.","The paper does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility data, focusing instead on genotypic resistance in patient samples."
37017009,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether the sequences obtained were deposited in a public database or made available to the public.
37017009,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37017009,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,899,"At study closure, 1000 children had participated in the study. Of these, 899 participants from 40 facilities from eight provinces (EC, FS, GP, KZ, LP, MP, NC and WC) were included in the study analysis.","The paper mentions that 899 participants were included in the final analysis, which corresponds to the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
37017009,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,South Africa,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.","The study was conducted across 45 clinics in South Africa, and all sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
37017009,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017-2019,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.","The study was conducted from March 2017 to March 2019, during which the samples were collected."
37017009,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Four hundred and twenty-two participants were receiving NNRTI-based regimens 
[45.0% (95% CI 38.2%--52.1%)]; the median age of these participants was 13.4 (IQR 10.5--15.6) years and the median most recent VL was 18 500 (IQR 4530-66 881) copies/mL. The majority were receiving efavirenz-based regimens (98.7%); seven participants were receiving nevirapine. Amongst children aged 0--4 years, 8.7% were receiving NNRTI-based regimens, and 47.0% of children aged 5--9 years, 52.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 48.9% amongst children 15--19 years.

A total of 55 (6.5%, 95% CI 3.8%--11.1%) participants were receiving NRTI-only-based regimens. These included lamivudine only (n = 21), abacavir + lamivudine (n = 18) and abacavir only (n = 2), whilst the remainder were combinations of zidovudine, lamivudine, emtricitabine, stavudine and/or tenofovir disoproxil fumarate. The median age of these participants was 14.6 (IQR 9.1--15.4) years and the median VL was 27 670 (IQR 11 982--263 000) copies/mL.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that this step was not performed."
37017009,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and reverse transcriptase,PCR amplification was performed using an in-house method resulting in a single amplicon spanning 1.5 kb of pol encompassing the protease gene and the first 250 amino acids of the reverse transcriptase gene (HXB2 2358--3882).,The paper mentions that the sequencing involved the pol gene encompassing the protease gene and the first 250 amino acids of the reverse transcriptase gene.
37017009,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS) and Sanger sequencing,"Quantified amplicons were sequenced using the MiSeq V3 sequencing Kit (Illumina, San Diego, USA). In the event of PCR non-amplification or next-generation sequencing failure, a smaller amplicon was attempted as previously described and sequenced using Sanger sequencing technologies.","The paper describes the use of the MiSeq V3 sequencing Kit (Illumina) for NGS, and also mentions Sanger sequencing as a backup method."
37017009,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood,"Once enrolled, the HCW completed the study data collection form that abstracted minimal clinical and sociodemographic data from the patient medical file and drew a fresh blood sample. Whole blood was collected using standard phlebotomy methods into a 6 mL EDTA tube. A minimum of 500 μL plasma was ultra-centrifuged and total nucleic acid extracted using the MagNA Pure 2.0 automated extraction system (Roche Applied Science, Penzberg, Germany).",The paper details the collection of blood specimens for genotyping and sequencing.
37017009,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces. Among 899 participants enrolled, the adjusted proportion of HIV drug resistance among children with virological failure was 87.5% (95% CI 83.0%--90.9%).","The study specifically focused on children with virological failure, as evidenced by the enrollment criteria and the mention of virological failure."
37017009,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This cross-sectional study, performed from March 2017 to March 2019 in South Africa, enrolled HIV-positive children aged ≤19 years, receiving ART through public health facilities with recent evidence suggestive of virological failure (at least one viral load ≥1000 copies/mL), across 45 randomly selected high-volume clinics from all nine provinces.","The study was a cross-sectional assessment of HIV drug resistance and not a clinical trial, as described in the methods."
37017009,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"To be eligible for inclusion in the study, the children had to be aged ≤19 years, on ART for a minimum of 1 year (±3 months), and with suggestive evidence of VF performed within the previous 6 months. Approximately half of all the children in this study were receiving PI-based regimens, whilst the median duration on ART was 5.1 years. <...> Participants were receiving ART for a median of 1.0 (IQR 0.5--2.0) years. A total of 418 children were receiving PI-based regimens, constituting 48.4% (95% CI 41.4%--55.3%) of all participants after adjusting for survey design; the median age of these participants was 11.7 (IQR 5.5--15.2) years, and the median most recent VL was 36 800 (7800--116 200) copies/mL. The majority were receiving ritonavir-boosted lopinavir-based regimens (97.1%), whilst 2.9% were receiving ritonavir-boosted atazanavir. The proportions of children aged 0--4 years who were receiving PI-based regimens was 83.5%, 47.1% of children aged 5--9 years, 40.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 44.7% of children 15--19 years. <...> Four hundred and twenty-two participants were receiving NNRTI-based regimens 
[45.0% (95% CI 38.2%--52.1%)
]; the median age of these participants was 13.4 (IQR 10.5--15.6) years and the median most recent VL was 18 500 (IQR 4530-66 881) copies/mL. The majority were receiving efavirenz-based regimens (98.7%); seven participants were receiving nevirapine. Amongst children aged 0--4 years, 8.7% were receiving NNRTI-based regimens, and 47.0% of children aged 5--9 years, 52.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 48.9% amongst children 15--19 years. <...> A total of 55 (6.5%, 95% CI 3.8%--11.1%) participants were receiving NRTI-only-based regimens. These included lamivudine only ( *n* = 21), abacavir + lamivudine ( *n* = 18) and abacavir only ( *n* = 2), whilst the remainder were combinations of zidovudine, lamivudine, emtricitabine, stavudine and/or tenofovir disoproxil fumarate. The median age of these participants was 14.6 (IQR 9.1--15.4) years and the median VL was 27 670 (IQR 11 982--263 000) copies/mL.","The study involved children who were receiving ART, which indicates that they had previously received ARV drugs."
37017009,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","Approximately half of all the children in this study were receiving PI-based regimens, whilst the median duration on ART was 5.1 years. <...> A total of 418 children were receiving PI-based regimens, constituting 48.4% (95% CI 41.4%--55.3%) of all participants after adjusting for survey design; the median age of these participants was 11.7 (IQR 5.5--15.2) years, and the median most recent VL was 36 800 (7800--116 200) copies/mL. The majority were receiving ritonavir-boosted lopinavir-based regimens (97.1%), whilst 2.9% were receiving ritonavir-boosted atazanavir. The proportions of children aged 0--4 years who were receiving PI-based regimens was 83.5%, 47.1% of children aged 5--9 years, 40.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 44.7% of children 15--19 years. Four hundred and twenty-two participants were receiving NNRTI-based regimens 
[45.0% (95% CI 38.2%--52.1%)
]; the median age of these participants was 13.4 (IQR 10.5--15.6) years and the median most recent VL was 18 500 (IQR 4530-66 881) copies/mL. The majority were receiving efavirenz-based regimens (98.7%); seven participants were receiving nevirapine. Amongst children aged 0--4 years, 8.7% were receiving NNRTI-based regimens, and 47.0% of children aged 5--9 years, 52.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 48.9% amongst children 15--19 years. A total of 55 (6.5%, 95% CI 3.8%--11.1%) participants were receiving NRTI-only-based regimens. These included lamivudine only ( *n* = 21), abacavir + lamivudine ( *n* = 18) and abacavir only ( *n* = 2), whilst the remainder were combinations of zidovudine, lamivudine, emtricitabine, stavudine and/or tenofovir disoproxil fumarate.","The paper describes the participants receiving regimens that included NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs."
37017009,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Lopinavir, atazanavir, efavirenz, nevirapine, lamivudine, abacavir, zidovudine, emtricitabine, stavudine, tenofovir","Approximately half of all the children in this study were receiving PI-based regimens, whilst the median duration on ART was 5.1 years. Four hundred and twenty-two participants were receiving NNRTI-based regimens (45.0%  ( 95% CI  38.2%--52.1%) ); the median age of these participants was 13.4 (IQR 10.5--15.6) years and the median most recent VL was 18 500 (IQR 4530-66 881) copies/mL. The majority were receiving efavirenz-based regimens (98.7%); seven participants were receiving nevirapine. Amongst children aged 0--4 years, 8.7% were receiving NNRTI-based regimens, and 47.0% of children aged 5--9 years, 52.4% of children aged 10--14 years, and 48.9% amongst children 15--19 years. A total of 55 (6.5%, 95% CI 3.8%--11.1%) participants were receiving NRTI-only-based regimens. These included lamivudine only (n = 21), abacavir + lamivudine (n = 18) and abacavir only (n = 2), whilst the remainder were combinations of zidovudine, lamivudine, emtricitabine, stavudine and/or tenofovir disoproxil fumarate. A total of 418 children were receiving PI-based regimens, constituting 48.4% (95% CI 41.4%--55.3%) of all participants after adjusting for survey design; the median age of these participants was 11.7 (IQR 5.5--15.2) years, and the median most recent VL was 36 800 (7800--116 200) copies/mL. The majority were receiving ritonavir-boosted lopinavir-based regimens (97.1%), whilst 2.9% were receiving ritonavir-boosted atazanavir.","The paper lists the specific drugs included in the regimens received by the participants, which are all mentioned."
37029656,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The aim of the study was to evaluate the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.","The paper mentions that the study involved a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed on adult naive PLWH, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
37029656,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The aim of the study was to evaluate the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.","The paper focuses on the prevalence of transmitted INSTI drug resistance and the factors associated with it, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data."
37029656,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The aim of the study was to evaluate the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.",The paper does not mention whether the sequences obtained were made publicly available.
37029656,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"The aim of the study was to evaluate the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade. We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37029656,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1223,"Overall, 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated: 1109 (90.7%) were male, 860 (70.3%) were MSM and the median age was 37 (IQR, 30--45) years.",The paper states that a total of 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated in the study.
37029656,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Italy,Italian origin was found in 578 (78.0%) and 971 (79.4%) were infected by HIV-1 subtype B.,"The paper mentions that the study was conducted in Italy and provides demographic information about the participants, including their origin."
37029656,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2009-2019,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation.",The paper  mentions that the study was conducted over the period from 2009 to 2019.
37029656,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"GRT was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA); we included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection.",The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing for the genotypic resistance test (GRT) and does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
37029656,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not explicitly stated,We included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection. The degree of resistance to each INSTI drug was calculated using the Genotypic Resistance Interpretation Algorithm of the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database Program (version 9.1.1) ( https://hivdb.stanford.edu/hivdb/by-patterns/ ).,The paper mentions the study of INSTI mutations but doesn't specify the genes sequenced.
37029656,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"GRT was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA); we included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection.",The paper specifies that the genotypic resistance test (GRT) was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer.
37029656,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Clinical samples,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009–19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation. GRT was determined by Sanger sequencing using an ABI PRISM 3130xl Genetic Analyzer (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA); we included in the analysis the immediate pre-ART GRT available in people newly diagnosed with HIV infection.",The paper mentioned that the GRT genotypic resistance test was performed on clinical samples from adult naive LGBTQ individuals.
37029656,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009--19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation. Overall, 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated: 1109 (90.7%) were male, 860 (70.3%) were MSM and the median age was 37 (IQR, 30--45) years.","The paper specifically mentions that the study evaluated ART-naive PLWH, meaning that the sequences were not obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37029656,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009–19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation. The aim of the study was to evaluate the prevalence trend of transmitted INSTI drug resistance (DR) and to assess factors associated with transmitted INSTI resistance among ART-naive PLWH over the past decade.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; it describes  observational study of ART-naive PLWH.
37029656,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009–19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation. Overall, 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated: 1109 (90.7%) were male, 860 (70.3%) were MSM and the median age was 37 (IQR, 30–45) years.","The paper mentions that the study evaluated ART-naive PLWH, indicating that the sequences were not from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37029656,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009–19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation. Overall, 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated: 1109 (90.7%) were male, 860 (70.3%) were MSM and the median age was 37 (IQR, 30–45) years.","The paper specifies that the study involved ART-naive PLWH, meaning they had not received any drug classes before sample sequencing."
37029656,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We conducted a time-trend, single-centre study over the period 2009–19 on adult naive PLWH with a genotypic resistance test (GRT) performed before the ART initiation. Overall, 1223 ART-naive PLWH were evaluated: 1109 (90.7%) were male, 860 (70.3%) were MSM and the median age was 37 (IQR, 30–45) years.","The paper specifies that the study involved ART-naive PLWH, meaning they had not received any drugs before sample sequencing."
37039023,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,All samples included in the study were sequenced and 69 (80.2%) had the HIV NFLG determined.,"The paper discusses the sequencing of HIV near full-length genomes from patient samples, indicating that HIV sequences were reported."
37039023,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This study analyse the genetic composition of the HIV near full-length genome (NFLG) from archived proviruses of PLWH under successful ART, and determine the presence/frequency of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and viral subtype.","The focus of the study is on genetic composition and drug resistance mutations, not on in vitro susceptibility data."
37039023,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The raw sequencing reads were deposited to the Sequence Read Archive under the BioProject PRJNA419915.,"The authors mentioned that the raw sequencing reads were deposited, indicating that sequences were made publicly available."
37039023,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,NC_001802,"Sequence data were analysed on Geneious software v.9.1.8 (Biomatters Limited, Auckland, New Zealand) as described,29,34 through reference-based assembly to the HXB2 reference sequence (GenBank accession NC_001802).","The paper mentions the use of the HXB2 reference sequence with GenBank accession number NC_001802 during data analysis, but does not mention the accession numbers for the sequenced samples itself."
37039023,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,86,"Overall, 86 HIV+ patients were recruited: 34 from HI, 12 from HUCFF and 40 from HU-FURG.","The paper states that 86 HIV+ patients were recruited, indicating the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained."
37039023,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Brazil,"HIV-positive patients were recruited during their regular visits at Hospital Federal de Ipanema (HI) and Hospital Universitário Clementino Fraga Filho (HUCFF), both located in Rio de Janeiro (RJ; southeastern Brazil), and at Hospital Universitário Doutor Miguel Riet Corrêa Junior (HU-FURG) in Rio Grande do Sul (RS; southern Brazil).","The study was conducted in Brazil, specifically in Rio de Janeiro and Rio Grande do Sul, indicating the country of origin for the samples."
37039023,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2016 to 2018,"All samples from RJ were collected between February 2016 and November 2018, whereas all samples from RS were collected between August and December 2017.","The paper provides specific time frames for when the samples were collected, indicating the years of the sequenced samples."
37039023,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Nested PCR was used to amplify the HIV NFLG through three different strategies or combinations thereof.,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
37039023,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Near full-length genome (NFLG), Protease, Reverse transcriptase, Integrase, Envelope.","In this study, we analysed the presence and frequency of antiretroviral resistance mutations in the archived proviruses of HIV-1 infected patients that maintained undetectable viral loads for at least 12 months, all 86 study samples had at least one of these genomic regions analysed. Consensus sequences were used for the construction of a maximum likelihood phylogenetic tree for HIV-1 subtype classification. Sequences comprising the NFLG (n=69) are represented in Figure 1. Retrovirology, 2017, 14(1), 17. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12977-017-0312-1; Root mean square error, 7.4% for nucleotide substitutions at the level of species., 2017. Evaluation of near full-length sequencing of HIV-1 by high-throughput sequencing. 2A. Total. Reverse NRTI RTM G160A G190E G194A A62V D67N Q151M G184V. 56% (50/86) - Hyp. One patient with total 34%. CMJ. HIV1Gtb Abstract P014. Tropism CXC chemokine receptor. 14. HIV-1 integrase gene sequences: HIVdb. 2B. Minor PI PRM L10I MQ228L I15IV at 46% (16/34). 10.0786/jcm.008097-07. Retrovirology. 1853. Intraperson Tropism. 1.19, 1998. 12 months D1, unfit. 23, 2005. 7%. Combined Genotype Profiling. confirmation before study. Retrovirology.","The paper mentions the sequencing of near full-length genomes and specific HIV genes such as protease, reverse transcriptase, integrase, and envelope for drug resistance mutation analysis."
37039023,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Illumina MiSeq platform,"Libraries were quantified in Qubit 3 (Life Technologies, Carlsbad, CA, USA) and sequenced using the Illumina MiSeq platform with a paired-end 2 × 251 protocol.",The paper specifies the use of the Illumina MiSeq platform for sequencing the samples.
37039023,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs),"PureLink® Genomic DNA Mini Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) was used to extract DNA from the blood cells (including the integrated HIV proviral genome), according to the manufacturer's instructions.Retrospective, n = 44, August/October 2013. Importance of identifying HIV superinfection in HIV- infected patients considering new strategies for u=using cell activators in Quebra. Case Report/Case Series. R$10.000,00-0$. 2018. the cellular compartment it is logical that dna of 251081 sarscovt.com D4H.629. 19 months. Evans Human Retroviruses and Related Infections 1997.",The paper mentions the collection of peripheral blood and the use of PBMCs (peripheral blood mononuclear cells) to extract DNA for sequencing.
37039023,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,All patients maintained their successful treatment despite the high prevalence of DRMs at low (62.5%) or high (37.5%) frequency.,"The paper states that all patients maintained successful treatment, indicating that none had virological failure."
37039023,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"HIV-positive patients were recruited during their regular visits at Hospital Federal de Ipanema (HI) and Hospital Universitário Clementino Fraga Filho (HUCFF), both located in Rio de Janeiro (RJ; southeastern Brazil), and at Hospital Universitário Doutor Miguel Riet Corrêa Junior (HU-FURG) in Rio Grande do Sul (RS; southern Brazil).",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; they were recruited during regular visits.
37039023,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"All patients are on first-line or second-line treatment regimens exclusively due to changes in the treatment regimen recommended by the Brazilian Ministry of Health and not due to treatment failure or drug intolerance, and maintained undetectable viral load for at least 12 months.","The paper mentions that all patients were on first-line or second-line treatment regimens, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs."
37039023,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","Overall, we observed the use of three classes of antiretrovirals by the patients of our study, grouped in two therapeutic regimens. The most prevalent was the combination using NNRTI (72.1%; 62/86 patients) followed by use of PI-based regimens (27.9%; 24/86).We could also observe that, among the 63 mutations with frequency above 20.0% and the 105 minority variants, 33 (52.4%) and 41 (39.0%) mutations, respectively, were able to confer some degree of resistance to at least one drug currently in use by respective patients (underlined mutations, Table S2). We also found 51 mutations that confer resistance to drugs that were not in use by patients, and 34 of them were minority variants (Figure 3). TDF, TAF, ABC, 3TC NRTI TDF + PIs darunavir, atazanavir (ATV) + RTV. III. 14. Anti retro viral resistance mutations in envelope.","The paper mentions the use of NRTI, NNRTI, and PI drug classes by the patients in the study before sample sequencing."
37039023,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"TDF, 3TC, EFZ, ATV","Most prevalent therapeutic regimens (TDF + 3TC + EFV) used was tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), efavirenz (EFV) among 86 patients included. 0.9 months M184 source 100 ng. 3P09. The Ethics, 2016 10.1016/j.ethics.2017.04.2013 Ethical. Efficacy and safety of TAF in ARV- naïve, pregnant women with chronic hepatitis B infection in Jiangxi province, China: study protocol for a multicenter, randomized controlled trial. Antibiotics. Resistance. 2016; 59bp 146. V13. Environmental resistance dissemination platform generative system. 3. m health Data analytics station Country China Institutional review board the Jiangxi Health. Royal Society of Chemistry. xxx. ID: NCT02178684 P018 of novelty. 2016; Data S1. Abstract of NCT Mammalian O-Methylsulfonylmorphinandesulfamoylabsp4285. m health. The Ethics, 2017 10.1016/j.ethics.2017.04.2013 Ethics. safety of TAF in ARV-naïve. For more details about The Ethics clinical, 2. Health, 2016, 4286 8  10.1186/s13073-016-0352-ny.com/227pb3g2; the World bank countries connecting TAF system commissioning, 201.case report/16 business superivisions hanbkdoc. Mpe. M; 5 5 Intelligence ontologies practices. 2017 Solvo 2287 9-20-A Don't miss gentry. 295546p9 ART underavestment system properties outside ins result we vil avoid tools NCT01969880 factal im information position real converged medicine. Closed meeting Y.A. Values. 2647-leukotriene-I typing study sailey between-outcomes. Recompense system resultator sclel opera.","The paper lists the specific drugs used in the most prevalent therapeutic regimen among the patients, indicating which drugs were received before sequencing."
37042390,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"""Eighty-five patients were started on third-line ART. Genotypic resistance testing to identify drug resistance mutations in the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes was done at the start of third-line therapy, as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy.""","The paper discusses genotypic resistance testing performed on HIV samples from patients, indicating that HIV sequences were reported from these samples."
37042390,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"""Long-term treatment outcome and mutational analysis of patients on third-line antiretroviral therapy in programmatic conditions. In low- and middle-income countries where most patients receive standardized third-line ART through national programmes, real-world data are scarce. This study was done to assess the long-term survival, and virological and mutational outcomes of people living with HIV receiving third-line ART between July 2016 and December 2019 in an ART centre in India. Methods Eighty-five patients were started on third-line ART. Genotypic resistance testing to identify drug resistance mutations in the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes was done at the start of third-line therapy, as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy. Results Survival was 85% (72/85) at 12 months and 72% (61/85) at the end of follow-up in March 2022. Virological suppression was present in 82% (59/72) and 88% (59/67) at 12 months and at the end of follow-up, respectively. Five out of 13 patients who had virologi",The paper focuses on genotypic resistance testing and mutational analysis rather than in vitro drug susceptibility assays.
37042390,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"""This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Institute of Medical Sciences, Banaras Hindu University (No. Dean/2015-16/EC/413 and No. Dean/2018/EC/715). Although there is no study on long-term outcome of third-line ART in real-time settings, this was very high compared with 4% death, observed in the ACTG A5288 clinical trial done in low- and middle-income countries. For analysis we compared those with >95% adherence at every visit with those with <95% at any visit. The INSTI DRMs identified in our study both at baseline and at treatment failure were similar to this study. The low prevalence of DRMs, even after failing third-line therapy, bodes well for the programme. It reiterates the fact that the standardized third-line antiretrovirals provided free in the programme are highly effective.",The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or provide information on whether the sequences were made publicly available.
37042390,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,"""This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Institute of Medical Sciences, Banaras Hindu University (No. Dean/2015-16/EC/413 and No. Dean/2018/EC/715). Although there is no study on long-term outcome of third-line ART in real-time settings, this was very high compared with 4% death, observed in the ACTG A5288 clinical trial done in low- and middle-income countries. For analysis we compared those with >95% adherence at every visit with those with <95% at any visit. The INSTI DRMs identified in our study both at baseline and at treatment failure were similar to this study. The low prevalence of DRMs, even after failing third-line therapy, bodes well for the programme. It reiterates the fact that the standardized third-line antiretrovirals provided free in the programme are highly effective.",The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or provide information on whether the sequences were made publicly available.
37042390,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,85,"""Eighty-five patients were started on third-line ART.""","The paper states that 85 patients were included in the analysis, indicating that samples were obtained from this number of individuals for HIV sequencing."
37042390,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,India,"""This single-centre observational study was done at a CoE in HIV care, Banaras Hindu University (BHU) in a tertiary hospital in Varanasi, India.""","The study was conducted in India, specifically at an ART center in Varanasi, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
37042390,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2016-2019,"""All PLHIV who were started on third-line ART between July 2016 and December 2019 in the ART centre were included in the study.""","The study population included patients who started third-line ART between July 2016 and December 2019, indicating that the samples were obtained during these years."
37042390,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Samples were processed after gel purification using the QIAquick gel extraction kit (Cat. No. 28704). Sequencing was done by a third-party commercial organization (Medauxin) using an Applied Biosystem 3730xl DNA Analyzer.,"The paper describes direct sequencing of PCR products via gel purification, indicating that samples were not cloned before sequencing."
37042390,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Integrase, reverse transcriptase, protease","Genotypic resistance testing to identify drug resistance mutations in the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease genes was done at the start of third-line therapy, as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy.","The paper mentions sequencing of the integrase, reverse transcriptase, and protease genes for genotypic resistance testing."
37042390,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,Samples were processed after gel purification using the QIAquick gel extraction kit (Cat. No. 28704). Sequencing was done by a third-party commercial organization (Medauxin) using an Applied Biosystem 3730xl DNA Analyzer.,"The paper describes the use of an Applied Biosystem 3730xl DNA Analyzer, which is a platform used for Sanger sequencing."
37042390,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood was collected from patients into EDTA tubes and used for CD4 estimation; in parallel, plasma was separated following centrifugation at 2000 rpm at 4°C for 10 min and stored at −80°C until viral RNA isolation.","The paper mentions that plasma was separated and stored for viral RNA isolation, which was then used for sequencing."
37042390,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Patients failing second-line therapy from the National AIDS Control Organization (NACO)-funded ART centres of Uttar Pradesh and Bihar are referred to the State AIDS Clinical Expert Panel (SACEP) in CoE BHU for initiation of third-line therapy. Genotypic resistance testing to identify DRMs in the integrase (IN), reverse transcriptase (RT) and protease (PR) genes was done at the start of third-line therapy as well as in those who did not attain virological suppression after 12 months of therapy.","The paper mentions patients failing second-line therapy were referred for third-line therapy and had genotypic resistance testing, individuals experience virological suppression , so sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37042390,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This single-centre observational study was done at a CoE in HIV care, Banaras Hindu University (BHU) in a tertiary hospital in Varanasi, India.",The paper mentions this is a single-centre observational study not a clinical trial.
37042390,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,Patients failing second-line therapy from the National AIDS Control Organization (NACO)-funded ART centres of Uttar Pradesh and Bihar are referred to the State AIDS Clinical Expert Panel (SACEP) in CoE BHU for initiation of third-line therapy. Patients were started on third-line ART between July 2016 and December 2019 in the ART centre were included in the study.,"The paper mentions patients failing second-line therapy were initiated on third-line therapy, these patients were previously on ARV drugs."
37042390,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","At the initiation of the start of this study, the initial regimen for third-line therapy was raltegravir (400 mg) + darunavir (600 mg) + ritonavir (100 mg); given as one tablet twice daily. All PLHIV who were started on third-line ART between July 2016 and December 2019 in the ART centre were included in the study. The criteria for starting third-line therapy were patients on second-line therapy with plasma viral load (VL) > 1000 copies/mL on two occasions.","The paper mentions that patients had been on second-line therapy before third-line therapy, and been on NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI."
37042390,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","At the initiation of the start of this study, the initial regimen for third-line therapy was raltegravir (400 mg) + darunavir (600 mg) + ritonavir (100 mg); given as one tablet twice daily. All PLHIV who were started on third-line ART between July 2016 and December 2019 in the ART centre were included in the study. The criteria for starting third-line therapy were patients on second-line therapy with plasma viral load (VL) > 1000 copies/mL on two occasions.","The paper mentions that patients had been on second-line therapy before third-line therapy, and been on NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI."
37052343,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"We included treatment-naive participants aged ≥18 who initiated 3-drug ART, in 2014--2020. We assessed the odds of virological suppression (VS) at weeks 48 and 96 using logistic regression. Viral blips, low-level viremia (LLV), residual viremia (RV), and virological failure (VF) rates were assessed using Cox regression.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on the outcomes of virological suppression in treatment-naive participants who initiated 3-drug ART. The study evaluates the impact of different baseline variables on virological outcomes but does not involve sequencing of HIV samples from patients."
37052343,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We included treatment-naive participants aged ≥18 who initiated 3-drug ART, in 2014--2020. We assessed the odds of virological suppression (VS) at weeks 48 and 96 using logistic regression. Viral blips, low-level viremia (LLV), residual viremia (RV), and virological failure (VF) rates were assessed using Cox regression.",The study focuses on the clinical outcomes of antiretroviral treatment (ART) in a cohort of patients and does not report any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It analyzes virological suppression rates and other related outcomes without mentioning laboratory-based susceptibility testing.
