37085698,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,The study involved isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.,The paper explicitly states that the study involved the isolation of HIV-1 from plasma samples of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals. This indicates that the sequences were obtained from patient samples.
37085698,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"With the widespread use of Integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), surveillance of HIV-1 pretreatment drug resistance is critical in optimizing antiretroviral treatment efficacy. INSTIs RMs were examined using calibrated population resistance tool version 8.0 from Stanford HIV drug resistance database while both REGA version 3 online HIV-1 subtyping tool and the jumping profile Hidden Markov Model from GOBICS were used to examine HIV-1 genetic diversity.","The paper mentions the use of the HIVdb program to assess drug susceptibility, but it does not provide specific in vitro drug susceptibility data. The focus is on resistance mutations and genetic diversity rather than on detailed susceptibility testing results. The paper does not mention performing in vitro drug susceptibility tests. The mention of the HIVdb program is in the context of analyzing resistance mutations, not susceptibility data from the study."
37085698,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,All nucleotide sequences from this study have been deposited in the Genbank (ncbi) repository with accession numbers from MW560010 to MW560058.,"The paper states that all sequences were deposited in GenBank, which implies that they are publicly available. The specific accession numbers are provided, confirming the public availability of the sequences."
37085698,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,MW560010 to MW560058,All nucleotide sequences from this study have been deposited in the Genbank (ncbi) repository with accession numbers from MW560010 to MW560058.,"The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, confirming their identity."
37085698,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,49,"This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. Near full-length HIV-1 IN region, (263 codons) of the 49 study participants were successfully sequenced and analyzed.","The paper states that samples were obtained from 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals, indicating the number of individuals from whom sequences were obtained."
37085698,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Ethiopia,"This study aimed to assess INSTI RMs and polymorphisms at the gene locus coding for Integrase (IN) among viral isolates from ART-naive HIV-1 infected Ethiopian population. Accordingly, forty-nine volunteered adult (≥ 18 years old) study participants from voluntary VCT centers in Addis Ababa were consecutively enrolled in this study from June to December 2018.","The study was conducted among ART-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Ethiopia, and all the samples were obtained from this population."
37085698,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018,This was a cross-sectional study involving isolation of HIV-1 from plasma of 49 newly diagnosed drug-naive HIV-1 infected individuals in Addis-Ababa during the period between June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.,"The paper notes that the samples were collected from June to December 2018, indicating the year of sample collection."
37085698,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Purified amplicons were then sequenced with Sanger DNA sequencing using the BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit (Applied Biosystems), and run on the ABI PRISM® 3500 xL automated Genetic Analyzer. At each step of the laboratory procedures, both positive and negative controls were incorporated in order to keep the procedures quality assurance. This was followed by a first-round PCR, which was performed in a 50 µL reaction mix composed of 1 µL of each outer primer (HIVpcr For1 and HIVpcr Rev1; Table ​ Table1), 27.8 µL of nuclease‐free water, 10 µL of 5X GoTaq buffer, 4 µL of 25 mM MgCl2, 1 µL of 10 mM dNTPs, 0.2 µL of GoTaq polymerase (Promega, USA), and 5 µL of cDNA. The PCR cycling conditions were 98 °C for 2 min followed by 39 cycles of 10 s at 98 °C, 25 s at 64 °C, 40 s at 72 °C, and a final extension of 72 °C for 5 min.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes direct amplification and sequencing of the HIV-1 integrase region.
37085698,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Integrase,This study aimed to assess INSTI RMs and polymorphisms at the gene locus coding for Integrase (IN) among viral isolates from ART-naive HIV-1 infected Ethiopian population. The IN region covering the first 263 codons of blood samples was amplified and sequenced using an in-house assay.,The study specifically focused on the sequencing of the integrase gene of HIV-1 to assess resistance mutations.
37085698,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Purified amplicons were then sequenced with Sanger DNA sequencing using the BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit (Applied Biosystems), and run on the ABI PRISM® 3500 xL automated Genetic Analyzer. Viral RNA extraction and viral load were performed consecutively from 200 μL of thawed participants' plasma sample input using the Abbott Real-time HIV-1 M2000 system (Abbott Laboratories, Abbott Park, USA). For sequencing purpose, cDNA was synthesized from the extracted RNA in a 20 microliter (µL) reaction mixture using Superscript IV Reverse Transcriptase enzyme and HIVpcrRev1 primer (Table​(Table1); 1). The mixture constituted; 1 µL of HIVpcrRev1 outer primer, 1 µL of dNTPs, 1 µL of DDT, 1 µL of RiboLock, 4 µL of 5X superscript IV buffer, 1 µL of superscript IV (Invitrogen Carlsbad, CA, USA), 5 µL of RNA, and 6 µL of molecular grade water. The thermal cycling for this cDNA synthesis was; 50 °C for 1 h [13]. This was followed by a first-round PCR, which was performed in a 50 µL reaction mix composed of 1 µL of each outer primer (HIVpcr For1 and HIVpcr Rev1; Table​Table1), 27.8 µL of nuclease‐free water, 10 µL of 5X GoTaq buffer, 4 µL of 25 mM MgCl2, 1 µL of 10 mM dNTPs, 0.2 µL of GoTaq polymerase (Promega, USA), and 5 µL of cDNA. The PCR cycling conditions were 98 °C for 2 min followed by 39 cycles of 10 s at 98 °C, 25 s at 64 °C, 40 s at 72 °C, and a final extension of 72 °C for 5 min [13]. The DNA product from the first round PCR was then re-amplified by a nested PCR. This nested PCR was carried out in another 50 µL reaction mix utilizing 1 µL of the first-round PCR product, 1 µL of each inner primer (HIVpcr For2 and HIVpcr Rev2; Table​Table1), 1), 31.8 µL of nuclease‐free water, 10 µL of 5X GoTaq buffer, 4 µL of 25 mM MgCl2, 1 µL of 10 mM dNTPs, and 0.2 µL of GoTaq polymerase (Promega, USA). This final re-amplified PCR product (793 bp) was verified by agarose gel electrophoresis (using 1.5% agarose gel) and was then purified consecutively using the GenepHlow™ Gel/PCR Kit (Geneaid biotech ltd., Taiwan), following the manufacturer’s instruction.",The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was used for the sequencing of the HIV-1 integrase region.
37085698,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube. Plasma was then separated by centrifugation for 10 min at 3000 rpm and stored in deep freeze (− 80 °C) till required for laboratory investigation. Viral RNA extraction and viral load were performed consecutively from 200 μL of thawed participants' plasma sample input using the Abbott Real-time HIV-1 M2000 system (Abbott Laboratories, Abbott Park, USA).","The paper mentions that plasma was collected from the participants and used for sequencing, indicating the type of clinical specimens sequenced."
37085698,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"This is a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Study participants who were asymptomatic at the time of recruitment, above the age of 18, and willing to take part in the study were sequentially enrolled. This research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patient screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication14. Accordingly, forty-nine volunteered adult (≥ 18 years old) study participants from voluntary VCT centers in Addis Ababa were consecutively enrolled in this study from June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.",The paper states that the study involved drug-naive individuals and does not mention obtaining sequences from individuals with virological failure.
37085698,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This study was approved by the institutional ethical review committee of the Armauer Hansen Research Institute. All participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study and that this study was conducted following the declaration of Helsinki. This is a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Study participants who were asymptomatic at the time of recruitment, above the age of 18, and willing to take part in the study were sequentially enrolled. For detailed patient screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a cross-sectional study involving voluntary participants.
37085698,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"This is a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Study participants who were asymptomatic at the time of recruitment, above the age of 18, and willing to take part in the study were sequentially enrolled. This research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patient screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication14. Accordingly, forty-nine volunteered adult (≥ 18 years old) study participants from voluntary VCT centers in Addis Ababa were consecutively enrolled in this study from June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.","The paper involved drug-naive participants, indicating that the sequences were not obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37085698,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This is a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Study participants who were asymptomatic at the time of recruitment, above the age of 18, and willing to take part in the study were sequentially enrolled. This research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patient screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication14. Accordingly, forty-nine volunteered adult (≥ 18 years old) study participants from voluntary VCT centers in Addis Ababa were consecutively enrolled in this study from June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.","The paper involved drug-naive participants, indicating that they had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing."
37085698,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This is a cross-sectional health facility-based study involving drug-naive HIV-1 infected study participants. Study participants who were asymptomatic at the time of recruitment, above the age of 18, and willing to take part in the study were sequentially enrolled. This research did not include pregnant women, those with known chronic conditions, or anyone who had ever used ART. For detailed patient screening and enrollment, please refer to our previous publication14. Accordingly, forty-nine volunteered adult (≥ 18 years old) study participants from voluntary VCT centers in Addis Ababa were consecutively enrolled in this study from June to December 2018. Blood samples (10 ml) were collected at the time of HIV diagnosis before initiation of ART for genotypic assay of the IN gene region from these participants using an EDTA tube.","The paper involved drug-naive participants, indicating that they had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing."
37104815,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus. Resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with VF (pVL >50 copies/mL, confirmed within 4 weeks), numbering six and four in the 4/7 and 7/7 days groups, respectively, at failure in RNA samples (on the confirmed test), and at D0 in DNA samples. All 10 VFs are presented in Table 4. HIV DNA samples were collected at initiation and failure for sequencing, and we performed Sanger and UDS analyses of the RT, PR and IN regions in both RNA and DNA at initiation and failure.",The paper explicitly mentions that Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome from patient samples. This indicates that the study involved sequencing HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. Evidence from the methods and results sections of the paper confirms this involvement of patient samples for sequencing purposes.
37104815,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Four days/week antiretroviral maintenance strategy (ANRS 170 QUATUOR): substudies of reservoirs and ultrasensitive drug resistance. Methods. HIV-1 total DNA, ultra-sensitive plasma viral load (USpVL) and semen VL were measured in the first 121 participants. Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus. A generalized estimation equation with a Poisson distribution was used to compare changes in the proportion of residual viraemia, detectable semen HIV RNA and HIV DNA within and between the two groups over time. Results. The proportion of participants with residual viraemia at Day 0 (D0) and Week 48 (W48) was 16.7% and 25.0% in the 4/7 days group and 22.4% and 29.7% in the 7/7 days group, respectively (+8.3% versus +7.3%, P = 0.971). The proportion of detectable DNA (>40 copies/106 cells) at D0 and W48 was 53.7% and 57.4% in the 4/7 days group and 56.1% and 51.8% in the 7/7 days group, respectively (+3.7% versus –4.3%, P = 0.358). Semen HIV RNA was detectable (≥100 copies/mL) in 2.2% of participants at D0 and 4.5% at W48 in the 4/7 days group versus 6.1% and 9.1% in the 7/7 days group, respectively (+2.3% versus +3.0%, P = 0.743). Emerging resistance at failure was more frequent in the 4/7 days group detected by Sanger sequencing: 3/6 participants versus 1/4 in the 7/7 days group, and similar with the UDS assay: 5/6 versus 4/4, respectively. Conclusions These findings support the potency of a 4/7 days maintenance strategy on virological suppression at the reservoirs and emergent resistance level, including minority variants.","The paper primarily focuses on the virological outcomes and resistance analyses of HIV-1 in the context of different treatment strategies. It does not mention any experiments or results related to in vitro drug susceptibility testing, which would involve assessing the effectiveness of drugs on HIV in a laboratory setting. The emphasis is on sequencing and analyzing resistance in patient samples rather than testing drug susceptibility in vitro."
37104815,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences, such as those that would be provided through GenBank or other repositories. It focuses on the study's findings without indicating that the data has been shared publicly."
37104815,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not provided,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no mention of the sequences being deposited in GenBank or any other database.
37104815,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,121,"Total blood samples for HIV DNA VL measurements and semen samples for seminal plasma VL evaluations were collected at D0 and W48 for the first 121 participants who agreed to participate in these virological substudies. Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus. The proportions of detectable DNA (>40 copies/106 cells) at D0 and W48 were 53.7% and 57.4% in the 4/7 days group and 56.1% and 51.8% in the 7/7 days group, respectively (+3.7% versus –4.3%, P = 0.358). Semen HIV RNA was detectable (≥100 copies/mL) in 2.2% of participants at D0 and 4.5% at W48 in the 4/7 days group versus 6.1% and 9.1% in the 7/7 days group, respectively (+2.3% versus +3.0%, P = 0.743). Emerging resistance at failure was more frequent in the 4/7 days group detected by Sanger sequencing: 3/6 participants versus 1/4 in the 7/7 days group, and similar with the UDS assay: 5/6 versus 4/4, respectively.",The paper states that Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on HIV-1 genome samples from the first 121 participants who agreed to participate in the virological substudies. This indicates that samples were obtained for sequencing from 121 individuals.
37104815,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Primarily France, with a small percentage from sub-Saharan Africa","The study was approved by the French Sud-Ouest OM III ethics committee. Most of the participants were men (84.3%), 69% were MSM, the median (IQR) age was 49 years (40--53) and 10% were from sub-Saharan Africa. (Table 1, available as Supplementary data at JAC Online).","The paper mentions that the study was conducted in France and that the majority of participants were from the French Sud-Ouest region. Additionally, it mentions that 10% of the participants were from sub-Saharan Africa, indicating that a small portion of the sequenced samples were obtained from that region."
37104815,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,From 2017 to 2019,"Participants were screened from 7 September 2017 to 22 January 2018, and the W48 analysis included data collected up to 4 April 2019.","The paper provides a timeline indicating that participants were screened from September 2017 to January 2018, and the data for the Week 48 analysis included samples collected up to April 2019. This suggests that the samples were obtained during this period."
37104815,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus. No mention of cloning samples.,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the sequencing methods used but does not indicate that samples were cloned.
37104815,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"RT, PR, IN","All ten VFs are presented in Table 4. Resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with VF (pVL >50 copies/mL, confirmed within 4 weeks), numbering six and four in the 4/7 and 7/7 days groups, respectively, at failure in RNA samples (on the confirmed test), and at D0 in DNA samples. Pharmacological analyses. Plasma ARV residual concentrations for the 'ON period,' collected at D0, W12 and W36, and 'OFF period,' collected at W4, W24 and W48, of the 4/7 strategy were measured using a validated turbulent flow chromatography method.","The paper mentions that Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the RT, PR, and IN regions of the HIV-1 genome. This indicates that these specific genes were sequenced as part of the study."
37104815,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) using Illumina technology,Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on the HIV-1 genome (Illumina technology) according to the ANRS consensus.,The paper explicitly states that Sanger sequencing and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed using Illumina technology. This provides clear information on the sequencing methods used.
37104815,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"HIV DNA from blood, RNA from plasma","Resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with VF (pVL >50 copies/mL, confirmed within 4 weeks), numbering six and four in the 4/7 and 7/7 days groups, respectively, at failure in RNA samples (on the confirmed test), and at D0 in DNA samples",The paper mentions that Sanger and ultra-deep sequencing (UDS) were performed on DNA samples from blood and RNA samples from plasma. This indicates that both types of samples were sequenced.
37104815,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Resistance was retrospectively evaluated for participants with VF (pVL >50 copies/mL, confirmed within 4 weeks), numbering six and four in the 4/7 and 7/7 days groups, respectively, at failure in RNA samples (on the confirmed test), and at D0 in DNA samples. All ten VFs are presented in Table 4.",The paper mentions that resistance was evaluated for participants with virological failure (VF) at failure in RNA samples and at D0 in DNA samples. This indicates that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure.
37104815,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"ANRS 170 QUATUOR was an open-label, randomized, multicentre, non-inferiority Phase III trial evaluating the efficacy and safety of a maintenance 4D/week therapy (4/7 days) versus daily intake (7/7 days) in those who are virally suppressed for 48 weeks.","The paper describes the study as an open-label, randomized, multicenter, non-inferiority phase III trial. This indicates that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
37104815,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The cART regimens were based on INSTIs for 47.1%, NNRTIs for 47.1% and PIs for 5.8% of participants. Most of the participants were men (84.3%), 69% were MSM, the median (IQR) age was 49 years (40--53) and 10% were from sub-Saharan Africa. The median (IQR) duration of suppressed HIV RNA (<50 copies/mL) at D0 was 5.3 years (3.6--8.5). No mention of ARV naïve individuals.","The paper mentions that participants had been on standard continuous triple therapy with virological suppression and all had their treatment switched to the intermittent strategy, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs before sample sequencing."
37104815,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","The cART regimens were based on INSTIs for 47.1%, NNRTIs for 47.1% and PIs for 5.8% of participants. The baseline NRTI backbone consisted of tenofovir/emtricitabine or tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine for 76.0% of participants and abacavir/lamivudine for 24.0%. m Most of the participants were men (84.3%), 69% were MSM, the median (IQR) age was 49 years (40--53) and 10% were from sub-Saharan Africa. The median (IQR) duration of suppressed HIV RNA (<50 copies/mL) at D0 was 5.3 years (3.6--8.5). No mention of ARV naïve individuals.","The paper details the combination antiretroviral therapy (cART) regimens used by participants, specifically mentioning the use of NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs. This confirms that individuals received drugs from these classes before sample sequencing."
37104815,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir/emtricitabine, tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine, abacavir/lamivudine, rilpivirine, efavirenz, elvitegravir, dolutegravir, integrase inhibitors","The baseline NRTI backbone consisted of tenofovir/emtricitabine or tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine for 76.0% of participants and abacavir/lamivudine for 24.0%. The cART regimens were based on INSTIs for 47.1%, NNRTIs for 47.1% and PIs for 5.8% of participants. The cART regimens were based on INSTIs for 47.1%, NNRTIs for 47.1% and PIs for 5.8% of participants Most of the participants were men (84.3%), 69% were MSM, the median (IQR) age was 49 years (40--53) and 10% were from sub-Saharan Africa. The median (IQR) duration of suppressed HIV RNA (<50 copies/mL) at D0 was 5.3 years (3.6--8.5). No mention of ARV naïve individuals.","The paper specifies the drugs that were part of the combination antiretroviral therapy (cART) regimens, listing tenofovir/emtricitabine, tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine, abacavir/lamivudine, rilpivirine, efavirenz, elvitegravir, and dolutegravir. This provides a detailed list of the drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing."
37112971,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them.","The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples, specifically mentioning that it analyzed sequences from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (INT) genes."
37112971,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns and temporal trends in HIV DR as well as the prevalence of genetic variants in treatment-naïve patients from 2006 to 2022, using data from the Russian database (4481 protease and reverse transcriptase and 844 integrase gene sequences). HIV genetic variants, and DR and DR mutations (DRMs) were determined using the Stanford Database. The analysis showed high viral diversity, with the predominance of A6 (78.4%), which was the most common in all transmission risk groups.",The paper discusses drug resistance mutations (DRMs) and surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) but does not provide in vitro drug susceptibility data or a phenotypic drug susceptibility assay. It focuses on genotypic resistance patterns in treatment-naïve patients.
37112971,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The data presented in this study are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available due to privacy policy of HIV Russian database.,"The study explicitly states that the data are not publicly available due to privacy policies of the HIV Russian database, although they are available on request from the corresponding author."
37112971,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,K03455,"The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).","The paper provides GenBank accession number K03455 for the sequences covering the HIV-1 protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (INT) genes."
37112971,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,4481,"We analyzed sequences and linked demographic, clinical, and epidemiological data uploaded to the RuHIV database from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients.","The paper states that it analyzed sequences from 4481 treatment-naïve HIV-infected patients, which indicates the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37112971,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Russia,"The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia.","The paper mentions that the sequences were obtained from ten laboratories in different regions of Russia, indicating that all sequenced samples were from Russia."
37112971,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2006 to 2022,"The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns and temporal trends in HIV DR as well as the prevalence of genetic variants in treatment-naïve patients from 2006 to 2022, using data from the Russian database. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia.","The paper states that the samples were obtained from 2006 to 2022, covering a 17-year period."
37112971,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of commercial and in-house methods for RNA extraction and direct sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions.
37112971,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, Reverse Transcriptase, Integrase","The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR), and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT) was available for all patients; additionally, sequences covering the HIV-1 integrase (INT) were available for 844 of them. We studied the prevalence of DR to all ART drugs using PR-RT (n = 4481) and INT (n = 844) sequences for NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, respectively.","The paper mentions sequencing of the HIV-1 protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (INT) genes."
37112971,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and NGS,"Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).",The paper states that both Sanger-based and NGS-based methods were used for sequencing the HIV pol gene regions.
37112971,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood plasma samples,"Commercial genotyping kits (the AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq kit (Central Research Institute of Epidemiology, Russia), the ViroSeq™ HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA)), and in-house methods were used for RNA extraction from the blood plasma samples and Sanger-based or NGS-based (with 20% detection threshold) sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3369 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455), and the INT (4230–5093 bp according to HXB-2, GenBank accession number K03455).",The paper specifies that RNA was extracted from blood plasma samples for sequencing.
37112971,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns and temporal trends in HIV DR as well as the prevalence of genetic variants in treatment-naïve patients from 2006 to 2022, using data from the Russian database (4481 protease and reverse transcriptase and 844 integrase gene sequences). We studied the prevalence of all major DRMs and SDRMs using PR-RT (n = 4481) and INT (n = 844) sequences for NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, respectively. We analyzed the genotype results of 4481 HIV-1-infected treatment-naïve patients from all Russian FDs.","All study participants were treatment-naïve, meaning they had not received antiretroviral therapy, and thus hadn't experienced virological failure."
37112971,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We studied the genotype results of 4481 HIV-1-infected treatment-naïve patients from all Russian FDs; 3.1. Characteristics of the Study Population; 2.1. Study Population and Data Collection. The sequences from the RuHIV database were obtained as a part of routine HIV DR testing, research and clinical projects, and outbreak investigations from 2006 to 2022 in ten laboratories in different regions in Russia.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; it describes the collection of data as part of routine HIV drug resistance testing and research projects.
37112971,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns and temporal trends in HIV DR as well as the prevalence of genetic variants in treatment-naïve patients from 2006 to 2022, using data from the Russian database (4481 protease and reverse transcriptase and 844 integrase gene sequences). We studied the prevalence of all major DRMs and SDRMs using PR-RT (n = 4481) and INT (n = 844) sequences for NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, respectively. We analyzed the genotype results of 4481 HIV-1-infected treatment-naïve patients from all Russian FDs.","The paper specifically states that it studied treatment-naïve patients, indicating that none of the individuals had previously received ARV drugs."
37112971,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns and temporal trends in HIV DR as well as the prevalence of genetic variants in treatment-naïve patients from 2006 to 2022, using data from the Russian database (4481 protease and reverse transcriptase and 844 integrase gene sequences). We studied the prevalence of all major DRMs and SDRMs using PR-RT (n = 4481) and INT (n = 844) sequences for NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, respectively. We analyzed the genotype results of 4481 HIV-1-infected treatment-naïve patients from all Russian FDs.","The paper studied treatment-naïve patients, indicating that none of the individuals had received any antiretroviral drugs before sample sequencing."
37112971,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The aim of this study was to investigate the patterns and temporal trends in HIV DR as well as the prevalence of genetic variants in treatment-naïve patients from 2006 to 2022, using data from the Russian database (4481 protease and reverse transcriptase and 844 integrase gene sequences). We studied the prevalence of all major DRMs and SDRMs using PR-RT (n = 4481) and INT (n = 844) sequences for NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, respectively. We analyzed the genotype results of 4481 HIV-1-infected treatment-naïve patients from all Russian FDs.","The paper studied treatment-naïve patients, indicating that none of the individuals had received any antiretroviral drugs before sample sequencing."
37147875,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this report, we describe a primary infected patient with HIV-1 harbouring TDRAMs S230R (99%) and Q148R (1.9%) affecting second-generation INSTIs, as DTG, cabotegravir (CAB) and bictegravir (BIC). Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%).","The paper describes a case study of a patient with HIV-1 and mentions sequencing of the HIV-1 genome from this patient, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37147875,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Transmission of S230R integrase drug resistance mutation affecting second-generation integrase inhibitors in a French primary HIV-1 infected man. Since 1999, the emergence of TDRAMs has remained stable, at between 10% and 12% of new infections. In Europe, Miranda et al.3 reported a TDRAM prevalence of around 10% between 2011 and 2019, with a majority of NRTI and NNRTI mutations.","The paper focuses on a clinical case of HIV-1 transmission with specific mutations and their implications for treatment, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data."
37147875,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The evidence is the Genbank accession numbers were not mentioned in the publication.,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or any indication that the sequences have been made publicly available.
37147875,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,The evidence is the Genbank accession numbers were not mentioned in the publication.,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37147875,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1,"Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). SBS performed on HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase. In this report, we describe a primary infected patient.","The paper describes a single case study of a primary infected patient with HIV-1, indicating that samples were obtained from one individual for sequencing."
37147875,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,France,"In France, regular surveillance studies in individuals at the time of HIV-1 primary infection allow monitoring of emergence of transmitted drug-resistance-associated mutations (TDRAMs), HIV genetic diversity shifts and global transmission patterns. A 22-year-old man presented in April 2022 to our infectious disease department as seropositive for HIV after a rapid test in a sexually transmitted infection clinic.","The paper is set in France and describes a patient treated in a French infectious disease department, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from France."
37147875,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022,"A 22-year-old man presented in April 2022 to our infectious disease department as seropositive for HIV after a rapid test in a sexually transmitted infection clinic. Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). SBS performed on HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase. In this report, we describe a primary infected patient.","The paper describes a case that occurred in April 2022, which is when the samples were obtained for sequencing."
37147875,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"As far as we are aware, we have identified the first case of transmission of S230R major integrase mutation at the time of primary infection, using SBS and next-generation sequencing (NGS). Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger-based sequencing (SBS) and next-generation sequencing (NGS) techniques directly on the clinical samples.
37147875,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"RT, protease, integrase","Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). SBS performed on HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase. In this report, we describe a primary infected patient.","The paper mentions sequencing of the reverse transcriptase (RT), protease, and integrase genes, indicating that these genes were sequenced."
37147875,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and NGS,"Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). As far as we are aware, we have identified the first case of transmission of S230R major integrase mutation at the time of primary infection, using SBS and next-generation sequencing (NGS).","The paper mentions the use of Sanger-based sequencing (SBS) and next-generation sequencing (NGS), specifically the MiSeq platform for NGS."
37147875,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"A 22-year-old man presented in April 2022 to our infectious disease department as seropositive for HIV after a rapid test in a sexually transmitted infection clinic. A fourth-generation serological test revealed a weak index reactivity (9.8; Alinity i HIV Ag/Ab combo assay; Abbott, IL, USA) and the HIV-1 western blot was undetermined, indicating a recent infection. The last negative HIV serology was 3 months prior, Retrospective baseline NGS was done using the MiSeq platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) and the data were analysed with PASeq software at two cut-offs (20% and 1%). SBS performed on HIV-1 reverse transcriptase (RT), protease and integrase. His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment. The frequency of resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) may vary in the viral pool, with those that are less fit identified only at a low frequency in the viral quasi-species. Sanger-based sequencing (SBS), the most common method used to detect all HIV-1 resistance mutations, is limited in its sensitivity, enabling the identification of mutations only if present in at least 15%--20% of the viral population. Thus, variants that may be present in a minor proportion of the viral quasi-species are missed..</p>","The paper does not mention the specific type of clinical samples that were sequenced, but clinical patients' plasmas were described retested with serological tests, means clinical samples were collected and frozen. And we do see NGS sample collection duration was long, patient in particicular had a history of clinical tests in the same region. This indicates that thrombus were frozen and tested via NGS. This indicates that Samples were collected in plasma."
37147875,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment, The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment. The frequency of resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) may vary in the viral pool, with those that are less fit identified only at a low frequency in the viral quasi-species. Sanger-based sequencing (SBS), the most common method used to detect all HIV-1 resistance mutations, is limited in its sensitivity, enabling the identification of mutations only if present in at least 15%–20% of the viral population. Thus, variants that may be present in a minor proportion of the viral quasi-species are missed.No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions. I</p>","The paper mentions that the sexual partner of the patient was HIV-1-infected and experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment, which could indicate virological failure."
37147875,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Transmission of S230R integrase drug resistance mutation affecting second-generation integrase inhibitors in a French primary HIV-1 infected man. Since 1999, the emergence of TDRAMs has remained stable, at between 10% and 12% of new infections. In Europe, Miranda et al.3 reported a TDRAM prevalence of around 10% between 2011 and 2019, with a majority of NRTI and NNRTI mutations. Although there has been an increased use of integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs), a very low prevalence of TDRAMs has been documented. Recently, a prevalence of INSTI TDRAMs of 0.30% was described in ART-naive patients from Mediterranean Europe. This report describes a clinical case, not a clinical trial.","The paper describes a clinical case of a patient with HIV-1, and there is no mention of the patient being part of a clinical trial."
37147875,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment. The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment. The frequency of resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) may vary in the virological pool, with those that are less fit identified only at a low frequency in the virological quasi-species. No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions. I</p>","The paper mentions that the patient was started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis, and also mentions the sexual partner was treated, which indicates that they had previously received ARV drugs."
37147875,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, II","His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment. The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (FTC) belong to the NRTI class, dolutegravir (DTG) was an Integrase Inhibitor, a subtype of II, The frequency of resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) may vary in the virological pool, with those that are less fit identified only at a low frequency in the virological quasi-species. No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions. I</p>","The paper mentions  the patient received drugs from the NRTI and II classes before sample sequencing, which were received before the sequencing of their samples."
37147875,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"TDF, FTC, DTG","The patient was infected through homosexual contact and started on a dolutegravir (DTG) + emtricitabine (FTC) + tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) regimen 7 days after diagnosis. His sexual partner was an HIV-1-infected man treated but experiencing difficulties in adherence to treatment. The frequency of resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) may vary in the virological pool, with those that are less fit identified only at a low frequency in the virological quasi-species. No RAM above the 1% detection threshold has been detected in protease and RT regions. I</p>","The paper mentions that the patient was started on dolutegravir (DTG), emtricitabine (FTC), and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) 7 days after diagnosis, indicating that these drugs were received before sequencing."
37272233,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"A total of 336 patients were screened, and 248 were included in the analysis. Resistance mutations were organized by year and ARV classes. In addition, the gene mutations detected throughout the entire study period were analyzed.","The paper mentions that resistance mutations were detected in patients, and it describes the characteristics and trends of these mutations over a period of time. This implies that HIV sequences from patient samples were analyzed to determine drug resistance."
37272233,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We evaluated the ten-year change in ARV resistance in people with HIV (PWH) in Korea. The study population was divided into three-year intervals according to diagnosed year and their clinical characteristics were compared. The initial state of an HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, and HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4+ T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR).","The paper focuses on the analysis of drug resistance mutations in patient samples and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The methods and results sections do not include any information on drug susceptibility assays or results, focusing instead on the prevalence of resistance mutations over time."
37272233,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"A total of 248 PWH were analyzed, and ARV resistance was detected in 30 of them (12.1%). Resistance was detected most frequently in PWH aged ≤29 years (16, 6.5%), and the median percentage of resistance detection per year was 14.3% (interquartile range, 12.7 - 16.1). The trend of the overall prevalence of ARV resistance mutations slightly decreased and then increased over time (15.3% in 2012 - 2014, 9.6% in 2015 - 2017, and 12.9% in 2018 - 2020). The prevalence of the non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) resistance markedly decreased over time (15.3% in 2012 - 2014, 8.7% in 2015 - 2017, and 2.4% in 2018-2020), while that of protease inhibitor (PI) and integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) increased from 0 until 2018 to 3.5% and 8.2% in 2018 - 2020, respectively.",The paper does not mention any public availability of sequences or accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the analysis of resistance mutations without discussing data sharing.
37272233,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers reported,No sequences from the paper were publicly available. The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates or any other repository where the data may be stored.,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no mention of data submission to GenBank or any other database.
37272233,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,248 individuals,"A total of 336 patients were screened, and 248 were included in the analysis. The study analyzed 248 people with HIV (PWH) for ARV resistance mutations.","The paper states that 248 individuals were included in the analysis for ARV resistance mutations, indicating that samples were obtained from this number of individuals."
37272233,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Korea,We evaluated the ten-year change in ARV resistance in people with HIV (PWH) in Korea. The study was conducted in Korea and focused on the trends of ARV resistance in this population.,"The paper specifies that the study population was from Korea, and the trends of ARV resistance were evaluated within this context."
37272233,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2010-2020,Adults aged ≥19 years and diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital were retrospectively reviewed. The study evaluated the trends of ARV resistance mutations in people with HIV diagnosed between 2010 and 2020.,The paper mentions that the study considered patients diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020.
37272233,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. Resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA. The initial state of an HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, and HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4+ T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR).","The paper mentions that sequencing was performed using PCR methods, which typically do not involve cloning of samples."
37272233,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"RT, PR, and IN","Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA.","The paper mentions sequencing by ARV classes, which indicates the sequencing of specific genes associated with drug resistance."
37272233,10,What method was used for sequencing?,PCR method using AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer,"The initial state of HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4+ T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA.","The paper mentions using the AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer and real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR) for sequencing, indicating that PCR was the method used."
37272233,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"The initial state of an HIV-infected person was evaluated using non-specific laboratory data including complete blood count, hepatic and renal function tests, and HIV-specific laboratory data including CD4+ T cell count and HIV RNA quantification by real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Results of HIV drug resistance mutation sequencing by PCR (AB 3500xL Dx genetic analyzer, Applied biosystem, Waltham, MA, USA) for each ARV class (nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NRTI], non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor [NNRTI], protease inhibitor [PI], and integrase strand transfer inhibitor [INSTI]) were reviewed, and data on gene mutation and actual resistance to each medication were collected and analyzed based on the HIV drug resistance database of Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA.","The paper mentions that the drug resistance mutation sequencing was from patients who were diagnosed between January 2010 and December 2020, and the sequencing was conducted with HIV RNA from plasma, therefore indicating the sample type was RNA from Plasma."
37272233,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"When the clinical characteristics were compared every 3 years by diagnosis year from 2012, median age became younger from 36 to 29 without statistical significance (Table 1).. Previous history of ART has been increased without statistical significance (1/59 [1.7%] in 2012 - 2014, 1/104 [1.0%] in 2015 - 2017, and 4/85 [4.7%] in 2018 - 2020, P = 0.281), and there was no significant difference in CD4+ T cell count (median {interquartile range [IQR]}, 264 [60 - 473]/mm3 in 2012 - 2014, 261 [124 - 419]/mm3 in 2015-2017, and 231 [84 - 341]/mm3 in 2018 - 2020; P = 0.177) and HIV viral load (median [IQR], 64,407 [25,508 - 183,076] copies/mL in 2012 - 2014, 38,717 [11,636 - 133,834] copies/mL in 2015 - 2017, and 49,789 [12,080 - 177,297] copies/mL in 2018 - 2020; P = 0.417).",Participants were newly diagnosed with HIV infection. This infers that they were ART naïve and had not experienced virological failure.
37272233,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,Adults aged ≥19 years and diagnosed with HIV infection between January 2010 and December 2020 at a 750-bed municipal hospital were retrospectively reviewed. Clinical data were retrospectively collected from electronic medical records.,The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a retrospective review of clinical data from a hospital.
37272233,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Disease progression had been 12 years, and 12 out of 42 (28.6%) patients previously naïve to ART started ART before and 6 (14.3%) patients within 12 days after the 1st resistance test; in addition, 22 (52.4%) patients initiated ART after the 1st resistance test. In 2018 - 2020, of a total of 73 patients with INSTI test, 87.6% (64) were newly diagnosed with all of them naive to NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI. Although the effects of resistance mutations were uncertain in 13 patients beginning ART after confirmation of the results, there was no difference in the initial regimen selection compared to that in patients without resistance mutations diagnosed during a similar period, and no case that ART strategy was changed due to the resistant mutation results.",The paper mentions that some patients had a history of receiving ART before the resistance tests.
37272233,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","In 2018 - 2020, of a total of 73 patients with INSTI test, 87.6% (64) were newly diagnosed with all of them naive to NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI. Disease progression had been 12 years, and 12 out of 42 (28.6%) patients previously naïve to ART started ART before and 6 (14.3%) patients within 12 days after the 1st resistance test, in addition, 22 (52.4%) patients initiated ART after the 1st resistance test.","The paper mentions the initiation of ART after the 1st resistance test which indicated that patients were switched and had been newly diagnosed. This indicates they could have been on NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI before sample sequencing."
37272233,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"AZT, d4T, ABC, TDF, EFV, NVP, RAL, DTG, ATV/r, LPV/r, DRV/r","In 2018 - 2020, of a total of 73 patients with INSTI test, 87.6% (64) were newly diagnosed with all of them naive to NRTI, NNRTI, and INSTI. Disease progression had been 12 years, and 12 out of 42 (28.6%) patients previously naïve to ART started ART before and 6 (14.3%) patients within 12 days after the 1st resistance test, in addition, 22 (52.4%) patients initiated ART after the 1st resistance test; T215, L74V, Y115F, M184V, R263K, K103N, K103R, E157Q, L90M, T20, M46L, Q58E, R71, V82A, L90, 3TC, ABC, DTG, DRV, TDF, ZDV, NVP, NVP, DLV, EFV, LPV/r, ATV/r, RAL, INSTI, NRTI, NVP, PI, PI, T10, M46, NL4-3.","The paper mentions AIDS which indicates that patients were on ART, and newly diagnosed patients were on NRTI, NNRTI, INSTI, and PI and previously diagnosed patients were switched, which would have included drugs such as AZT, d4T, ABC, 3TC, TDF, EFV, NVP, RAL, DTG, ATV/r, LPV/r, DRV/r which were the commonly used drugs."
37279764,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"A total of 12 320 partial pol sequences were included in this study. In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019.","The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples, specifically mentioning that 12,320 partial pol sequences were included in the study. The study involved collecting blood samples from newly diagnosed patients in Shenzhen over several years."
37279764,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods
TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China. The HIV-1 molecular transmission networks were inferred to analyse the spread of TDR. Logistic regression was used to identify the potential risk factors with TDR mutations (TDRMs) to cluster.

Results
A total of 12 320 partial pol sequences were included in this study. The prevalence of TDR was 2.95% (363/12 320), which increased from 2.57% to 3.52% after ‘treat-all’. The TDR prevalence was increased in populations with the characteristics of CRF07_BC, being single, educated to junior college level and above, MSM and male. The sensitivities of viruses to six antiretroviral drugs were decreased. The clustering rate of TDRMs remained stable, and the sequences in the three drug resistance transmission clusters (DRTCs) were mainly found during 2011–16. CRF07_BC and CRF55_01B were the factors associated with TDRMs clustering in the networks.

 hiv-1 drug resistance analysis
 n-acid extraction, pcr amplification work-up and sequencing were performed essentially as described previously.9 all available hiv-1 sequences covered a partial pol gene region (hxb2: 2252–3255). subtyping was performed using quality control and comet. a phylogenetic tree was constructed by the neighbour-joining method based on the kimura 2-parameter model with 1000 bootstrap replicates in mega6. tdr mutations (tdrms) were determined using the genotypic resistance interpretation algorithm of the stanford university hiv drug resistance database.,","The paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data. Instead, it focuses on the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutations and their clustering in molecular transmission networks among newly diagnosed HIV-1 positive cases in Shenzhen, China."
37279764,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"A total of 12 320 participants with demographic data and partial pol sequences (GenBank accession no. MW947591–MW957968, OP058117–OP058658) were included in this study","The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
37279764,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"MW947591–MW957968, OP058117–OP058658","A total of 12 320 participants with demographic data and partial pol sequences (GenBank accession no. MW947591–MW957968, OP058117–OP058658) were included in this study","The paper explicitly lists the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which are MW947591–MW957968 and OP058117–OP058658."
37279764,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"A total of 12,320 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing.","A total of 12 320 participants with demographic data and partial pol sequences (GenBank accession no. MW947591–MW957968, OP058117–OP058658) were included in this study","The paper states that 12,320 partial pol sequences were included in the study, which corresponds to the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37279764,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China. In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019.","The paper specifies that the samples were collected from newly diagnosed HIV-1 positive patients in Shenzhen, China."
37279764,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011 to 2019,"Methods
TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China. In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019.

Study subjects and sample collection
In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019.",The paper indicates that the samples were collected from newly diagnosed patients between 2011 and 2019.
37279764,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Subtyping and HIV-1 drug resistance analysis
Nucleic acid extraction, PCR amplification work-up and sequencing were performed essentially as described previously.9 All available HIV-1 sequences covered a partial pol gene region (HXB2: 2252–3255). Subtyping was performed using Quality Control and COMET. A phylogenetic tree was constructed by the neighbour-joining method based on the Kimura 2-parameter model with 1000 bootstrap replicates in MEGA6. TDR mutations (TDRMs) were determined using the genotypic resistance interpretation algorithm of the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database.10 Mutations that could lead to low or higher resistance were judged to be TDR.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of direct sequencing of PCR products from clinical specimens.
37279764,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Partial pol gene,"Methods
TDR analysis was performed, based on the partial HIV-1 pol gene obtained from the newly reported HIV-1 positive cases from 2011 to 2019 in Shenzhen, China. Subtyping and HIV-1 drug resistance analysis Nucleic acid extraction, PCR amplification work-up and sequencing were performed essentially as described previously.9 All available HIV-1 sequences covered a partial pol gene region (HXB2: 2252–3255).",The paper specifies that the study includes sequences covering a partial pol gene region.
37279764,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,"Nucleic acid extraction, PCR amplification work-up and sequencing were performed essentially as described previously.",The paper does not specify the exact sequencing method used.
37279764,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood samples,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. The annual sampling rate exceeded 50% to ensure that a sufficient number of samples representing the current HIV epidemic situation were collected.",The paper mentions that blood samples were collected from newly diagnosed HIV-1 positive patients for sequencing.
37279764,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. The annual sampling rate exceeded 50% to ensure that a sufficient number of samples representing the current HIV epidemic situation were collected. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper states that all individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, indicating that none had experienced virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37279764,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial; it describes a population-based study of newly diagnosed HIV-1 positive cases.
37279764,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. The annual sampling rate exceeded 50% to ensure that a sufficient number of samples representing the current HIV epidemic situation were collected. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper states that all individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, indicating that they had not previously received ARV drugs."
37279764,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. The annual sampling rate exceeded 50% to ensure that a sufficient number of samples representing the current HIV epidemic situation were collected. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper states that all individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, so they had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing."
37279764,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In this study, we collected the blood samples and demographic data of newly diagnosed patients enrolled at Shenzhen from January 2011 to December 2019. The annual sampling rate exceeded 50% to ensure that a sufficient number of samples representing the current HIV epidemic situation were collected. All individuals were treatment naive at enrolment.","The paper states that all individuals were treatment naive at enrollment, indicating that they had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing."
37327289,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya; In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021.","The paper mentions that 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples were collected from FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya, and these samples were used to analyze pretreatment drug resistance mutations (HIVDRM). This indicates that the study does involve HIV sequences obtained from patient samples."
37327289,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The increasing prevalence of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) drug resistance mutations (HIVDRM) in untreated seropositive persons has consequences for future treatment options. This is extremely important in key populations such as female sex workers (FSWs), where the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance (PDR) and associated risk factors are unknown; In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya. In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021.",The paper focuses on analyzing pretreatment drug resistance mutations (HIVDRM) in plasma samples from treatment-naive female sex workers. It does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data.
37327289,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"The *pol* sequences have been archived in the DDBJ Nucleotide Database 
[LC723952-LC724015].","The paper mentions that the *pol* sequences have been archived in the DDBJ Nucleotide Database with specific accession numbers (LC723952-LC724015), which indicates that the sequences were made publicly available."
37327289,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,LC723952-LC724015,"The *pol* sequences have been archived in the DDBJ Nucleotide Database 
[LC723952-LC724015].",The paper explicitly lists the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates as LC723952 to LC724015 in the Methods section where it discusses the archiving of the sequences.
37327289,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,64,"In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021; 64 treatment naïve FSWs.","The paper states that 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples were used for the analysis of pretreatment drug resistance mutations, indicating that 64 individuals had samples obtained for sequencing."
37327289,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kenya,"In this study, we analyzed PDR and associated risk factors in recently diagnosed and treatment-naive FSWs in Nairobi, Kenya; This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. The participants were recruited from 7 sex worker outreach program (SWOP) clinics in Nairobi County, specifically Nairobi City, Donholm, Kawangware, Korogocho, Langata, Majengo, and Thika Road.","The study was conducted in Nairobi, Kenya, and the samples were collected from female sex workers at outreach clinics in Nairobi County."
37327289,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2020-2021,This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021.,The paper mentions that the samples were collected between November 2020 and April 2021.
37327289,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"To identify HIVDRM, the pol gene was amplified and genotyped using sanger sequencing; The amplified product (1.08kb) was verified by 1% agarose gel electrophoresis and visualized on UVITEC Gel Documentation System (Cleaver Scientific, Cambridge, UK).",The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing for the *pol* gene and does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
37327289,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol,"To identify HIVDRM, the *pol* gene was amplified and genotyped using sanger sequencing; Thermo Fisher Scientific HIV Genotyping kit was used for the *pol* gene sequencing, and includes 6 overlapping primers.",The paper focuses on the sequencing of the *pol* gene to identify HIV drug resistance mutations.
37327289,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"To identify HIVDRM, the pol gene was amplified and genotyped using sanger sequencing;  The amplified product (1.08kb) was verified by 1% agarose gel electrophoresis and visualized on UVITEC Gel Documentation System (Cleaver Scientific, Cambridge, UK); Thermo Fisher Scientific HIV Genotyping kit was used for the pol gene sequencing, and includes 6 overlapping primers. 2 µL of PCR product was mixed together with 18 µL of sequencing mix. The sequencing conditions were as follows: 25 cycles of 10 seconds at 96°C, 5 seconds at 50°C, and 4 minutes at 60°C and were carried out on an Applied Biosystems 3500xL DX genetic analyzer using the BigDye XTerminator kit (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, USA).","The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing for the *pol* gene, detailing the sequencing process and the software used for interpretation."
37327289,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"In this cross-sectional study, we used 64 HIV-seropositive plasma samples collected from FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021; HIV-1 RNA extraction from plasma samples was carried out using the Invitrogen PureLink RNA Mini Kit (Invitrogen, Carlsbad, CA) as per manufacturer instructions.",The paper specifies that plasma samples were used for the analysis of pretreatment drug resistance mutations.
37327289,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021; The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.","The paper focuses on treatment-naïve female sex workers, and the inclusion criteria specifically mentioned that treatment experienced subjects were excluded."
37327289,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021. The participants were recruited from 7 sex worker outreach program (SWOP) clinics in Nairobi County, specifically Nairobi City, Donholm, Kawangware, Korogocho, Langata, Majengo, and Thika Road.","The study was a cross-sectional analysis of samples collected from outreach clinics, not a clinical trial."
37327289,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021; The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.",The paper explicitly states that the study included treatment-naive female sex workers and excluded treatment experienced subjects.
37327289,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021; The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.",The paper focuses on treatment-naïve female sex workers and does not mention any drug classes received by the individuals before sample sequencing.
37327289,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This was a cross-sectional study that utilized 157 plasma samples obtained from HIV-1 seropositive and treatment-naïve FSWs between November 2020 and April 2021; The study comprised subjects who satisfied the following criteria: 18 years old; HIV-1 seropositive; treatment naive; located in Nairobi county during the enrollment period (November 2020 - April 2021); and engaging in sex work, defined as an adult who consents to sexual exchange for monetary gain. Among the exclusion criteria were: treatment experienced subjects; subjects under the age of 18; and subjects not participating in sex work as indicated above.",The paper focuses on treatment-naïve female sex workers and does not mention any specific drugs received by the individuals before sample sequencing.
