37376649,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants (post-hoc analysis) to assess the impact of archived, pre-existing drug resistance on 144-week virologic outcomes by last on-treatment viral load (VL) and Snapshot.","The paper explicitly mentions that retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants, which indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37376649,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Assessing the Virologic Impact of Archived Resistance in the Dolutegravir/Lamivudine 2-Drug Regimen HIV-1 Switch Study TANGO through Week 144. The TANGO study (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573) demonstrated that switching to dolutegravir/lamivudine (DTG/3TC) was non-inferior to continuing tenofovir alafenamide-based regimens (TBR) through week 144. Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants (post-hoc analysis) to assess the impact of archived, pre-existing drug resistance on 144-week virologic outcomes by last on-treatment viral load (VL) and Snapshot. A total of 320 (86%) participants on DTG/3TC and 318 (85%) on TBR had both proviral genotype data and ≥1 on-treatment post-baseline VL results and were defined as the proviral DNA resistance analysis population. Archived International AIDS Society--USA major nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, protease inhibitor, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were observed in 42 (7%), 90 (14%), 42 (7%), and 11 (2%) participants, respectively, across both groups; 469 (74%) had no major RAMs at baseline. M184V/I (1%), K65N/R (<1%), and thymidine analogue mutations (2%) were infrequent. Through week 144, >99% of participants on DTG/3TC and 99% on TBR were virologically suppressed (last on-treatment VL <50 copies/mL) regardless of the presence of major RAMs. Results from the sensitivity analysis by Snapshot were consistent with the last available on-treatment VL. In TANGO, archived, pre-existing major RAMs did not impact virologic outcomes through week 144.","The focus of the paper is on the virologic outcomes and archived drug resistance mutations in patient samples, not on in vitro drug susceptibility testing data."
37376649,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper does not specify whether the sequences were made publicly available.,"Data included in this manuscript have been previously presented in part at the Conference on Retroviruses and Opportunistic Infections; 8–11 March 2020; Boston, MA; Poster 489.",The paper does not mention the specific GenBank accession numbers or whether the sequences are publicly available. It focuses on the outcomes and analyses of the genotypic data.
37376649,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers.,No specific GenBank accession numbers are mentioned in the provided content of the paper.,The paper does not mention the specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It discusses the genotypic results but does not provide accession numbers.
37376649,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,734,"Retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants (post-hoc analysis) to assess the impact of archived, pre-existing drug resistance on 144-week virologic outcomes by last on-treatment viral load (VL) and Snapshot.","The paper states that retrospective baseline proviral DNA genotypes were performed for 734 participants, indicating that samples were obtained from this number of individuals."
37376649,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the countries from which the samples were obtained.,The paper does not mention the specific countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,The content provided does not include information regarding the countries of origin for the sequenced samples. It focuses on the study's design and results.
37376649,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,The paper does not mention the specific years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,The content provided does not include information regarding the years of sample collection. It focuses on the study's findings and methodology.
37376649,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 proviral DNA genotyping was conducted retrospectively with the GenoSure Archive assay (Monogram Biosciences, South San Francisco, CA, USA), which uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region; a bioinformatic filter is used to remove APOBEC3G/3F-induced G to A hypermutations. encrypted-content",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It discusses the use of next-generation sequencing for genotyping.
37376649,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The HIV-1 polymerase region was sequenced.,"HIV-1 proviral DNA genotyping was conducted retrospectively with the GenoSure Archive assay (Monogram Biosciences, South San Francisco, CA, USA), which uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region; a bioinformatic filter is used to remove APOBEC3G/3F-induced G to A hypermutations.","The paper specifies that the GenoSure Archive assay analyzes the HIV-1 polymerase region, which indicates that this gene was sequenced."
37376649,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS) was used for sequencing.,"HIV-1 proviral DNA genotyping was conducted retrospectively with the GenoSure Archive assay (Monogram Biosciences, South San Francisco, CA, USA), which uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region; a bioinformatic filter is used to remove APOBEC3G/3F-induced G to A hypermutations.",The paper clearly states that the GenoSure Archive assay uses next-generation sequencing (NGS) to analyze the HIV-1 polymerase region.
37376649,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Whole blood samples were sequenced.,Participants' baseline whole blood samples were used for the GenoSure Archive assay.,"The paper mentions that participants' baseline whole blood samples were used for the GenoSure Archive assay, which indicates that this type of clinical specimen was sequenced."
37376649,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Participants were excluded if they had any evidence of IAS-USA major NRTI or INSTI RAMs in any historical genotype assay results, if available; any plasma HIV-1 RNA measurement of ≥50 copies/mL within 6 months of screening; a total of ≥2 measurements of ≥50 copies/mL or any measurement of >200 copies/mL within 6 and 12 months of screening; or a prior regimen switch for virologic failure (HIV-1 RNA ≥400 copies/mL).","The paper states that participants were excluded if they had any evidence of major NRTI or INSTI RAMs, or prior regimen switch for virologic failure, indicating that sequences were not obtained from these individuals."
37376649,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The TANGO study (ClinicalTrials.gov, NCT03446573) demonstrated that switching to dolutegravir/lamivudine (DTG/3TC) was non-inferior to continuing tenofovir alafenamide-based regimens (TBR) through week 144.","The paper mentions the TANGO study, which is registered as a clinical trial, indicating that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
37376649,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,The overall prevalence of any archived major RAMs across four drug classes was 26% in the PRAP and Archived NRTI RAMs were observed in 7% of participants.,"The paper discusses archived resistance mutations in participants who had received antiretroviral drugs, indicating that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37376649,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Archived International AIDS Society--USA major nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, protease inhibitor, and integrase strand transfer inhibitor resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were observed in 42 (7%), 90 (14%), 42 (7%), and 11 (2%) participants, respectively, across both groups; 469 (74%) had no major RAMs at baseline.76 Through week 144, >99% of participants on DTG/3TC and 99% on TBR were virologically suppressed (last on-treatment VL <50 copies/mL) regardless of the presence of major RAMs. Results from the sensitivity analysis by Snapshot were consistent with the last available on-treatment VL.77 In TANGO, archived, pre-existing major RAMs did not impact virologic outcomes through week 144.","The paper mentions that resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI were observed in the study participants, indicating that they had received these drug classes."
37376649,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"DTG, 3TC, TAF","The overall prevalence of any archived major RAMs across four drug classes was 26% in the PRAP ( Table 1 ). Archived NRTI RAMs were observed in 7% of participants and the frequency of M184V/I (n = 7; 1%) and K65N/R (n = 2; <1%) was low. Of 919 participants screened for the study, 543 (59%) participants had historical genotypic reports available and submitted for eligibility.86 Because of a lack of data, ramipril.874 accounts were excluded from the comparison of the prevalence of archived RAMs and K65R in the INTEGRA study (46%)73 and the A5321 study (28%).878 All participants in the A5321 study and the current study had a 33% elevated risk of treatment-emergent M184V/I, INSTI RAMs and history of viral failure on INSTI.89 Failing tenofovir diversion therapy on TAF had<70% led to developing K65R in greater than a third of patients. Of the 501 DTG-treated culture.907 411 switched to rescue dolutegravir or raltegravir therapy and developed CMV disease Toll-like receptor pathogenesis. All but one of these participants was cytochrome C KD, which increases in MHC class-restricted CD4+ response T-regulatory cell coculture.","The paper mentions that participants were switched to DTG/3TC and were on TAF-based regimens, indicating that these drugs were received before sample sequencing."
37381002,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This retrospective study aimed to characterize the distribution of HIV-1 genotypes and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in people with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure in Suzhou City, China; Pol gene of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment was successfully amplified by using an in-house assay; Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µl plasma sample following the instructions of the MagNA Pure Compact Nucleic Acid Isolation Kit I (Roche, Switzerland).",The paper mentions sequencing the Pol gene of HIV-1 viruses from blood samples of 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment. This indicates that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported.
37381002,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,"The paper discusses drug resistance mutations and their prevalence, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data related to drug susceptibility assays."
37381002,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any specific information regarding the public availability of the sequences, such as GenBank accession numbers or database submissions."
37381002,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, nor does it provide any details about their submission to public databases."
37381002,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,398,"Among these ART failure patients, a total of 398 viral pol gene sequences were successfully sequenced; This retrospective study aimed to characterize the distribution of HIV-1 genotypes and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in people with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure in Suzhou City, China. Pol gene of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment was successfully amplified by using an in-house assay.",The paper states that samples were obtained from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment for sequencing the Pol gene of HIV-1.
37381002,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This retrospective study aimed to characterize the distribution of HIV-1 genotypes and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in people with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure in Suzhou City, China.","The study was conducted in Suzhou City, China, and the samples were obtained from patients in this location."
37381002,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2020,"From 2017 to 2020, 450 people living with HIV who have received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year were confirmed to be ineffective (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml) in Suzhou city.","The paper mentions that the samples were collected from 2017 to 2020, during which the patients were confirmed to have failed antiviral therapy."
37381002,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The sequences of pol gene fragments were obtained by Sanger sequencing method; The near full-length genomes of recombinant forms of HIV-1 were amplified using the methods described previously [14, 15]. We mixed all amplified products of the same sample in one tube. The combined PCR products were purified by using AMPure XP purification beads. Nextera DNA Sample Prep Kit (Illumina, USA) was used for sequencing libraries prepared. Next generation sequencing (NGS) was performed on iseq platform by using i1 V2 300 cycles reagent kit (Illumina, USA).","The paper describes traditional PCR techniques and mixing together the PCR products from the same sample into one tube, indicating that cloning was not performed."
37381002,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Pol gene, near full-length genomes","Pol gene of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment was successfully amplified by using an in-house assay; Sequences analysis of the pol gene revealed that CRF 01_AE (57.29%, 228/398) was the dominant subtype circulating in Suzhou City, followed by CRF 07_BC (17.34%, 69/398), subtype B (7.54%, 30/398), CRF 08_BC (6.53%, 26/398), CRF 67_01B (3.02%, 12/398) and CRF55_01B (2.51%, 10/398).","The paper details that the *pol* gene of HIV-1 was sequenced from the samples, and it also mentions that near full-length genomes were obtained from some samples using NGS."
37381002,10,What method was used for sequencing?,"Sanger sequencing, Next Generation Sequencing","The sequences of pol gene fragments were obtained by Sanger sequencing method; Nextera DNA Sample Prep Kit (Illumina, USA) was used for sequencing libraries prepared. Next generation sequencing (NGS) was performed on iseq platform by using i1 V2 300 cycles reagent kit (Illumina, USA).",The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing for the pol gene fragments and next generation sequencing for near full-length genomes.
37381002,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood plasma samples,"Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µl plasma sample following the instructions of the MagNA Pure Compact Nucleic Acid Isolation Kit I (Roche, Switzerland). Viral nucleic acids were extracted from plasma or whole blood, using the MagNA Pure Compact Nucleic Acid Isolation Kit I (Roche, Switzerland) in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions. Plasma samples were collected in EDTA tubes for sequencing of the pol gene and near full-genome sequencing. Whole blood (200 μL) was used to extract viral nucleic acids for determining HIV-1 viral load, We followed the published full-length genome (FLG) consensus sequencing method for recombinant subtype CRF01_AE samples. The PCR products were purified with PureLink PCR Clean-Up Kit (Invitrogen, USA) and finished sequencing by Sanger sequencing method. No plasma specimens with HIV-1 viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml were not excluded in this study, which were people living with HIV who have received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year and in 13 years between 2005 and 2018 in the city. Of plasma samples, 67% were collected in pre-treatment clinical trials on safety and efficacy of a tenofovir df/emtricitabine/rilpivirine single tablet regimen between June 2012 and November 2014.    This sponsored one-arm open-label clinical study was registered in clinicaltrials.gov (NCT 0150218). Sequences of pre-treatment plasma samples in people living with HIV in this study were analyzed for resistance associated mutations.",The paper specifically mentions the use of plasma samples for the extraction of viral RNA and subsequent sequencing.
37381002,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"This retrospective study aimed to characterize the distribution of HIV-1 genotypes and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in people with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure in Suzhou City, China; pol gene of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment was successfully amplified by using an in-house assay","The paper focuses on patients with failed antiviral treatment, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
37381002,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,No evidence,"The paper doesn’t indicate that the subjects were part of a clinical trial; even though some pre-treatment plasma samples were collected during clinical trial, there is no evidence that they were part of a clinical trial in the context of this research."
37381002,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"This retrospective study aimed to characterize the distribution of HIV-1 genotypes and the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in people with antiretroviral treatment (ART) failure in Suzhou City, China; Pol gene of HIV-1 viruses in blood samples of EDTA anticoagulants from 398 patients with failed antiviral treatment was successfully amplified by using an in-house assay. Viral nucleic acids were extracted from plasma or whole blood, using the MagNA Pure Compact Nucleic Acid Isolation Kit I (Roche, Switzerland) in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions. Plasma samples were collected in EDTA tubes for sequencing of the pol gene and near full-genome sequencing. Whole blood (200 μL) was used to extract viral nucleic acids for determining HIV-1 viral load, We followed the published full-length genome (FLG) consensus sequencing method for recombinant subtype CRF01_AE samples. The PCR products were purified with PureLink PCR Clean-Up Kit (Invitrogen, USA) and finished sequencing by Sanger sequencing method. No plasma specimens with HIV-1 viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml were not excluded in this study, which were people living with HIV who have received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year and in 13 years between 2005 and 2018 in the city. Of plasma samples, 67% were collected in pre-treatment clinical trials on safety and efficacy of a tenofovir df/emtricitabine/rilpivirine single tablet regimen between June 2012 and November 2014.","The paper mentions that the study focused on patients with antiretroviral treatment failure, who had received antiviral therapy for more than 1 year."
37381002,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","According to the analysis of HIVdb Program, 257 strains were resistant to at least one drug of NRTIs, NNRTIs, or PIs, with an overall resistant rate of 64.57% (257/398). Frequency of drug-resistance associated mutations among cases with ART failure was shown in Fig. 1. The drug resistant mutations associated with NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs were 45.48% (181/398), 63.32% (252/398) and 3.02% (12/398), respectively. Among the PIs resistant HIV strains, four strains possessed PI-related major mutations (M46I/L, I54V, V82F) and the other 8 viruses had 3 accessory mutations (L33, FL10F and Q58E) in PR region. Respectively, 17 and 16 loci in RT region were found emerging NRTIs and NNRTIs resistance-associated substitutes. M184V/I (40.70%, 162/398), D67N/G/S/H (13.82%, 55/398) and K65R/N (12.81%, 51/398) were the three most common resistance-related mutations regarding to NRTIs, while V106M/I/A, K103N/S and V179D/E/T/L were the first three common mutations associated with NNRTIs resistance while M184V/I, D67N/G/S/H and K65R/N were the first three common mutations associated with NRTIs.","The paper discusses drug resistance mutations associated with NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs, indicating that individuals had received these drug classes before sequencing."
37381002,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"ABC, AZT, D4T, DDI, FTC, 3TC, TDF, ATV, FPV, IDV, LPV, NFV, SQV, DRV, ETR, EFV, RPV, NVP","We analyzed the effects of mutations on drug resistance based on Stanford University HIV drug resistance database (https://hivdb.stanford.edu/). No other HIV strains with high-level resistance to PIs were detected except 2020-SZ-83050 strain. Three samples (2017-SZ-08303, 2018-SZ-08066 and 2020-XC-00107) with M46I/L mutation were resistant to ATV, FPV and IDV at potential low level, ATV, LPV, FPV, IDV, NFV and SQV at intermediate level. Six samples were simultaneously resistant to FPV, TPV at low level, L33F mutation (above) and L10LF mutation at low level to NFV, FPV. S185C, K103N, V179D, prolonged RPV use mutation led DOR resistance, GI, EFV resistance and NVP resistance at high level. FTC and TDF is similar to K65G. FTC and 3TC close to K65A, K65R resistant sites. AZT, DDI, 3TC, D4T ranged 47%, 50%, 50%, 56% WW resistance site. FTC, 3TC, ZDV, DDI, ABC, TDF, D4T, DRV, LPV, ATV, FPV, NFV, SQV, ETR, RPV, NVP ene post treatment ongoing study drugs. Censor from post inoculation mentioning protocol patients were naïve not shown ARV","The paper lists specific drugs for which resistance was tested or observed, indicating that these drugs were received by individuals in the study before sequencing."
37439411,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort.,"The paper mentions that it included a large number of genotypes from patients, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37439411,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,Trend over time of HIV-1 drug resistance to nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) and their drivers: A cohort study from Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA).,"The paper focuses on resistance mutations and genotypic susceptibility scores rather than providing in vitro drug susceptibility data, evidenced by the absence of direct references to in vitro testing methods or results."
37439411,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections.","The paper mentions that the data was retrieved from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, which is described as a public observational database, indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
37439411,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,The text does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37439411,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,9387,We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort.,"The paper includes a detailed count of the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing, summing both naïve and treatment-experienced patients."
37439411,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Italy,Our study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020.,The paper specifies that the study was conducted on a cohort from Italy and mentioned that the ARCA database included data from more than 100 Italian centres.
37439411,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2000 to 2020,Our study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) for NNRTIs in people living with HIV (PLWH) in Italy from 2000 to 2020.,The paper states that the study aims to describe the circulation of major resistance-associated mutations in people living with HIV in Italy from 2000 to 2020.
37439411,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections. We retrieved 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH (67.6% from males and 64.4% from Caucasians) and the median age was 43 years (IQR: 37--49).","The paper does not mention any process of cloning samples prior to sequencing, suggesting that this step was not part of the methodology."
37439411,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease/reverse transcriptase,"We retrieved HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, demographic and clinical data of adult PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) database, a public observational database containing data from more than 100 Italian centres for the study of resistance mutations to antiviral drugs for HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C virus infections. We retrieved 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH (67.6% from males and 64.4% from Caucasians) and the median age was 43 years (IQR: 37--49).",The paper specifically mentions that HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences were retrieved and included in the study.
37439411,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,"The paper does not specify the exact method used for sequencing the HIV-1 RNA protease/reverse transcriptase sequences, nor does it detail any specific sequencing technologies employed in the study.","The text does not mention the sequencing method used, and assumes the reader has knowledge of standard techniques."
37439411,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"The paper indicates that genotyping was performed on plasma samples from adults living with HIV, specifically mentioning that genotyping on RNA stemmed exclusively from virological failure with detectable viraemia, which alludes to viral RNA from plasma being genotyped.","While the paper does not specify the exact type of clinical specimens, it does mention that genotyping was performed on RNA, which typically indicates plasma samples in the context of HIV studies."
37439411,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"This paper outlines a longitudinal cohort analysis (ARCA) of individuals living with HIV in Italy, examining the trends of NNRTI drug resistance from 2000 to 2020. The study differentiates between treatment-naïve and experienced individuals, with the latter category accounting for a significant portion of the genotypic data (n=28,505) analyzed. Notably, genotyping in this context was conducted exclusively in cases of virological failure, underscoring the importance of monitoring drug resistance in individuals who have experienced treatment failure.","The paper mentions that genotyping was performed exclusively in cases of virological failure with detectable viraemia, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
37439411,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study involved the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA), a public observational database, thus it was not a clinical trial but rather an analysis of real-life data from patients across multiple centers in Italy.",The paper describes the study as a cohort analysis from an observational database and does not mention any clinical trials.
37439411,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We retrieved 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH (67.6% from males and 64.4% from Caucasians); We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) was defined as the presence of RAMs transmitted at the moment of the infection, considering genotypes of naïve PLWH, while pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) identifies the sum of drug resistance mutations detected in all genotypes of experienced PLWH with previous ARV drug exposure initiating or reinitiating ART, including both transmitted and acquired RAMs, at the moment of the analysis. TDR and PDR were defined as the presence of at least one mutation listed in the updated International Antiviral Sociey-USA and Stanford lists. We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) was defined as the presence of RAMs transmitted at the moment of the infection, considering genotypes of naïve PLWH, while pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) identifies the sum of drug resistance mutations detected in all genotypes of experienced PLWH with previous ARV drug exposure initiating or reinitiating ART, including both transmitted and acquired RAMs, at the moment of the analysis. TDR and PDR were defined as the presence of at least one mutation listed in the updated International Antiviral Sociey-USA and Stanford lists. We included 5982 naïves and 28 505 genotypes from 9387 treatment-experienced PLWH from the Antiviral Response Cohort Analysis (ARCA) cohort. Transmitted drug resistance (TDR) was defined as the presence of RAMs transmitted at the moment of the infection, considering genotypes of naïve PLWH, while pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) identifies the sum o