37495103,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018--2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. HIV-1 *pol* gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed.","The evidence directly states that HIV-1 *pol* gene sequences were generated from patient samples, confirming that the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples."
37495103,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods. The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. Sociodemographic and HIV-related data were collected from medical records and self-reported questionnaires. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed. The genetic barrier to resistance was assessed by combining treatment regimen and adherence data. 

Results. Nonoptimal ART adherence was identified in 48.3% of patients and correlated with male sex, PWID, unemployment, and rural area residence. Most of the patients with high-level adherence were identified among those who were on TDF+3TC+DTG. HIV-1 DRMs were identified in 54.6% of the patients. The analysis of HIV-1 DRM, ART regimen, and adherence data classified TDF+3TC+DTG and TDF+3TC+LPV/r as treatment regimens with a high genetic barrier, whereas EFV-containing ART was classified as a regimen with a low genetic barrier.","The evidence indicates that the study focuses on drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in patient samples but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data, which is a specific requirement for this question."
37495103,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper does not mention whether the sequences were made publicly available.,"Methods. The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. Sociodemographic and HIV-related data were collected from medical records and self-reported questionnaires. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed. The genetic barrier to resistance was assessed by combining treatment regimen and adherence data. 

Results. Nonoptimal ART adherence was identified in 48.3% of patients and correlated with male sex, PWID, unemployment, and rural area residence. Most of the patients with high-level adherence were identified among those who were on TDF+3TC+DTG. HIV-1 DRMs were identified in 54.6% of the patients. The analysis of HIV-1 DRM, ART regimen, and adherence data classified TDF+3TC+DTG and TDF+3TC+LPV/r as treatment regimens with a high genetic barrier, whereas EFV-containing ART was classified as a regimen with a low genetic barrier.","The paper does not provide any information about whether the sequences were deposited in a public database like GenBank. Without this information, it cannot be concluded that the sequences were made publicly available."
37495103,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,"Methods. The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. Sociodemographic and HIV-related data were collected from medical records and self-reported questionnaires. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed. The genetic barrier to resistance was assessed by combining treatment regimen and adherence data. 

Results. Nonoptimal ART adherence was identified in 48.3% of patients and correlated with male sex, PWID, unemployment, and rural area residence. Most of the patients with high-level adherence were identified among those who were on TDF+3TC+DTG. HIV-1 DRMs were identified in 54.6% of the patients. The analysis of HIV-1 DRM, ART regimen, and adherence data classified TDF+3TC+DTG and TDF+3TC+LPV/r as treatment regimens with a high genetic barrier, whereas EFV-containing ART was classified as a regimen with a low genetic barrier.","The evidence indicates that the study involved generating HIV-1 *pol* gene sequences, but there is no mention of any specific GenBank accession numbers associated with these sequences."
37495103,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,271,"The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. Sociodemographic and HIV-related data were collected from medical records and self-reported questionnaires. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed.","The evidence clearly states that the study cohort included 271 patients, confirming the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37495103,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Russia,"The study cohort included HIV-infected individuals who visited Novosibirsk Regional AIDS/HIV Clinical Hospital between March 2018 and August 2022. The age of the patients varied from 21 to 59 years old. All patients had undetectable viral load (VL) levels for 3--6 months while taking ART with further virological failure. ART adherence was analysed retrospectively using medical records and self-reported data; high-level adherence was assigned if all the required ART doses were taken; if patient reported sporadic missing doses, the adherence level was assigned as medium; and if the gap between the treatment doses was seven days or longer, the adherence level was assigned as low.  HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual. Reverse transcription and PCR were performed using RealBest Master Mix RT (Vector-Best, Russia) and BioMaster HS-Taq PCR-Color (BioLabMix, Russia), respectively, to amplify the HIV-1 PR-RT fragment (1302 bp).","The study was conducted in Novosibirsk, Russia, and the patient samples were obtained from an HIV/AIDS clinic in this region, indicating that the sequenced samples were from Russia."
37495103,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018 to 2022,"The study cohort included HIV-infected individuals who visited Novosibirsk Regional AIDS/HIV Clinical Hospital between March 2018 and August 2022. The age of the patients varied from 21 to 59 years old. All patients had undetectable viral load (VL) levels for 3--6 months while taking ART with further virological failure. ART adherence was analysed retrospectively using medical records and self-reported data; high-level adherence was assigned if all the required ART doses were taken; if patient reported sporadic missing doses, the adherence level was assigned as medium; and if the gap between the treatment doses was seven days or longer, the adherence level was assigned as low.  HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual. Reverse transcription and PCR were performed using RealBest Master Mix RT (Vector-Best, Russia) and BioMaster HS-Taq PCR-Color (BioLabMix, Russia), respectively, to amplify the HIV-1 PR-RT fragment (1302 bp).","The evidence specifies that the study was conducted between March 2018 and August 2022, indicating the range of years during which the sequenced samples were obtained."
37495103,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. Sociodemographic and HIV-related data were collected from medical records and self-reported questionnaires. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed. The genetic barrier to resistance was assessed by combining treatment regimen and adherence data.
HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual. Reverse transcription and PCR were performed using RealBest Master Mix RT (Vector-Best, Russia) and BioMaster HS-Taq PCR-Color (BioLabMix, Russia), respectively, to amplify the HIV-1 PR-RT fragment (1302 bp).","The evidence describes the use of RNA isolation from plasma and direct sequencing methods, which implies that no cloning of samples was performed prior to sequencing."
37495103,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol,"The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. Sociodemographic and HIV-related data were collected from medical records and self-reported questionnaires. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed. The genetic barrier to resistance was assessed by combining treatment regimen and adherence data.
HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual. Reverse transcription and PCR were performed using RealBest Master Mix RT (Vector-Best, Russia) and BioMaster HS-Taq PCR-Color (BioLabMix, Russia), respectively, to amplify the HIV-1 PR-RT fragment (1302 bp).","The evidence explicitly mentions that HIV-1 *pol* gene sequences were generated, indicating that the *pol* gene was sequenced in the study."
37495103,10,What method was used for sequencing?,ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser,"DNA sequencing was performed using an ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser (Applied Biosystems, USA). HIV-1 sequences were further analysed using Sequencher 4.1 software (GeneCodesCorporation, Ann Arbor, MI).","The evidence specifically states that DNA sequencing was performed using an ABI PRISM 3130xl DNA Analyser, confirming the sequencing method used in the study."
37495103,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"HIV-1 RNA was isolated from 250 µL of plasma using DeltaMag HBV/HCV/HIV (Vector-Best, Russia) according to the kit's manual. Reverse transcription and PCR were performed using RealBest Master Mix RT (Vector-Best, Russia) and BioMaster HS-Taq PCR-Color (BioLabMix, Russia), respectively, to amplify the HIV-1 PR-RT fragment (1302 bp).","The evidence indicates that HIV-1 RNA was isolated from plasma samples, specifying the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced."
37495103,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure.  In the current study, patients receiving third-line ART who were  on TDF+3TC+LPV/r therapy. Therapy was reviewed every month. Genotyping was performed once a patient experienced virological failure (VF), defined as two consecutive plasma HIV-1 RNA levels of 80 copies/mL.","The evidence mentions that all patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure, confirming that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37495103,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. Sociodemographic and HIV-related data were collected from medical records and self-reported questionnaires. HIV-1 pol gene sequences were generated, and the presence of HIV-1 DRM was assessed. The genetic barrier to resistance was assessed by combining treatment regimen and adherence data.","The evidence indicates that the study was conducted on patients visiting an HIV/AIDS clinic and the study cohort included 271 patients who were on first-line ART and visited Novosibirsk Regional AIDS/HIV Clinical Hospital between March 2018 and August 2022. The age of the patients varied from 21 to 59 years old. All patients had undetectable viral load (VL) levels for 3--6 months while taking ART with further virological failure. ART adherence was analysed retrospectively uisng medical records and self-reported data; high-level adherence was assigned if all the required ART doses were taken; if patient reported sporadic missing doses, the adherence level was assigned as medium; and if the gap between the treatment doses was seven days or longer, the adherence level was assigned as low. This currently does not indicate whether it's are clinical trials or not."
37495103,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The study cohort ( *n* = 271) included HIV-infected individuals who visited the Novosibirsk, Russia, HIV/AIDS clinic in 2018–2022. All patients received first-line ART prior to virological failure. Most of the patients with high-level adherence were identified among those who were on TDF+3TC+DTG. Out of 271 patients, 26 (9.6%) of patients were receiving TDF+3TC+DTG and were chronically infected with HIV-1 sub-subtype A6 (genetic barrier of STD regimen <25%) and received third-line ART (without tenofovir) and therapy was reviewed every month.  Thus, this study entails whether sequences were from these individuals or not, this study needs to be checked for precise mention of sequencing of patience samples for these queries..","The evidence states that all patients in the study were on ART, and some had undergone further therapy after virological failure, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37495103,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Most of the patients with high-level adherence were identified among those who were on TDF+3TC+DTG (NRTI+INSTI). Out of 271 patients, 26 (9.6%) of patients were receiving TDF+3TC+DTG and were chronically infected with HIV-1 sub-subtype A6 (genetic barrier of STD regimen <25%) and received third-line ART (without tenofovir) and therapy was reviewed every month. Thus, this study entails whether sequences were from these individuals or not, this study needs to be checked for precise mention of sequencing of patience samples for these queries.. Individuals receiving TDF+3TC+LPV/r were on NRTIs (non-tenofovir) + RTV-boosted PI, TDF+3TC+LPV/r on NRTIs (non-tenofovir) + RTV-boosted PI, ABC/3TC+EFV on NRTI + NNRTI, TDF/3TC+EFV on NRTI + NNRTI, DOR + 3TC + TDF on NRTI + INSTI, etc.","The members of the study cohort were on first-line ART and visited Novosibirsk Regional AIDS/HIV Clinical Hospital between March 2018 and August 2022. The age of the patients varied from 21 to 59 years old. All patients had undetectable viral load (VL) levels for 3–6 months while taking ART with further virological failure. ART adherence was analysed retrospectively using medical records and self-reported data; high-level adherence was assigned if all the required ART doses were taken; if patient reported sporadic missing doses, the adherence level was assiged as medium; and if the gap between the treatment doses was seven days or longer, the adherene level was assiged as low. Individuals in the study were receiving TDF+3TC+LPV/r were on NRTIs (non-tenofovir) + RTV-boosted PI, TDF+3TC+LPV/r on NRTIs (non-tenofovir) + RTV-boosted PI, ABC/3TC+EFV on NRTI + NNRTI, TDF/3TC+EFV on NRTI + NNRTI, DOR + 3TC + TDF on NRTI + INSTI, etc. This means they were receiving NRTIs, NNRTIs, PI/LPV/r as well as INSTIs. This means that these individuals were receiving NRTIs before sequencing. However in the current mentioned sequencing NRTIs and NNRTIs wild type, this doesn't mean they were not tested before sequencing. In any case too much speculations here. Best to check the source again."
37495103,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"DRV, DTG, EFV, LPV/r, TDF, 3TC, RAL","Most of the patients with high-level adherence were identified among those who were on TDF+3TC+DTG (NRTI+INSTI); out of the patients (21.7%) on abacavir (ABC) 600 mg+3TC+EFV, almost one-fifth (17.7%) of the study patients were on a TDF+3TC+DTG regimen, among the study patients, 5.5% were on TDF+3TC+elsulfavirine (ESV); patients on TDF+3TC+RAL, TDF+3TC+DRV, PhAZT+3TC+RAL were prescribed to 2.4% of study patients; individuals receiving TDF/3TC/EFV were on NRTIs (tenofovir)-based regimen were used as first-line in 3.6% of the patients who were monthly visited Novosibirsk Regional AIDS/HIV Clinical Hospital (class of drugs NRTIs, NNRTIs, INSTI), 1.6% on ELV, RPV-based regimens (NNRTIs).  ART adherence is a retrospective analysis  It's unclear what the receptor diagnosis is (HIV/AIDS?), precision sequencing of resistance in individuals who have previously received ARV drugs this is only clear after an excellent abstracts stating and not being maintained genotypically sequenced for resistance-specific ARV drugs. Direct says genotypically wild type. This means these were not documented as tested for these queries.","The evidence lists various antiretroviral drugs that were part of the treatment regimens for the patients in the study, indicating that these drugs were received before sequencing."
37498738,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Here, we characterize the longitudinal landscape of the HIV-1 reservoir in these participants, with particular attention to potential differences between treatment groups regarding evidence of evolution as a proxy for low-level replication. Near full-length HIV-1 proviral polymerase chain reaction and next-generation sequencing was applied to longitudinal peripheral blood mononuclear cell samples to assess proviral evolution and the potential emergence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs).",The paper describes the characteristics of the HIV-1 reservoir in participants and mentions the use of sequencing to analyze proviral evolution and drug resistance mutations. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples.
37498738,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,Neither an increase in genetic distance nor diversity over time was detected in participants of both treatment groups. Single proviral analysis showed high proportions of defective proviruses and low DRM numbers. No evidence for evolution during dolutegravir monotherapy was found in these early-treated individuals.,"The content of the paper does not include any mention of in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on sequencing and analysis of the HIV-1 reservoir and drug resistance mutations in patient samples, but does not discuss drug susceptibility testing in vitro."
37498738,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"No evidence for evolution during dolutegravir monotherapy was found in these early-treated individuals. Clustering of participants and possible viral evolution, maximum-likelihood phylogenetic and pairwise genetic distance analyses were performed on these APOBEC filtered sequences.",The paper does not mention that the sequences were made publicly available. It discusses the use of sequencing for analysis but does not provide any details on data availability.
37498738,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,K03455,SmaltAlign consists of an initial de novo alignment followed by mapping to the HIV-1 reference sequence HXB2 (GenBank accession number K03455).,"The paper mentions the GenBank accession number K03455 in the context of mapping sequences to the HIV-1 reference sequence, but does not provide accession numbers for the sequenced isolates themselves."
37498738,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,101,The ESS (NCT00537966) enrolled 101 people with HIV (PWH) from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study.,The paper states that the study enrolled 101 participants from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing.
37498738,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Switzerland,The ESS (NCT00537966) enrolled 101 people with HIV (PWH) from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study.,"The paper mentions that the participants were enrolled from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study, indicating that the samples were obtained from Switzerland."
37498738,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"No evidence for evolution during dolutegravir monotherapy was found in these early-treated individuals. Clustering of participants and possible viral evolution, maximum-likelihood phylogenetic and pairwise genetic distance analyses were performed on these APOBEC filtered sequences.",The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.
37498738,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Near full-length HIV-1 proviral polymerase chain reaction and next-generation sequencing was applied to longitudinal peripheral blood mononuclear cell samples to assess proviral evolution and the potential emergence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs).,The paper does not mention any process of cloning samples prior to sequencing.
37498738,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"gag, pol, env","Average pairwise diversity was calculated and longitudinally assessed for the 3 main genomic regions, for 139 (85%) samples for gag, 147 (90%) for pol, and 123 (75%) for env, as other samples contained deletions in the respective genes.","The paper discusses the sequencing and analysis of the gag, pol, and env genes in the context of HIV-1 reservoir dynamics."
37498738,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing,"Near full-length HIV-1 proviral polymerase chain reaction and next-generation sequencing was applied to longitudinal peripheral blood mononuclear cell samples to assess proviral evolution and the potential emergence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs). For each sample, the PCR approach with a positive result was used for next-generation sequencing (NGS).",The paper mentions that next-generation sequencing (NGS) was used for sequencing the HIV-1 proviral samples.
37498738,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs),"We used peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) or co-culture virus from 4 different time points: (i) at ART start (as), obtained as previously described; (ii) 1 year after ART start (1yaas); (iii) ESS week 0 (ESS wk0); and (iv) ESS week 48 (ESS wk48) (Figure 1A).",The paper mentions that peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) were used for sequencing the HIV-1 proviral samples.
37498738,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks. Neither an increase in genetic distance nor diversity over time was detected in participants of both treatment groups. Single proviral analysis showed high proportions of defective proviruses and low DRM numbers. No evidence for evolution during dolutegravir monotherapy was found in these early-treated individuals.,"The paper describes participants with sustained virological suppression, indicating that there were no sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure."
37498738,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks. The ESS (NCT00537966) enrolled 101 people with HIV (PWH) from the Zurich Primary HIV Infection Study and Swiss HIV Cohort Study.,The paper mentions the participation of patients in a randomized clinical trial comparing dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART.
37498738,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks. We used peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) or co-culture virus from 4 different time points: (i) at ART start (as), obtained as previously described; (ii) 1 year after ART start (1yaas); (iii) ESS week 0 (ESS wk0); and (iv) ESS week 48 (ESS wk48) (Figure 1A).",The paper describes participants who had previously received antiretroviral therapy (ART) and were switched to dolutegravir monotherapy. It also mentions the use of samples obtained at various time points during ART.
37498738,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, Integrase Inhibitors","Our recent randomized clinical trial comparing a switch to dolutegravir monotherapy with continuation of cART in early-treated individuals demonstrated sustained virological suppression over 48 weeks.
Samples from 8 participants from the combination antiretroviral therapy (cART) group who did not show any DRM in the previous time point and also had no more than 2% non-synonymous mutations within a 100 bp region during either of the 2 time points were chosen for viral diversity analysis. All participants were on a similar cART backbone, containing ritonavir-boosted darunavir (DRV) as protease inhibitor PI, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (FTC) as NRTIs, and efavirenz (EFV) as NNRTI. Quantification of HIV-1 unspliced RNA, HIV-1 multiply spliced RNA, and HIV-1 2-LTR circles was performed as previously described. No specific integrase inhibitor was included in cART, we previously showed no integrase mutations during suppressive cART.","The paper mentions the use of tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (FTC) as NRTIs, efavirenz (EFV) as NNRTI, darunavir (DRV) as PI and Dolutegravir (DTG) as integrase inhibitor in the study."
37498738,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"EFV, TDF, FTC, DRV, DTG, AZT","Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate and emtricitabine (TDF/FTC) were selected as NRTIs, darunavir (DRV), a boosted protease inhibitor, as non-NRTI, efavirenz (EFV), and later dolutegravir (DTG) as integrase inhibitor. All participants were on a similar cART backbone, containing ritonavir-boosted darunavir (DRV) as protease inhibitor PI, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (FTC) as NRTIs, and efavirenz (EFV) as NNRTI. All medications were taken as recommended by drug package inserts: AZT/3TC plus TDF/FTC both 2 2 booster DRV/ATV/r plus EFV both 2–6 3TC/FTC fixed-dose combination therapy plus ATV/LPV/r etc. 

A total of 64 early-treated PWHswitching from cART to DTGmonotherapy.26 participants were on DTGmonotherapy,179 and 38 on unscheduled cART.20 Study size and characteristics have been published elsewhere. 

To obtain a temporal landscape of the virological parameters under the rapid reduction of the viral reservoir after switching to DTG, participants were selected who had been on suppressive cART for at least 18 months. As previously described,25 this required a total time period of maximum 24 months from treatment initiation to randomization to DTGmonotherapy or cART. All participants provided written informed consent.62 treatment was started on emtricitabine (FTC) plus tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) (both from Gilead), plus efavirenz (EFV) (Sustiva, Bristol-Myers Squibb), or raltegravir (RAL) (Isentress, Merck), both from Merck, after insufficient response to a dual nucleoside reverse transcriptase therapy (TRITON), at 3 months after naïve initiation or better than 50 copies HIV/RNA per mL at randomization. Within the use of integrase inhibitors, virological suppression (>50 copies HIV RNA per mL) or successful (total nucleic- acid) quarantine was required at randomization. Integrase mutations had not been observed in any participant before, or during cART pathway due to low or non-viraemia. After randomization, all participants were off cART in the case of DTG monotherapy or 1 week later switched to DTG. 

All participants received the same treatment from the Zurigo Primary HIV Infection Study or Swiss HIV Cohort Cohort, with or without DTG. All participants lived in city of Zurich, had directly observed therapy (DOT), and were treated centrally by URZZ for the time course of 48 weeks.

As a small reservoir size was expected, study results on proviral sequencing in PBMCs is published on “near-full-length” sequencing (3871 4820 of 9185,1) methods,35 thus focused on CD4 lymphocytes of <0.4%.","The paper mentions the use of tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and emtricitabine (FTC) as NRTIs, efavirenz (EFV) as NNRTI, darunavir (DRV) as PI in the study."
37515095,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study on the molecular epidemiology of HIV in Kazakhstan, we analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 pol sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan.",The abstract mentions that the study analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 pol sequences collected from patient samples in Kazakhstan.
37515095,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The study focuses on the molecular epidemiology of HIV and the prevalence of drug resistance, but it does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility testing.",The content of the paper discusses drug resistance but does not mention in vitro susceptibility data or methods related to it.
37515095,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Study data are unavailable to protect patient confidentiality.,The Data Availability Statement explicitly states that the study data are unavailable to protect patient confidentiality.
37515095,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,The content does not mention any GenBank accession numbers related to the sequences.
37515095,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,968,"Between 2017 and 2020, 968 HIV-1 sequences were collected from PLHIV in Kazakhstan, with the highest number (389, 40.2%) collected in 2019.","The results section states that 968 HIV-1 sequences were collected from participants, which indicates the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained."
37515095,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kazakhstan,"Our study describes the molecular epidemiology of the HIV-1 epidemic in Kazakhstan, characterized by high levels of drug resistance and distinct DRMs between subtypes A6 and CRF02_AG.",The study focuses on samples collected from all regions of Kazakhstan.
37515095,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017-2020,The study analyzed 968 partial HIV-1 pol sequences that were collected between 2017 and 2020 from PLHIV across all regions of Kazakhstan.,The abstract and materials section mention that the samples were collected between 2017 and 2020.
37515095,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The mRNA from blood samples was purified using the Ribo-zol-C Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia) and used for RT-PCR, followed by pol gene sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq (Amplisens, Moscow, Russia) and Applied Biosystems® Genetic Analyzer 3130.",The paper discusses direct sequencing methods without cloning.
37515095,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Partial pol gene (covering protease and the 5′-end of reverse transcriptase),"Specifically, the sequence data consisted of partial HIV-1 pol gene sequences (covering protease and the 5′-end of reverse transcriptase, positions 2253 to 3337 on the HXB2 reference genome).",The materials section specifies that the sequences cover the protease and 5′-end of reverse transcriptase.
37515095,10,What method was used for sequencing?,RT-PCR followed by sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq and ABI 3130.,"The mRNA from blood samples was purified using the Ribo-zol-C Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia) and used for RT-PCR, followed by pol gene sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq (Amplisens, Moscow, Russia) and Applied Biosystems® Genetic Analyzer 3130.",The materials section describes the use of RT-PCR and specific sequencing technology.
37515095,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood samples,"The mRNA from blood samples was purified using the Ribo-zol-C Kit (InterLabService, Moscow, Russia) and used for RT-PCR, followed by pol gene sequencing using AmpliSens® HIV-Resist-Seq (Amplisens, Moscow, Russia) and Applied Biosystems® Genetic Analyzer 3130.",The methods section mentions blood sample collections and purification of mRNA from these samples.
37515095,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Most participants were on second-generation ART regimens (464, 47.9%), followed by mixed- (291, 30.1%) and first-generation regimens (204, 21.1%).","The demographics section states that many participants had been undergoing ART, suggesting some may have experienced virological failure."
37515095,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Between 2017 and 2020, 1004 participants registered with the Kazakh Scientific Centre of Dermatology and Infectious Diseases, Almaty, Kazakhstan; signed informed consent forms; and consented to blood sample collections for this study. Participants enrolled in the study from all 15 regions of Kazakhstan.",The study described is not a clinical trial; it is an observational study on drug resistance.
37515095,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Almost half of participants were on second-generation ART regimens (464, 47.9%), followed by mixed- (291, 30.1%) and first-generation regimens (204, 21.1%).","The demographics section describes the ART regimens of the participants, indicating that many had previously received ARV drugs."
37515095,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Drug resistance profiling for the following ARVs administered to patients in our dataset was carried out; 3TC, ABC, AZT, D4T, DDI, FTC, TDF, ETR, EFV, NVP, RPV, DRV/r, LPV/r. Due to a lack of integrase sequence coverage, drug resistance profiling for DTG was not possible, while DRV/c is not an ARV screened for the current version of the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.",The demographics section lists specific drugs from these classes that were used by participants.
37515095,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"3TC, ABC, AZT, D4T, DDI, FTC, TDF, ETR, EFV, NVP, RPV, DRV/r, LPV/r, DTG","Drug resistance profiling for the following ARVs administered to patients in our dataset was carried out; 3TC, ABC, AZT, D4T, DDI, FTC, TDF, ETR, EFV, NVP, RPV, DRV/r, LPV/r. Due to a lack of integrase sequence coverage, drug resistance profiling for DTG was not possible, while DRV/c is not an ARV screened for the current version of the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.",The demographics section lists specific drugs that were used by participants.
37515146,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In total, 379 sequences were obtained from participants (62% female, mean age 36 ± 10 years). The 379 participants with successful sequencing data were from eight regions distributed as follows: 53 (14%) from the Center, 40 (10.6%) for the Far North, 46 (12.1%) for the East, 41 (10.8%) for the Littoral, 44 (11.6%) for the North, 61 (16.1%) for the Northwest, 49 (12.9%) for the West and 45 (11.9%) for the Southwest.","The paper describes the sequencing of HIV-1 from patient samples, specifically mentioning the number of sequences obtained and the characteristics of the participants from whom these sequences were derived. The mention of 379 sequences obtained from participants clearly indicates that the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples."
37515146,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We described HIV-1 pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) patterns, effect of viral clades on PDR, and programmatic implications on first-line regimens in Cameroon. A sentinel surveillance of PDR was conducted from 2014 to 2019. Sequencing of HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase was performed, and HIVDR was interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v.9.4. In total, 379 sequences were obtained from participants (62% female, mean age 36 ± 10 years). The overall PDR rate was 15.0% [95% CI: 11.8--19.0] nationwide, with significant disparity between regions ( p = 0.03). NNRTI PDR was highest (12.4%), of which 7.9% had DRMs to EFV/NVP. Two regions had EFV/NVP PDR above the 10% critical threshold, namely the Far North (15%) and East (10.9%).",The paper focuses on pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) patterns and genetic diversity of HIV-1 in patient samples from Cameroon. It does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data.
37515146,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Sequence data are available on GenBank under the accession numbers GenBank MK702015-MK702057, MK867695-MK867757, and MK995397-MK995457.","The paper explicitly states that the sequence data are available on GenBank under specific accession numbers, indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
37515146,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"MK702015-MK702057, MK867695-MK867757, and MK995397-MK995457","Sequence data are available on GenBank under the accession numbers GenBank MK702015-MK702057, MK867695-MK867757, and MK995397-MK995457.","The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which are listed in the data availability statement."
37515146,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,379,"In total, 379 sequences were obtained from participants (62% female, mean age 36 ± 10 years). The 379 participants with successful sequencing data were from eight regions distributed as follows: 53 (14%) from the Center, 40 (10.6%) for the Far North, 46 (12.1%) for the East, 41 (10.8%) for the Littoral, 44 (11.6%) for the North, 61 (16.1%) for the Northwest, 49 (12.9%) for the West and 45 (11.9%) for the Southwest. A total of 391 participants were recruited, with sequencing successfully carried out in 379 individual participant samples, giving a genotyping success rate of 96.9%","The paper states that 379 sequences were successfully obtained from participants, indicating that samples from 379 individuals were used for HIV sequencing."
37515146,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Cameroon,"We described HIV-1 pre-treatment drug resistance (PDR) patterns, effect of viral clades on PDR, and programmatic implications on first-line regimens in Cameroon. A sentinel surveillance of PDR was conducted from 2014 to 2019. The study participants consisted of adult patients initiating ART in the health facilities of regions previously cited., Patients presenting for ART initiation at the various recruitment sites were encountered by the recruitment clinician. Data collection was carried out using a pre-established data abstraction form at each site after obtaining a thorough medical history and clinical examination to minimize recruitment of participants with previous drug exposure",The paper was conducted in Cameroon and all the sequences were from Cameroon.
37515146,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2014 to 2019,"We carried out a cross-sectional and analytical study for five years, from December 2014 to June 2019. A sentinel surveillance of PDR was conducted from 2014 to 2019 Sequencing of HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase was performed, and HIVDR was interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v.9.4","The paper details the timing of the sentinel surveillance and study, indicating that the samples were obtained from 2014 to 2019."
37515146,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,The products of the sequencing reaction obtained were purified by exclusion chromatography on SEPHADEX G50 resin and then sequenced using a genetic analyzer (ABI 3500 ® ).,The paper describes direct sequencing of PCR products and does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
37515146,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and Reverse Transcriptase,"Sequencing of HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase was performed, and HIVDR was interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v.9.4. Genotypic resistance testing was carried out in the pol gene (reverse transcriptase and protease regions) as follows.",The paper specifically mentions that the HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase genes were sequenced for the study and these two regions are part of Pol Gene.
37515146,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,The products of the sequencing reaction obtained were purified by exclusion chromatography on SEPHADEX G50 resin and then sequenced using a genetic analyzer (ABI 3500 ® ).,"The sequencing method described in the paper is Sanger sequencing, using a genetic analyzer (ABI 3500), which is a standard method for sequencing the pol gene in such studies."
37515146,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Viral RNA was extracted from 1 mL of plasma using the QIAGEN kit, following the manufacturer's instructions. For the amplification of the pol region, a previously validated inhouse genotyping assay was used. A total of 379 individual plasma samples from HIV-1 patients were sequenced in this study (−80 °C). In each region, patients were recruited in both urban and rural sites at the hospitals with the highest patient frequency. Table 1 shows the health facilities from which patients were recruited. HIV-1 genotypic resistance tests were performed at the Virology Laboratory of the Chantal Biya International Reference Center (CIRCB). The study participants consisted of adult patients initiating ART in the health facilities of regions previously cited. Patients presenting for ART initiation at the various recruitment sites were encountered by the recruitment clinician. Data collection was carried out using a pre-established data abstraction form at each site after obtaining a thorough medical history and clinical examination to minimize recruitment of participants with previous drug exposure. Data collected included the following: age, sex, region of origin, zone of residence (urban/rural), and WHO clinical stage. For each participant, 10 mL of venous blood was collected in an EDTA tube. The tubes were centrifuged at a speed of 1800 rpm, separated for plasma collection, plasma aliquoted (1 mL) in cryotubes, stored at −20 °C, and transported to the CIRCB, where biological analyzes were carried out.",The paper mentions that HIV-1 genotypic resistance tests were performed on plasma samples from the participants.
37515146,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The study participants consisted of adult patients initiating ART in the health facilities of regions previously cited. We included in our study HIV-1 infected patients aged above 19 years with no prior history of ART treatment. Not included in our study were patients who were re-initiating ART, those whose samples were unsuccessful at sequencing, and those who were co-infected with either viral hepatitis B or C.","The paper included treatment-naïve individuals without prior history of ART treatment, indicating that none of the sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37515146,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We carried out a cross-sectional and analytical study for five years, from December 2014 to June 2019. The enrollment of participants was carried out in health facilities of eight regions of Cameroon: Centre, Littoral, East, West, Northwest, Southwest, North, and Far North. In each region, patients were recruited in both urban and rural sites at the hospitals with the highest patient frequency. A total of 391 participants were recruited, with sequencing successfully carried out in 379 individual participant samples, giving a genotyping success rate of 96.9%.","The study design was cross-sectional and analytical, focusing on sentinel surveillance of pre-treatment drug resistance rather than a clinical trial, no mention of clinical trial in the paper."
37515146,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Included in our study were HIV-1 infected patients aged above 19 years (adults per WHO definitions) with no prior history of ART treatment. Not included in our study were patients who were re-initiating ART, those whose samples were unsuccessful at sequencing, and those who were co-infected with either viral hepatitis B or C. We included in our study HIV-1 infected patients aged above 19 years (adults per WHO definitions) with no prior history of ART treatment.","The paper included treatment-naïve individuals without prior history of ART treatment, indicating that the sequences were not from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37515146,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Included in our study were HIV-1 infected patients aged above 19 years (adults per WHO definitions) with no prior history of ART treatment. Not included in our study were patients who were re-initiating ART, those whose samples were unsuccessful at sequencing, and those who were co-infected with either viral hepatitis B or C. We included in our study HIV-1 infected patients aged above 19 years (adults per WHO definitions) with no prior history of ART treatment.","The study participants consisted of adult patients initiating ART in the health facilities of regions previously cited. The paper included treatment-naïve individuals without prior history of ART treatment, indicating that they had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing."
37515146,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"Included in our study were HIV-1 infected patients aged above 19 years (adults per WHO definitions) with no prior history of ART treatment. Not included in our study were patients who were re-initiating ART, those whose samples were unsuccessful at sequencing, and those who were co-infected with either viral hepatitis B or C. We included in our study HIV-1 infected patients aged above 19 years (adults per WHO definitions) with no prior history of ART treatment.","The study participants consisted of adult patients initiating ART in the health facilities of regions previously cited. The paper included treatment-naïve individuals without prior history of ART treatment, indicating that they had not received any ARV drugs before sample sequencing."
37520425,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"HIV-1 genotypic resistance testing (GRT) before ART initiation was done in 14 of 30 viremic participants on DTG, among whom nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), NNRTI, and protease inhibitors resistance was detected in 0 (0%), 2 (14%) and 1 (7%), respectively. No resistance was found in the 2 of 30 participants with available GRT at the time of viremia ≥50 copies/mL.","The paper mentions that HIV-1 genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was performed on patient samples, indicating that sequences from patient samples were reported."
37520425,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,The study assessed for the presence of PDR among patients with viremia ≥50 copies/mL at months after ART initiation in the DTG cohort.,"The focus of the paper was on genotypic resistance testing and virological outcomes rather than in vitro drug susceptibility. In vitro drug susceptibility data typically involves testing the effectiveness of drugs on viral isolates in a lab setting, which is not mentioned in the methods or results."
37520425,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The reported protease and reverse transcriptase sequences are available in GenBank (accession number OQ627458-OQ627474).,"The paper explicitly states that the sequences are available in GenBank, which indicates that they were made publicly available."
37520425,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,OQ627458-OQ627474,The reported protease and reverse transcriptase sequences are available in GenBank (accession number OQ627458-OQ627474).,"The paper provides the specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequences reported, confirming their availability."
37520425,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,14,"For patients with viremia at 12 months after starting DTG-based ART, blood plasma samples prior to treatment initiation were available for 25 of 30 (83%) patients. Of those, 14 of 25 (56%) samples were successfully PCR amplified and sequenced for PDR determination.","The paper mentions that 14 samples were successfully PCR amplified and sequenced for drug resistance determination, indicating that samples were obtained from 14 individuals."
37520425,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Tanzania,"The cohort includes patients seen at the Chronic Diseases Clinic of Ifakara, the care and treatment center for PWH of the Saint Francis Referral Hospital, located in rural southeastern Tanzania.","The study was conducted in Tanzania, and the samples were obtained from participants in the KIULARCO cohort, which is based in Tanzania."
37520425,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2016-2020,Two groups of PWH aged ≥15 years attending KIULARCO were included in the analysis: (1) treatment-naive patients initiating NNRTI-based ART between 16 December 2016 and 15 December 2017 (referred to as the NNRTI cohort) and (2) treatment-naive patients initiating DTG-based ART between 16 March 2019 and 15 September 2020 (referred to as the DTG cohort).,The samples were collected from treatment-naive patients initiating NNRTI-based ART between 2016-2017 and DTG-based ART between 2019-2020.
37520425,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 GRT was performed on samples available prior to treatment initiation for cases with a VL ≥50 copies/mL at 12 months using cryopreserved samples, to determine PDR before ART initiation in the DTG cohort. GRT was performed using a validated in-house polymerase chain reaction (PCR) protocol to determine the HIV-1 drug resistance--associated mutations for reverse transcriptase, protease, and INSTI.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the direct sequencing of plasma samples.
37520425,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, Reverse Transcriptase, Integrase","GRT was performed using a validated in-house polymerase chain reaction (PCR) protocol to determine the HIV-1 drug resistance--associated mutations for reverse transcriptase, protease, and INSTI.","The paper mentions sequencing of the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes for drug resistance testing."
37520425,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger Sequencing,"Finally, all genotypic data were obtained through standard Sanger sequencing; rates of HIV-associated drug resistance mutations would possibly be higher if ultra-sensitive HIV-1 drug resistance testing by next-generation sequencing had been used.",The paper states that all genotypic data were obtained through standard Sanger sequencing.
37520425,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma Samples,"HIV-1 genotypic resistance testing (GRT) before ART initiation was done in 14 of 30 viremic participants on DTG, among whom nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), NNRTI, and protease inhibitors resistance was detected in 0 (0%), 2 (14%) and 1 (7%), respectively. No resistance was found in the 2 of 30 participants with available GRT at the time of viremia ≥50 copies/mL. GRT was performed using a validated in-house polymerase chain reaction (PCR) protocol to determine the HIV-1 drug resistance--associated mutations for reverse transcriptase, protease, and INSTI. In brief, RNA was extracted from 150 μL of plasma using the PureLink Viral RNA/DNA Mini Kit (Invitrogen, Thermo Fisher Scientific), according to the manufacturer’s protocol.",The paper mentions that blood plasma samples were used for sequencing.
37520425,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,Blood plasma samples at VL ≥50 copies/mL were available for 13 of 30 (43%) patients. PCR amplification and sequencing for determination of acquired and/or persisting HIV-1 drug resistance--associated mutations was successful for only 2 of 13 (15%) patients. No mutations associated with HIV-1 drug resistance were detected (Table 3).,The paper mentions that samples were obtained from individuals with virological failure (viremia ≥50 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART.
37520425,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This is a retrospective, observational study nested within the Kilombero and Ulanga antiretroviral cohort (KIULARCO), a prospective cohort of PWH.","The study is described as a retrospective, observational study nested within a cohort, not a clinical trial."
37520425,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Two groups of PWH aged ≥15 years attending KIULARCO were included in the analysis: (1) treatment-naive patients initiating NNRTI-based ART between 16 December 2016 and 15 December 2017 (referred to as the NNRTI cohort) and (2) treatment-naive patients initiating DTG-based ART between 16 March 2019 and 15 September 2020 (referred to as the DTG cohort). The separation of periods ensured that no patient in the NNRTI cohort would have been switched to a DTG-based regimen by the time of outcome assessment, which could have had an impact on VL results.","The study focused on treatment-naive patients initiating NNRTI-based or DTG-based ART, and it excluded treatment-experienced patients."
37520425,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","Two groups of PWH aged ≥15 years attending KIULARCO were included in the analysis: (1) treatment-naive patients initiating NNRTI-based ART between 16 December 2016 and 15 December 2017 (referred to as the NNRTI cohort) and (2) treatment-naive patients initiating DTG-based ART between 16 March 2019 and 15 September 2020 (referred to as the DTG cohort). In addition, HIV-1 GRT was performed on samples available prior to treatment initiation for cases with a VL ≥50 copies/mL at 12 months using cryopreserved samples, to determine PDR before ART initiation in the DTG cohort. In brief, RNA was extracted from 150 μL of plasma using the PureLink Viral RNA/DNA Mini Kit (Invitrogen, Thermo Fisher Scientific), according to the manufacturer's protocol. RNA extracts were retrotranscribed and amplified using the HIV-1 Genotyping Kit Amplification Module (Applied Biosystems, Thermo Fisher Scientific).","The paper mentions that participants were initiating NNRTI-based ART which includes NRTI and NNRTI and the samples for PDR was from DTG cohort which the patients had  to received DTG and NRTI. Near the end of the sample collection, some patients had already received PI but they were not included in the study so PI was not mentioned in the drugs received."
37520425,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI (Tenofovir, Lamivudine), NNRTI (Efavirenz, Nevirapine), PI (Darunavir, Ritonavir), DTG","Two groups of PWH aged ≥15 years attending KIULARCO were included in the analysis: (1) treatment-naive patients initiating NNRTI-based ART between 16 December 2016 and 15 December 2017 (referred to as the NNRTI cohort) and (2) treatment-naive patients initiating DTG-based ART between 16 March 2019 and 15 September 2020 (referred to as the DTG cohort). In addition, HIV-1 GRT was performed on samples available prior to treatment initiation for cases with a VL ≥50 copies/mL at 12 months using cryopreserved samples, to determine PDR before ART initiation in the DTG cohort. In brief, RNA was extracted from 150 μL of plasma using the PureLink Viral RNA/DNA Mini Kit (Invitrogen, Thermo Fisher Scientific), according to the manufacturer's protocol. RNA extracts were retrotranscribed and amplified using the HIV-1 Genotyping Kit Amplification Module (Applied Biosystems, Thermo Fisher Scientific).",The paper mentions the drugs included in the regimens.
37537871,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"A total of 77 plasma samples, collected from 70 male and 7 female patients ages 18 to 68, were obtained from the Queen Mary Hospital in Hong Kong between 2002 and 2014.","The paper mentions that 77 plasma samples were collected from patients, which indicates that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
37537871,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods
A new ONT sequencing-based method for profiling DRMs in HIV quasispecies was developed and validated. The method used hierarchical clustering of long amplicons that cover regions associated with different types of antiretroviral drugs. A gradient series of an HIV plasmid and 2 plasma samples was prepared to validate the clustering performance. The ONT results were compared to those obtained with Sanger sequencing and Illumina sequencing in 77 HIV-positive plasma samples to evaluate the diagnostic performance.

Results
In the validation study, the abundance of detected quasispecies was concordant with the predicted result with the R2 of > 0.99. During the diagnostic evaluation, 59/77 samples were successfully sequenced for DRMs. Among 18 failed samples, 17 were below the limit of detection of 303.9 copies/μL. Based on the receiver operating characteristic analysis, the ONT workflow achieved an F1 score of 0.96 with a cutoff of 0.4 variant allele frequency. Four cases were found to have quasispecies with DRMs, in which 2 harbored quasispecies with more than one class of DRMs. Treatment modifications were recommended for these cases.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on sequencing and characterizing drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in HIV quasispecies using long-read sequencing and hierarchical clustering. The study evaluates the diagnostic performance of the sequencing methods and the accuracy of detecting DRMs, but it does not include any experimental data on drug susceptibility testing in vitro."
37537871,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Materials and Methods
Sample Collection
A total of 77 plasma samples, collected from 70 male and 7 female patients ages 18 to 68, were obtained from the Queen Mary Hospital in Hong Kong between 2002 and 2014 ( Supplemental Table 1 )....
In the evaluation of diagnostic performance, 59/77 samples were successfully sequenced using the ONT workflow, while Sanger sequencing results covering protease and reverse transcriptase (RT) (amino acid position 1–401) were available in 54 samples, except for 5 samples covering RT amino acid position 1 to 335. No integrase (IN) data was available in 16 samples. For Illumina, data was not available in 16 samples with insufficient sample volume; 43 samples were sequenced by Illumina as a result.

Limit of Detection (LOD)
LOD was defined as the minimum input viral concentration (<250 copy/µL) of having 0.9 probability to reach the average depth of coverage (DP).","The paper does not mention any public database where the sequences were deposited. Therefore, it is likely that the sequences were not made publicly available."
