37574435,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We also collected the available reports of HIV genotypic resistance testing of included PWH. In Taiwan, genotypic resistance testing is performed before initiation of first-line therapy for the purpose of surveillance or at the time of virologic failure on an as-needed basis at several designated hospitals and Taiwan Centers for Disease Control.",The study does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing.
37574435,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not specified,"We also collected the available reports of HIV genotypic resistance testing of included PWH. In Taiwan, genotypic resistance testing is performed before initiation of first-line therapy for the purpose of surveillance or at the time of virologic failure on an as-needed basis at several designated hospitals and Taiwan Centers for Disease Control. For PWH who had available genotypic resistance reports, their archived RAMs to NRTIs or INSTIs before the switch was reviewed and the genotypic resistance scores (GSSs) were determined using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.","The paper mentions collecting genotypic resistance data, but it does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced."
37574435,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,"We also collected the available reports of HIV genotypic resistance testing of included PWH. In Taiwan, genotypic resistance testing is performed before initiation of first-line therapy for the purpose of surveillance or at the time of virologic failure on an as-needed basis at several designated hospitals and Taiwan Centers for Disease Control. For PWH who had available genotypic resistance reports, their archived RAMs to NRTIs or INSTIs before the switch was reviewed and the genotypic resistance scores (GSSs) were determined using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.",The paper does not mention the sequencing method used.
37574435,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH). PWH who were aged ≥20 years and received HIV care with at least 3 months of ART prescription between 1 January 2016 and 30 March 2022 were eligible for the study; retrospective review of medical records of PWH who had viral rebound with plasma HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL and were switched to either dolutegravir combined with 2 NRTIs or BIC/FTC/TAF.","The paper mentions that plasma HIV RNA was used to screen eligible participants, indicating the use of plasma samples."
37574435,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We retrospectively reviewed the medical records of PWH who had viral rebound with plasma HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL and were switched to either dolutegravir combined with 2 NRTIs or BIC/FTC/TAF. Seventy-nine PWH with viral rebound while receiving other antiretroviral regimens were included Fordable Four. We also collected the available reports of HIV genotypic resistance testing of included PWH. In Taiwan, genotypic resistance testing is performed before initiation of first-line therapy for the purpose of surveillance or at the time of virologic failure on an as-needed basis at several designated hospitals and Taiwan Centers for Disease Control. For PWH who had available genotypic resistance reports, their archived RAMs to NRTIs or INSTIs before the switch was reviewed and the genotypic resistance scores (GSSs) were determined using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. 11, 12, 13 Each antiretroviral agent would be assigned with a score of 0 (high-level resistance), 0.25 (intermediate-level resistance), 0.50 (low-level resistance), 0.75 (potential low-level resistance) or 1 (susceptible).",The paper specifically mentions including individuals with virologic rebound.
37574435,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH).","The study is described as a retrospective cohort study, not a clinical trial."
37574435,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We retrospectively reviewed the medical records of PWH who had viral rebound with plasma HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL and were switched to either dolutegravir combined with 2 NRTIs or BIC/FTC/TAF. Seventy-nine PWH with viral rebound while receiving other antiretroviral regimens were included Fordable Four. Among those eligible PWH, we included those who switched from their original background ART to a second-generation INSTI-based ART (either BIC/FTC/TAF or DTG plus 2 NRTIs).  This was a single-center, retrospective cohort study conducted in the National Taiwan University Hospital (NTUH).",The paper mentions including individuals who had viral rebound while receiving other antiretroviral regimens.
37574435,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Within the first 48 weeks of switch, the overall probability of re-achieving viral suppression was 79.7% (82.5% [33/40] and 76.9% [30/39] for BIC/FTC/TAF and dolutegravir-based regimens, respectively, p = 0.78). Most PWH (82.1%) in the DTG group switched from NNRTI-based ART to DTG-based regimens, while 75% of the PWH in the BIC group were receiving a first-generation INSTI-based ART before switch.","The paper mentions switching individuals from NNRTI-based ART to DTG-based regimens and from a first-generation INSTI-based ART to BIC/FTC/TAF, indicating prior exposure to these drug classes."
37574435,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Dolutegravir, bictegravir, emtricitabine, tenofovir alafenamide, NRTIs; Abacavir, Didanosine, Emtricitabine, Stavudine, Lamivudine, Tenofovir, Zalcitabine","Within the first 48 weeks of switch, the overall probability of re-achieving viral suppression was 79.7% (82.5% [33/40] and 76.9% [30/39] for BIC/FTC/TAF and dolutegravir-based regimens, respectively, p = 0.78). Most PWH (82.1%) in the DTG group switched from NNRTI-based ART to DTG-based regimens, while 75% of the PWH in the BIC group were receiving a first-generation INSTI-based ART before switch. Among those eligible PWH, we included those who switched from their original background ART to a second-generation INSTI-based ART (either BIC/FTC/TAF or DTG plus 2 NRTIs)","The paper mentions switching individuals to regimens containing dolutegravir, bictegravir, emtricitabine, and tenofovir alafenamide, and had been using NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs."
37585352,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We analysed near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs, which were defined according to the 2022 IAS--USA drug resistance mutation list and Stanford HIV drug resistance database. The analysis included a dataset of 5805 sequences, of which 1281 (22%) were obtained from treatment-naive PWH, and the other 4524 (78%) were from ART-suppressed PWH.","The paper reports that sequences were obtained from patient samples. Specifically, it mentions that 5805 proviral sequences were analyzed, with 1281 from treatment-naive individuals and 4524 from ART-suppressed individuals. This indicates that the study involved HIV sequencing from patient samples."
37585352,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We here evaluated the prevalence of archived RPV-RAMs in a cohort of people living with HIV (PWH). We analysed near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs, which were defined according to the 2022 IAS--USA drug resistance mutation list and Stanford HIV drug resistance database. RPV-RAMs were identified in 757/5805 sequences, giving a prevalence of 13.0% (95% CI 12%--13.9%).","The paper focuses on the prevalence of archived rilpivirine resistance-associated mutations (RVP-RAMs) in patient samples and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The study primarily analyzes genetic sequences to determine the presence of resistance mutations, rather than assessing the effectiveness of drugs against the virus in vitro."
37585352,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"All relevant data are within the paper and the Supplementary data , Figures and Tables. HIV-1 sequences are available on request through the PANGEA consortium.","The paper mentions that HIV-1 sequences are available on request through the PANGEA consortium, but does not provide GenBank accession numbers or confirm that the sequences are publicly available."
37585352,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It mentions that sequences are available on request through the PANGEA consortium, but no specific accession numbers are listed in the provided content."
37585352,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,5805,"We analysed near full-length HIV-1 pol sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs. In this analysis, we included a total of 5805 (95.5%) proviral sequences from Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) participants.","The paper states that 5805 proviral sequences were included in the analysis, indicating that samples were obtained from 5805 individuals for HIV sequencing."
37585352,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Botswana,This study provides the first data for archived RPV-RAMs in ART-naive and ART-suppressed individuals in Botswana. Archived rilpivirine-associated resistance mutations (RVP-RAMs) have been found to predict HIV-1 virological failure in those switching to long-acting injectable cabotegravir/rilpivirine.,The paper mentions that the study participants were from Botswana and that the sequences were analyzed for individuals living with HIV in Botswana.
37585352,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2013 to 2018,"Study participants were recruited from a large community HIV incidence trial described elsewhere that enrolled participants aged 16 to 64 years, followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018.","The paper states that the study participants were followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018, indicating the time frame during which the samples were obtained."
37585352,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 proviral DNA sequences were generated using next-generation sequencing (NGS) as previously described elsewhere. Briefly, a long-range HIV genotyping protocol was used to generate viral sequences and the first-round amplicon was subjected to the Illumina sequencing system. The NGS was performed at the Biopolymers Facility at Harvard Medical School or by the PANGEA HIV consortium at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute","The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. It describes the use of next-generation sequencing (NGS) and a long-range HIV genotyping protocol to generate viral sequences, but there is no mention of cloning."
37585352,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,HIV-1 pol,We analysed near full-length HIV-1 pol sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs.,The paper mentions that near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences were analyzed for the presence of rilpivirine resistance-associated mutations (RVP-RAMs).
37585352,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing (NGS) using Illumina MiSeq and HiSeq platforms,"HIV-1 proviral DNA sequences were generated using next-generation sequencing (NGS) as previously described elsewhere. Briefly, a long-range HIV genotyping protocol was used to generate viral sequences and the first-round amplicon was subjected to the Illumina sequencing system. The NGS was performed at the Biopolymers Facility at Harvard Medical School or by the PANGEA HIV consortium at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute utilizing Illumina MiSeq and HiSeq platforms.",The paper describes the use of next-generation sequencing (NGS) and specifies that the Illumina MiSeq and HiSeq platforms were utilized for sequencing.
37585352,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Proviral DNA,We analysed near full-length HIV-1 pol sequences from proviral DNA for the presence of RPV-RAMs.,"The paper mentions that near full-length HIV-1 *pol* sequences were generated from proviral DNA, indicating that the type of samples sequenced were proviral DNA."
37585352,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We did not include sequences for individuals experiencing virological failure since LA cabotegravir/rilpivirine is currently recommended only for virologically suppressed adults (HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL), and is not currently recommended for people with virological failure. To control for the impact of archived HIV proviral DNA mutations, an ART-naive group was used","The paper explicitly states that they did not include sequences for individuals experiencing virological failure, indicating that no sequences were obtained from such individuals."
37585352,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Study participants were recruited from a large community HIV incidence trial described elsewhere that enrolled participants aged 16 to 64 years, followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018.","The paper mentions that the study participants were recruited from a community HIV incidence trial, but does not indicate that they were part of a clinical trial."
37585352,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Study participants were recruited from a large community HIV incidence trial described elsewhere that enrolled participants aged 16 to 64 years, followed for 30 months from 2013 to 2018. In this analysis, we included a total of 5805 (95.5%) proviral sequences from Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) participants, of which 1281 proviral DNA sequences were obtained from the treatment-naive individuals, and 4524 were from ART-experienced participants with HIV-1 VL ≤400 copies/mL The most common ART regimens used were efavirenz + emtricitabine + tenofovir (EFV/FTC/TDF) (n = 1792; 39.6%), lamivudine + nevirapine + zidovudine (3TC/NVP/ZDV) (n = 719; 15.9%) and lamivudine + efavirenz + zidovudine (3TC/EFV/ZDV) (n = 621; 13.7%).","The paper mentions that sequences were obtained from ART-experienced participants, and it lists the specific ART regimens used by these individuals, indicating that some sequences were from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37585352,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI","The most common ART regimens used were efavirenz + emtricitabine + tenofovir (EFV/FTC/TDF) (n = 1792; 39.6%), lamivudine + nevirapine + zidovudine (3TC/NVP/ZDV) (n = 719; 15.9%) and lamivudine + efavirenz + zidovudine (3TC/EFV/ZDV) (n = 621; 13.7%).","The paper lists the specific antiretroviral (ARV) drugs used by the ART-experienced participants, which include drugs from the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI) and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) classes."
37585352,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"EFV, FTC, TDF, 3TC,  NVP, ZDV","The most common ART regimens used were efavirenz + emtricitabine + tenofovir (EFV/FTC/TDF) (n = 1792; 39.6%), lamivudine + nevirapine + zidovudine (3TC/NVP/ZDV) (n = 719; 15.9%) and lamivudine + efavirenz + zidovudine (3TC/EFV/ZDV) (n = 621; 13.7%).","The paper lists the specific antiretroviral (ARV) drugs used by the ART-experienced participants, which include efavirenz (EFV), emtricitabine (FTC), tenofovir (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), nevirapine (NVP), and zidovudine (ZDV)."
37593123,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022.",The paper mentions that sequences of the HIV-1 *pol* gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022.
37593123,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"To predict susceptibility to 12 antiretroviral drugs, the research utilized the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. The assessment of drug resistance was accomplished using Stanford University's HIVDR Database's online sequence analysis tool.","The paper discusses using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database to predict susceptibility to 12 antiretroviral drugs, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data were reported."
37593123,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 ( 6 ) positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022. These sequences were procured from both the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC and the HIV sequence databases of the Los Alamos National Laboratory.","The paper mentions that the sequences were procured from the HIV sequence databases of the Los Alamos National Laboratory, and the data from 2004 to 2022 was stratified into five distinct sampling periods that aligned with the NFATP developmental stages in China. suggesting that the data was publicly available."
37593123,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37593123,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"57,902","The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorized according to the development stages of the NFATP.","The results section indicates that the study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals."
37593123,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022.","The study focuses on Chinese individuals diagnosed with HIV infection, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from China."
37593123,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2004 to 2022,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 ( 6 ) positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022.","The study spans from 2004 to 2022, and the samples were obtained within this period."
37593123,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 ( 6 ) positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022. These sequences were procured from both the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC and the HIV sequence databases of the Los Alamos National Laboratory. A total of 57,902 HIV-1 pol gene region sequences that covered 31 PLADs made up the final dataset.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the direct extraction of sequences from the *pol* gene region.
37593123,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol gene,"Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022. A total of 67,739 sequences were extracted from the HIV-1 pol gene region (HXB2 ( 6 ) positions 2,253--3,312) of ART naïve HIV-infected individuals between the period of 2004 and 2022. The reference sequence for the study was HXB2.",The paper specifically mentions the sequencing of the HIV-1 *pol* gene region.
37593123,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention the specific sequencing method used in the study.
37593123,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not specify the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced.
37593123,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorized according to the development stages of the NFATP.","The paper focuses on ART-naïve individuals, implying that sequences were not obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37593123,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.
37593123,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorized according to the development stages of the NFATP.","The paper focuses on sequences from ART-naïve individuals, indicating that they had not received ARV drugs."
37593123,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorized according to the development stages of the NFATP.

The feasibility of ARV drugs with HIVDR mutations in individuals harboring HIVDR infections, the earliest infection time for TDR, and clinical trial of ARV drugs juxtaposed with HIVDR mutations before and post clinical trial in China were beyond the monitoring objective of this study, which aimed to reflect the national prevalence of TDR in ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals.

The data in drug resistance studies were acquired from both the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC and the HIV sequence databases of the Los Alamos National Laboratory, with needs of China Health Promotion and Disease Prevention Center in ARV monitoring, about the innovations of diseases in China, resistance of HIV-1 infections to ARV drugs in China.","The paper reports the sequences from ART naïve individuals, who had never received any antiretroviral drugs."
37593123,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"This comprehensive study aims to examine the alterations in HIV-1 subtypes or sub-subtypes and TDR among Chinese individuals, who have been diagnosed with HIV infection and are previously untreated with antiretroviral therapy (ART), across the span of 2004 to 2022. Sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene region were obtained from ART-naïve HIV-positive individuals across 31 provincial-level administrative divisions between 2004 and 2022. The study population consisted of 57,902 ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals, which were categorized according to the development stages of the NFATP.

The feasibility of ARV drugs with HIVDR mutations in individuals harboring HIVDR infections, the earliest infection time for TDR, and clinical trial of ARV drugs juxtaposed with HIVDR mutations before and post clinical trial in China were beyond the monitoring objective of this study, which aimed to reflect the national prevalence of TDR in ART-naïve HIV-infected individuals.

The data in drug resistance studies were acquired from both the National Center for AIDS/STD Control and Prevention, China CDC and the HIV sequence databases of the Los Alamos National Laboratory, with needs of China Health Promotion and Disease Prevention Center in ARV monitoring, about the innovations of diseases in China, resistance of HIV-1 infections to ARV drugs in China.","The paper  mentions that the study focuses on ART-naïve individuals, they had not received any ARV drugs."
37626789,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the *pol* gene region using in-house assay validated at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention, and the Stanford HIVDB v9.0 algorithm was used for identification of drug resistance mutations.",The paper explicitly states that HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the *pol* gene region using an in-house assay validated at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention. This indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples.
37626789,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"However, children were generally not considered despite their greater risk. Hence, this study was aimed to evaluate HIV-1 disease progression and drug resistance mutation among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia.","The focus of the paper is on drug resistance mutations in patient samples, and there is no mention of in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The objectives and methods described do not include in vitro testing."
37626789,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"Since data analysis for other objectives is ongoing, the raw data can be obtained from the first corresponding author.","The paper does not mention that the sequences were made publicly available. It only states that raw data can be obtained from the first corresponding author, which does not imply public availability."
37626789,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no mention of accession numbers in the methods or results sections related to sequencing.
37626789,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,551,A longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children (<15 years old) recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia.,The paper states that a longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia. This indicates that samples were obtained from 551 individuals.
37626789,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Ethiopia,"However, children were generally not considered despite their greater risk. Hence, this study was aimed to evaluate HIV-1 disease progression and drug resistance mutation among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia.","The study was conducted among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy in Ethiopia, as mentioned in the title and throughout the paper. All the participants were from Ethiopia."
37626789,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017-2019,A longitudinal study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children (<15 years old) recruited between 2017 and 2019 at 40 antiretroviral treatment delivery sites in Ethiopia.,"The paper states that the study was conducted among 551 HIV-positive children recruited between 2017 and 2019, indicating that the samples were obtained during this period."
37626789,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the *pol* gene region using in-house assay validated at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention, and the Stanford HIVDB v9.0 algorithm was used for identification of drug resistance mutations.",The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. The sequencing was performed directly on the patient samples.
37626789,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,pol,"For children with VF, HIV-1 genotyping and sequencing was performed for the *pol* gene region using in-house assay validated at the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention, and the Stanford HIVDB v9.0 algorithm was used for identification of drug resistance mutations.",The paper specifically mentions that sequencing was performed for the *pol* gene region of HIV-1.
37626789,10,What method was used for sequencing?,ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer,"In-house assay validated by the Chinese Center for Disease Prevention and Control (CCDC) was used for amplification and sequencing of the *pol* gene of HIV-1 using an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer [3]. The *pol* gene region of the HIV-1 gene was sequenced by an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer [21, 22].","The paper states that the *pol* gene of HIV-1 was sequenced using an ABI-3730 DNA genetic analyzer, which indicates the method used for sequencing."
37626789,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Whole blood was collected from children using two ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) coated test tubes of 3–5 mL each, and plasma was extracted using centrifugation at 2000 revolutions per minute (RPM).","The paper mentions that plasma was extracted from whole blood collected from children, indicating that plasma samples were used for sequencing."
37626789,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Moreover, viral load (VL) testing was conducted for children twice at 6-month intervals between March and September 2019 to determine virologic failure (VF). Then, children with VF (i.e., viral load ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were included for HIV drug resistance investigation, given those with VL < 1000 copies/mL were considered as susceptible and excluded from HIVDR testing [20].","The paper mentions that children with virologic failure (VF) were included for HIV drug resistance investigation, indicating that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37626789,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,A longitudinal study was conducted from 2017 to 2019 among HIV-infected children (<15 years old) on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART). It was part of a national cohort study on HIV-1 treatment failure and acquired drug resistance among people taking ART at 63 health facilities in the country [19].,The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a longitudinal study among children on first-line antiretroviral therapy.
37626789,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Out of the 63 health facilities and 13,649 patients in the initial study, 40 had pediatric ART service and all the children from these health facilities (n = 554) were included. HIV-infected children taking first-line ART for at least six months were included in the study.","The paper mentions that the study included HIV-infected children taking first-line ART for at least six months, indicating that these individuals had previously received ARV drugs."
37626789,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs","Moreover, those on d4T + 3TC + EFV had high-level resistance mutations against 3TC, AZT, TDF, EFV, and NVP. More than 80% were taking non-PI-based regimens; d4T + 3TC + NVP (35.9%), d4T + 3TC + EFV (30.3%), ZDV + 3TC + EFV (14.0%).",The paper mentions that children were on various NRTIs and NNRTIs before sequencing. Most of the children were receiving either NRTIs or NNRTIs.
37626789,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Zidovudine (AZT), Lamivudine (3TC), Nevirapine (NVP), Stavudine (D4T), Efavirenz (EFV), Abacavir (ABC), Tenofovir (TDF)","A majority of the children (80%) were taking one of the three HAARTs regimens, which were Zidovudine (AZT) + Lamivudine(3TC) +Nevirapine (NVP) (35.9%), Stavudine(D4T) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) (30.3%), and Zidovudine (AZT) + Lamivudine(3TC) + Efavirenz (EFV) (14.0%) (Table 1; Supplemental Table S1). Overall, 47.5% had at least a one-time HAART substitution history. Overall, 63% of the substitutions were to those on Abacavir (ABC) + Lamivudine (3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) followed by Abacavir (ABC) + Lamivudine (3TC) + Efavirenz (EFV) (21.2%), and Tenofovir (TDF) + Lamivudine (3TC) + Nevirapine (NVP) (16.0%).","The paper discusses HAART regimens that include Zidovudine (AZT), Lamivudine (3TC), Nevirapine (NVP), Stavudine (D4T), Efavirenz (EFV), Abacavir (ABC), Tenofovir (TDF), which indicates that these drugs were received by individuals in the study."
37632026,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We carried out a laboratory-based cross-sectional and analytical study from November 2011 to August 2019 with adults (≥15 years) who had failed first- and second-line ART, received for genotypic resistance testing (GRT) at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB) for Research on HIV and AIDS Prevention and Management, Yaoundé, Cameroon. Plasma samples were then separated from whole blood and stored between −20 °C to −80 °C when samples were not to be analysed immediately. Viral RNA was extracted using a commercially available protocol after an initial two-hour centrifugation of 1 mL of plasma at 14,000 rpm to improve viral RNA concentration and thus increase PCR sensitivity.","The paper mentions that sequences were derived from plasma samples of patients who had failed first- and second-line ART and had received genotypic resistance testing at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre in Cameroon. This indicates that the HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. Specifically, it states that 'Plasma samples were then separated from whole blood and stored between −20 °C to −80 °C when samples were not to be analysed immediately.' and 'Overall ADR was high (93.4% first-line; 92.9%-second-line). TDF, potentially active in 35.7% of participants after first-line and 45.1% after second-line, suggested sub-optimal TLD-efficacy in second-line (64.3%) and third-line (54.9%)' which indicates that samples were obtained from patient samples and then sequences were produced from these samples hence the paper reports sequences from patient samples."
37632026,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Increased HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) with antiretroviral therapy (ART) rollout may jeopardize therapeutic options, especially in this era of transition to fixed-dose tenofovir-lamivudine-dolutegravir (TLD). We studied acquired HIVDR (ADR) patterns and describe potentially active drugs after first- and second-line failure in resource-limited settings (RLS) like Cameroon. A laboratory-based study with 759 patients (≥15 years) experiencing virological failure was carried out at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB), Yaoundé, Cameroon. Socio-demographic, therapeutic and immunovirological data from patient records were analysed according to HIV-1 genotypic profiles. Median (IQR) ART-duration was 63 (50--308) months. Median CD4 and viremia were 153 (IQR:50--308) cells/mm ^3^ and 138,666 (IQR:28,979--533,066) copies/mL, respectively. Overall ADR was high (93.4% first-line; 92.9%-second-line). TDF, potentially active in 35.7% of participants after first-line and 45.1% after second-line, suggested sub-optimal TLD-efficacy in second-line (64.3%) and third-line (54.9%). All PI/r preserved high efficacy after first-line failure while only DRV/r preserved high-level efficacy (87.9%) after second-line failure. In this resource-limited setting (RLS), ADR is high in ART-failing patients. PI/r strategies remain potent backbones for second-line ART, while only DRV/r remains very potent despite second-line failure. Though TLD use would be preferable, blind use for second- and third-line regimens may be sub-optimal (functional monotherapy with dolutegravir) with high risk of further failure, thus suggesting strategies for selective ART switch to TLD in failing patients in RLS.","The paper focuses on acquired HIV drug resistance patterns in patients failing first- and second-line ART in Cameroon. It discusses the therapeutic efficacy of various drugs and the presence of drug resistance mutations but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results. The use of the Stanford HIVDR database for identifying drug resistance mutations is for clinical interpretation and not for reporting in vitro susceptibility data. The study aims to provide insights into the effectiveness of certain drugs in the presence of resistance mutations rather than their susceptibility in vitro, hence there is no in vitro data reported in the paper."
37632026,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Sequences generated for the purpose of this study were submitted to Genbank under the following accession numbers: MK580543-MK580618; JF273935-JF273965; JQ796152-JQ796169, MK867721-MK867755; and OQ985493-OQ985958.","The paper explicitly states that the sequences generated for the purpose of the study were submitted to GenBank under specific accession numbers. These accession numbers are provided in the text, indicating that the sequences are publicly available for reference and further research."
37632026,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"MK580543-MK580618, JF273935-JF273965, JQ796152-JQ796169, MK867721-MK867755, OQ985493-OQ985958","Sequences generated for the purpose of this study were submitted to Genbank under the following accession numbers: MK580543-MK580618; JF273935-JF273965; JQ796152-JQ796169, MK867721-MK867755; and OQ985493-OQ985958.",The paper provides a detailed list of GenBank accession numbers under which the sequences generated for the study have been submitted. These numbers are clearly listed in the methodology section discussing sequence interpretation and analysis.
37632026,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,759,"We retained a total of 759 participants with mean age (±SD) of 42 (±14) years. Majority of participants were female: 485/745 (63.9%). All 10 regions of the country were represented, with participants from the centre |n| = 436 (57.4%); northwest |n| = 86 (11.3%); and littoral |n| = 85/745 (11.2%) regions. Of the 759 participants, 575 (75.8%) were failing non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-based first-line ART, while 184 (24.2%) were failing protease inhibitor (PI/r)-based second-line (NB: The second-line failing patients had previously failed first-line regimens as recommended by national treatment guidelines).",The 'Results' section of the paper mentions that a total of 759 participants had their samples analyzed for HIV-1 genotypic resistance testing. This number represents the individuals from whom HIV sequences were obtained.
37632026,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Cameroon,"We carried out a laboratory-based cross-sectional and analytical study from November 2011 to August 2019 with adults (≥15 years) who had failed first- and second-line ART, received for genotypic resistance testing (GRT) at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB) for Research on HIV and AIDS Prevention and Management, Yaoundé, Cameroon. Our study was carried out at CIRCB, Yaoundé, Cameroon and included patients presenting for resistance testing from all ten regions of the country.","The paper specifies that the study was conducted at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre in Yaoundé, Cameroon, and included patients from all ten regions of the country. This makes it clear that the sequenced samples were obtained from Cameroon."
37632026,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011 to 2019,"We carried out a laboratory-based cross-sectional and analytical study from November 2011 to August 2019 with adults (≥15 years) who had failed first- and second-line ART, received for genotypic resistance testing (GRT) at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB) for Research on HIV and AIDS Prevention and Management, Yaoundé, Cameroon.",The Materials and Methods section of the paper mentions that the study was carried out from November 2011 to August 2019. This time frame indicates the years during which the sequenced samples were obtained.
37632026,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Using an in-house protocol and two-step polymerase chain reaction-PCR (RT-PCR and Nested-PCR), HIV-1 pol-gene (protease and reverse-transcriptase regions) was then amplified (~1600 base pairs). Using agarose gel electrophoresis, the effectiveness and integrity of PCR products was confirmed, following which the amplicons were purified. Purified products were submitted to a sequencing reaction.","The paper describes the use of two-step polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR and Nested-PCR) for amplifying the HIV-1 pol-gene regions before sequencing, which suggests that samples were not cloned prior to sequencing."
37632026,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,HIV-1 pol-gene (protease and reverse-transcriptase regions),"Using an in-house protocol and two-step polymerase chain reaction-PCR (RT-PCR and Nested-PCR), HIV-1 pol-gene (protease and reverse-transcriptase regions) was then amplified (~1600 base pairs). Using agarose gel electrophoresis, the effectiveness and integrity of PCR products was confirmed, following which the amplicons were purified. Purified products were submitted to a sequencing reaction.","The paper specifies that the HIV-1 pol-gene, which includes the protease and reverse-transcriptase regions, was amplified and sequenced. This is mentioned in the methodology section detailing the sequencing process."
37632026,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Applied Biosystems 3500 genetic analyzer,"Sequencing was performed using an Applied Biosystems 3500 genetic analyzer (HITACHI, Tokyo, Japan) and sequence assembly and editing with SeqScape v2.7 (Applied Biosystems) or Recall v2.28.",The methodology section of the paper describes the use of an Applied Biosystems 3500 genetic analyzer for sequencing. This detail is provided in the context of the sequencing procedure.
37632026,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"First, 10 mL of blood was drawn from treatment-failing patients who presented for GRT at CIRCB. Plasma samples were then separated from whole blood and stored between −20 °C to −80 °C when samples were not to be analysed immediately. Viral RNA was extracted using a commercially available protocol after an initial two-hour centrifugation of 1 mL of plasma at 14,000 rpm to improve viral RNA concentration and thus increase PCR sensitivity. Following this step, 140 µL of plasma was retained for HIV-1 RNA extraction following the QIAGEN protocol (QIAamp® DNA Minikit; QIAGEN, Courtaboeuf, France).","The methodology section describes that plasma samples were used for extracting viral RNA, which was then sequenced. This indicates that plasma was the clinical specimen sequenced in the study."
37632026,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"A laboratory-based study with 759 patients (≥15 years) experiencing virological failure was carried out at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB), Yaoundé, Cameroon. We carried out a laboratory-based cross-sectional and analytical study from November 2011 to August 2019 with adults (≥15 years) who had failed first- and second-line ART, received for genotypic resistance testing (GRT) at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB) for Research on HIV and AIDS Prevention and Management, Yaoundé, Cameroon.","The paper mentions that the study included patients who were experiencing virological failure, which means that the sequences were obtained from individuals who had failed to achieve viral suppression on their treatment regimens."
37632026,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We carried out a laboratory-based cross-sectional and analytical study from November 2011 to August 2019 with adults (≥15 years) who had failed first- and second-line ART, received for genotypic resistance testing (GRT) at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB) for Research on HIV and AIDS Prevention and Management, Yaoundé, Cameroon. A laboratory-based study with 759 patients (≥15 years) experiencing virological failure was carried out at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB), Yaoundé, Cameroon.","The paper describes a laboratory-based cross-sectional and analytical study, not a clinical trial. The participants were patients presenting for genotypic resistance testing at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre, indicating that they were not part of a controlled clinical trial."
37632026,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We carried out a laboratory-based cross-sectional and analytical study from November 2011 to August 2019 with adults (≥15 years) who had failed first- and second-line ART, received for genotypic resistance testing (GRT) at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre (CIRCB) for Research on HIV and AIDS Prevention and Management, Yaoundé, Cameroon. Of the 759 participants, 575 (75.8%) were failing non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-based first-line ART, while 184 (24.2%) were failing protease inhibitor (PI/r)-based second-line (NB: The second-line failing patients had previously failed first-line regimens as recommended by national treatment guidelines).","The paper mentions that the participants had failed first- and second-line ART, indicating that they had previously received antiretroviral (ARV) drugs before the samples were obtained for sequencing."
37632026,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI/r","Of the 759 participants, 575 (75.8%) were failing non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-based first-line ART, while 184 (24.2%) were failing protease inhibitor (PI/r)-based second-line. Using an estimated prevalence for HIVDR of 94%, as described by Kouamou et al. [18], and a 5% error margin at 95% confidence interval (CI) and taking into account our eight-year period, we obtained a minimal sample size of 696 participants. Participants were recruited following consecutive and exhaustive sampling. 3.4. Genetic Diversity We observed a total of 36 clades. The major viral clade was CRF02_AG, making up 60.3%, (56.8--63.8%) of the viral subtypes, followed by A1 (6.1%; 5.6--8.0%) then G (5.7%; 4.2--7.5%). Pure subtypes made up 24.2% of the viral subclades, while recombinant subtypes made up 75.8%. We did not find any association between HIV-1 viral clade and CD4, viral load or HIVDR occurrence (Table 2). Thus, rate of resistance, viral load and CD4 count are not dependent on the clade/subtype of HIV-1; rather they are linked to the clinical status and the therapeutic regimens used.","The paper lists the classes of antiretroviral drugs that the participants had been exposed to prior to sample sequencing. Specifically, it mentions that 575 participants were failing NNRTI-based first-line ART and 184 were failing PI/r-based second-line ART."
37632026,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir, Zidovudine, Lamivudine/Emtricitabine, Abacavir, Efavirenz, Nevirapine, Atazanavir, Lopinavir, Darunavir","Of the 759 participants, 575 (75.8%) were failing non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-based first-line ART, while 184 (24.2%) were failing protease inhibitor (PI/r)-based second-line (NB: The second-line failing patients had previously failed first-line regimens as recommended by national treatment guidelines). As concerns potentially active regimes after first-line failure, AZT preserved efficacy in 46% of patients, TDF in 35.7% and ABC in 18.5%. In this era of transition to TLD, this highlights the risk of sub-optimal therapy in about 65% of patients, given the poor efficacy of TDF (DTG functional monotherapy). Though many studies have shown good efficacy of TLD despite NRTI resistance, clinical monitoring of this group of patients is crucial in order to evaluate the long-term efficacy of such regimens and also permit opportune detection of failure. 3.5.1. Potential Efficacy of Commonly Used Antiretroviral Drugs after First- and Second-Line Failure We considered effective drugs as those having a susceptibility score of <30 according to the Stanford HIVDR database. After first-line failure, irrespective of NRTI exposure, zidovudine (AZT) was potentially active in 46.1% (95% CI: 42.1--50.2%) of participants; tenofovir (TDF) in 35.7% (95% CI: 31.9--39.7%); abacavir (ABC) in 18.5% (95% CI: 15.6--21.9%); and lamivudine/emtricitabine (3TC/FTC) in 8.7% (95% CI: 10.5--21.5%). As to NNRTI, efavirenz (EFV) was potentially active in 13.2% of participants (95% CI; 10.7--16.2%) and nevirapine (NVP) in 7.6%, (95% CI; 5.7--10.1%) of participants. Figure 2 Potential efficacy of commonly used antiretroviral drugs with respect to therapeutic lines. The figure shows the percentage of participants (y axis) in whom the listed antiretroviral drugs (x axis) appeared effective after first-line (left) and second-line (right) failure. Note the high levels of effective DRV/r even after second-line failure. AZT: zidovudine; TDF: tenofovir; 3TC: lamivudine; ABC: abacavir; EFV: efavirenz; NVP: nevirapine; ATV/r: atazanavir; LPV/r: lopinavir; DRV/r: darunavir. After second-line failure, AZT appeared potentially active in 35.2% (95% CI: 28.3--42.6%) of participants; tenofovir (TDF) in 45.1% (95% CI: 37.7--52.6%); ABC in 22.5% (95% CI: 16.7--29.3%); and 3TC/FTC in 15.4% (95% CI: 10.5--21.5%). Darunavir (DRV/r) appeared potentially active in 86.7% (84.8--94.8%) of participants, unlike atazanavir (ATV/r) and lopinavir (LPV/r) which appeared potentially active in 61.0% (95% CI 63.5--68.1%).","The paper provides a detailed list of the specific antiretroviral drugs that the participants had been exposed to prior to sample sequencing. This list includes various NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PI/r drugs that were part of the first and second-line ART regimens."
37632071,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The paper focuses on a retrospective cohort study of experienced HIV-1 patients who switched to a drug combination of BIC/FTC/TAF, describing their virological outcomes over a 24-month follow-up period. It does not mention obtaining or analyzing HIV sequences from patient samples.",The content of the paper does not include any mention of HIV sequencing from patient samples. It primarily discusses the clinical outcomes related to the switch to a new antiretroviral regimen and the factors influencing virological success.
37632071,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data or experiments. It focuses on the clinical outcomes of a specific treatment regimen in patients.,"The study is centered around the clinical efficacy and outcomes of a specific antiretroviral regimen, not on in vitro susceptibilities to drugs."
37632071,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The Data Availability Statement mentions that raw data on demographics and clinical status of participants are protected and not available due to data privacy laws. The processed data are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request, but this does not imply that any sequences were made available.",The paper does not mention any sequence data being made publicly available. It only states that processed data can be requested.
37632071,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers or mention the submission of sequences to GenBank.,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers as it does not report on any sequencing data.
37632071,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,None,"The paper does not report any individuals having samples obtained for HIV sequencing, as it is focused on clinical outcomes.","The study mentioned in the paper did not involve obtaining samples for sequencing, it included 197 patients."
37632071,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,The paper does not mention any sequenced samples or their countries of origin. It only states that the study was conducted in Italy.,The study was conducted in Italy but does not mention any samples being sequenced.
37632071,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,The paper does not provide any information regarding the years from which sequenced samples were obtained as it does not involve sequencing.,"The study focuses on the years of treatment and follow-up, not on sample collection for sequencing."
37632071,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing as it does not involve sequencing.,"The study does not involve any cloning of samples, as sequencing was not performed."
37632071,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,None,The paper does not report any sequenced HIV genes as it does not involve sequencing.,The paper does not mention any sequenced genes as it focuses on treatment outcomes.
37632071,10,What method was used for sequencing?,None,The paper does not mention any method used for sequencing as it does not involve sequencing.,The study does not mention any sequencing methods as it did not perform sequencing.
37632071,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,None,The paper does not report any type of samples that were sequenced as it does not involve sequencing.,The paper does not mention any clinical specimens that were sequenced.
37632071,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure, as it does not involve sequencing.",The study focused on patients who were successful on ART and switched to a new regimen.
37632071,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,The study is a retrospective cohort study and does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.,"The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial, it was a retrospective cohort study."
37632071,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,The paper focuses on patients who switched to a new ARV regimen and does not mention reporting HIV sequences.,The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.
37632071,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, nor does it provide information on drug classes received before sequencing.","The paper does not report on the drug classes received prior to sequencing, as it does not involve sequencing."
37632071,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs, nor does it provide information on specific drugs received before sequencing.","The paper does not report on the drugs received prior to sequencing, as it does not involve sequencing."
37649807,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not mention any sequencing of HIV samples from patients. It focuses on longitudinal viral load data and virologic outcomes among people living with HIV on antiretroviral therapy in Kenya.
37649807,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not discuss any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It primarily focuses on viral load results and outcomes related to low-level viremia and virologic failure.
37649807,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences. It does not involve sequencing or making any data publicly available.
37649807,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers as it does not involve sequencing of HIV isolates.
37649807,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not report any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing as it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not mention any sequenced samples or countries from which they were obtained because it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,"We calculated rates of virologic suppression (≤50 copies/mL), LLV (51--999 copies/mL), virologic non-suppression (≥1000 copies/mL), and virologic failure (≥2 consecutive virologic non-suppression results) among PLHIV aged 15 years and older who received at least 24 weeks of ART during 2015–2021. We analyzed risk for virologic non-suppression and virologic failure using time-dependent models (each viral load (VL) <1000 copies/mL used to predict the next VL).; We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021.",The paper does not mention any sequenced samples or the years from which they were obtained because it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.; Study design and participants We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes. We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021.,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing as it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,None,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not report any sequenced HIV genes because it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,10,What method was used for sequencing?,None,We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes.; We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes.,The paper does not mention any sequencing methods as it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,None,We used retrospective longitudinal VL data from the National AIDs and STIs Control Programme (NASCOP) VL database of PLHIV on ART to assess LLV prevalence and subsequent virologic outcomes. We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021.,The paper does not report any type of sequenced samples because it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We analyzed retrospective longitudinal data from people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Kenya to understand LLV prevalence and virologic outcomes. We calculated rates of virologic suppression (≤50 copies/mL), LLV (51--999 copies/mL), virologic non-suppression (≥1000 copies/mL), and virologic failure (≥2 consecutive virologic non-suppression results) among PLHIV aged 15 years and older who received at least 24 weeks of ART during 2015--2021. We analyzed risk for virologic non-suppression and virologic failure using time-dependent models (each viral load (VL) <1000 copies/mL used to predict the next VL).",The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure as it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021. No specific funding was received for the analysis. HIV program support was provided by the President’s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) through the CDC.,The paper does not mention that the patients were in a clinical trial. It discusses longitudinal data from a national VL database.
37649807,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021. No specific funding was received for the analysis. HIV program support was provided by the President’s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) through the CDC.,The paper does not report any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs as it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021. No specific funding was received for the analysis. HIV program support was provided by the President’s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) through the CDC. Approximately a quarter of patients experienced LLV and had increased risk of virologic non-suppression and failure. Drug regimens received during 2015--2021 included 53.5% on tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/lamivudine/emtricitabine/efavirenz (TDF/3TC/FTC/EFV), 12.2% on TDF/3TC/FTC/raltegravir (RAL), 19.2% on TDF/3TC/FTC/DTG, 8.8% on TDF/3TC/FTC/EVG/COBI, and 6.0% on TDF/3TC/FTC/DTG (Fig. 6). ART, antiretroviral therapy",The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs as it does not involve sequencing.
37649807,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We included PLHIV ≥15 years of age on standard first- or second-line ART for ≥24 weeks who had only plasma VL results between January 2015 and December 2021. No specific funding was received for the analysis. HIV program support was provided by the President’s Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) through the CDC. Approximately a quarter of patients experienced LLV and had increased risk of virologic non-suppression and failure. Drug regimens received during 2015--2021 included 53.5% on tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/lamivudine/emtricitabine/efavirenz (TDF/3TC/FTC/EFV), 12.2% on TDF/3TC/FTC/raltegravir (RAL), 19.2% on TDF/3TC/FTC/DTG, 8.8% on TDF/3TC/FTC/EVG/COBI, and 6.0% on TDF/3TC/FTC/DTG (Fig. 6). ART, antiretroviral therapy",The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs as it does not involve sequencing.
37662576,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.","The paper discusses HIV-1 protease dynamics, drug binding, and resistance but does not mention sequencing HIV samples from patients or reporting patient-derived sequences."
37662576,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.","The paper focuses on molecular dynamics and structural aspects of the HIV-1 protease and its interactions with drugs, but it does not provide any data on in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results."
37662576,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.","The paper does not mention any sequences being made publicly available, nor does it provide GenBank accession numbers for any sequences."
37662576,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers or specific details regarding sequenced HIV isolates.
37662576,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.",The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing.
37662576,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.",The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained.
37662576,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.",The paper does not provide any information regarding the years from which sequenced samples were obtained.
37662576,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Molecular dynamic simulation protocols. For each MD comparison (monomer versus dimer, WT versus mutant protease; protease bound and unbound to drug), large replicate sets of accelerated MD simulations were performed. MD simulation protocol was followed as previously described, with slight modifications (11, 12, 17-19). In brief, for each MD comparison, large replicate sets of accelerated MD simulation were prepared and then conducted using the particle mesh Ewald method implemented on A100 and V100 NVIDIA graphical processor units by pmemd.cuda running Amber20 (16, 20-23). The MD simulations were on a high-performance computing cluster hosted by Rochester Institute of Technology (Rochester, NY) (24). All comparative MD analysis via our Detecting Relative Outlier Impacts in Dynamic Simulation 4.0 (DROIDS 4.0) was based upon 100 replicated sets of 1-ns accelerated MD runs (i.e., 100×1 ns MD run in each comparative state, e.g., monomer versus dimer, WT versus mutant, protease bound to drug versus protease unbound to drug).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It Discusses Molecular dynamic simulation protocols.
37662576,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not reported,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.",The paper focuses on the HIV-1 protease but does not mention sequencing other HIV genes.
37662576,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not reported,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.",The paper does not mention any sequencing methods.
37662576,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not reported,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies.",The paper does not mention any type of samples that were sequenced.
37662576,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The HIV-1 protease is one of several common key targets of combination drug therapies for human immunodeficiency virus infection and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. During the progression of the disease, some individual patients acquire drug resistance due to mutational hotspots on the viral proteins targeted by combination drug therapies. To understand the binding interaction of darunavir with HIV-1 protease, we did MD simulations with HIV-1 protease bound and unbound to darunavir. Comparative analyses of protein dynamic simulations of darunavir-bound WT HIV-1 protease (PDB: 6DGX) and an unbound apo form of WT HIV-1 protease (PDB: 2PC0) show strong dampening of the key functional sight Ile50 on both chains A and B of the darunavir-bound dimer (Fig. 3) with more pronounced general dampening of atom fluctuation on chain A. We suspect this asymmetry in our result may be due to asymmetry in the protease structure caused by the asymmetry of the darunavir structure itself. We color-mapped the fluctuation of the atom motion. We observed dampening of the atom motion in the tightly closed flap region of the protease when darunavir is bound to HIV-1 protease, indicating that the flap region plays a role in the binding of darunavir (Fig. 3A–C). Other studies have also found that binding of darunavir alters the flap region, with some studies finding that darunavir binding exhibits a unique curling confirmation of the flap region, whereas others observing extended flap conformations (43, 44). We also calculated significance tests and p-values for these site-wise differences. We observed significant atomic fluctuation motion in the key functional region near Ile50 in both chains A and B as well as near the catalytic site on chain B (Fig. 3D and E).",The paper mentions HIV-1 protease evolution and drug resistance but does not mention virological failure of participants.
