37775947,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA 	extgreater1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS ( illumina MiSeq). Baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009--2013.,The paper mentions that stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analyzed using NGS (illumina MiSeq) and that baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants. This indicates that the study does report HIV sequences from patient samples.
37775947,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods for sample preparation, amplification of viral RNA and NGS, identification of DRM by means of VirVarSeq, and determination of HIV subtype have been described elsewhere [25, 26]. In this analysis, we considered all mutations used by the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVDB) HIVdb algorithm v8.6 to provide genotypic test result interpretation.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on the genotypic susceptibility score (GSS) using data from the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVDB) but does not provide in vitro susceptibility results for specific drugs against the virus studied.. The methods and results sections do not include any mention of performing in vitro drug susceptibility assays or providing susceptibility data for the drugs tested against HIV isolates.. The focus is on the analysis of NGS data and the association between pre-existing drug resistance and treatment failure, not on in vitro susceptibility testing or results.. Therefore, based on the content of the paper, it can be concluded that in vitro drug susceptibility data is not reported.. It focuses on genotypic data and clinical outcomes rather than in vitro efficacy of drugs against the isolates.. There is no indication of laboratory-derived susceptibility data for the drugs used in the treatment regimens of the study participants.. The analysis is based on genotypic resistance data and clinical outcomes rather than in vitro susceptibility testing."
37775947,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any information regarding whether the sequences obtained were made publicly available, such as in GenBank or any other sequence database. There is no mention of the deposition of sequence data in public repositories after the study was completed.. Without explicit mention of this process, it's safe to conclude that the paper does not report public availability of the sequences obtained.. Therefore, the answer to this question is no. The paper does not detail the process or repository where the sequences might be deposited, if at all."
37775947,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no information provided regarding the deposition of the sequences in GenBank or any other database that would typically assign accession numbers to sequenced data. Without this information, it is impossible to provide any accession numbers associated with the sequences reported in the paper. Therefore, the answer to this question is not reported. The absence of accession numbers in the methods or results sections indicates that this information was not provided."
37775947,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1380,"Baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009--2013. Overall, of the 1819 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth, 1380 (76%) who started ART with three or more drugs were included in this analysis.","The paper states that baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009--2013. This number is confirmed in the results section, which mentions that 1380 participants with NGS data at the ≥200 read depth were included in the analysis. This indicates that samples were obtained from 1380 individuals for HIV sequencing."
37775947,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Countries were not reported,No evidence,"The paper mentions that the START trial, which the study is based on, conducted by the International Network for Strategic Initiatives in Global HIV Trials, enrolled ART-naïve participants with HIV between April 2009 and December 2013. But it is not specifically mention the country. Without this explicit information, it cannot be determined from which countries the sequenced samples were obtained."
37775947,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2009-2013,"Baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009--2013. The START trial, conducted by the International Network for Strategic Initiatives in Global HIV Trials, enrolled ART-naïve participants with HIV between April 2009 and December 2013.","The paper states that baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009--2013. The START trial, which is the basis for this analysis, enrolled participants between April 2009 and December 2013. This indicates that the sequenced samples were obtained during this period."
37775947,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Methods for sample preparation, amplification of viral RNA and NGS, identification of DRM by means of VirVarSeq, and determination of HIV subtype have been described elsewhere [25, 26]. Sequence reads (FASTQ files) were analyzed with VirVarSeq version 20140929, which calls variants at the codon level [27].",The paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. The methods described involve direct NGS of plasma samples without any indication of cloning the samples before sequencing. This suggests that samples were not cloned prior to sequencing.
37775947,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, and IN","From the output, we extracted amino acid frequencies in the *pol* gene from amino acid position 1--935 where positions 1--99 encode protease protein, positions 100--659 encode reverse transcriptase protein, and positions 660--935 partially encode integrase protein.","The paper mentions that amino acid frequencies in the *pol* gene were extracted from the sequencing data. Specifically, it mentions positions that encode protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) proteins. This indicates that these genes were sequenced."
37775947,10,What method was used for sequencing?,NGS (illumina MiSeq),"Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). Methods for sample preparation, amplification of viral RNA and NGS, identification of DRM by means of VirVarSeq, and determination of HIV subtype have been described elsewhere [25, 26].",The paper mentions that stored plasma from participants was analyzed using NGS (illumina MiSeq). This is a specific method for sequencing that is mentioned in the methods section of the paper.
37775947,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). A plasma sample, taken within 60 days before enrolment was obtained from all participants who provided consent for stored specimens.",The paper specifies that plasma samples from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analyzed. This indicates that plasma was the clinical specimen sequenced.
37775947,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,Stored plasma from participants with entry HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL were analysed using NGS (llumina MiSeq). Baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants enrolled over 2009--2013.,"The paper mentions that baseline NGS data were available for 1380 antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve participants. It does not mention obtaining sequences from individuals with virological failure. The participants were ART-naïve, and the focus was on pre-existing drug resistance mutations, not on cases of virological failure."
37775947,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The START trial, conducted by the International Network for Strategic Initiatives in Global HIV Trials, enrolled ART-naïve participants with HIV between April 2009 and December 2013.","The paper describes  the study as part of the START trial, which is a clinical trial involving participants with HIV. This indicates that the patients in the study were part of a clinical trial."
