37817087,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Protease (PR) and reverse-transcriptase (RT) sequences were obtained from 4559 (39.6%) cases, and PR, RT and integrase (IN) sequences were obtained from 3097 (26.9%) cases.","The paper reports HIV sequences obtained from patient samples, specifically mentioning the number of cases from which sequences were derived."
37817087,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The transmission of resistant HIV variants jeopardizes the effective use of antiretrovirals for therapy and prophylaxis. Molecular surveillance of new HIV diagnoses with a focus on prevalence and type of resistance associated mutations and the subtype of circulating viruses is mandatory. From 2017 to 2020, 11,527 new HIV diagnoses were reported in Germany to the Robert Koch Institute (RKI). Protease (PR) and reverse-transcriptase (RT) sequences were obtained from 4559 (39.6%) cases, and PR, RT and integrase (IN) sequences were obtained from 3097 (26.9%) cases. The sequences were analyzed with data from the national HIV reports.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The focus is on the sequencing and analysis of patient samples for resistance mutations and subtyping.
37817087,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,All datasets including sequences are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.,"The paper states that datasets, including sequences, are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request, but there is no mention of public availability."
