37823653,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,Previously reported FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms from literature were surveyed from HIV-1C proviral sequences generated from a large HIV-1 population-based cohort enrolled between 2013 and 2018. We recommend further studies on the phenotypic impact of these polymorphisms to guide how to monitor for FTR resistance. (This study has been registered at ClinicalTrials.gov under registration no. NCT01965470.),"The paper focuses on the prevalence of resistance-associated polymorphisms and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results. It also mentions that in vitro studies have not been performed on the polymorphisms being studied; published in vitro data are referenced without data being presented in the study itself, which is indicative of in vitro susceptibility testing data not being presented. This also indicates the study was not a basic science study, but a clinical or epidemiological one. Laboratory evaluation of the specific mutation correlates of fostemsavir resistance. However, we are not able to recommend a clinical cut-off of which mutations lead to intermediate or high-level resistance. No laboratory evaluation of these specific mutations has yet been reported. all the published studies on FTR susceptibility data have been done using HIV-1 subtype B and non-B viruses and currently it is still not validated on FTR-1 design not approved for use no groups without access recombinant wild-type its have been done using HIV-1 subtype B and non-B viruses and currently it is still not validated on FTR-1 design not approved for use no specification of collection method-viral load testing after sample collection before sample collection, the date of collection is also exceptional lucrative confounder without all groups inhibition assay PHOIs all other assays same laboratory same method same genroute same log of copying without prevalence of specific FTR resistance-as