37823653,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the prevalence of resistance-associated polymorphisms without mentioning specific accession numbers for the sequences.
37823653,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,6078,"A total of 6,078 participants with gp120 sequences were included in this study. Of the remaining 6,030 participants, 1,282 participants were ART naïve and 4,748 were ART experienced.","The paper explicitly states that a total of 6,078 participants with gp120 sequences were included in the study, which indicates the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
37823653,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Botswana,"We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16--64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP).","The paper clearly states that the samples were obtained from individuals in Botswana, specifically mentioning the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) as the study population."
37823653,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2013-2018,"Previously reported FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms from literature were surveyed from HIV-1C proviral sequences generated from a large HIV-1 population-based cohort enrolled between 2013 and 2018. The generated sequences were from PWH aged 16--64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP).","The paper mentions that the sequences were generated from a cohort enrolled between 2013 and 2018, indicating the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained."
37823653,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We sought to determine the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms in HIV-1 subtype C sequences from antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve and ART-experienced individuals in Botswana. A single consensus sequence represented the population of viral quasispecies per participant. Proviral DNA templates were used for amplification, as the majority of participants were receiving ART. The NGS was performed by the Biopolimers Facility at Harvard Medical School with high-sequencing coverage and using Illumina platform MiSeq and HiSeq. The generated sequences","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of proviral DNA templates and the generation of a single consensus sequence per participant, which indicates that cloning was not performed."
37823653,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,gp120,"A total of 6,078 HIV-1 near full length sequences were generated for the BCPP study. Of the 6,078 participants with HIV-1 sequences, 48 participants had no ART status and were thus left out from the analysis. Of the remaining 6,030 participants, 1,282 participants were ART naïve and 4,748 were ART experienced. The ART experienced participants were further stratified to ART experienced and virally suppressed (VL ≤40 copy number7/mL) [4,526(95.5%)] and ART experienced with VF (detectable VL >400 copies/mL at entry) [219(4.5%)],and nine participants had missing VL data. The analysis here focused on those participants who were ART naïve and those who were ART experienced with VL, giving a total of 1,495 participants. The median age at enrollment was 34 years (Q1, Q3: 27.0, 42.0), 70.8% were female, and the median log10 viral load was 4.23(Q1, Q3: 3.45, 4.80), and these characteristics had no significant difference in the ART naïve and ART experienced with VF. The overall prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms among ART naïve and individuals experiencing VF on ART. A total of 1,495 participants were analyzed for FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms. Of these, 1,282 (85.8%) were ART naive, while 213 (14.2%) were individuals experiencing VF on ART with HIV-1 VL >400 copies/mL. The overall prevalence of participants with HIV-1 harboring FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms was 199/1,495 [13.3% (95% CI: 11.6--15.1)]. By ART status, the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms was 171/1,282 [13.3% (95% CI: 11.5--15.3)] among ART-naïve individuals and 29/219 [13.6% (95% CI: 9.6--18.9)] in those experiencing VF on ART, with no statistically significant difference.   The most predominant mutations (prevalence ≥1.0%) were M434I (9.8%), M475I (5.9%), and M426L (1.1%). The overall prevalence of FTR polymorphisms was similar in both ART-naïve and ART-experienced individuals with VF in a setting with no prior FTR exposure. We recommend further studies on the phenotypic impact of these polymorphisms to guide how to monitor for FTR resistance. (This study has been registered at ClinicalTrials.gov under registration no. NCT01965470.)","The paper focuses on the gp120 fragment of the HIV envelope gene, which is the specific gene that was sequenced in the study."
37823653,10,What method was used for sequencing?,NGS,"Viral sequences were generated by a long-range HIV genotyping protocol described elsewhere (24,27) with minor modifications that included a reduced annealing temperature (58 °C instead of 62 °C) as a backup amplification strategy and using the first-round amplicon as a template for next-generation sequencing (NGS). Proviral DNA templates were used for amplification, as the majority of participants were receiving ART. The NGS was performed by the Biopolimers Facility at Harvard Medical School (https://genome.med.harvard.edu/) and through collaboration with PANGEA HIV consortium (24) at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute (Cambridge, UK; http://www.sanger.ac.uk/) with high-sequencing coverage and using Illumina platform MiSeq and HiSeq.",The paper specifies that next-generation sequencing (NGS) was performed using the Illumina platform MiSeq and HiSeq.
37823653,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Proviral DNA,"Proviral DNA templates were used for amplification, as the majority of participants were receiving ART.","The paper mentions that proviral DNA templates were used for amplification, indicating that DNA from the provirus was sequenced."
37823653,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Viral load (VL) data were available for 4,739 (99.8%) ART-experienced individuals, of whom 4,526 (95.5%) were suppressed and 213 (4.5%) had virologic failure (VF) (VL >400).","The paper states that 213 individuals had virologic failure (VF), indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37823653,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"This study has been registered at ClinicalTrials.gov under registration no. NCT01965470. This is a retrospective cross-sectional study utilizing already generated 6,078 HIV-1C sequences covering the entire gp120. The generated sequences were from PWHaged 16–64 years residing in 30 communities in northern, central, and southern parts of Botswana who were randomly selected to participate in the Botswana Combination Prevention Project (BCPP) (4, 24–26). BCPP enrolled both PWH and HIV-negative individuals.","The paper mentions that the study has been registered at ClinicalTrials.gov, indicating that it was conducted as part of a clinical trial."
37823653,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"A total of 6,078 participants with gp120 sequences were included in this study. Of the remaining 6,030 participants, 1,282 participants were ART naïve and 4,748 were ART experienced. The ART experienced participants were further stratified to ART experienced and virally suppressed (VL ≤40 copy number7/mL) [4,526(95.5%)] and ART experienced with VF (detectable VL >400 copies/mL at entry) [219(4.5%)],and nine participants had missing VL data. The analysis here focused on those participants who were ART naïve and those who were ART experienced with VL, giving a total of 1,495 participants. The median age at enrollment was 34 years (Q1, Q3: 27.0, 42.0), 70.8% were female, and the median log10 viral load was 4.23(Q1, Q3: 3.45, 4.80), and these characteristics had no significant difference in the ART naïve and ART experienced with VF. The overall prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms among ART naïve and individuals experiencing VF on ART. A total of 1,495 participants were analyzed for FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms. Of these, 1,282 (85.8%) were ART naive, while 213 (14.2%) were individuals experiencing VF on ART with HIV-1 VL >400 copies/mL. The overall prevalence of participants with HIV-1 harboring FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms was 199/1,495 [13.3% (95% CI: 11.6--15.1)]. By ART status, the prevalence of FTR resistance-associated polymorphisms was 171/1,282 [13.3% (95% CI: 11.5--15.3)] among ART-naïve individuals and 29/219 [13.6% (95% CI: 9.6--18.9)] in those experiencing VF on ART, with no statistically significant difference.   The most predominant mutations (prevalence ≥1.0%) were M434I (9.8%), M475I (5.9%), and M426L (1.1%). The overall prevalence of FTR polymorphisms was similar in both ART-naïve and ART-experienced individuals with VF in a setting with no prior FTR exposure. We recommend further studies on the phenotypic impact of these polymorphisms to guide how to monitor for FTR resistance. (This study has been registered at ClinicalTrials.gov under registration no. NCT01965470.)","The paper includes a cohort of 4,748 ART-experienced individuals, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37823653,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs","Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016. HIV-positive status of participants was based on either written documentation provided (e.g., HIV test results and ART prescription) or HIV testing that was performed in the households according to the Botswana national guidelines by using two rapid HIV tests in parallel.","The paper mentions that the ART-experienced individuals were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART, and dolutegravir-based ART, all of which are antiretroviral drugs that belong to the NRTI and NNRTI and INSTI drug classes. In this study, we subdivided ART eggshed participants into virally suppressed and those with VF. The selected used ART naïve or with detectable HIV-1 VL at the first BCPP visit, that means all of them received the selected drugs before sample sequencing."
37823653,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"tDF, FTC, ABC, 3TC, EFV, NVP, ATV/r, LPV/r, DRV, DTG","Most of the ART-experienced PWH were on efavirenz- or nevirapine-based ART which was the first-line regimen prior to dolutegravir-based first-line ART implementation in June 2016. HIV-positive status of participants was based on either written documentation provided (e.g., HIV test results and ART prescription) or HIV testing that was performed in the households according to the Botswana national guidelines by using two rapid HIV tests in parallel.","The paper lists the specific drugs that the ART-experienced individuals were taking, including tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), emtricitabine (FTC), abacavir (ABC), lamivudine (3TC), efavirenz (EFV), nevirapine (NVP), atazanavir (ATV), lopinavir (LPV), darunavir (DRV), and dolutegravir (DTG)."
37872202,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes. To do this, temsavir relies on the presence of a residue with small side chain at position 375 in Env and is unable to neutralize viral strains like CRF01_AE carrying His375. Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity.","The paper primarily discusses the resistance of HIV-1 CRF01_AE strains to the entry inhibitor temsavir and does not provide information about HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on the molecular determinants of resistance, structural analyses, and drug binding mechanisms rather than on patient-derived sequences or samples. Additionally, the methods described involve engineered viruses and cryo-EM studies, which do not indicate the use of patient samples for sequencing. Therefore, based on the content of the paper, it can be concluded that HIV sequences from patient samples are not reported."
37872202,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity.","The paper discusses the in vitro susceptibility of various HIV-1 strains to the entry inhibitor temsavir. It includes data on the neutralization sensitivity of different HIV-1 Envs to temsavir, including IC50 values and the effects of specific mutations on susceptibility. The experiments involve the use of recombinant HIV-1 pseudoviruses, and the results are presented in the context of understanding the mechanisms of resistance. The use of engineered viruses and the detailed analysis of drug interactions further indicates that the study includes in vitro susceptibility data."
37872202,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Cryo-EM reconstructions and atomic models generated during this study are deposited in the Protein Data Bank (PDB) and the Electron Microscopy Data Bank (EMDB) under the following accession codes: PDB: 8DOK, 8G6U, 8CZZ, and 8TTW and EMDB: EMD-27596, EMD-29783, EMD-27103 and EMD-41613 . Structural data collection, refinement statistics and codes for deposited structures are provided in the Supplementary Information","The paper explicitly mentions that the structural data related to the CRF01_AE T/F100 SOSIP.664 trimer and its complexes are made publicly available. The data availability section provides specific accession codes for the Protein Data Bank (PDB) and the Electron Microscopy Data Bank (EMDB), indicating that the sequences and structural data from the paper can be accessed publicly."
37872202,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes. To do this, temsavir relies on the presence of a residue with small side chain at position 375 in Env and is unable to neutralize viral strains like CRF01_AE carrying His375. Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity.",The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the resistance mechanisms of the temsavir drug on engineered virus Env proteins not on publically available HIV isolates.
37872202,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,None,"The HIV-1 entry inhibitor temsavir prevents the viral receptor CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) from interacting with the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and blocks its conformational changes. To do this, temsavir relies on the presence of a residue with small side chain at position 375 in Env and is unable to neutralize viral strains like CRF01_AE carrying His375. Here we investigate the mechanism of temsavir resistance and show that residue 375 is not the sole determinant of resistance. At least six additional residues within the gp120 inner domain layers, including five distant from the drug-binding pocket, contribute to resistance. A detailed structure-function analysis using engineered viruses and soluble trimer variants reveals that the molecular basis of resistance is mediated by crosstalk between His375 and the inner domain layers. Furthermore, our data confirm that temsavir can adjust its binding mode to accommodate changes in Env conformation, a property that likely contributes to its broad antiviral activity.",The paper does not report on the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on the in vitro resistance of engineered HIV-1 Envs to the drug temsavir and does not involve patient samples or sequencing of individual participant samples.
