37878637,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Sequencing of protease and reverse transcriptase regions was performed for participants experiencing virological failure; Of the 141 participants in VF enrolled, the male-female ratio was 1:1; mean age was 15 (±3) years; and median [IQR] duration on ART was 51 [46--60] months.","The paper describes a cohort study involving 311 participants, specifically adolescents living with perinatally acquired HIV (APHI) in Cameroon, receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART). Among these participants, sequencing of protease and reverse transcriptase regions was performed for those experiencing virological failure, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37878637,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods A cohort study was conducted from 2018–2020 among 311 APHI receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Cameroon. Sequencing of protease and reverse transcriptase regions was performed for participants experiencing virological failure, VF, (Plasma viral load, PVL ≥ 1000 RNA copies/ml). HIV-1 subtypes were inferred by phylogeny; immuno-virological responses were monitored at 3-time points (T1-T3). Cox regression modeling was used to estimate adjusted hazard ratios (aHRs) of progression to: CD4 < 250, and PVL > 5log10, adjusted for acquired drug resistance, gender, ART line, adherence, and duration on treatment; p < 0.05 was considered statistically significant.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing. It focuses on sequencing of specific HIV-1 genes to understand diversity and treatment outcomes in patients, rather than testing the effectiveness of drugs in a laboratory setting."
37878637,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any public databases or repositories where the sequences can be accessed. It provides a DOI link to figshare, but that does not seem like sequences are in public accessible format."
37878637,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,No evidence,No GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates are provided in the paper. The paper focuses on the study findings without mentioning specific accession numbers.
37878637,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,141,"Of the 141 participants in VF enrolled, the male-female ratio was 1:1; mean age was 15 (±3) years; and median [IQR] duration on ART was 51 [46--60] months; Of these, 141 participants with PVL ≥ 1000 RNA copies/mL, and whose samples were successfully sequenced were included in the current assessment.",The paper states that 141 participants with virological failure (VF) had their samples successfully sequenced for the study.
37878637,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Cameroon,"A cohort study was conducted from 2018–2020 among 311 APHI receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Cameroon; Concerning virological response, the median time for the participants to experience high viral load (PVL > 5 log10 RNA copies/mL) was 64.00 (95% CI: 41.74--86.26) months. Those with CRF02_AG infection had a shorter median time to this endpoint (60.00: 95% CI: 52.72--67.28 months) as compared to participants infected by non-CRF02_AG clades (91.00: 95% CI: 75.38--106.62 months). The observed differences in survival curves across subtypes were not statistically significant, with p = 0.251 ( Fig 3b ).","The study was conducted among participants at selected health facilities within the ""Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings"" (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon."
37878637,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018-2020,"A cohort study was conducted from 2018–2020 among 311 APHI receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Cameroon; This study provided insight into the evolving HIV-1 diversity, the genomic complexity of URF infections, the structural dynamics of emerging recombinant forms, and possible implications for antiretroviral treatment. Thus, underlining the necessity of performing full/near-full length next-generation sequencing to achieve accurate HIV surveillance, subtyping, and genotypic analyses.",The paper states that the cohort study was conducted from 2018 to 2020.
37878637,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Eight in-house primers (B, F, SEQ1, SEQ2, SEQ3, SEQ4, SEQ5, TAK3) that completely covered the protease and reverse-transcriptase regions in the pol gene fragment (~1600 base pairs) were used for sequencing. PCR products were sequenced using the Applied Biosystems 3500 genetic analyzer.","The paper describes the use of in-house primers and PCR products were sequenced using the Applied Biosystems 3500 genetic analyzer, indicating direct sequencing of PCR products without cloning."
37878637,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and reverse transcriptase,"Sequencing of protease and reverse transcriptase regions was performed for participants experiencing virological failure; Eight in-house primers (B, F, SEQ1, SEQ2, SEQ3, SEQ4, SEQ5, TAK3) that completely covered the protease and reverse-transcriptase regions in the pol gene fragment (~1600 base pairs) were used for sequencing.",The paper explicitly mentions that sequencing was performed on the protease and reverse transcriptase regions of the HIV-1 genome.
37878637,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV-1 RNA was extracted manually from 140 μL of plasma using the QIAGEN protocol (QIAamp® DNA Minikit; QIAGEN, Courtaboeuf, France). GRT was done when the viral load was > 1000 copies/mL using an in-house protocol[13]. Eight in-house primers (B, F, SEQ1, SEQ2, SEQ3, SEQ4, SEQ5, TAK3) that completely covered the protease and reverse-transcriptase regions in the pol gene fragment (~1600 base pairs) were used for sequencing. The latter were designed such that each zone of the fragment was covered by at least two primers. The primer sequences used were as follows:","The paper describes the use of PCR and sequencing methods consistent with Sanger sequencing, as well as specific primers and sequencing technology."
37878637,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Moreover, plasma aliquots (for PVL and HIV genotypic resistance testing, GRT) were obtained after centrifugation at 1600 rpm for 10 minutes and stored at −80°C for further analyses.; Taking into account international recommendations[12] for 3 time points, in our longitudinal study, we sought to assess HIV-1 subtype diversity and the influence of subtypes on immuno-virological response at 3 study time points (T1-T3) in 11 the study population between 2018 and 2020. This 9 journal of virus eradication 11:5-16 2022 12 study was part of the “Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings” (READY-study) aimed at evaluating HIVDR among HAART-adherent Nigerian APHI failing first- and second-line ARV drug regimens at 3 time. This longitudinal cohort study was carried out at 4 selected health facilities in 5 the Centre region of Cameroon: HIV management units for ART naïve APHI, and 2 referral hospitals; ) />",The papper mentioned that plasma aliquots were used for HIV genotypic resistance testing and sequencing.
37878637,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,Sequencing of protease and reverse transcriptase regions was performed for participants experiencing virological failure; Included in the current analysis were only participants failing first- and second-line ART with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥ 1000 RNA copies/ml (Table 1).,"The paper specifies that sequencing was performed for participants experiencing virological failure (VF), indicating that sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37878637,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"A cohort study was conducted from 2018--2020 among 311 adolescents living with perinatally acquired HIV (APHI) receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART) in one of the selected health facilities within the ""Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings"" (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon. Participants were recruited following consecutive, and exhaustive sampling. Follow-up was performed at enrollment (T1), at 6 months (T2), and at 12 months (T3).","The paper describes a cohort  the ready study not a clinical trial, the participants were adolescents with HIV receiving ART, and it focused on sequencing and evaluating outcomes rather than being part of a clinical trial."
37878637,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Included in the study were APHI; aged 10--19 years, receiving a standard reverse transcriptase inhibitor-based first- or ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor-based second-line ART regimen for at least 6 months, The major outcomes from the study were the viral genetic diversity among APHI, immune-virological responses stratified by subtype, the trends of virological response stratified by subtype, and the potential emerging resistant variants among these APHI failing treatment. Adequate immunological status was defined as absolute CD4 count ≥ 250 cells/mm3 and Immunological failure (IF) as < 250 CD4 cells/mm3[17]. PVL was classified as high viral load (PVL > 5log10 RNA copies/ml), and low viral load (PVL < 5 log10)[18]. Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was performed for these participants failing treatment. Concerning virological response, the median time for the participants to experience high viral load (PVL > 5 log10 RNA copies/mL) was 64.00 (95% CI: 41.74--86.26) months. Those with CRF02_AG infection had a shorter median time to this endpoint (60.00: 95% CI: 52.72--67.28 months) as compared to participants infected by non-CRF02_AG clades (91.00: 95% CI: 75.38--106.62 months). The observed differences in survival curves across subtypes were not statistically significant, with p = 0.251 (Fig 3b).","The paper mentions that participants were receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART) and that 141 of these participants were failing treatment the study, indicating that they had previously received ARV drugs."
