37914679,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA). Among the 76 patients who underwent second-generation sequencing, the success rate of amplification was 100%.","The paper mentions that HIV-1-infected patients had their samples sequenced, confirming that sequences were obtained from patient samples."
37914679,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Pre-existing low-frequency resistance mutations increase the risk of antiretroviral treatment failure in HIV-1 naïve patients. High-frequency resistance (typically ≥15–25% viral quasispecies) detected by conventional resistance assays has long been associated with failure of antiretroviral therapy (ART) to suppress viral replication, but the impact of pre-existing low-frequency resistance on virological response is controversial.","The content focuses on the detection of low-frequency resistance mutations rather than on in vitro drug susceptibility tests. No mention of in vitro assays or susceptibility testing is made in the excerpts provided. The focus is on the clinical outcomes of these mutations after ART initiation rather than their sensitivity to drugs in a lab setting. The study focuses on clinical failure and success of ART based on the presence of these mutations, which is more about the effects of prior resistance, not susceptibility testing protocols or results, therefore still yields no in vitro susceptibility results."
37914679,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No publicly available sequence.,"The content does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or public availability of sequences. There is no indication that the sequences were deposited in a public database like GenBank. The focus of the paper is on the clinical outcomes and the presence of mutations, rather than on the sharing of sequence data."
37914679,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No GenBank accession numbers provided,No specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates are mentioned in the content provided.,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or public availability of sequences. There is no indication that the sequences were deposited in a public database like GenBank.
37914679,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,76,"All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA).","The paper mentions that a total of 76 HIV-1-infected patients had their samples sequenced, the number mentioned in relation to number of sampled sequenced are consistent."
37914679,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study.","The paper states that the study was conducted at the Second Hospital of Nanjing in China, and the patients were attending this hospital."
37914679,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018 to 2020,"HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study. The study was designed to detect pre-existing low-frequency resistance mutations in HIV-1-infected patients using the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform to investigate their impact on virological response in HIV-1 naïve patients.","The paper mentions that the patients were enrolled between January 1, 2018, and December 31, 2020 . these dates are mentioned in relation to the study of sample collection."
37914679,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA). A total of 76 patients enrolled in this study had been tested for pre-existing resistance by conventional Sanger sequencing at baseline, with results suggesting that no resistance mutations were detected. Of the overall 76 included patients, CRF01-AE (40.8%, 31/76) was the predominant subtype, followed by subtypes B + C (26.3%, 20/76) and B (18.4%, 14/76). Both the control and failure groups were predominantly male (92.1% [35/38] vs. 97.4% [37/38]), and the predominant subtype was CRF01-AE (44.7% [17/38] vs.36.8% [14/38]). There was no significant difference in age, baseline CD4+ T-cell count, and baseline HIV-1 RNA between the two groups (P>0.05) [Supplementary Table 1, http://links.lww.com/CM9/B780].",The paper does not mention any clipping of samples. It mentions that the samples were analyzed via conventional sequencing in which no cloning take part.
37914679,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene,"Of the overall 76 included patients, CRF01-AE (40.8%, 31/76) was the predominant subtype, followed by subtypes B + C (26.3%, 20/76) and B (18.4%, 14/76). Both the control and failure groups were predominantly male (92.1% [35/38] vs. 97.4% [37/38]), and the predominant subtype was CRF01-AE (44.7% [17/38] vs.36.8% [14/38]). There was no significant difference in age, baseline CD4+ T-cell count, and baseline HIV-1 RNA between the two groups (P>0.05) [Supplementary Table 1, http://links.lww.com/CM9/B780].","The paper mentions the detection of low-frequency resistance mutations in the pol gene of HIV-1, although not explicitly mentioned in the question."
37914679,10,What method was used for sequencing?,"MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform, conventional Sanger sequencing","All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA). A total of 76 patients enrolled in this study had been tested for pre-existing resistance by conventional Sanger sequencing at baseline, with results suggesting that no resistance mutations were detected.",The paper mentions that the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform was used for sequencing the samples. it has been clearly mentioned in the paper.
37914679,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study. All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA).",The paper mentions that plasma samples were used for sequencing. the mention of plasma specimens we can teel the clinical specimens were collected.
37914679,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Thirty-eight patients who failed antiviral therapy were defined as the failure group according to the study definition criteria, and 38 successful virological responders were matched as the control group based on the baseline characteristics of the failure group (baseline CD4+ T-lymphocyte counts and viral loads, 1:1 propensity score matching method, carboplatin value 0.05) [Supplementary Figure 1, http://links.lww.com/CM9/B780].","The paper mentions that 38 patients who failed antiviral therapy were included in the study, which implies that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
37914679,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Inclusion criteria for patients in this study included prior receipt of antiretroviral therapy (ART) for HIV-1 infection and a baseline pre-treatment peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC) HIV-1 DNA reservoir 78 copies/10 cells. Prior to ART initiation, all patients presented with a CD4+ T-lymphocyte count 25 cells/u03bcL and warned of increased mortality risk. Most patients 26%28 provided informed consent for blood sample collection. Patients in this study were enrolled from Our hospital eastern urban China. Between June 2021 and January 2022, participants were counseled by primary care physicians and received triple therapy pro-tease/NNRTI/nucleosides bid at a global sum of 1400	98	00 yuan ($206–155) per month.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial it focus on having the samples collected.
37914679,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Inclusion criteria for patients in this study included prior receipt of antiretroviral therapy (ART) for HIV-1 infection and a baseline pre-treatment peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC) HIV-1 DNA reservoir 78 copies/10 cells. HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study. This study aimed to investigate the effect of pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations in clinically relevant HIV-1 pol and integrase genes on virological response. All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform.","The paper mentions that patients had drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, indicating the clinical samples were form treatment experienced individuals."
37914679,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","The initial treatment regimen of patients in this study population was predominantly 2NRTI + NNRTI in 77.6% (59/76), while the remaining 17 patients were on an INSTI-based antiviral regimen.","The paper mentions that most patients were on regimens that included NRTIs, NNRTIs, or INSTIs at the time of sample collection, indicating they had received these drug classes."
37914679,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Exrtracitabine, Azvudine, Lopinavir-ritonavir, Raltegravir","HIV-1 ART-naïve patients attending the Second Hospital of Nanjing during January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2020, with drug-sensitive results from traditional pre-existing drug resistance test, were enrolled in this study. Overall, 76 participants who had never received ARV drugs were included. The post-treatment plasma HIV-1 DNA level, associated with virological failure, is the primary outcome. This study aimed to investigate the effect of pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations in clinically relevant HIV-1 pol and integrase genes on virological response. All of the 76 HIV-1-infected patients with baseline plasma pre-existing low-frequency drug resistance mutations were detected by the MiSeq second-generation sequencing platform.","The paper mentions that most patients were on regimens that included Tenofovir, Emtricitabine, Azvudine, Lopinavir-ritonavir, Raltegravir, at the time of sample  collection."
