37938856,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kenya,"The study was conducted in low‐resource, high‐HIV burden public sector facilities in Kisumu County, western Kenya, with one of the highest prevalence of HIV infections in Kenya. We conducted a pre/post‐intervention prospective cohort study among 820 pregnant women accessing HIV care at five public‐sector facilities in western Kenya from 2019 to 2022.","The study was conducted in Kenya, and the samples were obtained from pregnant and postpartum women accessing HIV care in public-sector facilities in Kisumu County, western Kenya."
37938856,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022,We conducted a pre/post‐intervention prospective cohort study among 820 pregnant women accessing HIV care at five public‐sector facilities in western Kenya from 2019 to 2022. Follow‐up of the control cohort overlapped in calendar time with enrolment of the intervention cohort (control group enrolment started on 26 February 2019 and follow‐up lasted until 22 April 2021; intervention group enrolment started on 7 October 2019 and follow‐up lasted until 31 December 2021).,The study period is mentioned as 2019 to 2022 when the samples were collected from the participants.
37938856,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml. Among women with viraemia (VL>1000 copies/ml) who underwent successful DRM testing in the intervention group, all (46/46, 100%) had some DRMs and 20 (43%) had major DRMs (of which 80% were nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitor mutations).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It mentions using Sanger sequencing for drug resistance mutation testing.
37938856,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not specified,"In the intervention group, from enrolment up until 6 months postpartum, we identified 54 episodes of VL>1000 copies/ml, among 48 (11.7%) participants (Table 3). In the intervention group, 52 DRM tests were requested, of which 46 (88%) were successfully conducted (six samples failed to amplify) and all identified at least one DRM (K103N [n = 12, 28%] and M184V [n = 10, 22%] were most commonly detected mutations).",The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced; it mentions drug resistance mutations without detailing the specific genes.
37938856,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV DRM testing was performed at the two accredited centralized laboratories in Kenya using Sanger sequencing for participants in the intervention group with VL ≥ 1000 copies/ml. Among women with viraemia (VL>1000 copies/ml) who underwent successful DRM testing in the intervention group, all (46/46, 100%) had some DRMs and 20 (43%) had major DRMs (of which 80% were nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitor mutations).",The paper specifies that HIV drug resistance mutation testing was performed using Sanger sequencing.
37938856,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not specified,"In the intervention group, from enrolment up until 6 months postpartum, we identified 54 episodes of VL>1000 copies/ml, among 48 (11.7%) participants (Table 3). In the intervention group, 52 DRM tests were requested, of which 46 (88%) were successfully conducted (six samples failed to amplify) and all identified at least one DRM (K103N [n = 12, 28%] and M184V [n = 10, 22%] were most commonly detected mutations).",The paper mentions that DRM testing was done on samples from women with VL >1000 copies/ml but does not specify the clinical type of samples sequenced.
37938856,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"In the intervention group, from enrolment up until 6 months postpartum, we identified 54 episodes of VL>1000 copies/ml, among 48 (11.7%) participants (Table 3). In the intervention group, 52 DRM tests were requested, of which 46 (88%) were successfully conducted (six samples failed to amplify) and all identified at least one DRM (K103N [n = 12, 28%] and M184V [n = 10, 22%] were most commonly detected mutations).",The paper mentions that sequences were obtained from women with viraemia (VL >1000 copies/ml) who underwent drug resistance mutation testing.
37938856,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted an open‐label, pre/post‐intervention (or intervention/control) prospective cohort study, enrolling pregnant women living with HIV during their ANC care and followed them through 6 months postpartum in five public‐sector HIV treatment facilities in Kenya from February 2019 to November 2022 (Figure 1). The pre‐intervention cohort served as the ""control"" group, receiving standard‐of‐care (SOC), which consisted of centralized VL testing approximately every 6 months, from all five facilities.","The paper mentions that the study was a pre/post-intervention prospective cohort study, not a clinical trial."
37938856,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Overall, 622 (76%) participants were on NNRTI‐, 71 (8.7%) on PI‐ and 80 (10%) on integrase‐containing ART at enrolment, and the median time on ART was 4.0 years (IQR 1.2, 6.3). Among 771 (94%) of the participants with a VL documented in the 24 months prior to enrolment, 672 (82%) had VS. Among 771 (94%) of the participants with a VL documented in the 24 months prior to enrolment, 672 (82%) had VS.",The paper mentions that the participants were on ART and had documented viral load in the 24 months prior to enrolment.
37938856,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Overall, 622 (76%) participants were on NNRTI‐, 71 (8.7%) on PI‐ and 80 (10%) on integrase‐containing ART at enrolment, and the median time on ART was 4.0 years (IQR 1.2, 6.3). First‐line ART regimens among adults during the study period included combinations of two nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) lamivudine and tenofovir with either (1) NNRTI efavirenz or (2) integrase inhibitors (INSTIs) dolutegravir, and protease inhibitors (PIs)-containing ART, for example with atazanavir/ritonavir, were considered alternative first‐line or second‐line regimens.","The paper mentioned the use of NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs in the regimens for HIV treatment for individuals in the study."
37938856,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"lamivudine, tenofovir, efavirenz, dolutegravir, atazanavir, ritonavir","Overall, 622 (76%) participants were on NNRTI‐, 71 (8.7%) on PI‐ and 80 (10%) on integrase‐containing ART at enrolment, and the median time on ART was 4.0 years (IQR 1.2, 6.3). First‐line ART regimens among adults during the study period included combinations of two nucleos(t)ide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) lamivudine and tenofovir with either (1) NNRTI efavirenz or (2) integrase inhibitors (INSTIs) dolutegravir, and protease inhibitors (PIs)-containing ART, for example with atazanavir/ritonavir, were considered alternative first‐line or second‐line regimens.","The paper mentioned the use of lamivudine, tenofovir, efavirenz, dolutegravir, atazanavir, and ritonavir in the regimens for HIV treatment for individuals in the study."
37941373,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022. Besides, amplification, sequence, and analysis of the portion of the HIV-1 pol gene that encodes the integrase protein were implemented to identify INSTI resistance. Integrase sequence data was harvested for a comprehensive cohort of 1032 patients infected with HIV-1.","The paper explicitly states that plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients, and sequences of the HIV-1 pol gene were analyzed to identify INSTI resistance, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported in the study."
37941373,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Prevalence and analysis of acquired and transmitted integrase strand transfer inhibitor-associated HIV-1 drug resistance in Chongqing, China: In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022. Besides, amplification, sequence, and analysis of the portion of the HIV-1 pol gene that encodes the integrase protein were implemented to identify INSTI resistance. Integrase sequence data was harvested for a comprehensive cohort of 1032 patients infected with HIV-1. This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naive patients, 465 ART-treated patients, and 3 patients with an unknown treatment history. Within the study group, we identified INSTI resistance in 21 patients (2.03%, 21/1032), including 17 ART-treated patients (3.66%, 17/465). Among the ART-treated patients, 12 were INSTI-treated (11.76%, 12/102), 5 were INSTI-naive (1.38%, 5/363), and 4 were ART-ineffective patients (0.71%, 4/564). The prevalent major resistance mutation was Q148R (0.48%, 5/1032), while the most prevalent accessory resistance mutation was E157Q (1.65%, 17/1032). In light of the above, it is recommended that the incidence of accessory genotype analysis should be considered before starting any future INSTI-based therapy, especially in patients with drug resistance to NRTIs and NNRTIs and the reduction of INSTI sensitivity should be carefully monitored and investigated. Regular monitoring for resistance should be implemented after the use of INSTIs, and, importantly, ongoing monitoring of the decreasing susceptibility to INSTIs is crucial following the initiation of treatment with INSTIs.","The paper focuses on sequencing and analyzing the HIV-1 pol gene for drug resistance mutations, but it does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data."
37941373,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The data in this study are available from the first author (HZ.Z) on reasonable request.,"The paper states that the data are available from the first author on reasonable request, but there is no mention of the sequences being made publicly available in a database such as GenBank."
37941373,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,The data in this study are available from the first author (HZ.Z) on reasonable request.,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37941373,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"1080 ""Samples were harvested from 1080 patients, and successful amplification of the IN gene was achieved in 1032 samples.""","Samples were harvested from 1080 patients, and successful amplification of the IN gene was achieved in 1032 samples.","The paper mentions that samples were collected from 1080 patients, and successful amplification of the IN gene was achieved in 1032 samples, indicating that 1080 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing."
37941373,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"Prevalence and analysis of acquired and transmitted integrase strand transfer inhibitor-associated HIV-1 drug resistance in Chongqing, China. In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022.","The study was conducted in Chongqing, China, and the samples were obtained from patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center."
37941373,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022,"In this study, we examined the occurrence of acquired and transmitted drug resistance to integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) in HIV-1 strains in Chongqing (China) for guiding for the routine testing of INSTI-associated HIV-1 genotype resistance. Plasma samples were obtained from HIV-1 patients at Chongqing Public Health Medical Center from July 2019 to August 2022.",The paper states that plasma samples were obtained from July 2019 to August 2022.
37941373,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Sanger dideoxy sequencing was employed for the identification of the clinically significant known drug resistance mutations.,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
37941373,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"pol gene, integrase gene","Besides, amplification, sequence, and analysis of the portion of the HIV-1 pol gene that encodes the integrase protein were implemented to identify INSTI resistance. The HIV-1 IN gene was amplified by a validated In-house method. Integrase sequence data was harvested for a comprehensive cohort of 1032 patients infected with HIV-1.",The paper mentions that the pol gene and the integrase gene were sequenced.
37941373,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Sanger dideoxy sequencing was employed for the identification of the clinically significant known drug resistance mutations. Even though Sanger sequencing is confirmed as the gold standard approach for HIV drug resistance testing, it is incapable of identifying variants within the viral population that exhibit less than 20% drug resistance. The method may underestimate the true prevalence of drug-resistant mutations in treatment-naïve patients.",The paper mentions that Sanger dideoxy sequencing was employed for the identification of the clinically significant known drug resistance mutations.
37941373,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Besides, amplification, sequence, and analysis of the portion of the HIV-1 pol gene that encodes the integrase protein were implemented to identify INSTI resistance. Whole blood samples were harvested and centrifuged at 3000×g for 15 minutes to remove leucocytes, and then plasma (supernatant) was harvested for RNA extraction. HIV-RNA was extracted utilizing a viral nucleic acid extraction kit (Jiangsu Shuoshi), followed by amplification of the target fragment by nested PCR. Blood samples were drawn into EDTA-containing tubes, and subsequently, plasma samples were obtained following centrifugation.",The paper mentions that plasma samples were obtained from patients for sequencing.
37941373,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naive patients, 465 ART-treated patients, and 3 patients with an unknown treatment history. Within the study group, we identified INSTI resistance in 21 patients (2.03%, 21/1032), including 17 ART-treated patients (3.66%, 17/465). who were all 465 treatment-experienced patients had a history of previously-instantiated ART, 378 suffered virological failure (VF) with a viral load exceeding 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies/ml or 0.5-2.0 log^{10} reduction of VL from baseline). b were on the INSTI class but had not achieved VL < 50 copies/ml; INSTIQ without- or nonaninsti-pooled of    363b patients were on INSTI (NRTI); none had genotype exegname except for 3 with IGRT antret, IDRET resistance at or before INSTI initation. \n \n The study participants were then categorized into ART-naïve and ART-treated groups. The incidence of major or accessory INSTI resistance mutations in the ART-naïve, INSTI-treated, and INSTI-naïve groups were 0.71 (411)%, 11.76 (1212.01)%, 1.38 (531.08)%, 0.71 (341.08)%, 0.44 (154.032)%, 0.30 (0320.03)%, I.19 (0430.02)%.",The paper mentions 388 patients were on ANTIRETROVIRAL THERAPY which inequivocally equates they'd received ARV drugs; virological failure is indicative of encountering treatment-interfering drug resistance meaning a VF-receiving patient obtained a sample.
37941373,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"HIV-1-infected inpatients and outpatients who visited Chongqing Public Health Medical Center between July 2019 and August 2022 were enrolled in this study. Plasma samples were collected from patients with HIV-1 viral load (VL) greater than 1,000 copies/ml and subjected to a subsequent polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to amplify the IN gene.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.
37941373,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naive patients, 465 ART-treated patients, and 3 patients with an unknown treatment history. The remaining patients on ART (45.06%, 465/1032) were treated with dolutegravir, rilpivirine, lamivudine, tenofovir, crizaline and efavirenz, among others. The study participants were then categorized into ART-naïve and ART-treated groups. We primarily observed major and/or accessory mutations, which resulted in resistance to INSTIs, within the group of ART-treated patients (3.66%, 17/465). Additionally, among those treated with INSTIs, varying combinations of different mutations led to different degrees of drug resistance to INSTIs. This cohort consisted of 564 ART-naïve patients, 465 on ART, 3 questionable treatment history patients, the primary focus is ART-treated people ~37.1% were on INSTI (MOST LIKELY DTG) all at INSTI resistance (12/102), remaining on a INSTI test in panel (363, receipt of INSTI drugs unknown), not on INSTIs (31/365) had a resistance genotypes of NRTI/NNRTI INSTI non-baded on at-insti initiation assay: all served to show low-/intermediate/lowpot/resistance to the INSTIs, at insti initiation. None had non-analogue genotyyps.하게.","The paper states 465 patients were on ART and treated with various drugs, indicating they had previously received ARV drugs. drug resistant patients were all on INSTIs"
