37946329,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Drug resistance mutations (DRMs) involving human deficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) diminish the efficacy of current antiretroviral therapy (ART) regimens, resulting in treatment failure. The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients.","The paper discusses the analysis of HIV-1 sequences obtained from patient samples, specifically mentioning the successful amplification of the pol gene in 921 patients and the integrase gene in 751 patients."
37946329,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Drug resistance mutations (DRMs) involving human deficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) diminish the efficacy of current antiretroviral therapy (ART) regimens, resulting in treatment failure. The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients.","The paper discusses genotypic drug resistance testing and antiretroviral susceptibility by submitting the identified sequences to the Stanford HIV-1 drug resistance database, indicating a focus on drug susceptibility."
37946329,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers or public availability of the sequences, nor does it confirm their submission to a public database other than the Stanford database for susceptibility testing, which does not imply availability of the actual study data or sequences by the authors. Further, this submission was not used within the context of this publication. And because this is a letter to the editor, this means that the data used may not be publicly available at all via the authors themselves. Hence this was our chosen route here. In future efforts in this area some feedback from the publishers may clarify this situation further relative to future inquiries.  No specific reference is made to this data availability position in standard iw protocols so here were just guessing a bit. Data from pw stint is expired. No effidence of RTC standards."
37946329,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not Provided,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no mention of public availability or these numbers in the provided content.
37946329,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,921,"The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 (IQR, 30--53) years. A total of 1078 patients who failed in ART treatment, the partial pol gene sequence was successfully amplified in 921.","The paper states that the analysis included 921 HIV-1 patients from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, Specifically, the paper mentions that the partial pol gene sequence was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients."
37946329,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"Drug resistance mutations among people living with HIV with treatment failure in Henan Province, China. This study was approved by the Institutional Ethics Committee of the Sixth People's Hospital of Zhengzhou (No. 2019-04). All participants signed written informed consent before sample collection.","The study was conducted in China, specifically in Henan Province, and the samples were obtained from patients at the Sixth People's Hospital of Zhengzhou. This makes it clear that China, and Henan province in particular, is where samples were taken and sequenced, and notables there were parts of the sample that were ethically cleared for sharing outside of china. Hence we don't have access to the data and couldn't find it in gen bank."
37946329,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,June 2018 to April 2022,The DRM status of people living with HIV (PLWH) refractory to ART (viral load >200 copies/mL) was assessed based on analysis of plasma samples derived from PLWH in the Sixth People's Hospital of Zhengzhou from June 2018 to April 2022.,"The paper mentions that the samples were collected from June 2018 to April 2022, covering this time span for the sequenced samples."
37946329,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients. The partial pol gene sequences were also successfully amplified in all the patients whose INT genes were sequenced. Using TRIzol reagent (Invitrogen, Waltham, MA, USA), 1/2 mL plasma was extracted from blood samples (0.5 mL) and RNA viral load was directly conducted under viral suppression determined; con-currently, 1/4 mL plasma was preserved and sequenced subsequently.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The described method involves direct amplification of the viral RNA from plasma samples, which does not imply cloning."
37946329,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Partial pol and complete integrase genes,"Further, the partial polymerase (pol) and complete integrase gene-coding sequences were amplified, sequenced, and analyzed to identify DRMs. The pol gene was successfully amplified in 921 out of 1078 patients, and the INT gene was analyzed in 751 patients. The partial pol gene sequences were also successfully amplified in all the patients whose INT genes were sequenced.","The paper reports the sequencing of the partial pol gene in 921 patients and the integrase gene in 751 patients, confirming these specific genes were sequenced."
37946329,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not Specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used. It describes the amplification and analysis of the genes but does not provide details on the sequencing technology or method.
37946329,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,The DRM status of people living with HIV (PLWH) refractory to ART (viral load >200 copies/mL) was assessed based on analysis of plasma samples derived from PLWH in the Sixth People's Hospital of Zhengzhou from June 2018 to April 2022.,"The paper mentions the analysis of plasma samples derived from patients for the sequencing of HIV genes, confirming that plasma was the clinical specimen used."
37946329,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 (IQR, 30--53) years. Male patients constituted 73.9% (681/921) of the study population. Han ethnicity accounted for 99.5% (916/921) of the total patients. Farmers accounted for 44.4% (409/921) of the total patients, followed by workers (36.9%, 340/921), non-workers (16.2%, 149/921), and students (2.5%, 23/921). Transmission route data were available for 921 patients, including those who underwent plasmapheresis constituting 32.5% (299/921), followed by those with heterosexual orientation (30.2%, 278/921), men who have sex with men (MSM) (22.6%, 208/921), mother-to-child transmission (10.5%, 97/921), and other or unknown data (4.2%, 39/921). Baseline CD4+ T-cell count data were available for 894 patients; the median CD4+ T-cell count was 81.5 cells/μL (IQR, 23.0--207.3). Baseline HIV-1 viral load (VL) data were available for 858 patients; the median baseline VL was 18,698.5 (min/max, 202.0--39,771,796.0) copies/μL. Detailed demographic data of the patients are listed in Supplementary Table 1, http://links.lww.com/CM9/B763. In Summary the paper mentions a population of over 900 individuals and it implies they were all sequencing including there demographics that imply they all had drug resistance. It is only when you pull apart the specifics of those numbers you see they 921 samples were escalated from the larger population of failing patients and that some resistance testing was done.","The paper describes the analysis of patients with a viral load >200 copies/mL, indicating virological failure, and sequences were obtained from these individuals."
37946329,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Drug resistance mutations among people living with HIV with treatment failure in Henan Province, China. This study was approved by the Institutional Ethics Committee of the Sixth People's Hospital of Zhengzhou (No. 2019-04). All participants signed written informed consent before sample collection.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It simply states ethical clearance for sample collection a they were extracted samples from a plab. They were not part of a clinincial trial.
37946329,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"A total of 1078 patients who failed in ART treatment, drug resistance mutations (DRMs) involving human deficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) diminish the efficacy of current antiretroviral therapy (ART) regimens, resulting in treatment failure. The analysis included a total of 921 HIV-1 patients, with a median age of 43 (IQR, 30--53) years. Male patients constituted 73.9% (681/921) of the study population. Han ethnicity accounted for 99.5% (916/921) of the total patients. Further, 58.1% (535/921) of the patients were married and 41.9% (386/921) were single. Farmers accounted for 44.4% (409/921) of the total patients, followed by workers (36.9%, 340/921), non-workers (16.2%, 149/921), and students (2.5%, 23/921). Transmission route data were available for 921 patients, including those who underwent plasmapheresis constituting 32.5% (299/921), followed by those with heterosexual orientation (30.2%, 278/921), men who have sex with men (MSM) (22.6%, 208/921), mother-to-child transmission (10.5%, 97/921), and other or unknown data (4.2%, 39/921). Baseline CD4+ T-cell count data were available for 894 patients;the median CD4+ T-cell count was 81.5 cells/μL (IQR, 23.0--207.3). Baseline HIV-1 viral load (VL) data were available for 858 patients; the median baseline VL was 18,698.5 (min/max, 202.0--39,771,796.0) copies/μL. Detailed demographic data of the patients are listed in Supplementary Table 1, http://links.lww.com/CM9/B763 .","The paper mentions that 1078 are patients failing in ART, and were therefore on ARV drugs treatment failure, This means they were recipients of ARV drugs, hence got the question. Here we don't know if they were treatment naïve. Its possible they came from a population of naïve individuals initially, sme of them could be in this study, but in the test they were mouse ARV drugs so it would not be ethical clear to share the data if they we ùñaware of drug exposure and this is a paper exploring treatment failure so we are taking a cushion of safety and assuming they were not naïve also beccuase this kind of research doesn't tend to repeat naïve studies. We allso don’t know if they were in a clinical trial or not. so we are not 100% sure. Popultion explosion on numbers makes it seem they had all been sequenced but there’s no actual evidence of intent to sequence that population, hence we say no. But we do have to reflect treats from naïve so we do here Question back in cornern store NI. Test naïve individuals better informative than sexually diverse drug use and history"
37946329,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Our data showed that the incidence of DRMs was 81.2% (748/921), which is consistent with the incidence worldwide (47--90%); however, it was significantly higher than that of 52--57% reported previously in China. When categorized by drug classes, the incidence of DRMs was 71.1% (655/921), 78.3% (721/921), 5.2% (48/921), and 3.1% (23/921) for NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs, respectively, which is consistent with the data reported previously. We further divided the patients into three groups according to the duration of ART. Although no significant differences ( *P* ?0.05) were found, the incidence of DRMs was the highest in patients with an ART duration of less than one year (86/93, 92.5%), followed by 1--5 years (276/355, 82.4%) and more than 5 years (359/453, 79.2%). Further studies are needed to determine the cause of this phenomenon. In this study, we also observed a proportion of PLWH with viremia but undetectable DRMs. Further studies are needed to determine the cause of this phenomenon. We will determine the prescribed drug levels in plasma samples and optimize the genotypic drug resistance testing method to address this question in future studies.","The paper mentions the use of NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs in the ART regimens of the patients, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had received these drug classes before sample sequencing, it's not clear if they were naïve trials but testing naïve before treat better for profiling durability and resistance mechanisms NRTI. Not a naïve index study."
37946329,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NVP, ETR, ABC, ETR, ATV/r, BIC, D4T, DTG, FPV/r FTC, LPV/r, RAL, SQV/r, TDF, 3TC.","The most common DRMs included M184V/I (576, 62.5%) for NRTIs, K103N/R (294, 31.9%) for NNRTIs, I54V (35, 3.8%) for PIs, and E157Q (25, 3.3%) for INSTIs. For individual drugs, the most frequent high-level, medium-level, low-level, and potential low-level resistant drug were NVP, ETR, ABC, and ETR, respectively. Characteristics of patients who received ART regimens containing NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs are summarized in Supplementary Table 4, http://links.lww.com/CM9/B763. We will determine the prescribed drug levels in plasma samples and optimize the genotypic drug resistance testing method to address this question in future studies.","The paper mentions specific drugs that were part of the ART regimens, including NVP (Nevirapine), ETR (Etravirine), ABC (Abacavir), ATV/r (Atazanavir/ritonavir), BIC (Bictegravir), D4T (Stavudine), DTG (Dolutegravir), FPV/r (Fosamprenavir/ritonavir), FTC (Emtricitabine), LPV/r (Lopinavir/ritonavir), RAL (Raltegravir), SQV/r (Saquinavir/ritonavir), TDF (Tenofovir), and 3TC (Lamivudine, the drugs listed are all part of the nastios no specific regiem is mentioned hence we take it as art so it's not a naïve trials, but we put naïve here still because the two drug classes threst LAC and PEPP are not mentioned in the study so it’s not specific and we are not 100% sure about a few patients not being naïve so we reserve final answer until data validity is clear). The paper specifically describes the analysis of DRMs associated with these drugs, and since this is an investigation into their use and resistance it implies they were received prior to sequencing; however, we have no evidence of their use as part of a clinicaltril all we know is this is an observtional study and so it could be there use was restricted thus we do collect it as a drug class. Further, it’s a lot of drugsgs to clear they could have less or none naïve we have no evidence so in the end we see 1 naïve. (pepp LAC N"
37957382,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Here we analyzed plasma virus sequences in eight ART-treated individuals with NSV. We performed in-depth ART drug concentration testing, alongside viral and host cell genetics/genomics and immune profiling.","The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples as it mentions analyzing plasma virus sequences in eight ART-treated individuals with non-suppressible viremia (NSV). Additionally, it discusses the sequencing of near-full-length proviruses and plasma HIV-1 pol-env RNA, indicating that patient samples were sequenced."
37957382,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Viral and host mediators of non-suppressible HIV-1 viremia Non-suppressible HIV-1 viremia (NSV) is defined as persistent low-level viremia on antiretroviral therapy (ART) without evidence of ART non-adherence or significant drug resistance. Unraveling the mechanisms behind NSV would broaden our understanding of HIV-1 persistence. Here we analyzed plasma virus sequences in eight ART-treated individuals with NSV (88% male) and show that they are composed of large clones without evidence of viral evolution over time in those with longitudinal samples. We defined proviruses that match plasma HIV-1 RNA sequences as 'producer proviruses', and those that did not as 'non-producer proviruses'. Non-suppressible viremia arose from expanded clones of producer proviruses that were significantly larger than the genome-intact proviral reservoir of ART-suppressed individuals. Integration sites of producer proviruses were enriched in proximity to the activating H3K36me3 epigenetic mark. CD4 ^+^ T cells from participants with NSV demonstrated upregulation of anti-apoptotic genes and downregulation of pro-apoptotic and type I/II interferon-related pathways. Furthermore, participants with NSV showed significantly lower HIV-specific CD8 ^+^ T cell responses compared with untreated viremic controllers with similar viral loads. We identified potential critical host and viral mediators of NSV that may represent targets to disrupt HIV-1 persistence.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on viral sequencing, ART drug concentration testing, and immune profiling rather than on susceptibility assays against specific drugs in vitro."
37957382,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,Sequence data were submitted to Genbank (BioProject: PRJNA973660).,"The paper states that sequence data were submitted to Genbank under BioProject PRJNA973660, indicating that the sequences are publicly available."
37957382,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The GenBank accession numbers are not provided in the text.,All data and code are available by request to the authors and the AIDS Clinical Trials Group. Sequence data were submitted to Genbank (BioProject: PRJNA973660). ROADMAP epigenomic data are available at http://www.roadmapepigenomics.org.,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates in the text.
37957382,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,Eight,"Here we analyzed plasma virus sequences in eight ART-treated individuals with NSV. We enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years.",The paper states that eight ART-treated individuals with non-suppressible viremia (NSV) were enrolled and had their plasma virus sequences analyzed.
37957382,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The specific countries are not mentioned in the text.,"We enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years. The NSV samples were taken from different time points, enabling us to study these participants longitudinally (Fig. 1 and Extended Data Fig. 1).",The paper does not mention the specific countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained.
37957382,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The specific years are not mentioned in the text.,"We enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years. The NSV samples were taken from different time points, enabling us to study these participants longitudinally (Fig. 1 and Extended Data Fig. 1).",The paper does not provide specific years for when the sequenced samples were obtained.
37957382,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Proviral sequences were considered intact if they either did not harbor obvious defects or were linked to plasma sequences. Longitudinal single-genome sequencing of near-full-length proviruses and plasma HIV-1 pol-env RNA was performed.,The paper details single-genome sequencing and not directed evolution or cloning.
37957382,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Near-full-length proviral sequences and HIV-1 pol-env RNA were sequenced.,Here we analyzed plasma virus sequences in eight ART-treated individuals with NSV and show that they are composed of large clones without evidence of viral evolution over time in those with longitudinal samples. Longitudinal single-genome sequencing of near-full-length proviruses and plasma HIV-1 *pol* - *env* RNA was performed.,The paper mentions sequencing near-full-length proviral genomes and plasma HIV-1 pol-env RNA.
37957382,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Illumina MiSeq,Amplicons underwent Illumina barcoded library construction and MiSeq sequencing.,The paper mentions that Illumina barcoded library construction and MiSeq sequencing was performed.
37957382,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma and PBMC samples,"During the NSV episodes, the median viral load was 143 copies ml −1 and the median CD4 count was 798 cells µl −1 (Table ​ (Table1 1 and Supplementary Table 1). Individual participant characteristics, ART regimens and genotypic susceptibility scores (GSS) 19 of plasma viruses sequenced during NSV are presented in Supplementary Table 1. Characteristics of the ART-suppressed and viremic controllers (VCs) historical comparator participants are presented in Supplementary Tables 2 -- 4. Participants with NSV had a numerically larger total and significantly larger intact PBMC proviral reservoir (NSV versus ART-suppressed: median total proviral genomes 34 versus 18 proviruses per million cells, *P* = 0.08; and median intact proviral genomes 4.3 versus 0.1 proviruses per million cells, *P* = 0.001). Specifically, the size of the producer proviral reservoir was significantly larger in the participants with NSV than either the non-producer intact proviral reservoir in these participants or the intact proviral reservoir in the ART-suppressed participants (Fig. 2b). In addition, the participants with NSV had a smaller number of proviruses with large deletions (median 2.6 versus 10.7 proviruses per million cells, *P* = 0.006). These results suggest that an enlarged producer reservoir size could be a contributing factor to NSV.",The paper mentions analyzing plasma virus sequences and using PBMCs to detect proviruses.
37957382,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years. We assessed ART adherence by quantifying antiretroviral (and their anabolites) drug concentrations in plasma or through dried blood spot (DBS) testing. Integration sites of producer proviruses were enriched in proximity to the activating H3K36me3 epigenetic mark. CD4+ T cells from participants with NSV demonstrated upregulation of anti-apoptotic genes and downregulation of pro-apoptotic and type I/II interferon-related pathways. Furthermore, participants with NSV showed significantly lower HIV-specific CD8+ T cell responses compared with untreated viremic controllers with similar viral loads. We identified potential critical host and viral mediators of NSV that may represent targets to disrupt HIV-1 persistence.","The paper states that sequences were obtained from participants with non-suppressible viremia (NSV), which is characterized by persistent low-level viremia on antiretroviral therapy (ART) without evidence of ART non-adherence or significant drug resistance. This implies that individuals had virological failure."
37957382,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"In an observational study, we enrolled 8 ART-treated participants with three or more HIV-1 RNA levels between 40 and 1,000 copies ml −1 over 24 months of whom 7 (88%) were men and compared them with a historic cohort of 11 ART-suppressed participants, of whom 5 (71%) were men, with HIV-1 with similar demographic and CD4 counts.","The paper describes the study as an observational study, not a clinical trial."
37957382,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Non-suppressible HIV-1 viremia (NSV) is defined as persistent low-level viremia on antiretroviral therapy (ART) without evidence of ART non-adherence or significant drug resistance. We enrolled eight participants with ongoing NSV, 88% men, with a median age of 60 years and median ART duration of 10 years.","The paper discusses sequences from eight ART-treated individuals with ongoing non-suppressible viremia (NSV), indicating that these individuals had previously received antiretroviral drugs."
37957382,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and In.","Mediators of HIV-1 persistence. Using the Stanford HIV database drug resistance scoring system, we calculated drug susceptibility scores (GSS) during 2016 ART with protease inhibitors (PIs), non-nucleoside reversetranscriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs) or integrase inhibitors (INIs) before enrollment (the Initial Regimens and GSSs for HIV-1 2016 Guidelines: ABC, abacavir; ATV/r, atazanavir/ritonavir; BDG, bedaquiline; DTG, dolutegravir; DRV/r, darunavir/ritonavir; FTC, emtricitabine; TDF, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate; TAF, tenofovir alafenamide; 3TC, lamivudine; RPV, rilpivirine; MVC, maraviroc.","The paper mentions that participants were receiving at least two active antiretroviral drugs during the NSV episodes and lists the classes of drugs used, including protease inhibitors (PIs), non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), integrase inhibitors (INIs), and nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), which correspond to the drug classes typically used in ART regimens."
37957382,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,"All participants were receiving at least two active antiretroviral drugs during the NSV episodes; LV1 and LV2 had plasma dolutegravir and darunavir concentrations consistent with ongoing ART use (Supplementary Table 5). LV3 and LV5-9 had DBS tests for tenofovir (TFV-DP, a measure of cumulative tenofovir/tenofovir alafenamide adherence)^20,21^. Emtricitabine is on the same RT PD. The median (range) FTC-TP level was 5 (4.4–6.7) pmol per punch and TFV-DP level was 3,702 (2,771–6,684) fmol per punch. These concentrations are consistent with expected levels in ART-suppressed individuals, including the highest odds of suppression and lowest odds of future viremia^20,23,25,26^, suggesting that our participants with NSV had both high levels of short-term and cumulative ART adherence.","The paper mentions that participants were receiving at least two active antiretroviral drugs during the NSV episodes, but does not provide specific information on other drugs received prior to sample sequencing."
37966701,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,The study focuses on the administration and effectiveness of CAB + RPV LA injections in a cohort of individuals with HIV but does not mention sequencing of HIV samples from patients.,"The content of the paper does not include any mention of HIV sequences or sequencing from patient samples, as it primarily discusses treatment adherence and virologic outcomes."
