37966701,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,The study focuses on the CAB + RPV LA treatment and does not report on the drug classes received prior to sequencing.,"The paper does not provide information on drug classes received prior to sequencing, as it does not involve sequencing."
37966701,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,The paper focuses on the CAB + RPV LA treatment and does not report on drugs received prior to sequencing.,"The content of the paper does not mention any drugs received prior to sequencing, as it does not involve sequencing."
37973713,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In our study among ART naïve, newly diagnosed Ukrainian migrants 44% were women, the majority of patients acquired HIV infection through heterosexual contacts, which corresponds with the presented epidemiological data. We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients. Among the analyzed cohorts we assessed the presence of HIV DRMs.",The paper mentions that HIV-1 drug resistance mutations were analyzed in a cohort of newly diagnosed patients. This implies that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples for the purpose of studying drug resistance.
37973713,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients. Surveillance of primary resistance can supply information to support the rational use of ART in prophylactic and therapeutic schemes.",The study focuses on the presence of drug resistance mutations rather than measuring the effectiveness of drugs against the virus in vitro.
37973713,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"The clinical trial received a positive opinion from the Bioethics Committee at the Medical University of Warsaw (Number of consent: AKBE/206/2020) and was registered on clinicaltrials.gov (Number: NCT04636736]. The patients were recruited during 2 years period (2020--2022). We have conducted multi-center, prospective, observational, cross sectional study.","The paper mentioned the study is registered on clinialtrials.gov which is a public database, the sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database."
37973713,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA). The sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database http://hivdb.stanford.edu in order to comparison of potential differences in the interpretation of the significance of genetic variants detected.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It only mentions that the sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV Drug Resistance Database for interpretation but does not detail the accession numbers.
37973713,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,117,"The inclusion and exclusion criteria met 117 patients. We have recruited 78 patients from two centers in Warsaw, 28 patients from Olsztyn, 6 from Lublin, 3 from Łódź and 2 patients from Łańcut center.","The study group section states that 117 patients met the inclusion criteria for the study, which implies that samples were obtained from this number of individuals for HIV sequencing."
37973713,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Poland, Ukraine, Belarus, Georgia, Kazakhstan","In the study group there were 61 patients (52.14%) from eastern countries (34 men, 27 women) and 56 patients (47.86%) from Poland (52 men, 4 women). Among patients from eastern countries there were 58 patients (95.08%) from Ukraine (11 war refugees and 47 patients who arrived to Poland before the onset of war), 3 patients (4.92%) from: Belarus, Georgia and Kazakhstan.","The study group section lists the countries of origin for the patients, indicating that samples were obtained from individuals in Poland, Ukraine, Belarus, Georgia, and Kazakhstan."
37973713,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2020-2022,"We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients. The clinical trial recruited 117 individuals during 2 years period (2020--2022).","The abstract mentions that the study recruited participants over a period from 2020 to 2022, which indicates the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained."
37973713,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA).",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger sequencing method but not mention of clonging of samples.
37973713,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease (PR) and Reverse Transcriptase (RT),"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA).",The methods section explicitly states that the drug resistance genotyping was performed for the protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding regions.
37973713,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The drug resistance genotyping of HIV-1 protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) coding region was performed based on a Sanger sequencing method with use of commercially available ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System (Abbott Molecular, Des Plains, IL, USA).",The methods section clearly states that the drug resistance genotyping was performed using the Sanger sequencing method.
37973713,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not specified,"Surveillance of primary resistance can supply information to support the rational use of ART in prophylactic and therapeutic schemes [16]. We collected epidemiological data such as: age, gender, BMI, origin, migration data, sexual preferences and clinical data: HIV viral load, CD4+ cells count, presence of AIDS-defining diseases, implemented ART, serology of HBV and HCV co-infections. All patients who had positive test result for any of the following: HBs-Ag, anti-Hbc, HBV-DNA were considered individuals with HBV co-infection and all patients who had positive test result for anti-HCV or HCV-RNA were considered HCV co-infected. In all patients, before starting ART, the presence of HIV-1 DRMs to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTI), non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI) and protease inhibitors (PI). Undetectable viral load was defined as a < 50 copies of HIV-RNA/ml of the peripheral blood and virological failure was defined as a detectable HIV-1 viral load after 6 months from the initiation of the ART.",The paper does not define the specific type of clinical specimens that were used for sequencing. It mentions the use of peripheral blood but not the exact type of samples.
37973713,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"Inclusion criteria were recent diagnosis with HIV-1 infection (newly recognized patients), no history of ART, age 18 years or older. We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients. Undetectable viral load was defined as a < 50 copies of HIV-RNA/ml of the peripheral blood and virological failure was defined as a detectable HIV-1 viral load after 6 months from the initiation of the ART.","The study included only ART naïve patients, hence the sequences weren't obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
37973713,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The clinical trial received a positive opinion from the Bioethics Committee at the Medical University of Warsaw (Number of consent: AKBE/206/2020) and was registered on clinicaltrials.gov (Number: NCT04636736]. We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients.","The paper mentioned the study was registered on clinicaltrials.gov, and it’s a multi-center, prospective study, this indicates the patients were in a clinical trial."
37973713,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Inclusion criteria were recent diagnosis with HIV-1 infection (newly recognized patients), no history of ART, age 18 years or older. We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients.","The study included only ART naïve patients, hence the sequences weren't obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
37973713,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None received ARV drugs,"Inclusion criteria were recent diagnosis with HIV-1 infection (newly recognized patients), no history of ART, age 18 years or older. We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients.","The study included only ART naïve patients, hence no ARV drugs were received by individuals before sample sequencing."
37973713,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None received ARV drugs,"Inclusion criteria were recent diagnosis with HIV-1 infection (newly recognized patients), no history of ART, age 18 years or older. We have conducted multi-center, prospective study, which aimed to establish HIV-1 subtype and assess the presence of primary drug resistance mutations to nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors and protease inhibitors in antiretroviral treatment naïve patients.","The study included only ART naïve patients, hence no specific drugs were received by individuals before sample sequencing."
37976080,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. PDR was analyzed using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database. Among 618 (24.2%) sequences (nodes) associated with 253 molecular transmission clusters, drug resistance mutation sites included K103, E138, V179, P225, V106, V108, L210, T215, P225, K238, and A98.","The paper discusses the collection of plasma samples from study participants for HIV sequencing. It mentions the analysis of sequences associated with molecular transmission clusters, indicating that sequences from patient samples were reported."
37976080,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. PDR was analyzed using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database, and the Tamura-Nei 93 model in HIV-TRACE was used to calculate pairwise matches with a genetic distance of 0.01 substitutions per site. Logistic regression was used to identify and estimate factors associated with PDR.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing. It focuses on pretreatment drug resistance analysis using sequences obtained from patient samples.
37976080,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. PDR was analyzed using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database, and the Tamura-Nei 93 model in HIV-TRACE was used to calculate pairwise matches with a genetic distance of 0.01 substitutions per site. Logistic regression was used to identify and estimate factors associated with PDR.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing. It focuses on pretreatment drug resistance analysis using sequences obtained from patient samples.
37976080,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,The content of the paper does not include any mention of GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
37976080,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,2568,"PDR test results were available for 90.3% (n=2568) of participants in RT/PR regions, thus yielding a final sample size of 2568.",The paper states that PDR test results were available for 2568 participants in RT/PR regions.
37976080,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. We selected 8 provincial-level administrative divisions (PLADs) in China as the study area to conduct a large multicenter observational cross-sectional study.,"The study was conducted in China, and the samples were obtained from 8 provincial-level administrative divisions within the country."
37976080,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022,"Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. Recruitment and specimen collection of HIV-positive individuals occurred in 2022 as individuals initiated ART. As a result, 84.5% (2170/2568) of participants were diagnosed in 2022, and all had their specimens collected in 2022.",The paper mentions that recruitment and specimen collection occurred in 2022.
37976080,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing. Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4–99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1–251 amino acids) [24].","The paper describes using Sanger sequencing and PCR methods without cloning the samples, hence no"
37976080,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and reverse transcriptase regions,"Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µL plasma samples using the QIAamp viral RNA mini kit (Qiagen, Hilton, Germany) according to the operational instructions. Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4-99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1-251 amino acids) [24]. The amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing.",The paper describes sequencing the protease and reverse transcriptase regions of the HIV pol gene.
37976080,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing. Extracted RNA was used for first-round polymerase chain reaction, and the products were used for second-round polymerase chain reaction amplification; both rounds were performed in 25 µL volume reactions to amplify the HIV pol gene region (HXB2: 2253-3553 nt). This covered the PR region (4-99 amino acids) and the partial RT region (1-251 amino acids) [24].",The paper explicitly mentions that the amplified products were sequenced by Sanger sequencing.
37976080,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants. Viral RNA was extracted from 200 µL plasma samples using the QIAamp viral RNA mini kit (Qiagen, Hilton, Germany) according to the operational instructions.",The paper mentions that plasma samples were obtained from study participants and used for sequencing.
37976080,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Of the participants, 3.2% (n=81) had prior ARV exposure. The minimum sample size requirements were 158–179 samples in each PLAD, assuming that 40% of collected specimens would be lost to factors such as laboratory failure. As a result, 84.5% (2170/2568) of participants were diagnosed in 2022, and all had their specimens collected in 2022. This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. We selected 8 provincial-level administrative divisions (PLADs) in China as the study area to conduct a large multicenter observational cross-sectional study. Two-stage systematic sampling in each PLAD was conducted according to the WHO PDR surveillance guidelines [23]. Provinces were stratified into the following 3 categories based on their HIV incidence rates: high (>.00 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Sichuan Province, Chongqing Municipality, and Yunnan Province), moderate (0.52–1.99 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Jiangsu Province and Zhejiang Province), and low (<0.52 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Jilin Province, Hebei Province, and Hubei Province). Guided by WHO PDR surveillance guidelines, we calculated the minimum necessary sample size by assuming the prevalence of PDR was 10% and the CI range was ±5%. We sampled 11–20 clinics in each PLAD according to the number of HIV-positive individuals starting or restarting ART in the past 12 months. The minimum sample size in each PLAD was 158–179 samples. To ensure a minimum necessary sample size, we assumed that 40% of collected specimens would be lost to factors such as laboratory failure and a design effect. Expected and actual sample sizes for each province are shown in Multimedia Appendix 1. Recruitment and specimen collection of HIV-positive individuals occurred in 2022 as individuals initiated ART. The study eligibility criteria for individuals included the following: (1) tested HIV-positive, (2) ≥18 years old, (3) started or restarted first-line ART between January and June 2022 (includes women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission of HIV, people who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis, and individuals reinitiating first-line ART), and (4) provided informed consent.","The paper mentions that 3.2% of participants had prior ARV exposure, which indicates that some individuals may have experienced virological failure."
37976080,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,We selected 8 provincial-level administrative divisions (PLADs) in China as the study area to conduct a large multicenter observational cross-sectional study.  This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. Demographic information and plasma samples were obtained from study participants.,"The study mentions it was a cross-sectional observation, not clinical trial."
37976080,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Of the participants, 3.2% (n=81) had prior ARV exposure. The minimum sample size requirements were 158–179 samples in each PLAD, assuming that 40% of collected specimens would be lost to factors such as laboratory failure. As a result, 84.5% (2170/2568) of participants were diagnosed in 2022, and all had their specimens collected in 2022. This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. We selected 8 provincial-level administrative divisions (PLADs) in China as the study area to conduct a large multicenter observational cross-sectional study. Two-stage systematic sampling in each PLAD was conducted according to the WHO PDR surveillance guidelines [23]. Provinces were stratified into the following 3 categories based on their HIV incidence rates: high (>.00 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Sichuan Province, Chongqing Municipality, and Yunnan Province), moderate (0.52–1.99 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Jiangsu Province and Zhejiang Province), and low (<0.52 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Jilin Province, Hebei Province, and Hubei Province). Guided by WHO PDR surveillance guidelines, we calculated the minimum necessary sample size by assuming the prevalence of PDR was 10% and the CI range was ±5%. We sampled 11–20 clinics in each PLAD according to the number of HIV-positive individuals starting or restarting ART in the past 12 months. The minimum sample size in each PLAD was 158–179 samples. To ensure a minimum necessary sample size, we assumed that 40% of collected specimens would be lost to factors such as laboratory failure and a design effect. Expected and actual sample sizes for each province are shown in Multimedia Appendix 1. Recruitment and specimen collection of HIV-positive individuals occurred in 2022 as individuals initiated ART. The study eligibility criteria for individuals included the following: (1) tested HIV-positive, (2) ≥18 years old, (3) started or restarted first-line ART between January and June 2022 (includes women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission of HIV, people who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis, and individuals reinitiating first-line ART), and (4) provided informed consent.","The paper states that 3.2% of participants had prior ARV exposure, which indicates that they had previously received ARV drugs."
37976080,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,NRTI and NNRTI,"Of the participants, 3.2% (n=81) had prior ARV exposure. The minimum sample size requirements were 158–179 samples in each PLAD, assuming that 40% of collected specimens would be lost to factors such as laboratory failure. As a result, 84.5% (2170/2568) of participants were diagnosed in 2022, and all had their specimens collected in 2022. This cross-sectional study was conducted using 2-stage systematic sampling according to the World Health Organization's surveillance guidelines in 8 provincial-level administrative divisions in 2022. We selected 8 provincial-level administrative divisions (PLADs) in China as the study area to conduct a large multicenter observational cross-sectional study. Two-stage systematic sampling in each PLAD was conducted according to the WHO PDR surveillance guidelines [23]. Provinces were stratified into the following 3 categories based on their HIV incidence rates: high (>.00 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Sichuan Province, Chongqing Municipality, and Yunnan Province), moderate (0.52–1.99 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Jiangsu Province and Zhejiang Province), and low (<0.52 cases per 10,000 individuals; included Jilin Province, Hebei Province, and Hubei Province). Guided by WHO PDR surveillance guidelines, we calculated the minimum necessary sample size by assuming the prevalence of PDR was 10% and the CI range was ±5%. We sampled 11–20 clinics in each PLAD according to the number of HIV-positive individuals starting or restarting ART in the past 12 months. The minimum sample size in each PLAD was 158–179 samples. To ensure a minimum necessary sample size, we assumed that 40% of collected specimens would be lost to factors such as laboratory failure and a design effect. Expected and actual sample sizes for each province are shown in Multimedia Appendix 1. Recruitment and specimen collection of HIV-positive individuals occurred in 2022 as individuals initiated ART. The study eligibility criteria for individuals included the following: (1) tested HIV-positive, (2) ≥18 years old, (3) started or restarted first-line ART between January and June 2022 (includes women exposed to ARV drugs for preventing mother-to-child transmission of HIV, people who have received pre-exposure prophylaxis, and individuals reinitiating first-line ART), and (4) provided informed consent.","The paper mentions drug resistance to nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, and protease inhibitors were detected, hence NRTI,NNRTI and PI were used."
37976080,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Abacavir, Zidovudine, Lamivudine, Stavudine, Didanosine, Tenofovir, Emtricitabine, Lopinavir/r, Atazanavir/r, Darunavir/r, Efavirenz, Nevirapine.","Of the 2568 cases studied, the prevalence of PDR to NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs were 6.3% (n=163), 1.2% (n=32), and 0.2% (n=5), respectively. NNRTI-related DR to EFV and NVP was 6.1% (n=157) and 6.3% (n=163), respectively. There were 14 NNRTI-related resistance mutations; the most common mutations were K103N/S (n=81, 3.2%), followed by V179D/E/IL (n=39, 1.5%), and E138A/G/K (n=31, 1.2%). Among NRTI-related DR, the most frequent PDR drug was abacavir (n=19, 0.7%), followed by stavudine (n=17, 0.7%), emtricitabine (n=15, 0.6%), lamivudine (n=15, 0.6%), didanosine (n=13, 0.5%), zidovudine (n=10, 0.4%), and tenofovir (n=8, 0.3%). We detected 10 NRTI-related resistance mutations, of which M184V/I (n=13, 0.5%) was the most common. Even in the PR region, 0.2% (n=5) PI-related PDR to atazanavir/r and lopinavir/r was detected, including the PI-related DRMs L90M/IM (n=3, 0.1%), N88T/D (n=1, 0.1%), and V82IM (n=1, 0.1%) [36].</p><p>These drugs were ZDV: zidovudine; ABC: abacavir; 3TC: lamivudine; FTC: emtricitabine; TDF: tenofovir disoproxil fumarate; DDI: didanosine; STV: stavudine; EFV: efavirenz; NVP: nevirapine; ATV/r: atazanavir/ritonavir; LPV/r: lopinavir/ritonavir. Specific drugs received by individuals U.S. Department of Health and Human Services recommended ARV drugs for treatment of HIV infection. Nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors: Efavirenz (EFV) or nevirapine (NVP); Abacavir (ABC) or tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) or tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) or emtricitabine (FTC) or lamivudina (3TC) (NRTIs); ATV/r atazanavir/r (PIs) or lopinavir/r or darunavir/r (PIs). Prior ARV exposed patients received drugs other than nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, NRTIs, and PIs.",The paper lists specific drugs for which drug resistance mutations were identified. These drugs correspond to those that individuals received before sequencing.
37976185,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). Disease investigation specialists attempt to interview everyone with newly diagnosed HIV to facilitate linkage to care, elicit sex and needle-sharing partners and capture sociodemographics, HIV risk, and PrEP and postexposure prophylaxis use In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department), and NYC surveillance case investigation records. HIV-related laboratory results (CD4 cell count, HIV viral load [VL], and HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted]) are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases. Resistance associated mutations were determined using the Stanford algorithm.","The paper mentions that HIV sequences (genotypes) were obtained from patient samples within 30 days of diagnosis. This indicates that the study involved sequencing HIV from patient samples as part of the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry data, where sequences and laboratory results are reported and stored for analysis of resistance mutations in relation to PrEP use history among individuals with newly diagnosed HIV in 2015–2022 and that the data were collected from people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, and had an available genotype within 30 days of diagnosis.   In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department). This also implies that the sample used had been sequenced."
37976185,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods
We used New York City surveillance and partner services data to measure the effect of PrEP use (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/tenofivir alafenamide fumarate with emtricitabine) history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV diagnosed in 2015--2022. PrEP use was categorized as ""recent"" (defined as PrEP stopped ≤90 days before diagnosis), ""past"" (PrEP stopped "">90 days before diagnosis), or ""no known use."" Resistance-associated mutations were determined using the Stanford algorithm. We used log binomial regression to generate the adjusted relative risk (aRR) of M184I/V by PrEP use history in people with or without acute HIV infection (AHI).

Abstract
Background
Drug resistance may be acquired in people starting human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) preexposure prophylaxis (PrEP) during undiagnosed infection. Population-based estimates of PrEP-related resistance are lacking.

Results
Of 4246 people with newly diagnosed HIV and a genotype obtained within ≤30 days of diagnosis, 560 (13%) had AHI; 136 (3%) reported recent and 124 (35%) past PrEP use; and 98 (2%) harbored M184I/V. In people with AHI, recent PrEP use was associated with a 6 times greater risk of M184I/V than no known use (aRR, 5.86 [95% confidence interval, 2.49--13.77). Among people without AHI, the risk of M184I/V in recent users was 7 times that in people with no known use (aRR, 7.26 [95% confidence interval, 3.98--13.24), and in past users, it was 4 times that in those with no known use (4.46 [2.15--9.24]).","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data. The focus is on the prevalence of specific mutations (M184I/V and K65R) in relation to PrEP use history and acute HIV infection, but it does not discuss any laboratory experiments or data related to drug susceptibility testing. The content discusses the sequencing and identification of mutations in patient samples but does not provide any information on how these mutations affect drug susceptibility in vitro."
37976185,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted] are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases","The paper does not mention that the sequences obtained were made publicly available. It  specifies that HIV-1 genotype sequences are stored in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry and laboratory databases, but there is no mention of these sequences being made publicly accessible via repositories like GenBank or any other database. The use of sequences is limited to surveillance and partner services data for public health purposes in New York City."
37976185,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted] are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases","The paper details the sequencing process and the use of genotypes for analysis but does not provide any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. There is no mention of depositing sequences in GenBank or any other public database, which is typically required for providing accession numbers. The focus is on the findings related to PrEP use and mutation prevalence rather than on the specifics of sequence availability."
37976185,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,4246,"Of 10 356 people with a new HIV diagnosis assigned for partner services in this time period, a total of 4246 (40%) had an available genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis and comprised our analytic population.","The paper states that out of 4246 people with newly diagnosed HIV and assigned for partner services, a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available for 4246 individuals. This indicates that samples for HIV sequencing were obtained from 4246 individuals as part of the study population, and these samples were used for the analysis of resistance mutations in relation to PrEP use history and acute HIV infection 4246 people with newly diagnosed HIV and a genotype obtained within ≤30 days of diagnosis comprised our analytic population."
37976185,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,United States,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). We used New York City surveillance and partner services data to measure the effect of PrEP use (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/tenofivir alafenamide fumarate with emtricitabine) history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV diagnosed in 2015--2022. We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry)","The paper describes a study conducted in New York City, focusing on individuals newly diagnosed with HIV in this specific location. The data sources and study population are based on NYC HIV Surveillance Registry and partner services data. There is no mention of samples obtained from other countries. The context and findings are centered around the NYC population, making it clear that the sequenced samples are from the United States."
37976185,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2015 to 2022,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry).","The paper specifies that the study included individuals with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained during these years. The focus is on the prevalence of mutations and PrEP use history in this time frame, making it clear that the samples were collected within this period."
37976185,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). Disease investigation specialists attempt to interview everyone with newly diagnosed HIV to facilitate linkage to care, elicit sex and needle-sharing partners and capture sociodemographics, HIV risk, and PrEP and postexposure prophylaxis use. In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department), and NYC surveillance case investigation records. Data are recorded on an electronic case investigation form and managed on a secure server, compliant with confidentiality and data security protocols. HIV-related laboratory results (CD4 cell count, HIV viral load [VL], and HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted]) are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases. Resistance associated mutations were determined using the Stanford algorithm. We used log binomial regression to generate the adjusted relative risk (aRR) of M184I/V by PrEP use history in people with or without acute HIV infection (AHI).","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The study describes the use of genotypes obtained within 30 days of diagnosis and the methods used for sequencing, but there is no indication that the samples were cloned. The sequencing methods mentioned (Sanger or next-generation sequencing) do not involve cloning of samples."
37976185,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene (RT and protease regions),"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). We described PrEP use history, AHI, and the prevalence of M184I/V and K65R mutations in a retrospective cohort of people with newly diagnosed HIV in 2015--2022. We measured the association of PrEP use history and timing with the presence of M184I/V at their baseline genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis, in people with or without AHI respectively.","The paper specifically discusses the sequencing of the HIV-1 genome to identify resistance-associated mutations. The mutations mentioned, such as M184I/V and K65R, are located in the reverse transcriptase (RT) and protease regions of the HIV genome. The study focuses on these specific mutations in relation to PrEP use history and acute HIV infection."
37976185,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and next-generation sequencing,"HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted] are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases. We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). Disease investigation specialists attempt to interview everyone with newly diagnosed HIV to facilitate linkage to care, elicit sex and needle-sharing partners and capture sociodemographics, HIV risk, and PrEP and postexposure prophylaxis use. In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department), and NYC surveillance case investigation records. Data are recorded on an electronic case investigation form and managed on a secure server, compliant with confidentiality and data security protocols. HIV-related laboratory results (CD4 cell count, HIV viral load [VL], and HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted]) are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases. Resistance associated mutations were determined using the Stanford algorithm. We used log binomial regression to generate the adjusted relative risk (aRR) of M184I/V by PrEP use history in people with or without acute HIV infection (AHI).",The paper mentions that HIV-1 genotype sequences were obtained using Sanger sequencing or next-generation sequencing (NGS). The study describes the use of these sequencing methods to identify resistance-associated mutations in the context of PrEP use and acute HIV infection.
37976185,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"HIV-related laboratory results (CD4 cell count, HIV viral load [VL], and HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted]) are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases. We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). Disease investigation specialists attempt to interview everyone with newly diagnosed HIV to facilitate linkage to care, elicit sex and needle-sharing partners and capture sociodemographics, HIV risk, and PrEP and postexposure prophylaxis use. In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department), and NYC surveillance case investigation records. Data are recorded on an electronic case investigation form and managed on a secure server, compliant with confidentiality and data security protocols. HIV-related laboratory results (CD4 cell count, HIV viral load [VL], and HIV-1 genotype sequences [Sanger or consensus sequences when next-generation sequencing is conducted]) are reported to the health department and stored in the Registry and laboratory databases. Resistance associated mutations were determined using the Stanford algorithm. We used log binomial regression to generate the adjusted relative risk (aRR) of M184I/V by PrEP use history in people with or without acute HIV infection (AHI).","The paper mentions that HIV-1 genotype sequences were obtained from plasma samples during or after acute HIV infection, and that the related laboratory results including HIV-1 genotype sequences were reported to the health department and stored, which means these plasma samples were sequenced."
