ARV drugs.","The paper does not specify that any sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure. The focus is on newly diagnosed individuals and their PrEP use history in relation to the development of resistance mutations, without mentioning virological failure."
37976185,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). This study used NYC HIV surveillance and partner services data, and the participants were newly diagnosed after they had been part of disease investigation services. In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department) We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022. This study used NYC HIV surveillance and partner services data, and the participants were newly diagnosed after they had been part of disease investigation services.","The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. The study is based on surveillance and partner services data in New York City, focusing on individuals newly diagnosed with HIV and their PrEP use history, without any indication of a clinical trial setting."
37976185,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"/We used New York City surveillance and partner services data to measure the effect of PrEP use (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/tenofivir alafenamide fumarate with emtricitabine) history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV diagnosed in 2015--2022. PrEP use has been systematically documented since 2015. In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department) We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). This study used NYC HIV surveillance and partner services data, and the participants were newly diagnosed after they had been part of disease investigation services, and without ARV drug history, which indicates that they had  not received ARV drugs or sequences were obtained before virological suppression.","The paper does not mention that the sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. The focus is on newly diagnosed individuals, their PrEP use history, and the prevalence of resistance mutations, without detailing any prior ARV drug history."
37976185,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"/We used New York City surveillance and partner services data to measure the effect of PrEP use (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/tenofivir alafenamide fumarate with emtricitabine) history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV diagnosed in 2015--2022. PrEP use has been systematically documented since 2015. In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department) We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). This study used NYC HIV surveillance and partner services data, and the participants were newly diagnosed after they had been part of disease investigation services, and without ARV drug history, which indicates that they had  not received ARV drugs or sequences were obtained before virological suppression.","The paper does not mention that the individuals received any drug classes prior to sample sequencing. The focus is on  the effect of PrEP use history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV newly diagnosed with PrEP use who had not  received ARV drugs, or sequences were obtained before virological suppression. The study does not provide details on the receipt of specific drug classes before sequencing."
37976185,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"/We used New York City surveillance and partner services data to measure the effect of PrEP use (tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/tenofivir alafenamide fumarate with emtricitabine) history on the baseline prevalence of M184I/V mutations in people with HIV diagnosed in 2015--2022. PrEP use has been systematically documented since 2015. In addition to client self-report, information is extracted from medical record review, provider interview, the Registry and laboratory databases, provider report form (a mechanism for providers to report new HIV diagnoses to the health department) We included people with HIV newly diagnosed between 2015 and 2022, assigned for partner services, for whom a genotype obtained within 30 days of diagnosis was available in the NYC HIV Surveillance Registry (henceforth, Registry). This study used NYC HIV surveillance and partner services data, and the participants were newly diagnosed after they had been part of disease investigation services, and without ARV drug history, which indicates that they had  not received ARV drugs or sequences were obtained before virological suppression.","The paper mentions that the participants had not  received ARV drugs, so hadn't received any specific drugs before sequencing. The focus is on newly diagnosed individuals and their PrEP use history without prior ARV drug history, and the effects of PrEP use on resistance mutations, without detailing any prior ARV drug history."
37993493,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. Mutations were interpreted using the Stanford HIVDR database.,"The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples. It mentions assessing the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Specifically, it states that patients with unsuppressed viral loads (≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples, and mutations were interpreted using the Stanford HIVDR database."
37993493,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence provided,The paper does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility data. It focuses on clinical samples and the genotypic analysis of drug resistance mutations rather than in vitro susceptibility testing. The study discusses the genotypic susceptibility scores (GSS) based on the patterns of mutations per patient but does not provide in vitro susceptibility data for the drugs tested against HIV.
37993493,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence provided,The paper does not mention whether the sequences were made publicly available. It does not provide any GenBank accession numbers or details regarding the deposition of sequences in public databases.
37993493,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,No evidence provided,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the study's findings and does not include details about the accession numbers for the sequences generated.
37993493,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,600,"In total 600 individuals participated in this survey, Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. VL testing was successful in 99.67% (598/600) of survey patients and only 33 had VL ≥ 1000 copies/mL, resulting in a viral suppression level of 94.48% (565/598, 95% CI 92.34--96.17%).","The paper states that a total of 600 individuals participated in the survey. Among these, patients with unsuppressed viral loads were tested for HIV drug resistance mutations."
37993493,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Tanzania,We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam.,"The study was conducted in Dar es Salaam, Tanzania, and all samples were obtained from this location."
37993493,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2023,Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam.,"The samples were collected between February and May 2023, as stated in the study design section."
37993493,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We used Management and Development for Health (MDH) WHO adapted protocol (Unpublished) to perform HIVDR testing using the 3500xL genetic analyser (Applied Biosystems), a capillary sequencer using Sanger-style sequencing. Analysis of mutations were performed using Stanford University HIVDR database version 9.4.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger-style sequencing and does not indicate that samples were cloned.
37993493,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, Reverse Transcriptase, Integrase","For 23 samples, protease and reverse transcriptase (RT) genotyping were successful, with 13 sequences containing RT inhibitor surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) (56.5%). No SDRM against protease inhibitors were detected. Thirty samples were successfully genotyped for integrase with 3 sequences (10.08%) containing integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) SDRMs.","The paper reports sequencing of the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes. It provides specifics on the success of genotyping in these regions and the presence of drug resistance mutations."
37993493,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger Sequencing,"Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. We used Management and Development for Health (MDH) WHO adapted protocol (Unpublished) to perform HIVDR testing using the 3500xL genetic analyser (Applied Biosystems), a capillary sequencer using Sanger-style sequencing. Analysis of mutations were performed using Stanford University HIVDR database version 9.4.","The paper mentions the use of the 3500xL genetic analyzer (Applied Biosystems), which is a capillary sequencer used for Sanger-style sequencing, the method used for HIVDR testing, standard sequencing method for retroviruses."
37993493,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples.  We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam.,"The paper specifies that plasma samples were used to test for HIVDR mutations, indicating that plasma was the clinical sample sequenced."
37993493,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam. Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples.,"The paper mentions that patients with unsuppressed viral loads (≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals experiencing virological failure."
37993493,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,We assessed the success of ART and the contribution of HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) to unsuppressed viral loads. Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam.,The paper does not indicate that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes a cross-sectional survey conducted in six clinics.
37993493,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"All adults (18 years and above) living with HIV and resident in the DUCS platform area and receiving antiretroviral therapy in CTCs found in the DUCS area were eligible to participate in the study, Between February and May 2023 a cross-sectional survey with random sampling was conducted in the six clinics in an urban cohort in Dar es Salaam. Patients with unsuppresed viral loads (local criteria viral load (VL) ≥ 1000 copies/mL) were tested for HIVDR mutations using the WHO adapted protocol for plasma samples. Mutations were interpreted using the Stanford HIVDR database.",The paper includes  all adults (18 years and above) living with HIV and resident in the DUCS platform area and receiving antiretroviral therapy in CTCs found in the DUCS area W
37993493,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Of 33 patients with high viral load (≥ 1000 copies/mL) despite taking ART, 30 (90.91%) were using a DTG-based regimen, mostly TDF + 3TC + DTG, two patients were using ABC + 3TC + LPV/r, and one was taking ABC + 3TC + ATV/r. Out of 33 patients, 32 (96.97%) had been initiated on a DTG-based regimen either as first therapy or switched from another first line, all of which because of a national policy change. Approximately, 99% of survey patients were taking TLD regimen. Many of these patients also had previous treatment changes, and two patients were switched away from DTG. The reason for previous changes is not known, however, given that all were switched from a first line suggests the previous changes might also have been policy changes or might have been related to side effects.","The paper mentions the use of various drugs, including tenofovir (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), dolutegravir (DTG), abacavir (ABC), lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), and atazanavir/ritonavir (ATV/r), indicating that participants had been exposed to NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs."
37993493,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir, Abacavir, Atazanavir, Ritonavir, Lopinavir","Of 33 patients with high viral load (≥ 1000 copies/mL) despite taking ART, 30 (90.91%) were using a DTG-based regimen, mostly TDF + 3TC + DTG, two patients were using ABC + 3TC + LPV/r, and one was taking ABC + 3TC + ATV/r. Out of 33 patients, 32 (96.97%) had been initiated on a DTG-based regimen either as first therapy or switched from another first line, all of which because of a national policy change. Approximately, 99% of survey patients were taking TLD regimen. Many of these patients also had previous treatment changes, and two patients were switched away from DTG. The reason for previous changes is not known, however, given that all were switched from a first line suggests the previous changes might also have been policy changes or might have been related to side effects.","The paper lists the specific drugs that participants were using, including tenofovir (TDF), lamivudine (3TC), dolutegravir (DTG), abacavir (ABC), lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), and atazanavir/ritonavir (ATV/r)."
