38020274,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group ( 10 ) using blood samples stored at -80˚C. The sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v8.8.","The paper discusses genotypic resistance testing (GRT) conducted on blood samples from patients, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. The sequences were used to evaluate HIV-1 mutational profiling and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) in a cohort of adolescents living with perinatal HIV in Cameroon."
38020274,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. It focuses on genotypic resistance testing using patient samples to assess drug resistance mutations in a cohort study without detailing any in vitro susceptibility results.
38020274,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences or data. It discusses the sequences obtained and their analysis but does not provide information on whether these sequences were deposited in a public database like GenBank.
38020274,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It discusses the sequencing and analysis but does not include specific accession numbers or details about data deposition.
38020274,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,311,"Overall, 311 APHI were included at the enrolment phase (T1) with 272 followed-up at 6-months (T2), and 243 at 12-months (T3).",The paper states that a cohort study was conducted among 311 adolescents living with perinatal HIV (APHI) receiving ART in Cameroon. This number represents the individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing and genotypic resistance testing.
38020274,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Cameroon,A prospective cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon.,"The paper explicitly states that the cohort study was conducted among adolescents living with perinatal HIV in Cameroon, where the sequencing and genotypic resistance testing took place."
38020274,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2018-2020,A prospective cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon.,"The paper mentions that the prospective cohort study was conducted from 2018 to 2020, during which the samples for HIV sequencing were obtained."
38020274,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of blood samples and the sequencing process without any reference to cloning.
38020274,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Reverse transcriptase and polymerase gene regions,"We observed a great diversity of HIV-1 genotypes with CRF02_AG predominance from T1-T3 with respective proportions of 69.1% (38/55), 59.4% (57/96), and 58.3% (35/60), followed by the pure subtypes F2 (7.3, 9.4, and 11.7%), A/A1 (9.1, 6.3, and 10.0%), and G (5.5, 6.3, and 6.7%).",The paper discusses the sequencing of the HIV-1 polymerase and reverse transcriptase gene regions to assess HIV-1 genetic diversity and drug resistance.
38020274,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C. The sequences obtained were assembled and edited using Recall CDC Atlanta GA USA software and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) interpreted using Stanford HIVdb.v8.8; Subtyping was done using MEGA v10 for molecular phylogeny.","The paper does not specify the exact sequencing method used. It mentions the use of an in-house protocol for genotypic resistance testing and the use of software for sequence assembly and editing, but does not provide specific details on the sequencing technology or methodology."
38020274,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood samples,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C.",The paper mentions that blood samples stored at -80˚C were used for genotypic resistance testing and sequencing.
38020274,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was carried out at each time point among participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml using an in-house protocol as previously described by our working group (10) using blood samples stored at -80˚C.","The paper discusses the sequencing and genotypic resistance testing in participants with plasma viral load (PVL) ≥1,000 RNA copies/ml, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure."
38020274,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,A cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon.,"The paper describes the study as a prospective cohort study, not a clinical trial, indicating that the patients were not part of a clinical trial."
38020274,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,A cohort-study was conducted from 2018-2020 among 311 APHI receiving ART in one of the selected health facilities within the 'Resistance Evolution among Adolescents in Yaoundé and its surroundings' (READY-study) in the Centre region of Cameroon.,"The paper mentions that the study included adolescents living with perinatal HIV who were receiving antiretroviral therapy (ART), indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
