38072961,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1,"A 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male ""reported a case of persistent CNS viral escape with recurrent symptomatic encephalitis.""","The content focuses on a single case study of a 27-year-old male patient. All the sequencing and clinical data are related to this one individual, indicating that only one person had samples obtained for sequencing."
38072961,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,A 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male,"The patient described in the case study is identified as a 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male, indicating that the samples were obtained from China."
38072961,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022,"Case presentation A 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male complained of intermittent headache, nausea, and vomiting (non-projectile vomiting three times in total) for two weeks in January 2022. ""Then, prednisone was tapered (60 mg daily followed by a reduction of 5 mg per week) and discontinued in May 2022. However, he complained of a headache relapse in June 2022.""","The case presentation mentions 2022: ""A 27-year-old HIV-positive Chinese Manchu male complained of intermittent headache, nausea, and vomiting (non-projectile vomiting three times in total) for two weeks in January 2022. One month later, the patient presented headache relief, and obvious improvement in MRI performance (Fig. 1 B, b) and laboratory tests in the CSF (Table 1). CD8 encephalitis was suspected, and thus prednisone and glycerol fructose were prescribed empirically to reduce inflammation reactions in the brain. He initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later with plasma HIV RNA 2.19 × 105 copies/mL and CD4+ T-cell count 14 cells/μL. The local doctor shifted the ART regimen to tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir without a genotypic drug resistance test, and his plasma HIV viral load was well controlled thereafter. One month after that, HIV suppression was achieved in both CSF and plasma. A complete remission of brain MRI lesions was achieved in December 2022 (Fig. 1 E, e). "" The timeline shows that the study was conducted between January 2022 to December 2022."
38072961,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger sequencing directly on the samples obtained.
38072961,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT","""the ART regimen was switched to zidovudine, lamivudine, and dolutegravir according to drug resistance tests. drug resistance tests of CSF and plasma (Sanger sequencing in Dongguan Medical Laboratory of Micro-scale and Presicion) presented identical results of major protease inhibitor (PI)-related mutations, nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-related mutations, and nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI)-related mutations (Table ​ (Table1). 1 "".","The paper mentions the detection of drug resistance mutations related to major protease inhibitor (PI)-related mutations, nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)-related mutations, and NRTI-related mutations, which correspond to the protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) genes of the HIV virus."
38072961,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,drug resistance tests of CSF and plasma (Sanger sequencing in Dongguan Medical Laboratory of Micro-scale and Presicion),The paper mentioned that Sanger sequencing was used for drug resistance testing of the CSF and plasma samples.
38072961,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"plasma, CSF","prednisone was resumed and the ART regimen was switched to zidovudine, lamivudine, and dolutegravir according to drug resistance tests. Persistent clinical recovery of symptoms, neuroimaging, and laboratory abnormalities were observed. Moreover, increased HIV replication was observed in both CNS and plasma, and further HIV drug resistance tests of CSF and plasma (Sanger sequencing in Dongguan Medical Laboratory of Micro-scale and Presicion)",The paper discusses the sequencing of both CSF and plasma samples for HIV RNA and drug resistance testing.
38072961,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"A 27-year-old man with HIV infection complained of recurrent headaches during the last year. During the gradual tapering of prednisone, his headache relapsed, and booming viral loads were detected in both CSF (4580 copies/mL) and plasma (340 copies/mL) with consistent drug-resistant mutations. The origin of CSF escape viral populations is either persistently replicating CNS reservoirs (likely in macrophages/microglia) or transient clonally expanded trafficking of infected T cells in the CNS [3]. Patients with CSF HIV escape may be symptomatic or asymptomatic [3, 5]. Active management was recommended for cases with symptomatic CSF HIV escape [6].","The paper discusses a patient who experienced treatment failure and had high viral loads while on ART, indicating virological failure."
38072961,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention that the patient was part of a clinical trial; the study focuses exclusively on a single case report without trial involvement.
38072961,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"He initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later. the local doctor shifted the ART regimen to tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir without a genotypic drug resistance test, and his plasma HIV viral load was well controlled thereafter.","The patient discussed in the paper had been on antiretroviral therapy (ART) for several years, indicating that he had received ARV drugs before the sample sequencing."
38072961,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","He initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later with plasma HIV RNA 2.19 × 105 copies/mL and CD4+ T-cell count 14 cells/μL. The local doctor shifted the ART regimen to tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir without a genotypic drug resistance test, and his plasma HIV viral load was well controlled thereafter. Prednisone 60 mg once daily was initiated to reduce intracranial inflammation, followed by a good clinical response, with CSF HIV RNA still detectable (31.1 copies/mL). During the gradual tapering of prednisone, his headache relapsed, and booming viral loads were detected in both CSF (4580 copies/mL) and plasma (340 copies/mL) with consistent drug-resistant mutations. Thereupon, prednisone was resumed and the ART regimen was switched to zidovudine, lamivudine, and dolutegravir according to drug resistance tests. Persistent clinical recovery of symptoms, neuroimaging, and laboratory abnormalities were observed in the follow-up visits.","The patient had been on various ART regimens that included drugs from the NRTI (tenofovir, lamivudine, zidovudine), NNRTI (efavirenz), and PI (lopinavir/ritonavir) classes."
38072961,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Efavirenz, Lopinavir, Ritonavir, Zidovudine, Dolutegravir","He initiated ART with tenofovir, lamivudine, and efavirenz, but treatment failure occurred one year later with plasma HIV RNA 2.19 × 105 copies/mL and CD4+ T-cell count 14 cells/μL. The local doctor shifted the ART regimen to tenofovir, lamivudine, lopinavir/ritonavir without a genotypic drug resistance test, and his plasma HIV viral load was well controlled thereafter. Prednisone 60 mg once daily was initiated to reduce intracranial inflammation, followed by a good clinical response, with CSF HIV RNA still detectable (31.1 copies/mL). During the gradual tapering of prednisone, his headache relapsed, and booming viral loads were detected in both CSF (4580 copies/mL) and plasma (340 copies/mL) with consistent drug-resistant mutations. Thereupon, prednisone was resumed and the ART regimen was switched to zidovudine, lamivudine, and dolutegravir according to drug resistance tests. Persistent clinical recovery of symptoms, neuroimaging, and laboratory abnormalities were observed in the follow-up visits.","The paper details the specific drugs the patient was treated with over time, including tenofovir, lamivudine, efavirenz, lopinavir, ritonavir, zidovudine, and dolutegravir."
38090027,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,This paper does not discuss the sequencing of HIV or the reporting of sequences from patient samples. It focuses on the efficacy and safety of a specific HIV treatment regimen without mentioning any genetic sequencing data.,"The content of the paper primarily discusses treatment outcomes, efficacy, and safety of the BFTAF regimen in a cohort of individuals living with HIV. There are no mentions of sequencing methodologies, patient samples for sequencing, or related data, indicating that the study does not report HIV sequences from patient samples."
38090027,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,The paper discusses the effectiveness and tolerability of a treatment regimen for HIV but does not provide any in vitro drug susceptibility data or results from susceptibility testing.,"The content of the paper focuses on clinical outcomes and observational data regarding the efficacy and safety of the BFTAF regimen. There are no references to in vitro testing, susceptibility assays, or related data, indicating that the study does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data."
38090027,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences or databases for the data presented. It focuses on treatment outcomes rather than genetic data sharing.,"The paper does not indicate that any sequences were generated or made publicly available. It focuses on clinical data and outcomes rather than genetic sequencing, implying that there are no publicly available sequences from this study."
38090027,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,N/A,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers since it does not report any sequenced HIV isolates or related genetic data.,"The content of the paper does not include any references to GenBank accession numbers or sequenced samples, as it primarily discusses clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen without involving genetic data."
38090027,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The paper does not report any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing, as it does not involve sequencing in its study.","The study focuses on the clinical outcomes of a treatment regimen in a cohort of individuals living with HIV, and there is no indication that samples were obtained for sequencing, resulting in a count of zero."
38090027,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,N/A,No samples were sequenced in this paper as it does not report any sequencing data or involved countries.,"The content of the paper does not involve any sequencing of samples, therefore there are no countries from which samples were obtained."
38090027,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,N/A,"The paper does not provide any information regarding the years from which sequenced samples were obtained, as no sequencing was conducted.","The study  does not report any sequencing or the years associated with such samples, indicating that there is no data regarding the years from which samples were obtained."
38090027,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,N/A,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples as it does not involve sequencing.,"The study does not involve any sequencing or cloning of samples, hence this question is not applicable."
38090027,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,None,The paper does not report any sequencing of HIV genes as it does not include genetic data.,"The content of the paper focuses on clinical outcomes, and there are no mentions of specific HIV genes being sequenced as it does not involve sequencing."
38090027,10,What method was used for sequencing?,N/A,The paper does not report any sequencing methods as it does not involve sequencing of HIV.,"The study does not discuss any sequencing methods or technologies, as it does not involve the sequencing of HIV."
38090027,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,None,"The paper does not report any type of samples being sequenced, as it does not involve sequencing.","The study does not involve sequencing of any samples, hence there is no information regarding the type of samples."
38090027,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,The paper discusses virological failure in the context of treatment outcomes but does not report any sequences obtained from individuals experiencing virological failure.,"The content of the paper focuses on treatment outcomes and virological failure, but there are no reports of sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure."
38090027,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,The paper does not mention that patients were part of a clinical trial; it is a cohort study of individuals who switched to a new treatment regimen.,"The study is described as a retrospective cohort analysis, not a clinical trial, indicating that the patients were not part of a clinical trial."
38090027,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"In our cohort, all participants were treatment-experienced and had prior ARV exposure. However, the paper does not report specific sequenced HIV isolates or genetic data.","The study discusses treatment-experienced individuals, but it does not report any sequences from these individuals, focusing instead on treatment outcomes."
38090027,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,N/A,"The paper does not report sequencing and hence, does not provide information on drug classes received by individuals before sequencing.","The study does not involve sequencing, therefore it does not report any information related to drug classes received."
38090027,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,N/A,"The paper does not report sequencing and hence, does not provide information on specific drugs received by individuals before sequencing.","The study does not mention any sequencing, therefore it does not detail any specific drugs received."
38140553,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"This study seeks to identify and analyze HIV-1 recombinants in Russia during the last decade of the epidemic. A comprehensive examination was conducted, encompassing 3178 partial pol sequences. We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data.","The paper discusses the identification and analysis of HIV-1 recombinants in Russia using a large dataset of HIV-1 pol sequences, indicating that the sequences were obtained from patient samples."
38140553,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This study seeks to identify and analyze HIV-1 recombinants in Russia during the last decade of the epidemic. A comprehensive examination was conducted, encompassing 3178 partial pol sequences. The study also involved phylogenetic analysis to trace the origins of the identified recombinants. Primary resistance (PrimDR) prevalence and Drug Resistance Mutations (DRMs) were assessed.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results, focusing instead on genetic diversity and recombination of HIV-1 in Russian populations."
38140553,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,All nucleotide sequences obtained in the study were deposited in the international database of the Los Alamos laboratory.,"The paper explicitly states that all nucleotide sequences obtained in the study were deposited in the international database of the Los Alamos laboratory, indicating that they were made publicly available."
38140553,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,K03455,"Sanger-based sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3554 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455) was performed.","The paper mentions the GenBank accession number K03455 for the HXB-2 strain, which corresponds to the sequences analyzed in the study."
38140553,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,3178,The genotyping results of 3178 HIV-infected patients from seven Russian federal districts (FDs) were examined.,"The Results section of the paper states that 3178 HIV-infected patients had their sequences examined, indicating the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing."
38140553,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Russia,"This study seeks to identify and analyze HIV-1 recombinants in Russia during the last decade of the epidemic. The material of the study were blood plasma collections (n = 2502), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes) collections (n = 613), and frozen whole blood collections (n = 63) obtained from HIV-infected patients at federal and regional centers for HIV/AIDS prevention (AIDS Centers) as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia in the period from 2011 to 2020.","The paper mentions Russia as the country where the study was conducted, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
38140553,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011 to 2020,"The material of the study were blood plasma collections (n = 2502), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes) collections (n = 613), and frozen whole blood collections (n = 63) obtained from HIV-infected patients at federal and regional centers for HIV/AIDS prevention (AIDS Centers) as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia in the period from 2011 to 2020.","The paper specifies that the samples were obtained during the period from 2011 to 2020, indicating the years from which the sequenced samples were collected."
38140553,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The ViroSeqTM HIV-1 Genotyping System kit (Celera Diagnostics, Alameda, CA, USA) was used for RNA extraction from blood plasma samples. Commercial kit (the QIAamp DNA Blood Mini Kit (“QIAGEN”, Hilden, Germany)) was used for DNA extraction from lymphocytes and frozen whole blood. Amplification of HIV-1 pol gene fragments was performed automatically by nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR) method according to the protocols presented in Table 1. PCR conditions are presented in Supplemental Table S1. In case of working with HIV-1 RNA, the first step involved performing a reverse transcription reaction as described in the ViroSeq HIV-1 Genotyping System user manual [23].","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, focusing instead on direct sequencing of the amplified HIV-1 pol gene fragments."
38140553,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,HIV-1 protease and part of the reverse transcriptase.,We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data. The sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR) and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT).,"The paper specifies that the sequences covered the HIV-1 protease (PR) and part of the reverse transcriptase (RT), indicating the genes that were sequenced."
38140553,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Sanger-based sequencing of the HIV pol gene regions encoding the PR-RT (2253–3554 bp according to the HXB-2 strain, GenBank accession number K03455) was performed. The sequencing reaction in the ViroSeq system was performed according to the manufacturer’s protocol.","The paper describes the use of Sanger-based sequencing for the HIV pol gene regions, indicating the sequencing method used."
38140553,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Blood plasma, peripheral blood mononuclear cells, frozen whole blood","The material of the study were blood plasma collections (n = 2502), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes) collections (n = 613), and frozen whole blood collections (n = 63) obtained from HIV-infected patients at federal and regional centers for HIV/AIDS prevention (AIDS Centers) as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia in the period from 2011 to 2020.","The paper mentions that blood plasma, peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes), and frozen whole blood were collected and sequenced, indicating the type of samples sequenced."
38140553,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In this study, we performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of “pure” subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University’s HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR) [33]. Since we could not estimate the duration of patients’ infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, and only knew that they were not receiving ART at the time of genotype analysis, we decided not to call this pretreatment drug resistance or transmitted drug resistance, and introduced the general name of primary resistance (PrimDR) in this context.","The paper states they performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, which indicates sequences were not from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen."
38140553,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The material of the study were blood plasma collections (n = 2502), peripheral blood mononuclear cells (lymphocytes) collections (n = 613), and frozen whole blood collections (n = 63) obtained from HIV-infected patients at federal and regional centers for HIV/AIDS prevention (AIDS Centers) as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia in the period from 2011 to 2020. We analyzed sequences and their associated demographic and epidemiological data. The study population had a median age of 35.0 years, ranging from 24.6 to 45.4 years. The population exhibited an aging trend, primarily comprising individuals of working age, with a range of 23.0 to 48.6 years. The main risk factor was heterosexual contacts (1642, 51.7%), followed by intravenous drug usage (1196, 37.6%), MSM contacts (148, 4.7%), and mother-to-child transmission (113, 3.6%). Out of the total patient count, 65 individuals (2.0%) did not have a clearly identified risk factor.","The paper does not mention whether the patients were part of a clinical trial, it instead focuses on the analysis of samples collected as part of molecular genetic monitoring of the HIV epidemic in Russia."
38140553,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"In this study, we performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of “pure” subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University’s HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR) [33]. Since we could not estimate the duration of patients’ infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, and only knew that they were not receiving ART at the time of genotype analysis, we decided not to call this pretreatment drug resistance or transmitted drug resistance, and introduced the general name of primary resistance (PrimDR) in this context.","The paper mentioned that they conducted the analysis on patients not on ART, which means the sequences were not from patients who had previously received ARV drugs."
38140553,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In this study, we performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of “pure” subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University’s HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR) [33]. Since we could not estimate the duration of patients’ infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, and only knew that they were not receiving ART at the time of genotype analysis, we decided not to call this pretreatment drug resistance or transmitted drug resistance, and introduced the general name of primary resistance (PrimDR) in this context.","The paper states they performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, meaning they were not on any antiretroviral drugs."
38140553,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In this study, we performed drug resistance analysis among HIV-infected patients not on ART, infected with both viruses of “pure” subtypes and its recombinant forms. The analysis utilized Stanford University’s HIVdb database tool and the Calibrated Population Resistance Tool (CPR) [33]. Since we could not estimate the duration of patients’ infection and the possibility of their treatment in the past, and only knew that they were not receiving ART at the time of genotype analysis, we decided not to call this pretreatment drug resistance or transmitted drug resistance, and introduced the general name of primary resistance (PrimDR) in this context.","The paper mentions that they analyzed among HIV-infected patients not on ART, which means they were not on any antiretroviral drugs."
38140649,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia.","The paper involves the investigation of genetic diversity and the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) in a cohort of 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia. This implies that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
38140649,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019--2022. The results of this research confirmed a high overall prevalence of TDR of 16.7%.",The paper discusses the prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) mutations among HIV-1 infected individuals but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. The focus is on genetic diversity and resistance mutations rather than susceptibility assays.
38140649,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,All sequences obtained in this study by SS were submitted to the GeneBank nucleotide sequence database under accession numbers OR605755-OR606279 and OR613869-OR613875. Sequences generated by DS are available from the ArrayExpress database at EMBL-EBI under accession number E-MTAB-13563.,The paper explicitly states that all sequences obtained in this study by Sanger sequencing (SS) were submitted to the GenBank nucleotide sequence database under specific accession numbers. It also mentions that sequences generated by deep sequencing (DS) are available from the ArrayExpress database at EMBL-EBI under a designated accession number.
38140649,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"OR605755-OR606279, OR613869-OR613875, E-MTAB-13563",All sequences obtained in this study by SS were submitted to the GeneBank nucleotide sequence database under accession numbers OR605755-OR606279 and OR613869-OR613875.,The paper provides specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequences obtained in the study. These numbers are listed in the results and methods sections where the sequencing and submission processes are described.
38140649,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,270,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia.","The paper states that 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia were included in the study. This number represents the individuals from whom the samples for HIV sequencing were obtained."
38140649,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Croatia,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia. The study was conducted at the Department for Immunological and Molecular Diagnostics at UHID, Croatia.","The paper mentions that the study was conducted at the Croatian Reference Centre for HIV/AIDS and involved patients from Croatia. Therefore, the sequenced samples were obtained from Croatia."
38140649,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022,"This study was conducted at the Department for Immunological and Molecular Diagnostics at UHID. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID. We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019--2022.","The study period is explicitly mentioned as 2019 to 2022, during which the samples were collected and analyzed."
38140649,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"We compared deep sequencing (DS) with Sanger sequencing (SS) on 60 randomly selected samples and identified additional surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) in 49 of them. Amplification of the HIV-1 pol gene was performed for two regions in two separate reactions: (1) sequencing of the HIV-1 protease and partial sequencing of the reverse transcriptase gene; and (2) sequencing of the HIV-1 integrase gene. The first round of amplification was performed by using the SuperScript III One-Step RT-PCR System with Platinum Taq (Invitrogen, Carlsbad, CA, USA) and a region-specific primer set while nested PCR was carried out using AmpliTaq Gold™ DNA Polymerase with Gold Buffer and MgCl2 (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA) with an inner primer set. Nested PCR amplicons were purified and sequenced with a BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit.","The paper does not mention any process of clonal sequencing. It describes the use of Sanger sequencing and deep sequencing (NGS) methods, which do not require cloning of samples prior to sequencing."
38140649,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. The IN region for six people failed to amplify. HIV-1 strains were defined as resistant if carrying at least one SDRM The overall prevalence was defined as the percentage of patients with SDRMs. The prevalence of TDR for the different drug classes (NRTI, NNRTI, PI, and INSTI), was defined in the same matter. We compared deep sequencing (DS) with Sanger sequencing (SS) on 60 randomly selected samples and identified additional surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) in 49 of them. Amplification of the HIV-1 pol gene was performed for two regions in two separate reactions: (1) sequencing of the HIV-1 protease and partial sequencing of the reverse transcriptase gene; and (2) sequencing of the HIV-1 integrase gene.","The paper mentions sequencing of the HIV-1 protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) regions. These genes are sequenced to analyze drug resistance mutations."
38140649,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing and deep sequencing (NGS),"We compared deep sequencing (DS) with Sanger sequencing (SS) on 60 randomly selected samples and identified additional surveillance drug resistance mutations (SDRMs) in 49 of them. Nested PCR amplicons were purified and sequenced with a BigDye Terminator v3.1 Cycle Sequencing Kit (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA, USA) with a set of four primers for both sequencing regions to obtain bidirectional sequences (Supplementary Table S1).",The paper describes the use of both Sanger sequencing (SS) and deep sequencing (DS) methods for analyzing the samples. Both methods are mentioned in the context of comparing their effectiveness in detecting drug resistance mutations.
38140649,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID. We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022.",The paper specifies that HIV-1 RNA plasma samples were used for sequencing. Plasma is the clinical specimen from which the HIV-1 RNA was extracted and analyzed.
38140649,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. All newly diagnosed treatment-naïve PLHIV over 18 years who entered clinical care at UHID in the period between January 2019 and December 2022 with plasma viraemia > 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies per ml were included in this study. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID.","The paper focuses on newly diagnosed, treatment-naïve individuals, which means these individuals had not received any antiretroviral therapy yet. Thus, there were no sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure."
38140649,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,This study was conducted at the Department for Immunological and Molecular Diagnostics at UHID. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID. All newly diagnosed treatment-naïve PLHIV over 18 years who entered clinical care at UHID in the period between January 2019 and December 2022 with plasma viraemia > 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies per ml were included in this study.,"The paper does not mention that patients were part of a clinical trial. It describes the study as a population-based investigation of newly diagnosed, treatment-naïve individuals."
38140649,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. All newly diagnosed treatment-naïve PLHIV over 18 years who entered clinical care at UHID in the period between January 2019 and December 2022 with plasma viraemia > 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies per ml were included in this study. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID.","The paper focuses on newly diagnosed, treatment-naïve individuals, which means these individuals had not received any antiretroviral therapy yet. Thus, there were no sequences obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
38140649,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. All newly diagnosed treatment-naïve PLHIV over 18 years who entered clinical care at UHID in the period between January 2019 and December 2022 with plasma viraemia > 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies per ml were included in this study. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID.","The paper focuses on newly diagnosed, treatment-naïve individuals, which means these individuals had not received any antiretroviral therapy yet. Thus, they had not received any drug classes before sample sequencing."
38140649,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We investigated the genetic diversity and prevalence of transmitted drug resistance (TDR) followed by phylogenetic analysis in 270 HIV-1 infected, treatment-naïve individuals from Croatia in the period 2019–2022. All newly diagnosed treatment-naïve PLHIV over 18 years who entered clinical care at UHID in the period between January 2019 and December 2022 with plasma viraemia > 1000 HIV-1 RNA copies per ml were included in this study. Data collection and analysis were performed on HIV-1 RNA plasma samples collected from newly diagnosed HIV patients over a four-year period during the diagnostic process and clinical follow-up at UHID.","The paper focuses on newly diagnosed, treatment-naïve individuals, which means these individuals had not received any antiretroviral therapy yet. Thus, they had not received any specific drugs before sample sequencing."
38140667,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"A Comprehensive Literature Review of Treatment-Emergent Integrase Resistance with Dolutegravir-Based Regimens in Real-World Settings. Methods 2.1. Literature Review We performed a comprehensive search of the National Center for Biotechnology Information PubMed, Embase (ProQuest), and Cochrane databases for all English-language papers as well as databases and congress abstract books for all English-language congress presentation abstracts, using the search strings listed in Tables S1--S3, last dated 25 July 2023. A post hoc search was performed to include presentations from 2 additional congresses through 20 October 2023.
Papers and abstracts were reviewed in multiple stages. First, duplicate publications were excluded, and the remaining publications were filtered by relevant study type (real-world evidence study, case study, or systematic review/meta-analysis [inclusive of observational studies]). Case studies; systematic reviews; and cohort studies that did not identify risk factors for treatment-emergent resistance to DTG (such as reporting use of DTG monotherapy; lack of viral load testing; or individuals with prior VF, suboptimal adherence, or with extensive treatment experience) were excluded.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that does not report original research data. It discusses previously published studies on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens but does not provide new data on HIV sequences from patient samples. The methods section details how the authors conducted their literature search and data extraction, but there is no mention of obtaining new patient samples or sequencing data from patients in this study. Instead, it focuses on the analysis of existing data from other studies regarding resistance mutations in HIV treated with dolutegravir-based regimens. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria of reporting HIV sequences from patient samples, as no new sequences were generated or shared publically in this review article."
38140667,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"A Comprehensive Literature Review of Treatment-Emergent Integrase Resistance with Dolutegravir-Based Regimens in Real-World Settings. Methods 2.1. Literature Review We performed a comprehensive search of the National Center for Biotechnology Information PubMed, Embase (ProQuest), and Cochrane databases for all English-language papers as well as databases and congress abstract books for all English-language congress presentation abstracts, using the search strings listed in Tables S1--S3, last dated 25 July 2023. A post hoc search was performed to include presentations from 2 additional congresses through 20 October 2023.
Papers and abstracts were reviewed in multiple stages. First, duplicate publications were excluded, and the remaining publications were filtered by relevant study type (real-world evidence study, case study, or systematic review/meta-analysis [inclusive of observational studies]). Case studies; systematic reviews; and cohort studies that did not identify risk factors for treatment-emergent resistance to DTG (such as reporting use of DTG monotherapy; lack of viral load testing; or individuals with prior VF, suboptimal adherence, or with extensive treatment experience) were excluded.","The paper is a comprehensive review of existing literature on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens in real-world settings. It does not present new experimental data or in vitro drug susceptibility results. Instead, it synthesizes findings from multiple studies regarding resistance mutations and their prevalence in various cohorts. The focus is on clinical outcomes and resistance profiles observed in previously published studies, rather than on laboratory-based susceptibility testing of the drugs. Therefore, it does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data."
38140667,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"A Comprehensive Literature Review of Treatment-Emergent Integrase Resistance with Dolutegravir-Based Regimens in Real-World Settings. Methods 2.1. Literature Review We performed a comprehensive search of the National Center for Biotechnology Information PubMed, Embase (ProQuest), and Cochrane databases for all English-language papers as well as databases and congress abstract books for all English-language congress presentation abstracts, using the search strings listed in Tables S1--S3, last dated 25 July 2023. A post hoc search was performed to include presentations from 2 additional congresses through 20 October 2023.
Papers and abstracts were reviewed in multiple stages. First, duplicate publications were excluded, and the remaining publications were filtered by relevant study type (real-world evidence study, case study, or systematic review/meta-analysis [inclusive of observational studies]). Case studies; systematic reviews; and cohort studies that did not identify risk factors for treatment-emergent resistance to DTG (such as reporting use of DTG monotherapy; lack of viral load testing; or individuals with prior VF, suboptimal adherence, or with extensive treatment experience) were excluded.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that does not report original research data. It discusses previously published studies on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens but does not provide new data on HIV sequences from patient samples. The methods section details how the authors conducted their literature search and data extraction, but there is no mention of obtaining new patient samples or sequencing data from patients in this study. Instead, it focuses on the analysis of existing data from other studies regarding resistance mutations in HIV treated with dolutegravir-based regimens. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria of reporting HIV sequences from patient samples, as no new sequences were generated or shared publically in this review article."
