38140667,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The study is a comprehensive review of existing literature focused on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens in real-world settings. It does not provide any new sequencing data or protocols regarding cloning of samples prior to sequencing, as it is not an original research study but rather a synthesis of previously published data.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that does not report original research data. It discusses previously published studies on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens but does not provide new data on HIV sequences from patient samples. The methods section details how the authors conducted their literature search and data extraction, but there is no mention of obtaining new patient samples or sequencing data from patients in this study. Instead, it focuses on the analysis of existing data from other studies regarding resistance mutations in HIV treated with dolutegravir-based regimens. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria of reporting whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing, as no new sequences were generated or shared publically in this review article."
38140667,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,No sequences were reported.,"The study is a comprehensive review of existing literature focused on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens in real-world settings. It does not provide any sequencing data or information on specific HIV genes sequenced, as it is not an original research study but rather a synthesis of previously published data.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that discusses treatment-emergent integrase resistance with dolutegravir-based regimens. It focuses on the mutations detected at specific loci in the integrase gene that confer resistance to dolutegravir and other integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs). While it mentions the detection of mutations in the integrase gene, it does not provide detailed information on the sequencing of the entire gene or other genes. The discussion is primarily on the resistance mutations themselves rather than the sequencing process or the specific regions sequenced. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria of reporting which HIV genes were sequenced."
38140667,10,What method was used for sequencing?,No sequencing methods were reported.,"The study is a comprehensive review of existing literature focused on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens in real-world settings. It does not provide any sequencing methods or protocols, as it is not an original research study but rather a synthesis of previously published data.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that discusses treatment-emergent integrase resistance with dolutegravir-based regimens. It focuses on the mutations detected at specific loci in the integrase gene that confer resistance to dolutegravir and other integrase strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs). While it mentions the detection of mutations in the integrase gene, it does not provide detailed information on the sequencing method used. The discussion is primarily on the resistance mutations themselves rather than the sequencing process or the specific methods used for sequencing. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria of reporting the sequencing method."
38140667,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,None,"The study is a comprehensive review of existing literature focused on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens in real-world settings. It does not provide any new sequencing data or information on the type of samples sequenced, as it is not an original research study but rather a synthesis of previously published data.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that does not report original research data. It discusses previously published studies on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens but does not provide new data on HIV sequences from patient samples. The methods section details how the authors conducted their literature search and data extraction, but there is no mention of obtaining new patient samples or sequencing data from patients in this study. Instead, it focuses on the analysis of existing data from other studies regarding resistance mutations in HIV treated with dolutegravir-based regimens. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria of reporting the type of samples sequenced."
38140667,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"A Comprehensive Literature Review of Treatment-Emergent Integrase Resistance with Dolutegravir-Based Regimens in Real-World Settings. Abstract After a decade of dolutegravir (DTG) use in various antiretroviral therapy combinations and in diverse populations globally, it is critical to identify HIV strains with reduced drug susceptibility and monitor emergent resistance in people living with HIV who experience virologic failure while on DTG-based regimens. We searched the PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane databases to identify studies that reported DTG resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) emerging under selection pressure. Our review showed that RAMs conferring resistance to DTG were rare in 2-drug and 3-drug regimens used in real-world cohorts, corroborating data from clinical trials.","The paper discusses the emergence of resistance mutations in individuals living with HIV who experience virological failure (VF) while on dolutegravir (DTG)-based regimens. It mentions that RAMs conferring resistance to DTG were rare in 2-drug and 3-drug regimens used in real-world cohorts. These statements imply that resistance was detected in individuals with virological failure, but do not provide any sequence data."
38140667,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Method 2.1. Literature Review We performed a comprehensive search of the National Center for Biotechnology Information PubMed, Embase (ProQuest), and Cochrane databases for all English-language papers as well as databases and congress abstract books for all English-language congress presentation abstracts, using the search strings listed in Tables S1--S3, last dated 25 July 2023. A post hoc search was performed to include presentations from 2 additional congresses through 20 October 2023.
Papers and abstracts were reviewed in multiple stages. First, duplicate publications were excluded, and the remaining publications were filtered by relevant study type (real-world evidence study, case study, or systematic review/meta-analysis [inclusive of observational studies]). Case studies; systematic reviews; and cohort studies that did not identify risk factors for treatment-emergent resistance to DTG (such as reporting use of DTG monotherapy; lack of viral load testing; or individuals with prior VF, suboptimal adherence, or with extensive treatment experience) were excluded. The remaining studies were then included in subsequent rounds of review if they discussed drug resistance, then direct or likely INSTI RAMs, then confirmed/likely clinical failure of any INSTI regimen to treatment-emergent resistance mutations.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that does not involve original research or clinical trials. It discusses previously published studies on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens in real-world settings. The methods section details how the authors conducted their literature search and data extraction, but there is no mention of conducting new clinical trials or involving patients in a clinical trial setting. Instead, it focuses on the analysis of existing data from other studies regarding resistance mutations in HIV treated with dolutegravir-based regimens. Therefore, the patients were not in a clinical trial."
38140667,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"A Comprehensive Literature Review of Treatment-Emergent Integrase Resistance with Dolutegravir-Based Regimens in Real-World Settings. Methods 2.1. Literature Review We performed a comprehensive search of the National Center for Biotechnology Information PubMed, Embase (ProQuest), and Cochrane databases for all English-language papers as well as databases and congress abstract books for all English-language congress presentation abstracts, using the search strings listed in Tables S1--S3, last dated 25 July 2023. A post hoc search was performed to include presentations from 2 additional congresses through 20 October 2023.
Papers and abstracts were reviewed in multiple stages. First, duplicate publications were excluded, and the remaining publications were filtered by relevant study type (real-world evidence study, case study, or systematic review/meta-analysis [inclusive of observational studies]). Case studies; systematic reviews; and cohort studies that did not identify risk factors for treatment-emergent resistance to DTG (such as reporting use of DTG monotherapy; lack of viral load testing; or individuals with prior VF, suboptimal adherence, or with extensive treatment experience) were excluded. The remaining studies were then included in subsequent rounds of review if they discussed drug resistance, then direct or likely INSTI RAMs, then confirmed/likely clinical failure of any INSTI regimen to treatment-emergent resistance mutations.","The paper is a comprehensive literature review that does not report original research data. It discusses previously published studies on treatment-emergent resistance to dolutegravir-based regimens but does not provide new data on HIV sequences from patient samples. The methods section details how the authors conducted their literature search and data extraction, but there is no mention of obtaining new patient samples or sequencing data from patients in this study. Instead, it focuses on the analysis of existing data from other studies regarding resistance mutations in HIV treated with dolutegravir-based regimens. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria of reporting HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
38140667,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Integrase inhibitors, NNRTIs, NRTIs","After a decade of dolutegravir (DTG) use in various antiretroviral therapy combinations and in diverse populations globally, it is critical to identify HIV strains with reduced drug susceptibility and monitor emergent resistance in people living with HIV who experience virologic failure while on DTG-based regimens. Our review showed that RAMs conferring resistance to DTG were rare in 2-drug and 3-drug regimens used in real-world cohorts, corroborating data from clinical trials. The RAMs detected after virologic failure, often in heavily treatment-experienced individuals with prior exposure to integrase strand transfer inhibitors, were G118R, E138K, G140A/C/R/S, Q148H/K/R, N155H, and R263K.","The paper discusses the use of dolutegravir (DTG)-based regimens, which are integrase inhibitors.  It also mentions the detection of nucleoside/tide (NRTI) and non-nucleoside (NNRTI) reverse transcriptase mutations in individuals who experienced virologic failure on DTG-based regimens, implying prior exposure to these drug classes. Therefore the individuals had been previously exposed to integrase inhibitors, NRTIs, and NNRTIs."
38140667,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Dolutegravir, lamivudine, abacavir, rilpivirine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, emtricitabine","A Comprehensive Literature Review of Treatment-Emergent Integrase Resistance with Dolutegravir-Based Regimens in Real-World Settings. Abstract After a decade of dolutegravir (DTG) use in various antiretroviral therapy combinations and in diverse populations globally, it is critical to identify HIV strains with reduced drug susceptibility and monitor emergent resistance in people living with HIV who experience virologic failure while on DTG-based regimens. Our review showed that RAMs conferring resistance to DTG were rare in 2-drug and 3-drug regimens used in real-world cohorts, corroborating data from clinical trials. In a Canadian database study of 928 individuals who started INSTI-based regimens between 2012 and 2014, 392 were on a DTG-based regimen with a 3TC + ABC or TDF + FTC/3TC backbone. A study based on database searches investigated 113 treatment-naive individuals with failure of first-line TLD therapy in Brazil. In Tanzania in March 2019, eligible children (n = 92) and adults (n = 45) with high-level viremia (viral load ≥1000 copies/mL) on ART were surveyed for prevalence and patterns of acquired RAMs. Dolutegravir/Lamivudine/Abacavir (DTG/3TC/ABC) Two large cohort studies described VF and emergent resistance with DTG/3TC/ABC. One multicenter study in France evaluated RAMs in people living with HIV and on first- or second-line INSTI-based ART from 2019 to 2022. Of the 3219 individuals receiving DTG-based regimens in the analysis, 179 (5.6%) experienced VF and 24 (0.75%) had treatment-emergent INSTI RAMs (DTG/3TC/ABC, 11/1709 \\[0.64\	extbackslash%\	extbackslash]; DTG + 3TC, 6/644 \\[0.93\	extbackslash%\	extbackslash]; DTG + RPV, 7/866 \\[0.81\	extbackslash%\	extbackslash]): G140S, Q148H, E92K, and N155H. Additionally, 7 individuals failing first-line DTG-based regimens had emergent M184V/I mutations and 14 failing second-line DTG-based regimens had emergent NRTI (n = 7, M184V) and NNRTI (n = 3, E138A; n = 1 each, M230L, K101E, Y181C, V179L) mutations at VF. In Mozambique the transition from Dolutegravir (DTG) and Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/Emtricitabine (TDF/FTC) to Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/Lamivudine/Dolutegravir (TLD) was evaluated in 716 people with HIV between August 2021 and February 2022. After 1 year of treatment, 216 individuals experienced virologic failure, and of those, 169 underwent genotyping. Intermediate- or high-level DTG resistance was seen in 35 individuals who underwent resistance testing (21.0%).","The paper mentions specific drugs used in the regimens of individuals who experienced virologic failure and developed resistance mutations. For example, it discusses the use of dolutegravir (DTG), lamivudine (3TC), abacavir (ABC), rilpivirine (RPV), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF), and emtricitabine (FTC) in various studies. These drugs are part of the treatment regimens that the individuals in the reviewed studies had received before the emergence of resistance mutations. Therefore, while the paper does not provide a specific list of all drugs received by individuals before sequencing, it does imply that these drugs were part of the treatment history of the cohorts studied."
