38152686,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, Integrase inhibitors","The patient received NRTI (Tenofovir/Lamivudine), NNRTI (Efavirenz), and Integrase inhibitors (Dolutegravir) as part of his antiretroviral therapy.","The paper lists the specific drugs received, which fall under these drug classes."
38152686,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir, Efavirenz","The patient's antiretroviral therapy included Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir, and Efavirenz at various times during his treatment.",The paper details the specific drugs administered to the patient.
38314093,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) epidemic, resulting from human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, exhibits distinct regional characteristics. This study undertakes a retrospective analysis of the epidemiological and clinical features of 195 HIV-positive cases in Meizhou, China, from May 1, 2018 to December 31, 2019. Western blotting (WB) confirmed and assessed these cases.","The paper mentions that the study undertakes a retrospective analysis of HIV-positive cases, which implies that HIV sequences from patient samples are reported."
38314093,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) epidemic, resulting from human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection, exhibits distinct regional characteristics. This study undertakes a retrospective analysis of the epidemiological and clinical features of 195 HIV-positive cases in Meizhou, China, from May 1, 2018 to December 31, 2019. Western blotting (WB) confirmed and assessed these cases. Notably, the majority of cases emanated from socio-economic groups with comparatively lower levels of education, with 80% being male. Strikingly, 90% of the cases were found to be in the middle to late stages of infection based on CD4+ T cell counts. Among the 30 different serum antibody profiles examined, reactivity with seven bands (p24, p31, gp41, p51, p66, gp120, and gp160) emerged as the most commonly observed WB pattern. The absence of specific bands, specifically p55 (17.44%), p39 (32.31%), and p17 (25.64%) were most frequent, with the detection frequency of p17 bands significantly reduced among cases in the AIDS and middle stages. An analysis of drug resistance genotypes indicated that, despite viral mutations conferring resistance to certain reverse transcriptase inhibitors, the first-line treatment regimen remained effective for patients in Meizhou. Notably, mutations resistant to protease inhibitors were infrequent (2.7%), suggesting that incorporating protease inhibitors into the treatment regimen may enhance therapeutic outcomes for local patients. These findings provide essential insights into the specific epidemiological patterns, serum antibody profiles, and drug resistance genotypes of HIV-infected patients in Meizhou. Significantly, this research contributes to the formulation of future treatment strategies tailored to the local context.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data, as it focuses on drug resistance genotypes rather than susceptibility testing."
38314093,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"In this study, serum samples collected from the HIV Confirmatory Laboratory of Meizhou People's Hospital Medical Laboratory Center (From May 2008 to December 2019) were analyzed. Primary HIV screening by ELISA or CLIA revealed HIV-positive rates of 0.11% and 1.94%, respectively. ELISA and CLIA are simple and high-throughput; however, they are associated with relatively high false-positive outcomes ( Liu and Jiang, 2004 ; Chen, 2012 ; Wu et al., 2018 ). More rigorous strategies, such as western blotting, are needed for verification. Herein, 195 samples were confirmed as HIV-1 positive by western blotting, with no HIV-2 positive samples being observed. Most HIV-1 infected patients were determined to be in the middle and AIDS stages of the infection. The HIV-1 infected patients are mainly farmers and migrant workers, whose education level is generally low, and who may have less knowledge about AIDS prevention. It is particularly important to further enhance the coverage of AIDS prevention knowledge in the rural population. The highest detection rate of HIV-infected was in July, nonetheless, the reason behind this observation remains to be further investigated. A contemporaneous comparison of HIV-diagnosed cases in 2018 and 2019 suggested an increasing trend in Meizhou. Therefore, HIV prevention and control in the Meizhou community cannot be relaxed. It is necessary to further improve the coverage of HIV screening to achieve early detection and treatment. WB is the gold standard for confirming HIV infection and provides information on virus and host responses. The full WB pattern contains 10 bands that are specific to HIV-1. However, the band patterns of WB vary according to stages of the infection, prevalent viral strains, and cohorts, thus displaying significant regional characteristics. In this study, we observed 30 types of WB patterns from the 195 HIV-positive samples. Reactivity with seven bands (p24, p31, gp41, p51, p66, gp120, and gp160) was the most commonly observed pattern with a proportion of 28.21%, while the complete 10-band pattern occurred in only 9.74% of the samples, which was different from other studies that reported the full pattern as the main outcome ( Sudha et al., 2006 ). Envelope protein bands (gp160, gp120, and gp41), pol protein (p66), and gag protein (p24) were the most prevalent, which was consistent with the previous studies ( Zhong et al., 2012 ; Xie and Chen, 2018 ). p55, p39, and p17 were the most frequently missing bands. The frequencies of p55 and p39 bands were low in samples from every infection stage. Although the detection methods of HIV infection are becoming more and more mature and diversified, there are still some problems that need to be studied and solved. One of the prominent problems is the long incubation period of HIV-infected people and the different window periods of HIV detection methods. We combine the characteristics of the regional HIV epidemic in the detection population and adopt appropriate detection methods to reduce the missed detection rate of HIV-infected people. In the process of antiretroviral drug treatment, clinical laboratory detection of drug resistance genes appears in various problems, we need to further explore, such as how long the interval of drug resistance monitoring detection is appropriate, can timely understand the characteristics of HIV antiviral resistance, is conducive to HIV infected people to develop effective treatment plans.",The paper does not mention any public availability of sequences. The original contributions presented in the study are included in the article.
38314093,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,The original contributions presented in the study are included in the article. Supplementary Material Further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding authors.,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.
38314093,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,195,"In this study, serum samples collected from the HIV Confirmatory Laboratory of Meizhou People's Hospital Medical Laboratory Center (From May 2008 to December 2019) were analyzed. Primary HIV screening by ELISA or CLIA revealed HIV-positive rates of 0.11% and 1.94%, respectively. ELISA and CLIA are simple and high-throughput; however, they are associated with relatively high false-positive outcomes ( Liu and Jiang, 2004 ; Chen, 2012 ; Wu et al., 2018 ). More rigorous strategies, such as western blotting, are needed for verification. Herein, 195 samples were confirmed as HIV-1 positive by western blotting, with no HIV-2 positive samples being observed. Most HIV-1 infected patients were determined to be in the middle and AIDS stages of the infection. The HIV-1 infected patients are mainly farmers and migrant workers, whose education level is generally low, and who may have less knowledge about AIDS prevention. It is particularly important to further enhance the coverage of AIDS prevention knowledge in the rural population. The highest detection rate of HIV-infected was in July, nonetheless, the reason behind this observation remains to be further investigated. A contemporaneous comparison of HIV-diagnosed cases in 2018 and 2019 suggested an increasing trend in Meizhou. Therefore, HIV prevention and control in the Meizhou community cannot be relaxed. It is necessary to further improve the coverage of HIV screening to achieve early detection and treatment. WB is the gold standard for confirming HIV infection and provides information on virus and host responses. The full WB pattern contains 10 bands that are specific to HIV-1. However, the band patterns of WB vary according to stages of the infection, prevalent viral strains, and cohorts, thus displaying significant regional characteristics. In this study, we observed 30 types of WB patterns from the 195 HIV-positive samples. Reactivity with seven bands (p24, p31, gp41, p51, p66, gp120, and gp160) was the most commonly observed pattern with a proportion of 28.21%, while the complete 10-band pattern occurred in only 9.74% of the samples, which was different from other studies that reported the full pattern as the main outcome ( Sudha et al., 2006 ). Envelope protein bands (gp160, gp120, and gp41), pol protein (p66), and gag protein (p24) were the most prevalent, which was consistent with the previous studies ( Zhong et al., 2012 ; Xie and Chen, 2018 ). p55, p39, and p17 were the most frequently missing bands. The frequencies of p55 and p39 bands were low in samples from every infection stage. Although the detection methods of HIV infection are becoming more and more mature and diversified, there are still some problems that need to be studied and solved. One of the prominent problems is the long incubation period of HIV-infected people and the different window periods of HIV detection methods. We combine the characteristics of the regional HIV epidemic in the detection population and adopt appropriate detection methods to reduce the missed detection rate of HIV-infected people. In the process of antiretroviral drug treatment, clinical laboratory detection of drug resistance genes appears in various problems, we need to further explore, such as how long the interval of drug resistance monitoring detection is appropriate, can timely understand the characteristics of HIV antiviral resistance, is conducive to HIV infected people to develop effective treatment plans.","The paper states that 195 samples were confirmed as HIV-positive, indicating that samples were obtained from this number of individuals."
38314093,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This study undertakes a retrospective analysis of the epidemiological and clinical features of 195 HIV-positive cases in Meizhou, China, from May 1, 2018 to December 31, 2019.","The paper mentions that the study was conducted in Meizhou, China, and the samples were obtained from this region."
38314093,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2008 to 2019,"In this study, serum samples collected from the HIV Confirmatory Laboratory of Meizhou People's Hospital Medical Laboratory Center (From May 2008 to December 2019) were analyzed. This study undertakes a retrospective analysis of the epidemiological and clinical features of 195 HIV-positive cases in Meizhou, China, from May 1, 2018 to December 31, 2019.",The paper states that the serum samples were collected from May 2008 to December 2019.
38314093,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"In this study, serum samples collected from the HIV Confirmatory Laboratory of Meizhou People's Hospital Medical Laboratory Center (From May 2008 to December 2019) were analyzed. Primary HIV screening by ELISA or CLIA revealed HIV-positive rates of 0.11% and 1.94%, respectively. ELISA and CLIA are simple and high-throughput; however, they are associated with relatively high false-positive outcomes ( Liu and Jiang, 2004 ; Chen, 2012 ; Wu et al., 2018 ). More rigorous strategies, such as western blotting, are needed for verification. Herein, 195 samples were confirmed as HIV-1 positive by western blotting, with no HIV-2 positive samples being observed. Most HIV-1 infected patients were determined to be in the middle and AIDS stages of the infection. The HIV-1 infected patients are mainly farmers and migrant workers, whose education level is generally low, and who may have less knowledge about AIDS prevention. It is particularly important to further enhance the coverage of AIDS prevention knowledge in the rural population. The highest detection rate of HIV-infected was in July, nonetheless, the reason behind this observation remains to be further investigated. A contemporaneous comparison of HIV-diagnosed cases in 2018 and 2019 suggested an increasing trend in Meizhou. Therefore, HIV prevention and control in the Meizhou community cannot be relaxed. It is necessary to further improve the coverage of HIV screening to achieve early detection and treatment. WB is the gold standard for confirming HIV infection and provides information on virus and host responses. The full WB pattern contains 10 bands that are specific to HIV-1. However, the band patterns of WB vary according to stages of the infection, prevalent viral strains, and cohorts, thus displaying significant regional characteristics. In this study, we observed 30 types of WB patterns from the 195 HIV-positive samples. Reactivity with seven bands (p24, p31, gp41, p51, p66, gp120, and gp160) was the most commonly observed pattern with a proportion of 28.21%, while the complete 10-band pattern occurred in only 9.74% of the samples, which was different from other studies that reported the full pattern as the main outcome ( Sudha et al., 2006 ). Envelope protein bands (gp160, gp120, and gp41), pol protein (p66), and gag protein (p24) were the most prevalent, which was consistent with the previous studies ( Zhong et al., 2012 ; Xie and Chen, 2018 ). p55, p39, and p17 were the most frequently missing bands. The frequencies of p55 and p39 bands were low in samples from every infection stage. Although the detection methods of HIV infection are becoming more and more mature and diversified, there are still some problems that need to be studied and solved. One of the prominent problems is the long incubation period of HIV-infected people and the different window periods of HIV detection methods. We combine the characteristics of the regional HIV epidemic in the detection population and adopt appropriate detection methods to reduce the missed detection rate of HIV-infected people. In the process of antiretroviral drug treatment, clinical laboratory detection of drug resistance genes appears in various problems, we need to further explore, such as how long the interval of drug resistance monitoring detection is appropriate, can timely understand the characteristics of HIV antiviral resistance, is conducive to HIV infected people to develop effective treatment plans.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The process mentions prover sequences from samples were obtained but no other method mentioned.
38314093,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,PR-RT,"In brief, cDNA containing the protease-reverse transcriptase (PR-RT) sequence of HIV-1 was amplified and subjected to high-throughput sequencing.",The paper mentions that the PR-RT gene sequences were obtained and analyzed for drug resistance-related mutations.
38314093,10,What method was used for sequencing?,High-throughput sequencing,"HIV-infected blood samples were sent to the Guangdong AIDS Prevention Center for genotypic resistance testing. In brief, cDNA containing the protease-reverse transcriptase (PR-RT) sequence of HIV-1 was amplified and subjected to high-throughput sequencing. Sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV resistance database ( http://hivdb.stanford.EDU ) for subtype identification, resistance loci, and drug sensitivity analysis.",The paper mentions that the samples were subjected to high-throughput sequencing.
38314093,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Blood samples,"HIV-infected blood samples were sent to the Guangdong AIDS Prevention Center for genotypic resistance testing. In brief, cDNA containing the protease-reverse transcriptase (PR-RT) sequence of HIV-1 was amplified and subjected to high-throughput sequencing. Sequences were submitted to the Stanford University HIV resistance database ( http://hivdb.stanford.EDU ) for subtype identification, resistance loci, and drug sensitivity analysis.",The paper mentions that blood samples were sent for genotypic resistance testing.
38314093,12,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In this study, serum samples collected from the HIV Confirmatory Laboratory of Meizhou People's Hospital Medical Laboratory Center (From May 2008 to December 2019) were analyzed. Primary HIV screening by ELISA or CLIA revealed HIV-positive rates of 0.11% and 1.94%, respectively. ELISA and CLIA are simple and high-throughput; however, they are associated with relatively high false-positive outcomes ( Liu and Jiang, 2004 ; Chen, 2012 ; Wu et al., 2018 ). More rigorous strategies, such as western blotting, are needed for verification. Herein, 195 samples were confirmed as HIV-1 positive by western blotting, with no HIV-2 positive samples being observed. Most HIV-1 infected patients were determined to be in the middle and AIDS stages of the infection. The HIV-1 infected patients are mainly farmers and migrant workers, whose education level is generally low, and who may have less knowledge about AIDS prevention. It is particularly important to further enhance the coverage of AIDS prevention knowledge in the rural population. The highest detection rate of HIV-infected was in July, nonetheless, the reason behind this observation remains to be further investigated. A contemporaneous comparison of HIV-diagnosed cases in 2018 and 2019 suggested an increasing trend in Meizhou. Therefore, HIV prevention and control in the Meizhou community cannot be relaxed. It is necessary to further improve the coverage of HIV screening to achieve early detection and treatment. WB is the gold standard for confirming HIV infection and provides information on virus and host responses. The full WB pattern contains 10 bands that are specific to HIV-1. However, the band patterns of WB vary according to stages of the infection, prevalent viral strains, and cohorts, thus displaying significant regional characteristics. In this study, we observed 30 types of WB patterns from the 195 HIV-positive samples. Reactivity with seven bands (p24, p31, gp41, p51, p66, gp120, and gp160) was the most commonly observed pattern with a proportion of 28.21%, while the complete 10-band pattern occurred in only 9.74% of the samples, which was different from other studies that reported the full pattern as the main outcome ( Sudha et al., 2006 ). Envelope protein bands (gp160, gp120, and gp41), pol protein (p66), and gag protein (p24) were the most prevalent, which was consistent with the previous studies ( Zhong et al., 2012 ; Xie and Chen, 2018 ). p55, p39, and p17 were the most frequently missing bands. The frequencies of p55 and p39 bands were low in samples from every infection stage. Although the detection methods of HIV infection are becoming more and more mature and diversified, there are still some problems that need to be studied and solved. One of the prominent problems is the long incubation period of HIV-infected people and the different window periods of HIV detection methods. We combine the characteristics of the regional HIV epidemic in the detection population and adopt appropriate detection methods to reduce the missed detection rate of HIV-infected people. In the process of antiretroviral drug treatment, clinical laboratory detection of drug resistance genes appears in various problems, we need to further explore, such as how long the interval of drug resistance monitoring detection is appropriate, can timely understand the characteristics of HIV antiviral resistance, is conducive to HIV infected people to develop effective treatment plans.",The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure. The process mentions prover sequences from samples were obtained but no other method mentioned.
38314093,13,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This study undertakes a retrospective analysis of the epidemiological and clinical features of 195 HIV-positive cases in Meizhou, China, from May 1, 2018 to December 31, 2019. All samples were screened by using an HIV antibody/antigen enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) kit (Wantai Bio-Pharm) and HIV Ag/Ab Combo Reagent Kit (Abbott Germany Limited Partnership, USA). The protocol was performed according to the manufacturer's instructions.",The paper does not mention any clinical trial involvement of the patients.
38314093,14,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"In Meizhou, the resistance mutation of NNRTIs is the most prevalent, followed by that of NRTIs. Moreover, the cross-resistance of NRTIs and NNRTIs co-occurred in patients and accounted for 40.54% of drug-resistant mutations, indicating that multidrug resistance is widespread in the region. This may be related to the long-term use of first-line therapeutic drugs, resulting in the continuous accumulation of cross-drug-resistant mutant HIV-1 strains ( Ma et al., 2010 ). Studies have shown that the accumulation of thymidine analogue-resistant mutations causes almost all NRTI resistance ( Zha and Zha, 2006 ).","The paper mentions that some of the patients had long-term use of first-line therapeutic drugs, which indicates they had previously received ARV drugs."
38314093,15,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs","In Meizhou, the resistance mutation of NNRTIs is the most prevalent, followed by that of NRTIs. Moreover, the cross-resistance of NRTIs and NNRTIs co-occurred in patients and accounted for 40.54% of drug-resistant mutations, indicating that multidrug resistance is widespread in the region. This may be related to the long-term use of first-line therapeutic drugs, resulting in the continuous accumulation of cross-drug-resistant mutant HIV-1 strains ( Ma et al., 2010 ). Studies have shown that the accumulation of thymidine analogue-resistant mutations causes almost all NRTI resistance ( Zha and Zha, 2006 ).","The paper mentions drug resistance mutations to NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs"
38314093,16,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"AZT, 3TC, EFV, NVP","The initial antiviral treatment regimen consisted of two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) plus efavirenz (EFV)/nevirapine (NVP) in 31 cases (83.79%) or zidovudine (AZT)/lamivudine (3TC) plus EFV in 6 cases (16.21%). Pro-RT gene sequences from the samples were obtained and analyzed for drug resistance-related mutations. Among the 37 PCR-positive cases, 23 carried one or more drug-resistance mutations.","The paper mentions that the patients had received AZT, 3TC, EFV, or NVP as part of their treatment regimen before sample sequencing."
38427738,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"To elucidate INSTI resistance mechanisms, we propagated HIV-1 in the presence of escalating concentrations of the INSTI dolutegravir. HIV-1 became resistant to dolutegravir by sequentially acquiring mutations in the envelope glycoprotein (Env) and the nucleocapsid protein. To investigate how HIV-1 develops high-level resistance to INSTIs, we infected the SupT1 T cell line with the HIV-1 NL4-3 clone and serially propagated the virus in the presence of increasing concentrations of DTG.","The paper does not mention any HIV sequences obtained from patient samples. Instead, it focuses on laboratory-adapted strains of HIV-1 and their mutations in response to drug pressure."
38427738,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"We demonstrate that the Env variants selected in the presence of DTG exhibit multi-log resistance to INSTIs in spreading infections but more modest (~10-fold) resistance to RT and PR inhibitors and ~2- to 3-fold resistance to the entry inhibitor T-20 and the capsid inhibitor LEN. We previously reported that mutations at Env-541 and Env-558 reduce sensitivity to DTG. We also detected multiple mutations in the matrix (MA)-, capsid (CA)-, and RT-coding regions, but these mutations did not confer resistance (fig. S2, A and B).","The results presented in this paper demonstrate that HIV-1 can accumulate mutations in Env and NC independently of drug target gene mutations, leading to broad phenotypic resistance to multiple antiretrovirals, including DTG, EFdA, and RT inhibitors. To verify the in vitro drug susceptibility data with the resistant variants selected in our study, we had focused on the spread and multi-round infections of M-tropic VSV-G--pseudotyped NL4-3/7XEnv and VanD APPROACH derivatives."
38427738,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any public accession numbers for the sequences obtained during the study.
38427738,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not provided,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.
38427738,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"To elucidate INSTI resistance mechanisms, we propagated HIV-1 in the presence of escalating concentrations of the INSTI dolutegravir. To verify these results, we performed repeated passaging experiments with WT NL4-3 in the presence of DTG (denoted as culture 2 and culture 3 in fig. S2). We also initiated long-term propagation experiments with the NL4-3 Env-A541V mutant, as this mutant is frequently detected early in selection experiments. We also initiated long-term propagation experiments with the NL4-3 Env-A541V mutant, as this mutant is frequently detected early in selection experiments.  The NL4-3 strain used in the experiments described above is a laboratory-adapted, subtype B, CXCR4-tropic HIV-1 isolate.",The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for HIV sequencing. It focuses on laboratory experiments with HIV-1 strains.
38427738,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,No evidence,The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained since it does not report on patient samples.
38427738,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,0,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any information about the years from which sequenced samples were obtained, as it does not involve patient samples."
38427738,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Not relevant,"To investigate how HIV-1 develops high-level resistance to INSTIs, we infected the SupT1 T cell line with the HIV-1 NL4-3 clone and serially propagated the virus in the presence of increasing concentrations of DTG. The NL4-3 strain used in the experiments described above is a laboratory-adapted, subtype B, CXCR4-tropic HIV-1 isolate.",The paper does not discuss any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It focuses on the sequencing of genomic DNA extracted from infected cells.
38427738,9,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"gag, pol, env, vpu","To identify the genetic changes in the viral genome responsible for DTG resistance, we extracted genomic DNA from the infected cells at a number of time points and performed sequencing analysis of gag, pol, env, and vpu.","The paper mentions sequencing analysis of the *gag*, *pol*, *env*, and *vpu* regions to identify genetic changes responsible for DTG resistance."
38427738,10,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,"To investigate how HIV-1 develops high-level resistance to INSTIs, we infected the SupT1 T cell line with the HIV-1 NL4-3 clone and serially propagated the virus in the presence of increasing concentrations of DTG. We also initiated long-term propagation experiments with the NL4-3 Env-A541V mutant, as this mutant is frequently detected early in selection experiments. The NL4-3 strain used in the experiments described above is a laboratory-adapted, subtype B, CXCR4-tropic HIV-1 isolate. All of the NL4-3 cultures selected the Env-A541V mutation and Env mutations that disrupt N-linked glycosylation in the gp120 V1/V2 region. All samples were analyzed using flow cytometry. We also initiated long-term propagation experiments with the NL4-3 Env-A541V mutant, as this mutant is frequently detected early in selection experiments. The NL4-3 strain used in the experiments described above is a laboratory-adapted, subtype B, CXCR4-tropic HIV-1 isolate. All of the NL4-3 cultures selected the Env-A541V mutation and Env mutations that disrupt N-linked glycosylation in the gp120 V1/V2 region. All samples were analyzed using flow cytometry.","The paper does not mention the specific sequencing method used for sequencing the *gag*, *pol*, *env*, and *vpu* regions."
38427738,11,What type of samples were sequenced?,Cell culture supernatants,"We demonstrate that the Env variants selected in the presence of DTG exhibit multi-log resistance to INSTIs in spreading infections but more modest (~10-fold) resistance to RT and PR inhibitors and ~2- to 3-fold resistance to the entry inhibitor T-20 and the capsid inhibitor LEN. We previously reported that mutations at Env-541 and Env-558 reduce sensitivity to DTG. We also detected multiple mutations in the matrix (MA)-, capsid (CA)-, and RT-coding regions, but these mutations did not confer resistance (fig. S2, A and B). To verify these results, we performed repeated passaging experiments with WT NL4-3 in the presence of DTG (denoted as culture 2 and culture 3 in fig. S2).","The paper describes the sequencing of genomic DNA extracted from infected cells, indicating that the samples sequenced were from cell cultures, not patient samples."
