38864613,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"ISL and LEN are being investigated as once-weekly combination oral therapy for the treatment of HIV-1. Here, we characterized ISL and LEN in vitro to assess combinatorial antiviral activity, cytotoxicity, and the potential for interactions between the two compounds.",The paper focuses on in vitro studies and resistance selection experiments related to HIV-1 and the drug combination of islatravir and lenacapavir. There is no mention of patient samples or clinical data involving HIV sequences from patients.
38864613,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"To assess potential antagonistic interactions between ISL and LEN that could affect the ability of the combination to inhibit HIV-1 replication, the antiviral activities of the combination across a range of concentrations were evaluated in a multiple-cycle antiviral assay using the combination of d4T and RBV as a positive control for antagonism. Multiple-cycle antiviral assay in MT4 cells which express green fluorescent protein upon infection (MT4-GFP cells) ISL and LEN were evaluated for their ability to prevent HIV-1 infection in MT4-GFP cells as described in Diamond et al. (6). In this method, HIV-1 replication is monitored using MT4-gag-GFP clone D3, MT4 cells with a GFP reporter gene, the expression of which is dependent on the HIV-1 expressed proteins, Tat and Rev (13). Infection of MT4-GFP cells with HIV-1 results in GFP expression approximately 24 h post-infection. Proviral variants used for these studies were generated by site-directed mutagenesis in the WT R8 proviral plasmid (14). Virus was produced by transfection in 293T cells.","The abstract and results sections contain information about the in vitro susceptibility of HIV-1 to the drug combination, including IC50 values and the effect of mutations on drug efficacy."
38864613,3,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,Sequences from resistance selection experiments have been deposited in GenBank under accession numbers PP827803 -- PP828572.,"The paper explicitly states that sequences from the resistance selection experiments have been deposited in GenBank, indicating that they are publicly available."
38864613,4,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PP827803 -- PP828572,Sequences from resistance selection experiments have been deposited in GenBank under accession numbers PP827803 -- PP828572.,The paper provides the GenBank accession numbers for the sequences obtained from the resistance selection experiments in the data availability section.
38864613,5,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,The paper does not mention any individuals from whom HIV sequences were obtained. It focuses on in vitro studies and resistance selection experiments rather than clinical samples.,The content of the paper indicates that the study was conducted on in vitro systems rather than on clinical samples from individuals.
38864613,6,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,The paper does not mention any countries from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on in vitro studies and resistance selection experiments rather than clinical samples.,The study does not report any geographic information regarding the samples as it does not involve clinical samples.
38864613,7,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,The paper does not mention any years from which sequenced samples were obtained. It focuses on in vitro studies and resistance selection experiments rather than clinical samples.,The content does not provide any information regarding the years of sample collection.
